\par
Editorial New York Herald.\par
\par
_Referring to a Whole Page._\par
\par
"If an American dramatist or novelist had taken for the ground work of a\par
play or work of fiction the story of the Bidwell family to-day related\par
on another page of the Herald, all European critics would have told him\par
that the story was too 'American,' too vast in its outlines, too high in\par
its colors, too merely 'big' in fact.\par
\par
"The story has its lesson. The play is not a mere spectacle. The lesson\par
is that in the doing and undoing of wrong the Bidwell family expended\par
enough ability and energy to stock a good many reigning European\par
families for generations.\par
\par
"Let the Comedie Humaine write itself and it will outwrite Balzac."\par
\par
\par
Hon. Lyman J. Gage.\par
\par
Having read the Bidwell book I believe it will benefit every one to read\par
this marvellous history of human experience.\par
\par
Aside from its dramatic interest there are great moral lessons involved\par
of especial value to young men and employees in positions of trust.\par
\par
Therefore, I recommend this book as unique and a valuable acquisition\par
for home and office.\par
\par
\par
From Chas. M. Stead, Union League Club, New York.\par
\par
"_Dear Sir_--I read your book with a good deal of interest, and would\par
like to change it for a higher-priced binding if you have one."\par
\par
\par
The Worcester Spy.\par
\par
"Mr. Bidwell's book has been compared with Dumas' famous 'Monte\par
Christo.' The extraordinary character of its adventures, indeed, would\par
render it dramatic and powerful as fiction; as human truth, it is simply\par
overwhelming. No one can read this book unmoved. From every conceivable\par
standpoint, physiological, sociological, and literary, it is a marvel."\par
\par
\par
Philip W. Moen.\par
\par
Mr. Moen, of Washburn & Moen, Worcester, Mass., writes: "I have read Mr.\par
George Bidwell's book with the deepest interest. It is a book that\par
deserves to be widely read, and I am very glad to recommend it."\par
\par
\par
A Niece of Oliver Wendell Holmes\par
\par
writes: "_Few books have so stirred my mind_ for years as the book by\par
George Bidwell. Hearing of the book, prejudice immediately seized me\par
against it. The history given by himself, to be interesting at all must\par
be sensational, therefore disastrous to morals. _So avowed prejudiced\par
thought; and, determined to find fault, I began this remarkable\par
history._ IT IS IMPOSSIBLE TO FIND FAULT WITH THE BOOK, WHICH IS\par
VALUABLE AND WONDERFULLY ABSORBING."\par
\par
\par
From Ira D. Sankey, Esq.\par
\par
"MR. GEORGE BIDWELL, _Dear Sir_--I have read with great interest your\par
book, and believe it will do much good among young men wherever read.\par
Your life is a proof and your book a burning record of the truth that\par
'Whatsoever a man soweth that shall he also reap.' I believe in throwing\par
light into all the dark places of this life, that men, seeing the\par
dangers, they may avoid them. I wish you success."\par
\par
\par
From Hon. Robert G. Ingersoll.\par
\par
"GEORGE BIDWELL, ESQ.:\par
\par
_My Dear Sir_--Knowing as I do that you will tell a candid story of your\par
career, I believe you will do good. Crime springs mostly from a lack of\par
intelligence and imagination. Only the foolish can think that the\par
practice of vice is the road to joy. As a matter of fact, the wrong does\par
not pay. You have, in your remarkable book, made this fact perfectly\par
clear, and you will enforce this great truth on the platform. _In the\par
world of crime success is failure._ Good luck to you."\par
\par
\par
Rev. Dr. Edward Beecher\par
\par
writes; "I recommend this book to the friends of morality."\par
\par
\par
Office of Street's Insurance Agency, Hartford, Conn.\par
\par
"MR. GEORGE BIDWELL, _Dear Sir_--A clergyman consulted with me regarding\par
his son, who had fallen into bad associations, taken part in many small\par
thefts, and seemed hardened against shame or dread of exposure. I\par
believe the mean, dangerous boy has become a man by reading your book."\par
Yours very truly,\par
\par
F. F. STREET, Hartford, Conn.\par
