\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER I.\par
\par
HAD THERE BEEN WISDOM THERE?\par
\par
\par
We lived in South Brooklyn, near to old No. 13, the Degraw Street Public\par
School. To that I was sent, and there got all the education I was ever\par
fated to have at any school, except the school of life and experience.\par
\par
I attended for some years, and even now I cannot recall without a smile\par
the absurd incompetency of every one connected with the institution and\par
their utter ignorance of the art of imparting knowledge to children.\par
\par
At home I had picked up that grand art of reading, and went to school to\par
learn the other two R's, with any trifle that I might come across\par
floating around promiscuously.\par
\par
I certainly hope our much-lauded public schools are conducted on better\par
lines now than then; if not, they are frauds from the foundation. The\par
instruction in No. 13 was so lax and radically bad that the whole\par
governing body and the principal ought to have been sent to the\par
penitentiary on the charge of false pretense for drawing their salaries\par
and giving nothing in return. And yet I remember when examination day\par
came, instead of the committee investigating the progress of the pupils,\par
it usually turned into a mere hallelujah chorus upon our "grand public\par
school system."\par
\par
Here is a remarkable fact: I seldom missed a promotion and passed from\par
grade to grade until within two years I found myself in Junior "A," the\par
next to the highest class in the school, just as ignorant as my\par
classmates, and that is saying much.\par
\par
It was all very pitiful. My blood boils even now when I think of the\par
traitors chosen and paid to see me fully equipped and armed to begin the\par
battle of life who left me with phantom weapons which would shiver into\par
fragments at the first shock of conflict.\par
\par
I left Junior A of old No. 13, with its algebra, logic, philosophy\par
(heaven save the word!) and advanced grammar, unable to write a\par
grammatical sentence. I had been taught spelling out of an expositor--a\par
sort of pocket dictionary containing about fifteen hundred words. Most\par
of these, with their definitions, parrotlike, I had learned to spell,\par
but never once in all my school experience had I been taught the\par
derivation of a single word. Indeed, I took it for granted that in the\par
good old days Adam had invented the words much as he named the animals,\par
and, of course, supposed that he spoke good English. The knowledge of\par
history I gained at No. 13 was strictly limited and exceedingly\par
primitive. I knew the Jews in the old days were a bad lot. That Brutus\par
had slain Caesar. That the Mayflower had landed our fathers on Plymouth\par
Rock. That wicked George III. was a tyrant, and that the boys in Boston\par
had thrown a tea-kettle at his head. I knew all about our George and the\par
cherry tree, and there my historical knowledge ended.\par
\par
So here I was launched out in the world a model scholar! Stamped as\par
proficient in grammar, history, logic, philosophy and arithmetic, but\par
yet in useful knowledge a barbarian, unable to spell or even write a\par
grammatical letter and unversed in the ways of the world--a world, too,\par
where I would be cast entirely upon my own resources.\par
\par
My home life was happy. My father had lost his grip on the world, but\par
his faith in the Unseen remained. My mother, caring little for this\par
life, lived in and for the spiritual. To her heaven was a place as much\par
as the country village where she was born. She was never tired of\par
talking to us children about its golden streets and the rest there after\par
the toils and pains of life. But, boylike, we discounted all she said,\par
and felt we wanted some of this world before we knocked at the gates of\par
the next.\par
\par
We loved our mother, but her soul was too gentle to keep in restraint\par
hot, fiery youths like my brothers and myself. On the whole we were good\par
boys, and I suppose caused her no more pain than the average youngsters.\par
Perhaps the keynote of her character can best be found in the following\par
incident, if that which was of daily occurrence could be called an\par
incident:\par
\par
Every night of my life in those days she would come to my bed to pray\par
over me, ever saying, as she kissed me or clasped my hand: "My son,\par
remember if you were to pass your whole life here in poverty and\par
hardship it would not much matter so long as you attain to the Heavenly\par
Rest." This teaching would have been well had she only taught me some\par
worldly wisdom with it, but that all-essential knowledge was kept from\par
me, I being left to learn the ways of man in that terrible school of\par
experience. The consequence being that when after some months I was\par
launched out in life I was a ripe and apt victim to be caught in the\par
world's huge snare. In fact, had my parents designed me to become a\par
traveler in the Primrose Way they could not have educated me to better\par
purpose.\par
\par
Save when in the school I had never been permitted to associate with\par
other boys, but was kept in the house, and up to my sixteenth year\par
hardly dreamed there was evil in the world. I was told much about the\par
"wicked," but thought that meant those who smoked tobacco or drank\par
whisky. I hardly thought any women came under that category, but if any,\par
then it must mean those who came around selling apples and oranges. The\par
reader will see that when once away from the shelter of home, in\par
threading the world's devious ways, I would be crossing the roaring\par
torrent "on the perilous footing of a spear," all but certain to fall\par
into the flood beneath.\par
\par
During my last year at school and for a long time after leaving it, my\par
father and mother were never tired of talking about my good education.\par
Possibly they were not very good judges, but I am confident that they,\par
after all, did not realize the importance of a boy being well equipped\par
in that regard. Their thoughts and minds were so bent on the other\par
world, and things unseen bulked so hugely on their mental vision, that\par
there was small space left for things of this earth. They, good, simple\par
souls, were made for and ought to have lived in the Golden Age, when all\par
men were brave and all women true, where neighborly eyes reflected the\par
love and faith within; but in our utilitarian days they were sadly out\par
of place, and little wonder if they had lost their way in this world.\par
\par
In their intense longing for the life beyond the grave, their passionate\par
desire to walk the streets of gold, they, by their actions, seemed to\par
forget that we were on this earth, and that we were here with many sharp\par
reminders of the fact.\par
\par
The same guilelessness was manifested in their choice of our home\par
reading. The books I was allowed access to in the house were "The Life\par
of King David," "The History of Jerusalem," "Baxter's Saints' Rest,"\par
"The Immortal Dreamer's Pilgrim" and Fox's "Book of Martyrs." His first\par
martyr is Stephen, and such was my gross ignorance of history that I\par
always supposed Stephen had been martyred by the Church of Rome. Here\par
was mental food for a boy who had his own way to make in the world.\par
\par
[Illustration: A HOME CHRISTMAS DINNER VS. IN A CELL. "WHERE IS OUR\par
WANDERING BOY TO-NIGHT?"]\par
\par
Craving other mental food than "The Life of David," I used to club\par
pennies with a chum and buy that delectable sheet, "Ned Buntline's Own,"\par
then in fear and trembling would creep to an upper room and read "The\par
Haunted House" or "The Ghost of Castle Ivy" until my hair stood on end\par
in a sort of ecstatic horror; or the stirring adventures of "Jack the\par
Rover" or "Pirate Chief" until my brain took fire and a mighty impulse\par
stirred every fibre impelling me to follow in their footsteps.\par
\par
I had remained idly at home for some six months after my release from\par
school, when one night my father returned from New York and said: "My\par
son, I have found a situation for you." That was delightful news, and\par
when I went to bed that night I was too excited to sleep.\par
\par
The future was full of color, red and purple, of course. Happily for me\par
the future in all its black misery was hidden behind those gilded\par
clouds.\par
\par
So now at sixteen I was about to sail out of harbor, and how equipped!\par
\par
Absolutely without education, void of worldly wisdom, and in my boyish\par
brain dividing the world into two sections. In one was King David\par
slaying the Phillistines or dancing before the Ark. In the other was\par
Jack the Rover and the Pirate Chief. How easy to guess the rest! Yet I\par
was not a bad boy--far from it. I only needed wise guidance and good\par
companionship, and as the ignorance and crudity of my character dropped\par
off, the innate virtue--mine by lawful heritage--would have been\par
developed. But pitchforked into the wild whirl of Wall street and its\par
fast set of gilded youth, the gates of the Primrose Way to destruction\par
were held wide open to my eager feet.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER II.\par
\par
"'TWAS EVER THUS." OF COURSE IT WAS.\par
\par
\par
The situation my father had obtained for me was with a sugar broker by\par
the name of Waterbury. He was a partner in a large refinery, his office\par
being in South Water street. He was a nice, conservative old man, and\par
let things run on easily. His chief clerk, Mr. Ambler, was every inch a\par
gentleman, who, quickly perceiving what an ignoramus I was, out of the\par
goodness of his heart resolved to teach me something.\par
\par
There were two sharp young men in our office. They liked me well enough,\par
but used to guy me unmercifully for my simplicity and clumsiness. One of\par
them, Harry by name, was something of a scapegrace, and soon acquired\par
quite a power over me. I stood in much fear of his ridicule, and\par
frequently did things for which my conscience reproached me, rather than\par
stand the fire of his raillery. The greatest harm he did me was in\par
firing my imagination with stories of Wall street, of the fortunes that\par
were and could be made in the gold room or on 'Change. He made tolerably\par
clear the modus operandi of speculators, and I secretly resolved that\par
some day I, too, would try my fortune.\par
\par
My friend Mr. Ambler's health was bad, and frequent attacks of illness\par
caused him to be away from the office for weeks at a time, and that\par
meant much loss to me. When I had been there about a year, he resigned\par
his position and went as manager for a factory in New Haven. But before\par
leaving he interested himself so far in my welfare as to secure me a\par
position with a firm of brokers in New street, at a salary of $10 a\par
week. My employers were good fellows, lovers of pleasure and men of the\par
world, not scrupling to talk freely with me of their various adventures\par
out of business hours. I had lost much of my awkwardness and gauche\par
manners, and under the $10 a week arrangement began to dress fairly\par
well. My employers did a brokerage business and speculated as well on\par
their own account. My duties were decidedly light and pleasant, and\par
brought me into contact with some of the sharpest as well as the most\par
famous men in the street. Among them was a brilliant young man of my own\par
age, who took a great fancy to me, and frequently proposed that we\par
should start for ourselves. Being doubtful of my powers, I shrank from\par
risking my scanty funds in any speculative venture. Much to my mother's\par
concern, I had begun attending the theatre, and one night, on my friend\par
Ed Weed's invitation, I went with him to Niblo's. After the performance\par
we went to supper at Delmonico's, and I was perfectly fascinated by the\par
company and surroundings, going home long past midnight a different man\par
than I had last left it.\par
\par
The next day Ed came to the office and invited me to lunch, where, after\par
making some disparaging remarks about the country cut of my garments, he\par
offered to introduce me to his tailor, who was never in a hurry for his\par
money. After business that day we walked uptown together, and, prompted\par
by Ed, I ordered $150 worth of garments, then went to his outfitter and\par
ordered nearly an equal amount in shirts, ties, gloves, etc.\par
\par
One amusing result was that when, a few days later, I walked down to our\par
office, comme il faut in garb, my employers raised my salary to $30 a\par
week, but this left me poorer than when I had husbanded my poor little\par
$10. Soon after, piloted by Ed, I ventured $50 on a margin in gold.\par
Unluckily, I won, invested again and again, and within fourteen days was\par
$284 ahead. I paid my tailor and outfitter's bill, bought a $100 watch\par
on credit, and gave a wine supper on borrowed money. Soon after this I\par
went to board at the old St. Nicholas, the then fashionable hotel. From\par
that time I began to drift more and more away from home influences.\par
\par
Soon after the wine supper episode I threw up my position, and Ed and I\par
started on our own account under the name of E. Weed & Co. My partner's\par
parents were wealthy, and his father had been well known in the street,\par
which fact gave us standing.\par
\par
The years I speak of were fortunate ones for Wall street, stocks of\par
every kind on the boom, the general wealth of the country massing up by\par
leaps and bounds, and every kind of speculative enterprise being\par
launched. Our firm history was the usual one of broker firms in that\par
tumultuous arena--the Wall street of those days--commissions in plenty,\par
a large income, but one's bank account never growing, for what was made\par
by day in the wild excitement of shifting values was thrown away amid\par
wilder scenes at night. Those, too, were, indeed, the flush times for\par
the professional gambler; for men were not content unless they burned\par
the candle at both ends. Day faro banks were open everywhere around the\par
Exchange, and enormous sums were nightly staked in the uptown games.\par
These were everywhere--all protected, and the proprietors invested their\par
money for rent, fixtures, etc., with as much confidence, and kept their\par
doors open as freely, as if embarked in a legitimate speculation.\par
Hundreds who spent the business hours of the day in the mad excitement\par
of the Exchange flocked around the green cloth at night, devoting the\par
same intensity of thought and brain to the turning of a card which\par
earlier in the day they had given to the market reports of the world.\par
Small wonder that death cut such wide swaths in the army of brokers.\par
Statistics show that it was more fatal to belong to that army than to an\par
army in the field.\par
\par
Ed loved to have me with him, and I used to accompany him to a game,\par
then quite famous, run by John Morrissey, who later became a member of\par
Congress. At this time I never ventured a single bet, and did not like\par
to visit the place. But Ed would beg me to go, and always promised\par
faithfully not to remain more than twenty minutes. Of course, his twenty\par
minutes would lengthen into hours. Frequently I would take a chair into\par
a corner and go to sleep until he left the game, that being almost any\par
hour between midnight and morning. As usual, in such places, an elegant\par
supper was served free at midnight. The proprietor was always rather\par
attentive to me, and, to give him the credit due, seemed anxious that I\par
should not play. At supper he always reserved the chair next to himself\par
for me. One night while standing beside the roulette wheel, no one was\par
playing, and the dealer was idly whirling the ball, a sudden impulse\par
seized me, and the ball then rolling, I pulled a $20 bill from my pocket\par
and threw it down on the red remarking, "I'll lose that to pay for my\par
suppers." Unhappily I won, and, laughing, turned to the dealer and said:\par
"Here, give me my money. I am done," and a moment later went out with my\par
friend, fully determined never more to gamble. But, being in there the\par
next night, I, of course, ventured again. Again I was so unfortunate as\par
to win, and within a short time staked and lost or won nightly. But\par
something worse than gambling was ahead of me, just at the very door.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER III.\par
\par
A LICENSED PIRATE.\par
\par
\par
We had latterly somewhat neglected business--our real business being at\par
night, when we made the pursuit of pleasure hard work. Soon the finances\par
of our firm not only ran low, but were on three several occasions\par
exhausted, so that we not only had recourse to borrowing, but were\par
barely saved from bankruptcy by liberal donations from Ed's parents. His\par
father was a fine, jolly old gentleman, and took it quite a matter of\par
course that it was his duty to help us off the rocks when we ran on\par
them. My partner took everything easy, but I, having no indulgent parent\par
behind me ever ready to draw a check, began to be uneasy over the\par
financial situation. Strangely enough, however, it never occurred to me\par
to cut down my personal expenses, and I continued living at the same\par
extravagant rate as when money was plenty--dining and wining and being\par
dined and wined. Just here an important character, one destined to have\par
an influence for evil on my future life, came upon the scene, and I will\par
halt for a moment in my narrative to give some account of him.\par
\par
This man was James Irving, popularly known as Jimmy Irving, chief of the\par
New York Detective Force, and a bad-hearted, worthless scamp he was. I\par
was with several friends in the Fifth Avenue Hotel one cold January\par
night when he came in, and one of our party, knowing him, introduced us.\par
He was a man of medium height, rather heavy set, blond mustache,\par
pleasant eyes, but with a weak mouth and chin, and a flushed face,\par
telling a tale of dissipation. It was when Boss Tweed ruled supreme in\par
New York and the whole administration was honeycombed with corruption.\par
Except under similar political conditions could such a man attain to so\par
responsible an office in a great city as that of chief of the detective\par
force--a position which at that time invested him with all but\par
autocratic power. An old rounder and barroom loafer, without one\par
attribute of true manliness and not possessed of any quality which would\par
point him out as a fit man for the place. Nevertheless, when the\par
position became vacant his political pull caused his selection. From\par
being a mere detective on the staff he became chief. And truly this\par
meant something in those days. The great civil war had but lately ended,\par
and the country was still reeling from the mighty conflict. The flush\par
times, resultant from the enormous money issue of the Government, kept\par
everything booming. The foundations of society were shaken and vice no\par
longer hid itself in the dark caves and dens of the great city. The\par
Tenderloin, with its multifarious and widereaching influence for evil,\par
was then created, and the police of the city reaped a royal revenue from\par
its thousand dens of vice for their protection. To be captain of the\par
Tenderloin precinct meant an extra weekly income of $1,000 at least. He\par
had the lion's share; about an equal amount went to Headquarters, to be\par
divided between the Chief of Police and the gang, Irving being one of\par
the half dozen who had pull enough to get in the ring. The Tenderloin\par
lieutenant, roundsman and sergeant came in for about $100, $50 and $25 a\par
week, while the common patrolman got what blackmail he could on his own\par
account from the unhappy women of the street. These were considered\par
lawful game, and woe betide the poor unfortunate who refused to pay the\par
tax. Too well she found it meant a violent arrest, accompanied with\par
brutal treatment, a night in a filthy cell, and then to be dragged\par
before the magistrate, who was some ward heeler, hand in glove with the\par
police. The form of a trial and a speedy "six months on the island" from\par
the lips of the judge followed.\par
\par
From Spring street to Tenth, Broadway was full of night\par
games--faro--each and all paying large sums for protection. This money,\par
however, did not all go to Police Headquarters, there being a host of\par
parasites aside from the police. The shoulder-hitter politicians, each\par
with his pull, and each having a claim to his percentage. Most of the\par
Broadway games were known as square games, but then there was the host\par
of skin games in the Bowery, Chatham square, Houston, Prince and other\par
streets. The Eighth Ward and all Broadway were considered the lawful\par
happy hunting grounds for Headquarters detectives, and this by long\par
prescription. Outside of that they had no claim save only to a\par
percentage from the Tenderloin. But the protection money paid by the\par
swindling games around Chatham square, Bayard street, and the whole\par
length of the Bowery, by a sort of sacred prescription, belonged to the\par
captains of those precincts, save only that part absorbed by the\par
politicians of the district who had a pull. These usually were the\par
Aldermen and Councilmen with their henchmen.\par
\par
[Illustration: "PULLING OUT A $20 BILL, I THREW IT DOWN."--Page 27.]\par
\par
But to return to my friend, Capt. Jim Irving, who, before our party\par
separated, had opened three bottles of wine. Before leaving I had asked\par
him to call on me at the St. Nicholas. The next day he came and invited\par
me to take a drive with him to Fordham the following Sunday. On Sunday\par
he appeared behind a fast trotting horse, and in every respect an\par
elegant turnout. During our drive he casually remarked that he had paid\par
a thousand dollars for the rig, and as his pay was some two thousand\par
dollars per annum I easily figured that his rig and diamond pin had cost\par
him about a year's salary. It was a lovely morning, not cold, but\par
bracing, just the day for a ride. We started for Fordham, but changed\par
our minds and drove to the High Bridge, through Harlem lane, and well\par
out into Westchester County. Returning, we stopped at O'Brien's Hotel\par
for dinner. We fared sumptuously the whole day through, our dinner being\par
particularly fine, my companion paying for everything, and really it was\par
all highly enjoyable. He had a vast fund of anecdote, and many strange\par
stories of city life and adventure, which naturally would be expected\par
from one in his position. Many of those we passed or met during the day\par
were personally known to him, and some, both women as well as men, who\par
were then clothed in purple and fine linen, had histories, and many had\par
at some period of their lives looked on life from the seamy side, having\par
passed through strange vicissitudes.\par
\par
Soon after dark we returned to my hotel, and after dinner, lighting our\par
cigars, we started for Police Headquarters. There he attended to some\par
routine business, having introduced me to two of his chief detectives.\par
Many who read this will recognize the men, but in this narrative they\par
will be known as Stanley and White. I will not further describe them\par
now; as they will appear in the story from time to time, the reader will\par
be able to judge what manner of men they were.\par
\par
For the next eight weeks my life went on much the same as usual. In our\par
business we made some money, but by one unfortunate investment lost our\par
entire capital, and what proved worse for me, my partner's health began\par
to fail. Dissipation, late and heavy dinners and irregular hours began\par
to break a not over-strong constitution; consequently one Saturday he\par
abruptly announced his intention of withdrawing from the partnership to\par
take a trip to Europe. There was nothing to divide save the furniture in\par
our office, which he presented to me. The following Wednesday he sailed\par
with two members of his family. I saw him off, bidding him what proved\par
to be a last farewell. I left the wharf feeling very lonely and\par
miserable. It may be well to remark here that he died a year later in\par
Italy, one more victim of a fast life, while I was spared, but took no\par
warning from his fate. In truth, I was in the Primrose Way, which is\par
ever found a most tormenting and unhappy thoroughfare.\par
\par
How I grieved all through the twenty years of captivity that I had not\par
had the moral courage to start afresh upon a basis of truth, sobriety\par
and honorable endeavor.\par
\par
Instead of cutting down my expenses, I rather became more extravagant,\par
fearing my companions would suspect I was pressed for money. How much\par
more manly had I called them together and told them we must part\par
company.\par
\par
Meeting Irving from time to time, he was most flattering in his\par
attentions, while I was young enough and silly enough to be pleased with\par
his notice. One evening about this time I met him while coming out of\par
Wallack's Theatre. Shaking hands warmly, he invited me to supper at what\par
was then known as upper Delmonico's. After supper, walking to the St.\par
Denis Hotel at Broadway and 11th street, we found Detectives Stanley and\par
White. Here wine was ordered, and long after midnight we parted, they\par
first having exacted a promise to dine with them the following night at\par
Delmonico's, at the same time stating that they wished to make me a\par
business proposition.\par
\par
The next evening White came in and said we would dine at a restaurant at\par
Sixth avenue and 31st street, instead of at Delmonico's; then he left\par
me, upon my promise to be on hand.\par
\par
At eleven I arrived, and entering the restaurant was at once recognized\par
by a waiter, evidently on the lookout, and ushered into a private room\par
upstairs. Only White had arrived, but soon Irving and Stanley came, and\par
supper was ordered. With such gentry as these wine is always in order.\par
Then they became confidential, and the conversation turned to the\par
subject of making money. Very skillfully they extracted the confession\par
that I had none. When excited by the talk and the wine I cried out, "By\par
heaven, I want money!" Stanley grasped my hand and said: "Of course you\par
do; a man's a fool without it." Irving interjected: "Are you game to do\par
us a favor and make ten thousand for yourself?" "But how?" I gasped. "Go\par
to Europe and negotiate some stolen bonds we have, will you?"\par
\par
For $10,000 to become accessory to a crime!\par
\par
It was an appalling proposition, and I shrank from it with an aversion I\par
could not conceal any more than he and his confederates could conceal\par
their chagrin over the way I took it, and over the fact that their\par
secret had been imparted to another. More wine was ordered, and before\par
we parted I had promised not only secrecy, but, worse still, I had also\par
promised to consider the proposition and give my answer the following\par
night.\par
\par
As my evil genus would have it, that very morning I had a visit in my\par
office from the agent of my landlord, requesting arrears of rent, and\par
from a tradesman whom I was owing, demanding immediate payment of an\par
overdue bill.\par
\par
Pressed for money as I was, the $10,000 seemed a large sum and offered\par
an easy way out of my difficulties. I shall never forget that day nor\par
how its slow minutes dragged during the mental struggle. Time after time\par
I said: "What could I not do with $10,000?" How vast the possibilities\par
before me with that sum at my command! Then, after all, had not the\par
owner of these bonds lost them forever, and why should not I have a\par
share instead of letting these villain detectives keep all? And through\par
all I kept saying to myself: "This, of course, is only speculation. I\par
will never do this thing."\par
\par
At last the stars came out, and I started for a long walk alone up\par
Broadway to Fifth avenue and into the Park. Since that Park was formed\par
few men have ever passed its walks in whose bosoms raged such a tumult\par
as in mine. I was young, in love with pleasure, and poverty seemed a\par
fearful thing. I kept saying; "I cannot do this thing!" and then I would\par
add: "How am I to keep up appearances, and how am I to pay my debts?"\par
Unhappily, I had taken an enemy into the citadel. In the misery of the\par
struggle I drank heavily.\par
\par
In my excitement I exaggerated my poverty until it seemed impersonated\par
and assumed the guise of an enemy threatening to enslave me. From 8\par
o'clock to 11 I paced that mall, and then left it to keep my appointment\par
with Irving & Co., with one thought surging through my brain, and that\par
was that I dared not be poor, the result being that before we parted, to\par
their renewed question: "Will you do this for us?" "Of course I will!" I\par
cried, and my feet had slipped a good many steps further down the\par
Primrose Way to death.\par
\par
