\par
\par
CHAPTER IV.\par
\par
FOOLS STUMBLING ON FORTUNES.\par
\par
\par
The present generation has become tolerably familiar with defalcations\par
and robberies involving enormous sums. Previous to 1861 they were\par
comparatively unknown, the reason being that the currency of the country\par
was strictly limited. There were absolutely no Government bonds or\par
currency, while the few bonds issued by corporations were not usually\par
made payable to bearer, and, therefore, were not negotiable, and were of\par
no use to the robber. But in 1861, to meet the expenses of the war, the\par
State banks were taxed out of existence and our present national\par
currency system came into being. In addition to the enormous issue of\par
greenbacks, bonds payable to bearer, amounting to hundreds of millions,\par
were issued by the general Government, by the individual States,\par
counties, towns and cities, all becoming popular investments.\par
Patriotism, and profit as well, led banks, corporations and individuals\par
all over the world to invest surplus funds in bonds, those of the\par
Government being most popular of all. The various issues authorized by\par
act of Congress were known as "seven-thirties," "ten-forties,"\par
"five-twenties," etc., these terms denoting either the rate of interest\par
or the period of years, dating from the first issue, wherein it was\par
optional with the Government to redeem them. Everywhere, at home, in the\par
theatres and public resorts not less than on the Exchange, were heard\par
animated discussions about "seven-thirties" and "ten-forties." The\par
business of the express companies of the United States took a new phase,\par
and for the first time in their history they began to be the carriers of\par
vast sums from city to city.\par
\par
Then it was that those gentlemen who work without the pale of the law\par
discovered new prospects of wealth, and realized that even to crack a\par
safe or vault of a private firm would be rewarded by a find of bonds\par
that might amply repay all risks of robbery under police protection,\par
while to execute a successful raid on a car or even an express delivery\par
wagon on the street would mean wealth. To burglarize the vaults of a\par
bank meant, if undetected, anything from opening a magnificent bar or\par
hotel in New York to a steam yacht and Winter cruises in the tropics and\par
Summer nights on the Mediterranean.\par
\par
The first coup in this line, which at once became famous, was startling\par
in its ease and magnitude. It was known, and still is, as "The Lord Bond\par
Robbery." Lord was a very wealthy man, who had inherited his millions.\par
His office was in Broad street, where he managed his estates. He had\par
invested $1,200,000 in seven-thirty bonds, all payable to bearer. For\par
the thief, if he had any knowledge of finance, and knew how to negotiate\par
them, such a sum as this in bonds was better than the same amount in\par
gold, it being more portable. One million two hundred thousand dollars\par
in gold would weigh upward of a ton, and would be difficult to handle,\par
but that sum in bonds would hardly fill a carpet-sack. In our day, with\par
safety deposit vaults everywhere, it seems strange that any sane man\par
would keep so vast a sum in an old-fashioned vault in his private\par
office, but Lord did so. His office was a very quiet one, with but few\par
visitors, there being no business transacted in it but that of his\par
estate.\par
\par
[Illustration: "BY HEAVEN, I WANT MONEY."--Page 33.]\par
\par
At this time there were three or four gangs in New York, all well known\par
and friendly with the police--that is, some or all were more or less\par
under "protection," and had pulls at Police Headquarters. But the pull\par
could not be depended upon at all times, particularly if the robbery\par
made a noise and the press took it up. Then there would be violent kicks\par
at Headquarters, and a general all-around scramble to get the thieves,\par
and so far as safe, stick to more or less of the plunder. The gang that\par
got Mr. Lord's bonds was what in police and thieves' slang was known as\par
"On the Office," so named because they went around visiting offices in\par
the business part of the city, one of the gang going in on pretense of\par
making some inquiry and so engaging the attention of one of the clerks.\par
Then the second member would come in and endeavor to attract the\par
attention of any remaining clerks, while the third would try to get in\par
without attracting attention, and, if unnoticed by those now busy\par
talking, would slip around behind the counter to the money drawer or\par
vault and carry off any cash box or package visible which appeared to be\par
of value. This gang consisted of three men, Hod Ennis, Charley Rose and\par
a man by the name of Bullard, afterward made notorious by engineering\par
the Boylston Bank robbery in Boston.\par
\par
In the absence of Lord the office was under charge of two men,\par
old-fashioned fellows, who had grown gray in the service of the Lord\par
estate. The bonds were all in a tin box something larger than a soap\par
box. The interest on the bonds being due, the box had been taken out in\par
order to cut off the coupons, and was left in the door of the open\par
vault. None of these circumstances was known to these men; in fact,\par
while "looking for chances," they stumbled on the prize. The night\par
previous they had spent at a well-known faro game and had lost their\par
last dollar. At 9 o'clock in the morning they met at a saloon on Prince\par
street, where none but crooks consorted, and, borrowing a dollar from\par
the barkeeper, they took a South Ferry stage and started downtown on one\par
of many similar piratical expeditions. Of course, each paid his own\par
fare, as from the moment of starting until their return they appeared to\par
be strangers. Alighting at the ferry, they started up Front street, Rose\par
in lead, he being pilot-fish. From Front they turned into Broad, and up\par
Broad to No. 22, where there were a number of offices. Rose mounted the\par
staircase, it now being five minutes to 10, Bullard coming close behind.\par
Rose entered the first office to the left at the head of the stairs,\par
which was Lord's, and at once inquired by name for a member of a\par
well-known firm located a few doors down across the street. Lord was\par
away. The clerk, in his desire to serve the gentleman, went to the front\par
windows to point out the location of the firm. Bullard, who had lingered\par
in the hall, entered, leaving the office door open behind him, and at\par
once engaged the attention of the remaining clerk with a letter. Ennis,\par
seeing the coast clear, slipped in, went softly to the vault, and\par
perceiving the tin box, seized and carried it out, unseen by all save\par
his companions. They, seeing him safely off, found a quick pretext to\par
follow without any suspicion arising in the minds of the clerks. As a\par
matter of fact, they did not miss the box for nearly an hour.\par
\par
Ennis carried it to Peck Slip, closely followed by his chums, and there\par
the three boarded a Second avenue car, all unsuspecting as to what a\par
prize they had. At the corner of the Bowery and Bayard street they got\par
out and entered that old red brick hotel on the corner--I forget the\par
name. They were acquainted and occasionally rendezvoused there, hiring\par
and paying for the room. They speedily opened the box, and were amazed\par
to find it packed full of bonds--five hundreds, thousands,\par
five-thousands, all payable to bearer. The very magnitude of their\par
plunder terrified them, and, knowing as much as I do about such men, I\par
am free to affirm that if a buyer of stolen property had appeared on the\par
scene and said: "Here, I'll give you $10,000 apiece," they would have\par
closed the deal at once and turned over the bonds, glad to get them off\par
their hands. What they did was this: Rose went out and bought a\par
second-hand carpet bag and put the bonds into it, save sixty\par
five-hundreds, which they divided, and Bullard resolved to leave the bag\par
with a friend of his. This friend, strangely enough, was the widow of a\par
policeman and sister of two others. But she knew nothing of Bullard's\par
character, believing him to be a workingman. Ennis and Rose were two\par
ignorant fellows, without the remotest idea of how to negotiate bonds,\par
but Bullard had, and, realizing how important it was to get some cash\par
before the thing was noised around, he started out to sell some,\par
agreeing to meet Rose and Ennis at No. 100 Third avenue, a large beer\par
saloon then, as now.\par
\par
Going to different brokers' offices, he disposed of ten for $5,000\par
without any difficulty, and stopped at that. He met his two friends and\par
divided the $5,000 with them. Then, as a natural consequence with that\par
class of men, all got drunk, and before the next morning had spent,\par
loaned or gambled away every dollar of the $5,000.\par
\par
I remember perfectly the tremendous sensation created when a rumor of\par
the robbery spread in Wall street and over the city, and what mystified\par
and intensified the matter was the fact that no complaint had been made\par
to the police. When Mr. Lord was interviewed by them and by reporters he\par
would not admit that he had been robbed, and said if he had been he\par
would prefer to lose the money rather than have a fuss made about the\par
affair.\par
\par
This was really the first of many great bond robberies, and it struck\par
the popular fancy; but if it stirred Wall street greatly, who shall\par
describe the frenzy of excitement that broke out at 300 Mulberry\par
street--Police Headquarters--when the first vague rumors of a gigantic\par
robbery were fully confirmed, and it became known that Hod Ennis and his\par
gang had a million and more of plunder?\par
\par
All rings and pulls and gangs were smashed, combined and recombined\par
again, while each and all were in an agony of fear lest the booty should\par
be returned to the owner--minus a percentage divided between the gang\par
and the ring, or sold to some clever fence, who would plant them away\par
safely and sell them in Europe from time to time, keeping all for\par
himself and they to have no share. What visions of diamond pins, of\par
eight or twelve carats, all Brazilian stones; of swift, high-stepping\par
horses; of the heaven of Harlem lane on Sunday afternoons, with a bottle\par
or two under the vest, haunted the sleep of all the detective force. I\par
say the police knew Hod Ennis and his gang had stolen the bonds, for in\par
those days there was not a gang of confidence men, card sharpers, bank\par
burglars, counterfeiters or forgers traveling the country but that the\par
gang and every member of it was well known to the Police Department of\par
each of our large cities. Whenever a job was done a score of detectives\par
all over the country could say such and such a gang did the job, and\par
they were almost always right.\par
\par
Whether there was "something in" for the force to arrest and convict or\par
not, as a matter of fact the thieves were sooner or later hocus-pocussed\par
out of their share, either by the police, by some untrustworthy fence,\par
or by some lawyer who was pitched upon to work back the securities on a\par
percentage. In case the thief succeeded in saving part of the proceeds\par
he immediately lost it at faro or in revelry, and then risked his\par
liberty for more.\par
\par
I know two men who to-day walk the streets of New York, the types of\par
conservative respectability, members of many fashionable clubs, who, in\par
the sixties, were known as fences, and were always ready to invest cash\par
for stolen bonds. Both of these men compromised with their conscience by\par
beating down the price and giving the thieves but a moiety of their\par
value. Both of them have their fads; one is a connoisseur in violins,\par
the other has a penchant for orchids, and has much local fame for the\par
rarities in his collection.\par
\par
Before midnight of the day of the robbery it became known to the force\par
and many of the hangers-on of the gambling saloons and barrooms of the\par
Eighth Ward that Hod Ennis and his gang had money, and it was surmised\par
that it must be from the Lord business. In the mean time Bullard took\par
the bag of bonds up to Norwalk, Ct., and placed them for safe-keeping\par
with a trusty friend, first taking out one hundred bonds of five hundred\par
each and fifty of one thousand each, and, returning to the city, divided\par
them with his comrades. During his absence the photographs of the three\par
men had been shown at Police Headquarters to the two clerks, but they\par
were unable to identify them.\par
\par
Within the next few days the $100,000 in bonds were completely\par
dissipated; some were sold to buyers of stolen goods for a percentage of\par
the value, some were lost at the gambling games--mostly at Morrissey's,\par
or at Mike Murray's on Broadway, near Spring street, and probably some\par
went Mulberry street way. Matters were thickening, and, fearing arrest,\par
Ennis fled to Canada, Bullard to Europe and Rose went West to\par
California. Eventually Ennis was convicted of a crime committed some\par
time before. He was sentenced to a long imprisonment, and came out an\par
old, broken-down man, without a dollar and without a friend. Rose was\par
sentenced to five years for another crime, and then disappeared. Bullard\par
settled down in Paris. He afterward returned and planned the Boylston\par
Bank affair in Boston. With his share of the plunder he went back to\par
Paris and opened an American bar at the Grand Hotel and flourished for\par
some years; but, wanting money, he committed a robbery in Belgium, was\par
arrested, and is now serving a long sentence for the same; no doubt, if\par
he survives, he will emerge friendless, penniless, a stranger in a\par
strange world.\par
\par
If I were inclined to indulge in reminiscences, what a catalogue could\par
be given of men who had, like myself, drifted into the Primrose Way, and\par
all, or nearly all, have paid a terrible penalty for their\par
wrongdoing--none more terrible than myself. As for our violin virtuoso,\par
he seems to have conquered fate. So, too, with the connoisseur in\par
orchids; but let us wait until the end before we say all is well with\par
them.\par
\par
Some time later on, meeting one of these detectives, now dead, who then\par
ranked as the best in New York, in the confidence of the bankers, he\par
said: "I am getting old and am now working for reputation, and\par
consequently am not taking any more percentages. Of course, I don't\par
molest any of my old friends, but those who are not under protection I\par
run in and send them up the river (Sing Sing) as fast as I get them to\par
rights."\par
\par
This need not be considered a condemnation of all detectives, for there\par
were, even in my time, a few honest ones of the Pinkerton and John\par
Curtin class--the latter being now one of San Francisco's most reliable,\par
who, by unusually considerate judgment, has made honorable citizens of a\par
very large number of clerks whom he had been called upon to detect and\par
arrest. This he accomplished by extracting a confession in writing,\par
filing it among his secret papers, then saying to the trembling clerk:\par
"I shall have you reinstated in your position, but if you go wrong again\par
this confession will be made public."\par
\par
The following incident will further enlighten the reader as to the way\par
things were done in those good old days:\par
\par
When Boss Tweed was in the full zenith of his power and glory and of the\par
wealth so easily acquired by certain methods, his daughter was married.\par
All of the then chiefs and district officers of Tammany, city officials,\par
judges and heads of departments vied with each other in the presentation\par
of wedding gifts, among which was a check for $100,000 from the father.\par
Seldom has any bride received a more magnificent tribute, for, coming\par
from such sources, they were nothing less than a tribute. Especially was\par
this the case with one much-admired gift which was contributed by us\par
just after an illicit operation of $40,000 in Wall street, $4,000 of\par
which was paid to Irving.\par
\par
In the column list of wedding gifts in the next morning's papers was:\par
"One solid silver punch bowl, value $500, presented by Superintendent\par
Kelso." Shortly after paying Irving the $4,000 percentage we met him one\par
evening at the St. Cloud Hotel. Mentioning the approaching Tweed\par
marriage, he suggested that it would be the thing, and make us more\par
solid with the Superintendent of Police, for us to make a fine present\par
to "the old man," one that he could use as a gift to the bride. As $500\par
was not much to our party in those days, we assented, and handed over\par
that amount.\par
\par
Tiffany's was then located down Broadway, and among other things on\par
exhibition in the window was a large, handsome silver punch bowl. This\par
was purchased with our money, which was known to have been obtained by\par
forgery, and presented to Superintendent Kelso. A few days later the\par
bowl reappeared in the window of Tiffany's thus inscribed:\par
\par
