\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER V.\par
\par
WHEN BOSS TWEED WAS NEW YORK'S OWNER AND JIM FISK, PROPRIETOR OF OUR\par
JUDGES.\par
\par
\par
What a look of relief and triumph swept over the faces of Irving,\par
Stanley and White when I gave my consent to their proposal to take the\par
stolen bonds to Europe and negotiate them there. We understood each\par
other now, and casting aside all reserve, their tongues wagged freely,\par
and they eagerly told me how confident they were of my ability to\par
dispose of the bonds successfully, and also of my good faith; and,\par
furthermore, told me I was the only man they would have trusted. Of\par
course, they had no security save my word, for under the circumstances\par
they could hardly ask me for a receipt, and even had I given one it\par
would have been valueless had I chosen to retain the proceeds of the\par
bonds. Thus, becoming the important member of the firm, I told them to\par
produce the securities and I would sail immediately. It was finally\par
settled that I should go by the steamer Russia of the Cunard line, which\par
was down for sailing at 7 a.m. Wednesday, and they were to deliver the\par
bonds to me on Tuesday night. Upon my demanding cash to pay expenses,\par
their faces fell, but quickly brightened when I told them to give me a\par
thousand-dollar bond and I would borrow that amount from a friend, using\par
it for security. There was no danger of the number of the bond being\par
inspected, and, of course, I would pay the note upon my return and\par
receive the bond again.\par
\par
[Illustration: WALL STREET AND SOME OF ITS CHARACTERS IN MY TIME.--Page\par
26.]\par
\par
They told me many amusing lies as to how the securities came into their\par
possession, and as to who were the rightful owners. The truth was, as I\par
afterward learned, they were a part of the stolen Lord bonds.\par
\par
Bonds issued by our Government and held in Europe, chiefly in Holland\par
and Germany, were so enormous in volume and passed so freely from hand\par
to hand, that it was easy for a well-dressed, business-appearing man to\par
sell any quantity, even if stolen, as by law the innocent holder could\par
not be deprived of them. One great advantage a dishonest man had at that\par
date in Europe, especially an American, was that if he dressed well they\par
considered he must be a gentleman, and if he had money that was a proof\par
of respectability--one they never thought of questioning, nor how he\par
came by it; then, again, it was an article of their creed that all\par
Americans are rich.\par
\par
The next morning (Tuesday), Irving met me near the Exchange, and, with\par
some trepidation, drew from an inner pocket an envelope containing the\par
thousand-dollar bond. Without waiting to examine it, I walked off,\par
saying: "I'll be back in ten minutes." He was evidently alarmed, and,\par
like all rogues, suspicious of every one. He probably had some wild idea\par
that I was laying a trap for him. In his ignorance of money methods he\par
thought it would be a long, perhaps difficult, negotiation to borrow\par
money on the bond, but, of course, I made short work of it; and "Jimmy"\par
was more than delighted when within the ten minutes I walked in with ten\par
one hundreds in my hand. A trifle like this made a great impression upon\par
Irving, and from that time on I had his entire confidence. Tuesday\par
evening I said good-bye to my mother, merely remarking in explanation of\par
my journey that I had a commission given me to execute in Europe.\par
\par
Leaving her, I went to our rendezvous, near Broadway and Astor place,\par
where I found Irving, who handed me over his "boodle" (as he termed\par
it), remarking confidentially that I was to give him on my return his\par
share into his own hands; and, singularly enough, each of the others did\par
precisely the same thing. About 11 o'clock the other two came in, and\par
after some parley White handed over his bonds, and Stanley informed me\par
he would give me his on board before the steamer sailed the next\par
morning. I had already paid my bill and sent my baggage over to Jersey\par
City, so about midnight I set out, they accompanying me as far as the\par
ferry, and there, after shaking hands a half dozen times, we said\par
good-bye. Having bought my ticket and engaged my cabin, I went direct to\par
the steamer and went to bed. In the morning Stanley appeared and gave me\par
his bonds. Ten minutes later the hawsers were cast off and we were\par
steaming down the bay. Two hours later Fire Island sank beneath the\par
horizon, and we were alone on the sea.\par
\par
Alone on the sea! and a fitting place to tell the story of a famous New\par
York bank robbery.\par
\par
In the good old days when Bill Tweed was New York's owner, when Jim Fisk\par
was the proprietor of our judges and Kelso sat in Mulberry street, the\par
king of those good men, the police, who defend our lives and property,\par
this city became a spectacle to gods and men such as we thought then\par
could never be equaled. We thought so then, but we were not endowed with\par
second sight, nor with the gift of prophecy, or we might, perhaps, have\par
reserved our judgment. Still, our masters were a unique collection, and\par
if they have been equaled or surpassed since, they held with easy grasp\par
the pre-eminence among all American rulers who had shone and flourished\par
up to the time when those great men gave us new ideas upon the science\par
of government. The average and quiet citizen, shocked as he might be and\par
grumble as he did at the impudent plundering by our masters, their\par
contempt of public opinion and the cynical display of their luxury,\par
would doubtless have confined himself to grumbling and to calling for\par
slow-arriving thunderbolts to crash the oppressors who were despoiling\par
him had he felt certain that the plunder would be confined to them, that\par
his property would be safe, at least, from the attacks of those\par
insignificant, despicable but eminently dangerous plunderers who became\par
known to the police as common criminals. This, however, was not so.\par
After being flayed by iniquitous taxes, which he knew were destined to\par
add to the stores of Tweed, Connolly & Company, he had every day\par
abundant proof that what the big rascals left him, the little ones would\par
soon try, by burglary or robbery, to ravish from him, and that they\par
would do it with perfect immunity, unterrified either by the fear of\par
present arrest or of later punishment. The Mulberry street office was\par
divided into three or four little pools, each with its clientele of\par
dependents, all of whom faithfully and immediately reported to their\par
patrons the result of any little job they had been engaged in, handing\par
over to the representative of the pool the 20 per cent. of the result,\par
which was Headquarters' established commission. This was the ordinary\par
rate when gentlemen skilled in transferring other people's watches and\par
portemonnaies from the pockets of their owners to their own, or when\par
others who had devoted their talents to demonstrating practically the\par
enormous power of the jimmy and wedge originated and carried out by\par
themselves the operations peculiar to those classes of industries.\par
\par
It sometimes happened that special cases offered, for which special\par
terms were arranged. Such cases stood by themselves. They were confided\par
only to the acknowledged heads of the profession. Standing outside of\par
all recognized rules, they were treated apart. Headquarters men were\par
always sent to the seat of operations to prevent interference, and, in\par
case of need, to protect their partners. Many a mysterious robbery was\par
perpetrated to which no clue was ever found; many an anxious search was\par
undertaken by the bloodhounds of the law to find the robbers, that they\par
might crack a bottle together and rejoice over the success of their\par
operations, and sometimes they were joined by men the mention of whose\par
names in such company would have excited incredulous and unbounded\par
amazement.\par
\par
The gigantic heavings of the war were struggling to rest, but the men\par
whose minds were unhinged and thrown off their balance by the possession\par
of large sums flowing from transactions, a little irregular, perhaps,\par
but which the necessities of Government permitted, were endeavoring, by\par
any means, to open up new fountains of wealth in place of those which\par
the close of the war had exhausted.\par
\par
One of the resources presenting itself most naturally to men in a\par
position to profit by it was speculating with other people's money, and\par
very naturally the result of such speculation was disastrous in the\par
highest degree. When detection became inevitable the defaulter generally\par
fled, hoping to find in a foreign land safety from the stroke of justice\par
and a shelter from the reproaches of his victims.\par
\par
Occasionally, one more resolute, dreading flight as much as detection,\par
flung himself into schemes which, if they failed, meant the most hideous\par
and utter ruin, but which, if they succeeded, rendered discovery\par
impossible, and made his position more solid than ever before. One day,\par
late in the sixties, in the parlor of a bank in Greenwich street, a\par
gentleman was anxiously scanning the books of the establishment. He\par
alone in all the institution knew of a secret which would horrify his\par
brother officials and carry desolation to scores of homes, the first to\par
suffer being his own. Perhaps had it been possible to exempt this one\par
home, the misery of the others would not have greatly affected him. But\par
suffering must be kept from his own house, and all and any means to\par
banish it would be and must be good.\par
\par
The gentleman in whose mind these thoughts were passing was the\par
president of the bank, who knew himself to be a defaulter to an enormous\par
amount, and who was now anxiously reflecting upon the means to cover up\par
his robberies. Fortunately for him he was acquainted with the one man\par
who more than any other in all America was able to help him. This was\par
Capt. Irving. The president was a man of nerve. He knew, as everybody\par
else knew, the relations in which the police stood to the thieves, and\par
he felt that if he could arrange to have his own bank robbed, his\par
difficulties would vanish, and his share in the defalcations be covered\par
up.\par
\par
Little time was left to him before the inevitable discovery, but the\par
prompt and skillful use he made of it to extricate himself from the\par
fearful danger of his position makes one almost regret that a man of\par
such resolution and such opportunities should prove to the world that\par
high qualities may exist when the moral sense is entirely wanting.\par
Irving was quickly taken into his confidence, the position explained,\par
the proposition to rob the bank broached, all possible co-operation in\par
the way of leaving safes unlocked and doors open, or what, of course,\par
amounts to the same thing, of furnishing keys and information to open\par
everything, promised, and then Irving was asked if he could find men to\par
carry the job into execution. New York in those days was well supplied\par
with such artists, but the right men to carry out so momentous an\par
operation had to be sought. The difficulty, however, was not great, and\par
Irving promptly assured the honorable president that he might\par
confidently count on the right men at the right time.\par
\par
Among the professionals who twenty-three or four years ago were\par
considered "valuable" men at Police Headquarters were Mike Hurley,\par
Patsey Conroy and Max Shinburn. These were the men whom Irving instantly\par
determined to employ, and whom he forthwith set about to find. That not\par
being a matter of any difficulty, the same night the three men met\par
Irving at his own house, and were delighted over the revelation he made\par
to them.\par
\par
One would like to know with what sentiment a man occupying an honorable\par
and responsible position, a Sunday-school superintendent, the head of a\par
great financial institution, well known in the money world and respected\par
in society, slunk to a midnight meeting with burglars.\par
\par
Did no feeling of shame crimson his face, no sinking of disgust oppress\par
his heart, as he slipped into a house, where, although he kept aloof\par
from actual contact with the ruffians, the details of an enormous crime\par
of which he was the author were debated and settled?\par
\par
Prudential reasons doubtless kept him from forming a personal\par
acquaintance with his agents. The risk of exposing himself to future\par
blackmail must not be incurred, and one may well believe that he shrank\par
from clasping the hands of these men, who were eagerly awaiting him.\par
Whatever were his feelings, his desperate position suffered no halting.\par
The storm was ready to break at any moment. In an instant he might be a\par
wretched fugitive, with terror before him and infamy howling behind. But\par
one way led out of this labyrinth. He had resolutely planted his feet in\par
that way, determined to tread it to the end. He did tread it to the end,\par
and he came out victorious.\par
\par
If the suspicions of any afterward pointed toward him, no syllable of\par
the suspicions was breathed. Who dared suspect that an honorable citizen\par
had ever, in the dead of night, crept like a robber to a meeting of\par
outlaws, to concoct the details of an outrageous breach of trust, of a\par
crime which--none knew it better than he--would carry life-long misery\par
and suffering to the families of nearly every man who trusted him?\par
\par
[Illustration: "THE DETECTIVES SIGNALED THE BURGLARS: 'THE COAST IS\par
CLEAR.'"--Page 57.]\par
\par
"The evil that men do lives after them," but where does the\par
responsibility of its author end? Who will ever say what crimes may\par
spring from the one act of wrongdoing, crimes committed, it may be,\par
by persons who were directly led into them by the consequences of an act\par
the perpetrator of which had never heard of those affected by it? How\par
far does the responsibility of the wrongdoer extend? What weight of\par
horror is he accumulating on his head?\par
\par
Such questions may perhaps occur afterward, when the pleasure has been\par
tasted and is gone, and nothing remains of the detected crime but the\par
ruin it has wrought; but in the excitement of laying the plot, in the\par
glamour which the hope of success casts over the schemer, they probably\par
never intrude, conscience is smothered, and he is left to carry out his\par
schemes to the end.\par
\par
Doubtless no such thoughts disturbed the president, as he waited that\par
night while Irving acted as go-between, carrying messages from him to\par
the agents and from the agents back again to him. At last the\par
arrangements were made. Duplicate keys of the safe were to be provided,\par
and a way, to be presently explained, was to be left open to each of\par
them. Whatever the robbers found in the safes was to be theirs, and the\par
task of getting it was to be of the easiest. This, of course, was highly\par
satisfactory to the thieves, but something more must be prepared for the\par
stockholders and the public. Bank safes are not so easily emptied; there\par
must be the appearance, at least, of great effort to effect the robbery,\par
and marks of the effort must be left behind.\par
\par
It was, therefore, settled that powerful tools were to be provided,\par
tools able to tear open any strong-box in the world. Such articles are\par
expensive, and the burglars had no money to procure them. No man who\par
knows those people will be surprised at this, for, however much money\par
they may obtain, they never have anything. It melts out of their hands,\par
and they would be themselves embarrassed to say what becomes of it.\par
\par
The president's first necessity, therefore, was to pay out about a\par
thousand dollars for the jimmies, wedges and all the paraphernalia of\par
the burglars' industry. This he did. Irving took charge of the money,\par
and he had far too great an interest in the scheme to suffer the cash to\par
be squandered. The agreement was that on the following day Conroy should\par
present himself at the bank to hire a vacant basement, the roof of which\par
formed the floor of the room where the safes were lodged. The president\par
undertook to smooth any difficulties in the way of requiring references,\par
and promised that he should be accepted as a tenant.\par
\par
This agreement was punctually carried out. Conroy made his application,\par
the basement was granted to him, the rent paid in advance for the\par
edification of the clerks, and he at once entered in possession. Hurley\par
and Shinburne joined him, and the following Saturday they removed so\par
much of the ceiling that but a few minutes' work was required to\par
complete a hole which should serve as a doorway to the vaults above when\par
the bank closed in the evening.\par
\par
[Illustration: MACHINE FOR WEIGHING GOLD.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER VI.\par
\par
CHEATED VISIONS AND VANISHED HOPES.\par
\par
\par
Saturday night was the time chosen to get into the bank, and the\par
plunderers were to remain there until Sunday. The members of Irving's\par
ring were to keep watch to prevent any officious interference from\par
passers-by or from ward policemen. Carriages were to be in waiting at\par
some convenient place on Sunday morning, and when the men inside\par
received a signal from their police accomplices on the outside, they\par
were to leave the bank, abandoning their tools, and carrying away\par
nothing but the money and the securities they had stolen. So far, the\par
way was plain; the keys had long before been prepared, tested and found\par
to work properly; full instructions were given as to the way to use\par
them, but the way inside was not yet open.\par
\par
A night watchman was employed on the premises, and he, of course, was to\par
be got rid of. Little ceremony was to be used in treating him. He was to\par
be seized, overcome by any means, bound, gagged and rendered helpless\par
until Monday, and the fact that he always passed Sunday in the bank,\par
prevented any remark at home upon his continued absence. The details of\par
the plot were thus satisfactorily settled, and at a late hour the\par
conspirators separated.\par
\par
In the early morning of that day the three burglars were standing in the\par
cellar to which they had lowered their booty, waiting for the signal to\par
come out. At last it was given, when the precious trio slipped out,\par
carrying their precious bags. A covered carriage was posted in an\par
adjoining street, into which the whole party entered, flurried and\par
excited, and rapidly drove to Irving's residence. There the contents of\par
the bags were carefully examined. The actual cash was easily disposed\par
of, but what was to be done with the bonds?\par
\par
The arrangement finally agreed upon, to be detailed presently, shows\par
that if there be circumstances in which a little learning is a dangerous\par
thing, one of them is not just after the perpetration of a gigantic\par
burglary.\par
\par
The Monday following its execution confusion and amazement reigned in\par
the bank. The clerks on their arrival were astounded to find the safe\par
doors wide open, torn and smashed by the tools which lay scattered over\par
the floor, and the night watchman, gagged and bound, was discovered,\par
nearly dead, in a neighboring room. One of the clerks jumped into a cab\par
and rushed to Police Headquarters in Mulberry street to report the\par
robbery. Irving was sitting in his office, busy with the night reports,\par
when the messenger was introduced to tell of the bank's calamity.\par
\par
The excellent chief listened with breathless attention, and was\par
naturally horror-struck at the perpetration of such a crime. Calling a\par
couple of his trusted sleuths, he hastily communicated the surprising\par
news, and the three hurried with the clerk back to Greenwich street.\par
Arrived there they minutely examined the premises, and gave it as their\par
opinion, judging from the style of the work and from the tools which lay\par
around, that the burglary had been committed by a well-known burglar\par
named Harry Penrose, and that the night watchman, whom they immediately\par
placed under arrest, must have been his accomplice.\par
\par
The president had sent word to the bank that he was unwell, and would\par
not be able to attend to business that day, but the terrible news was\par
immediately telegraphed to him, and, in spite of his illness, he hurried\par
to town. It is impossible to describe his astonishment and distress at\par
the sight which met his eyes. In the presence of the clerks he held\par
anxious consultations with the detectives, who assured him that they had\par
already taken the first steps to unravel the mystery, and that every\par
possible effort would be made to discover the criminals. In the privacy\par
of his own office he explained to the reporters that he had left in the\par
bank four hundred thousand dollars in cash and bonds, every farthing of\par
which had disappeared.\par
\par
As soon as the news was published the excitement among the depositors\par
and the stockholders of the bank was, of course, immense. A run set in,\par
which the directors by the help of friends and of their own private\par
resources were able to meet, but the Wall street appreciation of the\par
calamity was shown in the drop in value of the bank's stock from 130 to\par
40.\par
\par
I repeat, a little learning is a dangerous thing. Much knowledge is not\par
to be looked for among men who engage in such crimes, but one would\par
fancy that the everyday experience of Irving and his people would have\par
given them some idea of financial business. The fact is, they were, if\par
possible, more ignorant than their felonious partners. The financial\par
ideas of the latter scarcely went further than "making cheap pennyworths\par
of their plunder, giving to courtesans and living like lords till all be\par
gone," so that negotiating the sale of bonds was a mystery far too high\par
for them--something which they could never hope to attain to. But the\par
company included one man who was a rare exception to the ordinary ride\par
of such society. This was Max Shinburne, a German, a man of considerable\par
education, who, in some inexplicable way, had fallen so far from honor\par
and respectability that when he saw a thief he "consented unto him."\par
\par
How is it that such men are often found in the ranks of professional\par
criminals? They would probably have difficulty to explain it themselves.\par
A want of savoir faire, the fact that they have never been taught to\par
make a practical use of their acquirements, the pressure of temptation\par
at a critical moment, the absence, possibly, from harm, leading to the\par
hope of immunity--all, perhaps, enter into the explanation of the secret\par
promptings which have led to the first false step, to the first planting\par
of the feet in the path which leads to destruction. Once the step is\par
taken, to retrace it seems impossible. The line which society draws, and\par
which it proclaims no man shall overstep without punishment, may be\par
approached very closely, but once on the wrong side, once the fateful\par
step is taken, the act is irretrievable; to attempt to retrace it is to\par
attempt to undo the past; it is all but impossible.\par
\par
Thus probably it is that the fall of an educated man is more hopeless\par
than that of one who knows no better. A carpenter or a blacksmith who\par
has got himself in a tangle has only to move to another town, and if he\par
shakes off perverted thoughts and perverted influences, he is not much\par
worse off than before. He has kept his trade, and his trade will keep\par
him.\par
\par
Nobody is going to inquire about a workman who can do his work. The\par
employer requires nothing more than that the work be done, and if it be\par
done he neither thinks nor cares anything more about either it or the\par
worker.\par
\par
With the educated man the case is different. The sentiments of the class\par
he belongs to are less yielding, the fineness of his own feelings has\par
been too deeply wounded, and when he has stabbed his reputation, he is\par
apt, foolishly, of course, to fling the rest of his respectability after\par
it.\par
\par
With qualities and advantages which might have fitted him for a useful\par
and honorable position in life, Shinburne was at less than 30 years of\par
age the companion of outcasts. But whatever his moral failings, his\par
knowledge remained, and it was for him, at least, to be valuable.\par
\par
To get rid of the bonds in America was impossible, except by sacrificing\par
them to a stolen goods receiver, who would have given but a small\par
percentage of their value.\par
\par
A steamer was to sail for Europe that day, and it was agreed that\par
Shinburne should go by her, with one of the other robbers as company,\par
sell the bonds before the news of the robbery could get across the\par
ocean, then return and fairly divide the proceeds.\par
\par
This was the arrangement, but Shinburne had already begun to have other\par
dreams and other ambitions. He saw a chance to restore himself, or, at\par
least, to snatch at a position which would give him weight to crush down\par
sinister reports or envious whisperings, and he determined forthwith to\par
seize it. What the bank president had done to save himself from infamy,\par
Shinburne would do to recover himself from infamy. It can be, therefore,\par
easily understood that he accepted without hesitation the other's\par
proposal.\par
\par
The steamer did not sail until noon. There was, therefore, plenty of\par
time to make preparations, and, besides, he had a little private\par
business to attend to. Leaving the securities in Irving's charge, with a\par
promise to meet the party at 11, he took his share of the cash and\par
departed.\par
\par
Some time before this, with a skill and forethought rarely to be found\par
in the class he then belonged to, he had bought some building lots near\par
the park. Fortunate, indeed, the speculation eventually proved to be. In\par
the mean time, placing his lots in the hands of a responsible agent, and\par
taking drafts on Europe for his money, he rapidly made the little\par
preparation he needed, and at 11 joined his party, there to receive\par
nearly $200,000 in bonds, and to set out with Mike Hurley for the\par
steamer.\par
\par
After hurried parting injunctions from the Headquarters men, the two\par
travelers, accompanied by Conroy, to see them off, were rapidly driven\par
to the steamer. Punctually to the hour the hawsers were cast off, and\par
with barely time to say good-bye the cronies parted. A moment after the\par
screw began to turn, and the Cunarder's bow pointed toward England.\par
\par
Arrived in Liverpool, the pair proceeded at once to London. Hurley, who\par
was as ignorant of foreign travel as of everything else, was easily\par
tricked by some tale of no evening trains for the Continent. Shinburne\par
plied him well with liquor, taking care to mix the bottles, and when he\par
had got him helplessly drunk he took the bonds and with his little\par
luggage slipped quietly off to the Continent, never to see his dupe or\par
his New York friends again.\par
\par
He went to Germany, called himself "Count" Shinburne, bought an estate\par
and began to exercise large hospitality toward his neighbors.\par
\par
No man on all the length of the Rhine was so popular as he. No man's\par
house and table, horses and gardens were so praised as his. In the eyes\par
of the beggar nobles of the Fatherland the man who could give such\par
dinners and in such succession, must belong to the choice members of the\par
human race. Day by day Max's position grew more solid. No breath was\par
ever whispered against him, and with a little prudence he might have\par
kept up his state and died in the odor of sanctity. But the taste of\par
grandeur was too sweet, the incense of his little world's flattery too\par
precious to run the smallest risk of losing it. His display exceeded his\par
means, but for nothing in the world would he have curtailed it.\par
\par
Matters were in this way until he awoke one day to find his account\par
overdrawn on his bankers. Then it was that he began to remember his\par
operation in Greenwich street, and he seems to have thought that if he\par
succeeded in New York, surely nothing could stand in his way in some\par
sleepy town in Europe.\par
\par
[Illustration: "WITH HORROR THE SISTERS SAW THE COUNTESS AIRING THE\par
HISTORIC BRACELET."--Page 68.]\par
\par
He went to Brussels prospecting, and soon pitched upon an establishment\par
which he thought likely to reward his industry. But the result showed\par
that to walk into a bank when the way was left open, with the\par
authorities anxious to see him there, and to force his way in when the\par
entrance was jealously barred with the guardians determined he should\par
stay out, were two very different things. He made the attempt, was\par
arrested and sentenced to sixteen years' imprisonment. His German\par
friends heard of his mishap, and his glory faded like the early dew.\par
\par
Naturally, every one thought that the count's career had closed, that\par
the star of his fate had declined, that the bars of his prison house\par
were about him forever. They were greatly mistaken. After some twelve or\par
thirteen years he succeeded in getting a pardon and managed to make his\par
way to America. His first visit was to the agents in whose hands he had\par
left the management of his park lots. He went into their office, not\par
knowing whether or not he was a pauper. He came out knowing himself to\par
be nearly a millionaire.\par
\par
During the almost twenty years of his absence his lots had increased\par
enormously in value. Once more he was a rich man, once more he might\par
emerge from his eclipse and become a power of a certain kind in the\par
class of society he could get access to, but his experience had taught\par
him something. His advancing years had left him but little desire for\par
display. He came back to a world which knew him not: and few of those\par
who notice a benevolent-looking old gentleman, who often passes an\par
afternoon in upper Broadway, suspect that under an assumed name he hides\par
the identity of Max Shinburne, the bank burglar.\par
\par
When Hurley awoke from his drunken fit in London and recognized that his\par
partner had both robbed and deserted him, he felt that his mission was\par
over, and that nothing remained but to return at once to America. Loud\par
and long and wrathful were the complaints over Shinburne's treachery.\par
Whatever he did to others, all felt that his dealings with them ought to\par
have been "on the square," but there was no help for it. He had\par
disappeared, and faint, indeed, was the chance that they would ever see\par
him again. The success of the crime, so far as they were concerned, had,\par
after all, been a failure. Vanished hopes and cheated visions were\par
their share, instead of the wealth they had anticipated, and in their\par
devouring rage they tried to console themselves with the thought of what\par
they would do to him if they ever met Shinburne.\par
\par
The only man who had any real success from the scheme was the president.\par
Exposure had become impossible. He had taken good care not to leave too\par
much in the safes for his accomplices, and he was henceforth a wealthy\par
man. The bank, desperately shaken by the robbery, fell so greatly in the\par
esteem of the public that not long after it failed. The president gave\par
up banking, and began to speculate in real estate. He increased in\par
riches and prospered in the world. He called his lands after his own\par
name. He thought his house would continue forever, and men praised him,\par
because he did well to himself. He settled his children comfortably in\par
life, and when he died, not so very long ago, all felt that the world\par
was better because he had lived in it, and that, although their loss\par
when he was taken was heavy, it was, nevertheless, his great gain.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER VII.\par
\par
GILDED SIRS WHO ARE NOT WISE.\par
\par
\par
After a pleasant voyage the Russia arrived, and one May morning I walked\par
into the Northwestern Railway station in Liverpool to take the train for\par
London. The bonds were in a little handbag, and I was free to look\par
around. Everything was novel and strange, and all things told me I was\par
in a foreign land. I had, like most young people, a particularly good\par
opinion of myself and something of an idea as to my own importance.\par
\par
We arrived in London amid a drizzling rain, and I was much impressed\par
with the mighty roar of the traffic in the streets. We drove to Langham\par
place, where I had a regular English tea, and liked it immensely, too.\par
The next night I left Victoria Station for Dover, and crossing the\par
Channel to Ostend, went through to Brussels and stopped there, having\par
wanted, ever since boyhood, to visit the field of Waterloo. I looked\par
through the city that day, visiting the famous City Hall and one of the\par
art galleries. Retiring early I arose early and drove out to the plain\par
immortalized by the giant struggle of those valiant hosts, but did not\par
purchase any of the relics which were freely offered. These have been\par
sold by shiploads to two generations of visitors. Returning to Brussels,\par
I paid my bill at the Hotel de Paris, and was amused over the\par
inventiveness of the proprietor in making charges--towels, candles,\par
soap, attendance, paper, envelopes, being among them.\par
\par
Going to the station I bought my ticket for Frankfort--that old town I\par
was destined to see so much of during the next few years. On my journey\par
I would pass through Cologne, and from there the railway skirts the bank\par
of the Rhine. This being my first visit to Europe, I was intensely\par
curious to see everything, especially the Cathedral at Cologne, and was\par
eager to linger a few days along the banks of the Rhine. But I was more\par
eager to complete the bond negotiations, and wisely resolved to go\par
direct to Frankfort, sell the bonds, then, with the money in my pocket\par
and all anxiety over, I would be in a state of mind to enjoy a short\par
holiday.\par
\par
I traveled through Belgium and some parts of Germany by daylight, and\par
was, as most Americans are who travel on the Continent, shocked to see\par
the employment of women. Soon after leaving Brussels I saw the, to me,\par
novel sight of a number of women shoveling coal, handling the shovel\par
like men. In other places I saw them laboring in the brick yards,\par
digging and wheeling clay, and everywhere they were to be seen working\par
at men's work in the fields.\par
\par
A traveler in my compartment proved a most entertaining companion. He\par
described himself to me as one who "went about pottering over a lot of\par
antiquities and fooling around generally."\par
\par
But my friend, the pottering old antiquary, gave me something of a\par
surprise. At Chalours all of our fellow travelers in the compartment\par
left us. Two of them were voluble French women, and they kept it up with\par
amazing energy for the six hours from Brussels to Chalours. At every\par
unusual swaying of the car there would be a volley of "Mon Dieus!" and\par
ear-piercing exclamations, and it was certainly a relief when they left.\par
\par
Bringing out a box of cigars, and my companion producing a flask of\par
wine, we soon became confidential. Presently, to my great amusement, my\par
Old Antiquary, warmed by the wine, confided to me that he was a\par
detective police officer and chief of the secret service at Antwerp,\par
that he was then working on a famous case, and had been shadowing one of\par
the ladies who had journeyed with us from Brussels. Before leaving\par
Brussels, he had discovered his quarry was to quit the train, and as he\par
had to go on to Mayence, he had turned the business over to a\par
confederate.\par
\par
I was young, and no doubt he thought me innocent; certainly he did not\par
withhold his confidence. This is the case he was investigating:\par
\par
There was a wealthy gentleman by the name of Van Tromp living in\par
Antwerp, a widower, 70 years of age, the father of a grown-up family,\par
and many times a grandfather. It had been his custom to go to\par
Baden-Baden every Summer, spending money freely both in pleasure and in\par
the famous gambling resorts there. The last time he had met a woman, the\par
Countess Winzerode, one of the many adventuresses to be found there, and\par
speedily became infatuated. This Van Tromp was a descendant of old\par
Admiral Van Tromp, who, in the mighty life-and-death struggle between\par
Holland and Spain, and in the two wars with England, the first when\par
Cromwell ruled, the second when the Second Charles was on the throne,\par
held up the fame and glory of Holland. In one case he swept the proud\par
navies of Spain from the seas and carried the Dutch flag around the\par
world. In the other, he was only vanquished after stubborn sea-fights\par
lasting for days, and only ended then because the stout admiral lay on\par
his deck with an English bullet in his heart. This Van Tromp was the\par
heir of the fame and the wealth of all the Van Tromps, and both had gone\par
on accumulating for 300 years.\par
\par
The self-styled Countess knew all this, and, as the sequel shows, knew\par
her man. She was 40, had been beautiful, was still comely, with good\par
figure, fair-haired, but with steel-blue eyes. She spoke many languages\par
and had dwelt in every land from Petersburg to Paris. It is needless to\par
tell how they first met or of the intimacy that sprang up between them,\par
but I will merely say in passing that within five days of their first\par
meeting he had given her a magnificent diamond bracelet, which had been\par
in his family more than a century. This alarmed his two daughters, who\par
were terrified at the mere suspicion that their father was in earnest,\par
and might possibly present them with a stepmother, above all, a\par
comparatively young stepmother, and, so far as physique went, a\par
magnificent animal, with promise of a long life--so long that her rights\par
of dower would make a cut in the Van Tromp estates and treasures, which\par
might well cause the old Admiral to rouse himself from his three-century\par
sleep in Dordrecht Church and once more walk these glimpses of the moon\par
in protest of the sacrilege. Then the scandal of a Countess-adventuress\par
becoming a Van Tromp--head of that family, too! They knew of his\par
penchant for the Countess, and cared nothing for it, until, with a\par
feeling akin to horror they observed at the dress ball one night the\par
Countess airing the historic bracelet. It would require a volume to\par
relate the scenes that followed in the Van Tromp domicile on this\par
paralyzing discovery; but prayers, tears and histrionic touches were all\par
met by the stolid reply of Van Tromp: "I please myself."\par
\par
As a last resort the daughters appealed to the Countess, offering all\par
their ready cash and a pension if she would only disappear. But visions\par
of the Van Tromp diamonds and of the Van Tromp bank account were in her\par
head and she was deaf to every appeal. In fact, she despised these\par
heavy, matter-of-fact Dutch ladies, and rather gloried to think that she\par
would soon be the female head of the Van Tromp house and stepmother to\par
these two highly respectable dames, who would perforce have to live in\par
her shadow. But then, of course, the Countess was a woman, and it is to\par
be feared that even good women love to triumph over others. She, of\par
course, could have no love for this portly old gentleman of seventy.\par
But it is pitiful to think he was madly infatuated. The poor old man, in\par
spite of his unromantic appearance, had warm blood in his veins and\par
plenty of romance in his heart. At last, in spite of gossip and\par
opposition, they were married, and then, instead of settling down, as\par
the happy groom had hoped, to a life of wedded bliss in one of his\par
country houses at Dordrecht, Lady Van Tromp insisted on spending her\par
honeymoon in Paris. There they went, and the very day of their arrival\par
the bride resumed a liaison with a beggarly count, who, not being an\par
actual criminal, yet was written black enough in the books of the Paris\par
police, and for whom the Countess had as warm an admiration as one of\par
her cold, calculating nature was capable of feeling.\par
\par
Van Tromp speedily found his dream of bliss blown to the winds, but he\par
was not so blind as not to see that his wife not only did not love him,\par
but was false to him as well. Poor old Van Tromp felt he had made his\par
last throw for happiness, and hoping against hope, dreamed she in time\par
would learn to appreciate his devotion and would love him, and so tried\par
to persuade himself of her truth. The first anniversary of the marriage\par
found them at Baden-Baden, and there the unhappy husband, thinking to\par
give his wife a pleasant surprise, entered her chamber at an unusual\par
hour bearing a diamond necklace for a present, and found her in a\par
position which could no longer leave any doubt as to her faithlessness.\par
Seizing a chair he felled her companion, who never stirred again; but\par
the shock was too great for the husband, who himself fell to the floor\par
and instantly expired--the doctors said of heart disease, and I think\par
they were right. This event was only a few weeks old. The will had been\par
read, and it was found that he had literally left everything "to my\par
wife, Elizabeth."\par
\par
Here my friend, the chief of police and a distant relative of Van Tromp,\par
came to the front, determined quietly on his own account to investigate\par
Lady Van Tromp. He found this last was at least her third venture on\par
the stormy sea of matrimony. He had a fancy that some one of her\par
husbands might still be living and undiscovered. If this could be\par
proved, then her marriage to Van Tromp was no marriage, and the ducats,\par
dollars and diamonds bequeathed by Van Tromp to "my wife, Elizabeth,"\par
would instantly melt into air--into very thin air, so far as the\par
Countess was concerned; provided, of course, they had not actually\par
passed into her clutches. In fact, they were legally hers, for the will\par
had been admitted to probate. Those of the family objecting could offer\par
no valid opposition, and she had been put in possession, but, by a\par
strange neglect on her part, left everything intact, save a deposit of\par
300,000 gulden in the Bank of Amsterdam, which she secured and set out\par
for Naples with a new lover.\par
\par
The detective--whom I will call Amstel--discovered that she had first\par
been married when only 15 years old to a young Swiss in Geneva, who soon\par
left her and fled to America. He had subsequently returned to Europe,\par
but Amstel was unable to discover his whereabouts or if he was living.\par
He suspected that the Swiss was not only alive but in communication with\par
the Countess, and that she, in fact, might be his legal wife. He had\par
followed the Countess from Naples to Paris. There she left her lover and\par
was now on her way to Nuremberg, as Amstel believed, to meet her first\par
husband, but she had arranged to remain a few days with some old friends\par
of hers. Every movement she made there would be watched, while Amstel,\par
going on to Cologne to look up some clues, intended to wait there until\par
informed that she had departed, and when the train arrived at Cologne he\par
proposed to enter it and follow my lady on, hoping to witness a meeting\par
between her and the much hoped-for husband. Happily we had arrived at\par
Cologne at this point in the story, and as Amstel was to remain here we\par
had to say good-bye; but for the whole twenty minutes of my stay we\par
walked up and down the platform talking eagerly of the case. I had\par
become much interested, so deeply, indeed, that had I had leisure I\par
certainly should have turned amateur detective and joined Amstel.\par
\par
[Illustration: LONDON POLICEMAN.--ST. PAUL'S IN DISTANCE.]\par
\par
The train started, and, promising to write me in New York the outcome of\par
the case, we shook hands warmly and parted. He wrote me twice, and the\par
following year I returned to Europe and met Amstel at Brussels. We had a\par
very delightful time together, during which he told me the sequel of the\par
Van Tromp episode. Instead of one, the Countess had two husbands living;\par
but the Van Tromps preferred to buy off the woman at a good round sum\par
rather than have a public scandal.\par
\par
Amstel interviewed the Countess, and gave her the choice between arrest\par
and a full release of all claims on the Van Tromp property for the sum\par
of 100,000 gulden. She made a hard fight, but at last gave in\par
gracefully. But my chapter has grown too long already, and I will close\par
it with the remark that I myself met the lady at Wiesbaden in 1871, and\par
became acquainted with the brilliant adventuress. She will appear again\par
in the sequel.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER VIII.\par
\par
THE MERRY SUMMER OVER AND NO HARVEST STORED.\par
\par
\par
From Cologne to Frankfort is about 140 miles, and swiftly our train sped\par
along up the Rhine--the lovely stream about which poets have raved for\par
twenty generations. What classic ground! What scenes have its waters\par
reflected, its mountains looked upon! In the old days its rolling floods\par
made a deep impression on the stout Roman heart. More than one army,\par
carrying with it the hearts of the Roman world, had crossed that river\par
and plunged into the unknown forests beyond, only to go down in the\par
shock of conflict with the brave but barbarian foe, leaving not one\par
solitary survivor to carry back tidings to Rome of the fate of her army.\par
And down through all the linked centuries the history of the Rhine has\par
been the history of giant armies marching against each other, and of\par
brothers slaughtering brothers. To-day the plains of Germany and France\par
bear a million of armed men, ranged face to face, with only the Rhine\par
between, eagerly awaiting the signal to pour a deadly rain on each\par
other. And for what?\par
\par
The last face that I saw at the Cologne station was that of Amstel, lit\par
up with smiles as he waved his hand in adieu. Sitting cozily in the\par
corner of the carriage, eager to see all that was to be seen, I found,\par
as all tourists do, much to charm and delight. But my thoughts were on\par
the bonds I had to sell, and I was glad enough when at 5 o'clock our\par
train drew into the depot at Frankfort.\par
\par
Alighting I took a cab and drove to the Hotel Landsberg, and, although\par
tired, the scenes and surroundings were too novel for me to think of\par
sleep. So I dined and went out to view the city, but as I will have\par
occasion to refer to the place again, I will leave any description of it\par
until another chapter.\par
\par
In London there was an American banking house that has since failed, but\par
which at this time was doing a large business in the way of issuing\par
letters of credit. The firm was patronized chiefly by Americans. It\par
issued credits, or letters of credit, without inquiry, to any one\par
applying for them. While in London I called at their office, 449 Strand,\par
and paying $750 was given a credit for L150, which I took under an\par
assumed name. I wanted this letter to serve as an introduction to some\par
of the bankers at Frankfort, and to open the way for the negotiation of\par
the bonds. The Frankfort correspondents of the London firm were Kraut,\par
Lautner & Co., on the Gallowsgasse. The next morning I repaired to the\par
office of this firm, and producing my letter was very cordially\par
received, and invited to make my headquarters in their office during my\par
stay at Frankfort, which for the next day or two I did. However, I\par
called on several other bankers, also feeling the way, and finally\par
selected the firm of Murpurgo & Wiesweller, bankers widely known and of\par
enormous wealth. I had several talks with Murpurgo, and told him I was\par
arranging to purchase a number of copper mines in Austria, and if the\par
deal was closed I should sell a large block of American bonds and use\par
the cash I realized to pay for the purchase of the mines. I suppose he\par
thought to make a good thing out of it, and was eager to purchase.\par
\par
My reader will recall that payment upon all United States bonds payable\par
to bearer, as mine were, could not be stopped, and so far as the\par
innocent holder was concerned he was perfectly secure. But the custom\par
among bankers was, whenever any bonds were lost by theft or fraud, to\par
send out circulars containing the numbers, asking that the parties\par
offering them might be questioned and held. But as American bonds were\par
sold in millions all over the Continent, and were passing freely from\par
hand to hand, as a matter of fact, little or no attention was paid to\par
such circulars, but, of course, had strangers of disreputable appearance\par
offered bonds in large sums, the lists might have been scrutinized and\par
awkward questions asked. Therefore I felt a trifle nervous, and\par
determined to run no chance of losing my bonds--at least not all of\par
them. So I resolved to go to Wiesbaden, some fifteen miles away, stop at\par
some hotel under a different name, leave the bonds there, and take the\par
morning train for Frankfort, conduct my negotiations, and return to\par
Wiesbaden every evening. It was at this time easy to lose one's identity\par
in Wiesbaden, for the town then was, along with Baden-Baden, the Monte\par
Carlo of the Continent, and adventurers, men and women, from all over\par
Europe flocked there in thousands to chance their fortune in the\par
gambling halls. Although a little in advance of this portion of my\par
history, I will here relate an adventure of mine there, some years after\par
the period of which I am speaking.\par
\par
I will, however, preface my narrative with a brief account of the\par
history of the place. The city of Wiesbaden, previous to the\par
Franco-German war of 1870, was the chief town of one of those petty\par
principalities which were plentifully sprinkled over the face of Europe.\par
Since the old Roman days the town had been famous for its hot springs,\par
and consequently for its hot baths, and a good many people--during the\par
Winter particularly--resorted there to bathe and to drink the waters. As\par
a matter of course, the townspeople, as the custom of such places is,\par
have recorded many a marvelous cure, ranging all the way from headache\par
to hydrophobia. But still the town was of little importance save\par
locally. The petty ruler, with a title longer than his income, lived in\par
the pretentious castle, beguiling the time by smoking cheap cigars or\par
ordering on banquets whose piece de resistance consisted of Gebratene\par
Gans und Kartoffeln, the unlucky bird being tribute in kind from the\par
farmyard of some peasant subject living in a miserable hut on black\par
bread.\par
\par
But a change was impending. A mighty wizard had visited the place, with\par
an eye quick to see the possibilities of the situation, with a brain to\par
plan and a hand to execute. His name was Francois Blanc, the head of the\par
great gambling establishment at Homburg. Vast as were his ambition and\par
achievements, he was a man of the simplest tastes.\par
\par
To see him--as I often have--in his seedy coat, his old-fashioned\par
spectacles on the tip of his nose, one would have taken him for a\par
country advocate whose wildest dreams were of a practice of two thousand\par
thalers a year, with an old gig and wheezy mare to haul him around the\par
country side from client to client. Before his Wiesbaden days he had\par
been the guiding spirit in the direction of the splendid gambling halls,\par
the Casino at Homburg. Blanc was impervious to flattery; a hard-headed,\par
silent man, a man without enthusiasm and without weaknesses, who kept a\par
lavish table and ate sparingly himself, who had a wine cellar rivaling\par
that of the Autocrat of All the Russias and yet contented himself with\par
sipping a harmless mineral water; who kept and directed a huge gambling\par
machine--a mighty conglomeration of gorgeously decorated halls, wine\par
parlors and music rooms, crammed day and night by giddy and excited\par
throngs, but himself never indulging in anything more exciting than an\par
after-dinner game of dominoes or a quiet drive with his wife through the\par
country lanes.\par
\par
Thus this Francois Blanc, with perfect equanimity, watched the thousand\par
thousands of butterflies and moths of society scorch their wings in the\par
terrific flame that glowed in his Casino, while he looked on, a cynical\par
observer, despising the fools enraptured with roulette and fascinated\par
with rouge-et-noir.\par
\par
But one thing he was not afraid of, and that was spending money. To\par
compass his business ends he laid it out lavishly, and in the end he\par
drew all Europe to Wiesbaden. Still broader and still deeper he laid the\par
foundations of the fortune that ultimately grew to colossal proportions.\par
But he did not make Wiesbaden famous without keen opposition. He made\par
the fortune of the beggarly Prince Karl and the whole hungry crowd of\par
royal highnesses in spite of themselves. At every fresh opposition he\par
simply opened his purse and a golden shower fell on them.\par
\par
It required a hard head to withstand the attacks made on him when it\par
became known that he had bought up both prince and municipality, and\par
proposed to make Wiesbaden par excellence the gambling city of the\par
Continent. But, despite of all, he pushed on his plans to wonderful\par
success. A great park was laid out and stately buildings arose, all\par
dedicated to the goddess of chance. Slim was the chance the votaries of\par
the game had in his gorgeous halls. He threw out his money in millions,\par
but he knew the weak, foolish heart of man, the egotism of each and\par
every one of us, that leads us to ignore for ourselves the immutable law\par
of numbers. So he counted upon his returns, and never counted in vain.\par
\par
As I say, he had a hard head to withstand the attacks made upon him.\par
Every day the post brought hundreds of letters containing propositions\par
of threats from people who had lost their money and demanded its return\par
with fierce threats, pitiful supplications and warnings of intended\par
suicide, place, date and hour carefully specified, so there could be no\par
mistake, and more than one attempt was made upon his life. But the\par
equanimity of Francois Blanc was equal to all adventures. Threats,\par
prayers, temptations, left him untouched. This man of ice,\par
self-possessed, cold, indifferent to the ruin of the thousands of\par
victims of his will, had a fad or fancy. It was for raising red and\par
white roses, and while the mad throngs were fluttering in frenzy around\par
the tables in his halls at Homburg, Wiesbaden and Monte Carlo, he, hoe\par
or trowel in hand, would be training and transplanting his roses,\par
solicitous over an opening bud or deploring the ravages of an insect;\par
or, again, refusing all invitations, would sit down with his wife to a\par
dinner of boiled turnips and bacon, washed down with a glass of Vichy\par
water and milk. This was the town and these the scenes constantly\par
occurring there.\par
\par
Now for my adventure. In 1870, just before the war cloud burst, covering\par
all that part of the world, I was stopping for some weeks at the Hotel\par
Nassau. It stands in the main street, opposite the park gate leading to\par
the Casino. All the world went to Wiesbaden to be amused. However\par
fashionable frivolity and vice may be elsewhere, here it was strictly de\par
rigueur, and to pretend to decency and sobriety would be to stamp one's\par
self a heathen and barbarian, all unversed in the glorious\par
flower-wreathed Primrose Way of our orb.\par
\par
The daily routine for the throng began with coffee in bed at 8 a.m.,\par
then dressing gowns were donned, and the bath in underground floors of\par
the hotel were sought and a bath had in the hot mineral waters, which\par
were conducted to all the hotels direct from the hot springs of the\par
town. Half an hour in the bath, then a light breakfast, preparatory to\par
sallying out for an hour on the Spaziergang around the Quellen to drink\par
the water, listen to the band, see and be seen, but, above all, to\par
gossip and tell lies. At 11 a.m. the gambling began in the Casino, and\par
with a rush the seats around the tables would be filled. Then speedily\par
there would be rows behind rows of eager players or spectators, and what\par
a sight it all was to the cool-headed observer.\par
\par
With what keen interest all watched the result of the first turn of the\par
card at the card tables and the color of the first hit at roulette. For\par
all gamblers are superstitious, and are devout believers in omens. Those\par
whose luck or pocketbooks held out gambled steadily on, or, if luck\par
turned against them, would leave the table, go to do some fantastic\par
thing to change their luck and then return. At 2 p.m. the band (a very\par
fine one) played in the Musik Saal, and most of the idlers and morning\par
players gathered there to listen to the music and to drink and dine.\par
Here in this hall the intrigues begun on the promenade or in the\par
gambling-rooms were helped along by the ample opportunities of meeting,\par
with the passions stimulated by the music and the wine. At 4 o'clock\par
many took an afternoon nap. Then came the chief event of the day, the\par
ponderous table d'hote. At 9 p.m. every one flocked to the Casino, and\par
the game went merrily on until midnight. Then to bed, each and all with\par
more or less Rudesheimer or Hochheimer stowed away.\par
\par
At the time of which I speak many were my idle days, in which I was free\par
to seek pleasure. I used to find much enjoyment in frequenting the\par
Casino to watch the people and to play the role of "looker-on in\par
Vienna," which, by the way, is a star role and therefore rather\par
agreeable. One evening while watching the rouge-et-noir I noticed a lady\par
just in front of me, magnificently dressed in all, save that there was\par
an entire absence of jewelry. She was literally dressed to kill, and,\par
although near 50, yet to the casual observer she seemed no more than 40,\par
or even less. She was a well-preserved woman of the world, and was known\par
as the Countess de Winzerole. This was the adventuress who had married\par
Van Tromp some two years before. What a career had been that of this\par
woman!\par
\par
She had been mistress from first to last of a dozen men, noblemen,\par
diplomats, soldiers, but being an inveterate gambler, one after another\par
saw, with dismay, the cash, estates, diamonds, carriages, costly furs\par
and laces he showered upon her all go whirling into the ever-open maw of\par
the Casino, or in the drawing-room games of the bon-ton in Paris or\par
Petersburg. One brave youth, an officer in the Prussian Guards, had, in\par
his infatuation for the Countess, and impregnable, as he thought,\par
against bankruptcy by reason of his great fortune, tried to satisfy her\par
cravings for splendor of entourage and her infatuation for gambling. The\par
result was that one day the crack of a pistol-shot was heard in the\par
Countess' chamber, and the servants rushing in found the young bankrupt\par
dead, lying across the bed, with a bullet through the heart. The next\par
day a horde of clamorous creditors besieged the house, where the\par
Countess calmly told them she had sent for her bankers and on the morrow\par
they would be paid. That night his comrades buried their dead friend\par
with military honors. At midnight the cortege passed the hotel, and all\par
eyes watched the lovely Countess robed in white as she appeared, her\par
bosom heaving with emotion, while she waved a farewell to her dead\par
lover. Ten minutes later she fled through the back door and over the\par
garden wall, falling into the arms of another lover waiting there. He\par
himself did not go the way of the last, but half of his fortune did; so\par
one morning, leaving a polite note of farewell, he, taking for companion\par
the dressing maid of his mistress, embarked for America.\par
\par
At the time I met her the Countess' reputation was too well known and\par
her beauty too much fallen off for her to make any more grand catches. A\par
local banker at Wiesbaden became very friendly. However, the friendship\par
lost all its warmth when the banker's stout wife one day caught them\par
together, and having already provided herself with a whip in\par
anticipation, visited them both with a jealous woman's rage and a sound\par
thrashing.\par
\par
Now, the Countess spent her time around the tables, following the\par
winners and getting douceurs from them. These were by no means\par
small--most of them being gifts pure and simple, given from mere\par
goodness of heart or sheer prodigality for there were too many gay and\par
beautiful women flocking around ready to smile on winners in the game\par
for the Countess now to make even a temporary conquest. However, at\par
this period she lived well--even extravagantly--but, of course, saved\par
nothing. As related, I first met the Countess here at the table where\par
the game was going on. She had just staked and lost her last gulden. She\par
was betting on the black, and four times in succession the red had won.\par
She turned, and looking in my face, implored me to bet a double\par
Frederick on the red. I instantly placed the money on the red and won.\par
She begged me to transfer the stake to the black. I did so, and black\par
won. Placing her hand on the stake, she said: "Sir, leave it; black will\par
win again." Sure enough, it did. She seized the cash, $80, and handing\par
me a double Frederick, said in her most bewitching manner: "Oh, sir; be\par
generous and let me keep this!" I said: "Certainly, madame." She\par
promptly staked it, and in two turns of the cards it was gone.\par
\par
We met several times the next few days, but merely bowed without\par
speaking.\par
\par
One afternoon, entering the Musik Saal, I took a small table, and,\par
ordering a bottle of wine, sat down to listen to the music and watch the\par
throng. The Countess came in, and seeing me alone, came straight to me,\par
shook hands warmly and sat down. I, of course, invited her to have a\par
glass of wine. We soon finished that bottle and ordered another. We had\par
what was to me a most amusing talk. She was a character--had been\par
everywhere and spoke all the modern languages. She assured me that I was\par
a very charming gentleman. In paying my bill I incautiously displayed a\par
gold piece or two, and, seeing she was going to ask me to give her one,\par
I saved her the trouble by placing one in her hand. In time we became\par
quite good friends. Twice I paid her board bill in order to rescue her\par
wardrobe from the clutches of her landlord, and once I saved her from\par
the hands of an irate washerwoman. When, after a time, I left Wiesbaden,\par
I left her as gay, as prosperous and as extravagant as ever.\par
\par
I did not see Wiesbaden again for over two years, but the second week\par
of January, 1873, found me there. The Prussian Government now ruled in\par
the town, and refused to renew the license of M. Blanc. It had expired\par
fourteen days before my arrival. What a change had fallen on the town!\par
The Casino was gloomy and cold, the gay crowds had fled. All the life\par
and movement of the street and promenade was forever a thing of the\par
past. I had located there simply as a precaution, disposing of large\par
amounts of bonds in Frankfort, fifteen miles away, and returning to\par
Wiesbaden each night. At this time I put up at the Hotel Victoria, near\par
the railroad station. One Saturday, going up to Frankfort rather late,\par
my business detained me until after dark. On reaching the station I\par
happened to look into the third-class waiting-room, and there I spied a\par
figure alone that looked familiar. I soon recognized the Countess. From\par
her appearance and surroundings it was plain that there was now no\par
wealthy lover at her beck and call. Because she looked so unhappy I gave\par
her a cordial greeting, which she returned rather wearily. It was very\par
cold, and I was clad in furs from head to foot; besides, I was,\par
apparently, on the full floodtide of fortune, having with me then a very\par
large sum of money, some of which she could have had for the asking.\par
\par
I said: "Come, Countess; let us go together first class to Wiesbaden."\par
She replied that she lived at Bieberich, a small town on the Rhine, four\par
miles below Mayence, and four miles from Wiesbaden. As the train was\par
starting I bade her good-bye, but asked permission to call on her the\par
next day. She consented, giving her address as Hotel Bellevue.\par
\par
The next morning was very cold, but I enjoyed that, so, after a light\par
breakfast, I started over the hills for a walk to the town, arriving\par
there soon after noon. I found the hotel, a fifth-rate one, and\par
entering, was shown to the room of the Countess. What a change for her\par
from the past! Her room was a small one, plastered, but unpapered, and\par
with a few articles of furniture of the cheapest. The poor woman was\par
too evidently in a state of frightful depression, and well she might be.\par
Hers had been a butterfly existence, life all one Summer holiday, no\par
hostages given to fortune, no bond taken against future wreck or change.\par
Like the butterfly, she had roamed from flower to flower, sipping the\par
sweet only, or, like the cricket, had merrily piped all the Summer\par
through, thinking sunshine and bloom eternal. Even when youth and beauty\par
had fled, and lovers no longer stood ready to attend and serve, she\par
still found a good aftermath in her happy harvest field on the floors of\par
the Casino, but when the Casino lights at Wiesbaden went out, then, for\par
the Countess, had the Winter indeed come.\par
\par
My walk had given me something of an appetite, and it now being 2\par
o'clock I at once proposed to have dinner. To my surprise she said she\par
had already dined, and upon my remarking that it was early for dinner,\par
she replied that it was, but as she was owing quite a hotel bill she\par
feared to give any trouble lest the landlord might present his bill, and\par
in default of payment she was liable to arrest and a very considerable\par
imprisonment. I need hardly tell my readers that they do these things\par
differently in Germany than with us. I could easily afford to be\par
generous with other people's money, and did not mean to see the Countess\par
suffer for a hotel bill. Ringing the bell, I told the waiter to bring me\par
some dinner and a bottle of wine. The Countess looked very uneasy over\par
my order. Of late years she had seen life from the seamy side and had\par
observed so much of the falseness and cruelty of men that she had\par
apparently lost all faith in them, and no doubt thought me an\par
adventurer, one who might possibly dine and order expensive wines,\par
leaving her to face an angry landlord. While dinner was being prepared\par
she told me she was in the greatest distress; had not even a single\par
kreutzer to pay postage, and, worst of all, was owing for two weeks'\par
board. She had no means to fly, no place to fly to, and if she remained\par
incarceration awaited her. She had for weeks been writing everywhere to\par
every one she had known, former lovers, distant, but long-neglected\par
relatives. The result--dead silence; no response from anywhere. She at\par
last was alone, caught in the world's great snare, with no friendly hand\par
to shelter or save. It was a sight to read this woman's face. There\par
swept over it all the conflicting waves of regrets over might-have-beens\par
and the gloomy shades of despair. Both proprietor and waiter appeared to\par
set the table; it was for one, but wineglasses for two were brought\par
unsolicited. They were officiously anxious to please "Your Highness," as\par
they christened me. The Countess sat looking gloomily out of the window\par
across the Rhine, while I watched her face until an infinite pity for\par
the shipwrecked soul filled my mind. Dismissing the waiter I went to the\par
window, and standing by her chair I said: "Don't worry any more,\par
Countess; I will pay your bill." At the same time drawing from an inner\par
pocket a book crammed with notes, I placed seven 100-thaler notes in her\par
lap, saying: "This one is for your board bill, and the other six are for\par
your pocket money." I need not attempt to picture her amazement and\par
delight. Certainly she appeared very grateful. We had a long\par
conversation and I was talking to her like a brother. Perhaps had she\par
still been beautiful and young my manner and language might have been\par
less brotherly. I told her she had danced and sung, but at last the time\par
had come for toil, and suggested she should go to Brussels, which is\par
ever thronged with tourists, where her knowledge of languages and her\par
savoir faire could be made available in one of the many shops where\par
gimcracks are sold to travelers. I advised her to offer a small premium\par
for a position. This she said she would do.\par
\par
In saying good-bye I promised to see her again the next night, but I\par
found a telegram awaiting me on my arrival at my hotel which called me\par
to meet two of my companions at Calais, and I was forced to leave by an\par
early train. The next time I saw the Countess was at Newgate. She\par
visited me there, and was in perfect despair over my position and her\par
inability to serve me. For those who may care to know more of her, I\par
will say that, following my advice, she went to Brussels and obtained a\par
position in a Tourist Exchange and within a year married the proprietor,\par
who was a Councilman and a man of considerable local importance. She\par
made him a good wife and became a true mother to his five daughters.\par
When he died he made her guardian to both of them and his wealth. She\par
became very religious, and to the last was a devout member of the Roman\par
Church. She died in 1886, thirteen years after the episode at Rieberich.\par
Her ashes rest in the little graveyard of the Convent des Soeurs de Ste.\par
Agnes, on the Charleroi road, two miles from the city, and on her\par
monument is engraved:\par
\par
     TO ELIZABETH, The Beloved Wife, Pious and True. She Served God and\par
     Has Gone to Live with the Angels\par
\par
[Illustration: "THE LOVELY COUNTESS WAVED A FAREWELL TO HER DEAD\par
LOVER."--Page 81.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER IX.\par
\par
"WE HAVE ANOTHER JOB FOR YOU."\par
\par
\par
About every second day I called on Murpurgo & Weissweller in Frankfort,\par
and talked over matters, and easily saw that everything would go right.\par
All that was necessary was to produce the bonds, and they would hand\par
over the cash. Here in America, though we scrutinized a man's garments,\par
the quality and fit of the same having a certain value, we never take\par
much stock in a stranger because an artist tailor has decorated him, or\par
because he has plenty of money. But in the seventies, all over Europe,\par
from the mere fact that a man was an American and had the appearance,\par
dress and manner of a gentleman, they always took it for granted that he\par
must be a gentleman.\par
\par
Therefore, seeing that I was taken for a capitalist, and that no\par
question would be asked, I told the firm my deal in Austrian copper\par
mines appeared so certain to be completed that I had ordered the\par
securities I intended to dispose of to be forwarded from London. Giving\par
them a list, they gave me a memorandum offer for the lot. I accepted\par
their offer. The next hour was a very bad sixty minutes for me. There\par
was considerable delay, and my suspicions were fully aroused, and at one\par
time I thought they had made some discovery; but, as a fact, my\par
suspicions were wholly unfounded.\par
\par
The banker and clerks were simply hurrying around, anxious to oblige me\par
and have the money out of the bank before it closed. At last the amounts\par
were figured up and verified by myself. One of the partners hastened\par
off to the bank and in five minutes returned with a very pretty parcel\par
of 200,000 gulden; but, in spite of the evident safety of the business,\par
I was nervous, and resolved to put a good distance between me and the\par
town as speedily as possible. Before 5 o'clock I was in Weisbaden, and,\par
going directly to the Casino, where they kept at all times a million\par
francs, in addition to German money, and where the possession of large\par
sums attract no attention, I readily exchanged my money for 350\par
one-thousand-franc notes.\par
\par
       *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
Going to Rothschild's, I bought exchange on New York for $80,000, and\par
left the same night for London. Very many times I journeyed over that\par
route in after years, but never with so light a heart. I was young and\par
enthusiastic; all the glamour and poetry of life hung around me, while I\par
was too inexperienced to notice whither I was drifting, or to understand\par
the powerful current upon which I had embarked. In fact, I had sold\par
myself to do the devil's work, and day by day the chain would tighten,\par
while all the time I thought I could when I pleased stop short on the\par
downward grade and take the back track. More experience would have\par
taught me that every one who forsook the path of honor not only thought\par
the same, but had a purpose to even everything up some day and make\par
restitution. And to-day there is not a criminal but who, at the start,\par
looks forward to the time when he will no longer war against society,\par
but will go out and come in at peace with all men. But when one comes to\par
think of it, what a fool's game is that of a man who fights against\par
society!\par
\par
[Illustration: "THEY FOUND A BODY, RAGGED, EMACIATED, FORLORN. IT WAS\par
BREA."--Page 120.]\par
\par
The criminal has but two arms, very short and weak they are, and of\par
flesh, too. He has but two eyes that cannot possibly see around the\par
nearest corner, while society has a million arms of steel that can reach\par
around the world, and a million eyes which are never closed, that can\par
pierce the thickest gloom with sleepless vigilance. The poor, unhappy\par
criminal, by fortunate dexterity, may escape for a little, but at last\par
society lays her iron grasp on him, and with giant force hurls him into\par
a dungeon. As for the short-lived, tempestuous success that some few\par
criminals have, is there any sweetness in it? I say no; success won in\par
honest fight is sweet, but I know from my own experience that the\par
success of crime brings no sweetness, no blessing with it, but leaves\par
the mind a prey to a thousand haunting fears that make shipwreck of\par
peace.\par
\par
There were no sleeping cars in all Europe then, so I sat up in a\par
compartment and really enjoyed the ride, viewing the country by\par
moonlight. At midnight we arrived at Calais, and took the boat for\par
Dover. Then the express for London. Arriving at Victoria Station I took\par
a cab to Mrs. Green's, where I had breakfast a l'anglaise.\par
\par
I had a little adventure that night going down the Strand. At Bow\par
street, on the corner, is the "Gaiety," a famous drinking saloon,\par
flooded with light inside and out, with more than a half-dozen handsome\par
barmaids. Barmaids are a great institution in England--that is, they\par
have never more than one man behind a bar, none at all in the railway\par
bars. And a fearful source of ruin to the girls, as they are to\par
thousands of young men--I might say tens of thousands every year. These\par
girls are chosen for their beauty and attractiveness. Yearly, in London\par
and in other large cities of England, a "Beautiful Barmaid Show" is one\par
of the stated features, and is held in some public garden or monster\par
hall. These exhibitions are wonderfully popular, and thousands flock to\par
them. Various beauty contests are got up, and all the popular features\par
of voting, etc., are in vogue. Those of the young women who win the\par
prizes make their fortunes, for they are at once engaged at high\par
salaries for the more aristocratic barrooms. Fancy what an attraction\par
and even fascination the gin palace with lovely girls behind the bar\par
must have to the youth of a great city. Many of them strangers, busy\par
during the day, but with nothing to do at night, with the choice of the\par
street or a sombre room, but sure of a sweet smile of welcome from a\par
fascinating woman in the barrooms. How easily and how naturally, too,\par
does a young man become ensnared. But how if he has no money? No smiles\par
and no welcome for him! And then what a temptation to help himself to\par
his master's cash!\par
\par
Happy for our country that our laws forbid women entering that\par
occupation!\par
\par
While standing in the brilliant light of the Gaiety, watching the\par
thronging crowd of passers-by, with its sprinkling of unfortunates, I\par
saw one poor, bedraggled creature, wan-faced and hollowed-eyed, with\par
hunger and despair imprinted on every feature. Looking sharply at her\par
she caught my eye, and, crossing the street, she spoke to me. The poor\par
thing looked as if she had been dragged through all the gutters of\par
London. She said that herself and her baby were actually starving--that\par
her husband had been out of work thirteen weeks and had then deserted\par
her, owing twelve weeks' rent, and the landlady had just told her that\par
unless she paid her some rent before 9 o'clock that night she would be\par
turned out with her baby into the streets.\par
\par
Those of my readers who have been in London know something of what it\par
would mean for this woman to be turned out into the streets of that\par
fearful Babylon. No wonder, then, the poor soul was frantic with\par
despair. In her poverty a shilling looked as big as a cartwheel, and\par
when I said to her: "Will you promise to go direct home if I give you a\par
sovereign?" she cried out: "Oh, sir, God forever bless you if you will!"\par
I gave her the $5, and as she started to run I caught her by the sleeve\par
and said: "I will go home with you to see if you have told me the\par
truth." She lived close by, in one of those teeming courts that run off\par
from the Strand. We found her baby naked on a heap of rags, in a small,\par
dirty room, containing two broken chairs for furniture. I felt that\par
there were in the large city thousands of similar cases, but this one\par
was brought home to me. I was young and impressionable--more than that,\par
I had other people's money to be liberal with; so I called up the\par
landlady, who, almost dumb with surprise, received the arrears of rent,\par
along with a month in advance. Eliza, for that was her name, told me she\par
could get work if she had clean clothes for herself and baby, which she\par
could buy for L2. I gave her five, and giving her my address in New\par
York, told her to find work and let me know how she got on. She did find\par
work in an eel-pie shop in Red Lion Square, High Holborn. I saw her two\par
years later in London, and possibly may refer to her again in this\par
story.\par
\par
       *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
I went down to Liverpool and embarked on the good ship Java. Ten days\par
later we sailed through the Narrows.\par
\par
During my last day in London I went to Westminster Abbey, and spent\par
three hours in that Valhalla of the Anglo-Saxon race. It made a\par
tremendous impression upon my mind. In no other work of human hands do\par
the spirits of so many departed heroes linger, certainly in no other\par
does the dust of so many of the great dead rest, and as I read memorial\par
upon memorial to departed greatness I realized that the path of honor\par
and of truth was the only one for men to tread. All through the voyage\par
the influences of the Abbey were upon me; I felt I was treading on\par
dangerous ground, and resolved I would have no more of it. Would I had\par
then resolved, when I met Irving & Co., to throw all the plunder in\par
their faces and say: "I'll have none of it, and here we part!" I felt\par
that I ought to do that, but weakly said: "I need the $10,000, and I'll\par
give the rogues their share and then see them no more." I had fully made\par
up my mind to that, knowing Irving would be on the wharf, eager to meet\par
me.\par
\par
In sailing through the Narrows and past Staten Island I was making up my\par
mind as to the little speech I would make. We rapidly neared the wharf\par
in Jersey City, and I quickly recognized Irving standing on the edge of\par
the closely packed crowd, watching the steamer with a nervous look on\par
his face. A rogue suspects every one, and although by this time he had\par
become pretty well satisfied as to my good faith, no doubt he would be\par
happier when he had his share of the plunder safe in his pocket. I was\par
standing close to the rail between two ladies, and saw Irving before he\par
saw me. Waving my handkerchief, his eye suddenly fell on me. With a\par
smile and pointing significantly to my pocket, I gave him a salute. An\par
eager look came into his face, and waving his hand he cried out: "I am\par
glad to see you!" and no doubt he spoke the truth. When the gangplank\par
was thrown ashore, and I saw him making his way toward it, evidently\par
intending to board the steamer, I thought how surprised he would be when\par
I told him I would have no more of his game. He sprang on board, rushed\par
to me with a beaming face, grasped my hand, and putting the other on my\par
shoulder, led me toward the gangway. He had not spoken yet, but as we\par
were going down the gangplank he said: "My boy, you have done\par
splendidly," and then, putting his mouth close to my ear, whispered: "We\par
have got another job for you, and it's a beauty!"\par
\par
I don't mean to pester my reader with a moral, or by too much\par
moralizing, although I am tempted to do so. There is ample material for\par
a course of sermons in that "we have another job for you" coming to me\par
just then. But, leaving my reader to draw his own moral, I must go on\par
with my narrative.\par
\par
Going up the wharf with Irving, I was on the point of telling him I\par
wanted no more jobs, but weakly put it off, and by so doing, of course,\par
made it more difficult. He told me Stanley and White were waiting at\par
Taylor's Hotel on Montgomery street, a few doors up from the wharf. We\par
soon were there, and they gave me a warm and even enthusiastic\par
reception. Then I began to tell some of my adventures on the journey, to\par
which they listened with unfeigned admiration, and, opening my bag, I\par
produced the sixteen bills of exchange for $5,000 each, informing them\par
they should have their cash in ninety minutes. It was curious to see\par
these men handle the bills of exchange, passing them from one to\par
another, examining them with anxious care. But where were my good\par
resolutions, and what had become of them? Why, they, under the effect of\par
the wine and the magnetic influence of these three minds, had gone\par
flying down the bay, and under a favorable gale were fast speeding\par
seaward beyond the ken of mortal eye, not to be found by me again until\par
years after, when, with the toils about me, I found myself in Newgate.\par
Then the fugitives all came back, this time to stay.\par
\par
My three graces who adorned the Police Department of New York were full\par
of matter of a new enterprise, which by my co-operation was to make the\par
fortunes of us all. But they were too evidently anxious, too eagerly\par
desirous to handle the greenbacks my bills of exchange represented, to\par
fix their minds upon anything else.\par
\par
Stanley and White went away together, but first each once more told me\par
privately that he depended upon me to put in his own hands his share,\par
showing how these rogues suspected each other, and, indeed, were full of\par
suspicions of every one and every thing. Irving crossed the ferry with\par
me, but on the New York side dropped behind, and, although I paid no\par
more attention to him, no doubt he followed me. The excitement of\par
success and of being at home again banished any possible regrets or\par
fears over the course I had entered, and with a light heart and buoyant\par
step I quickly made my way to a friend of mine, a well-known broker in\par
New street, shook hands with him, and, telling him, very much to his\par
surprise, that I had just returned from Europe, asked him to step around\par
the corner to the office of the bankers and identify me. In a minute we\par
were there. Indorsing the drafts, I told them to make it in\par
five-hundreds; they sent out to the bank for them, and I was speedily on\par
my way to our rendezvous with 160 $500 greenbacks in a roll, and meeting\par
the three at the wineroom I made their eyes grow big when I flashed the\par
roll on their delighted orbs. The division was speedily made, I\par
retaining $10,000 for my share, and each promptly threw out a thousand,\par
and we shook hands all around and parted.\par
\par
Here were four conspirators of us, and it was comical to see how anxious\par
we all were to get away so that each could stow his plunder in a safe\par
place. For my part I went home, but I shall say nothing of the meeting\par
with the members of my family. I told them I had made a lot of money in\par
a speculation, and not knowing the inside history, or suspecting\par
anything, they rejoiced with me and were proud and happy for their boy.\par
I spent about a thousand dollars making things comfortable for them, but\par
to their grief I told them that circumstances required me to take up my\par
former quarters at the St. Nicholas.\par
\par
It would be interesting to tell of my reception among my acquaintances\par
on Wall street and other parts of the city. Rumor magnified my\par
resources, and it was reported I had cleared a hundred thousand dollars\par
in some fortunate deal. It was strange to see the new-found deference\par
all around, from my former employers down to my old waiter at downtown\par
Delmonico's, where I dined; but I will pass over all these matters and\par
proceed with my history of the Primrose Way.\par
\par
The next few days I went about engaged in the to me very agreeable task\par
of paying all my debts. The largest debt I was owing was one of $1,300,\par
partly borrowed money and partly a long-standing balance due on a\par
speculation negotiated on my account, and which did not pan out, but\par
entailed a loss. Then I indulged pretty freely in many little\par
extravagances in the way of tailor bills, etc. Two friends struck me for\par
a loan, and, strange to say, both remain unpaid to this hour, along with\par
some twenty-five years' interest. So, within a fortnight of my landing I\par
found my $13,000 reduced quite one-half, and as I was cherishing visions\par
of unbounded wealth, I began to feel quite poor, and anxious to see some\par
outcome to this "other job" my friends said they had ready for me. It\par
was at the very door.\par
\par
[Illustration: MANSION HOUSE, ILLUMINATED.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER X.\par
\par
A NINETEENTH CENTURY PRODIGAL.\par
\par
\par
Let no man who may be tempted to commit a crime ever fancy that if he\par
takes the first step down hill he will stop until he reaches the bottom.\par
If one of my readers flatters himself he can go one step, with no more\par
to follow, on the downward road, let such an one read this story to the\par
end and then forever abandon such an idea as a fancy born of\par
inexperience. For this history is as a handwriting on the wall, full of\par
warning to all and every one who may be tempted to take one step in any\par
other path than the path of honor.\par
\par
In 1865 there lived in London a famous Queen's Counsel, Edwin James.\par
Fame and fortune were his. A born orator, a talented scholar, he rapidly\par
pushed his way from the very bottom of the legal profession to all but\par
its topmost height. At 40 he found himself facile princeps of the\par
English Bar, and public opinion, that potent factor in popular\par
government, had already singled him out for the high position of\par
Attorney-General. That secured, only one step remained to place him in\par
the seat of the Lord Chancellor. Truly, an imperial position--one that\par
satisfied the proud ambition of a Wolsey and fitted the genius of a\par
Thomas a Becket. It carries with it the position of keeper of the\par
conscience of Her Majesty, giving the possessor precedence in all\par
official functions over the English aristocracy, next to royalty itself.\par
\par
But about this time dark whispers began to fly about through the clubs\par
of London. Soon it became known that Edwin James, the Lord Chancellor\par
to be, was in the toils, and it shortly transpired that, in spite of the\par
fact that his income from his profession was nearer twenty than ten\par
thousand pounds per annum, it had proved insufficient and he was heavily\par
in debt, and worse.\par
\par
It would seem he was keeping up what in the polite language of society\par
are known as dual houses. A woman of brilliant beauty presided over one,\par
and the marvelous beauty of its mistress was only equaled by her\par
extravagance. He also had a fondness for associating with younger men\par
than himself, and had got into a particularly fast set of young lords\par
and army men. At his club he had lost large sums at baccarat and loo,\par
and, in an unhappy hour for himself and his, he stooped from his high\par
position and--miserable to think of--committed a crime. This, in the\par
expectation that he would relieve himself from some of the more crushing\par
obligations he had heaped upon himself, either through the extravagant\par
vagaries of his imperious mistress, or by his own rashness in trying his\par
luck among a lot of titled sharpers. He had among his clients one fast,\par
even madly extravagant youth, heir of an historic name and of a lordly\par
estate. To supply his extravagance "my lord" had applied to the money\par
lenders--those sharks that in London, as elsewhere, fatten on such game.\par
These gentry were eager to lend the young blood money upon what are\par
known in English law as post-obits, which loans in this particular case\par
carried the trifling interest of about 100 per cent. per annum. James\par
was cognizant of his friend's excursions among the money lenders, and no\par
doubt he thought the young spendthrift, when he came into his fortune,\par
would never know within a good many thousands how much he had borrowed,\par
nor even the number of post-obits he had given.\par
\par
I will just explain that a post-obit is a form of note or due bill given\par
by the heir of an estate (usually of an entailed estate), which matures\par
the moment the drawer of the document enters into that estate. That is\par
to say, the tender-hearted son discounts his father's death to provide\par
fuel to feed his flame. So Edwin James, driven to his own destruction,\par
stooped from his imperial position into what one might call ankle-depth\par
of crime.\par
\par
How little he dreamed there was a beyond--a huge, seething sea of crime;\par
an ocean whose billows are of ink, and which would soon sweep him from\par
his high place into the black waters, there to be buffeted until, honor\par
and hope all gone, he would, throwing his hands to heaven, with one\par
despairing cry, sink into its inky depths, adding one more ruined life\par
to the millions already engulfed. In that long, sad catalogue of the\par
dead there is probably not one, who, when taking the first step into\par
crime, ever thought a second would follow the first.\par
\par
But to come back to our gilded sir. He made out two post-obits for\par
L5,000, wrote his client's name at the bottom of each, gave them to the\par
money lenders, who, never doubting that the prodigal son had signed and\par
given them to his counsel, made no question, but gave James the money\par
for them at once. But James had reckoned without his host, for this\par
nineteenth century prodigal was made of keener metal than he of the\par
first. Strange to say, and utterly unexpected as it was to all who knew\par
him and had looked upon his riotous living, he kept his books straight,\par
and knew to a single guinea how much and to whom he was owing.\par
\par
His discovery of the forgery was accelerated by the sudden and most\par
unexpected death of his father, his return home and stepping into his\par
estate.\par
\par
The various post-obits were presented and placed before him. He\par
instantly pronounced the two for five thousand pounds each to be\par
forgeries, and the crime was easily laid at the door of the Queen's\par
Counsel. The heir indignantly refused to condone the offense, and,\par
revealing the fatal secret to a few, within a month it was known in\par
every clubroom in London. From there it got into the newspapers, and\par
they, under a thinly disguised alias of a "distinguished member of the\par
Bar," gave more or less accurate details of the damning truth. His\par
former client eventually said he would not prosecute the forgery if the\par
criminal left England; if not, he would immediately go before the Grand\par
Jury, procure an indictment, and have this man, who had moved a prince\par
among men, arraigned in the dock at the Old Bailey, there to plead and\par
stand trial like any common criminal.\par
\par
And he fled. Of course, like all fugitives from justice throughout the\par
Old World, he looked to America for a city of refuge, and here he came.\par
Not to keep my readers too long from the main narrative, it will suffice\par
to say that soon after his arrival he applied for admission to the Bar\par
of New York, but first he won to his cause the high-souled Richard\par
O'Gorman, then a leader of his profession.\par
\par
It was for Edwin James a lucky stroke, for at this time O'Gorman was in\par
full possession of his magnificent powers. Few could resist his magic.\par
His great heart was stirred, and he took up the cause of his friend as\par
if he had been his brother. The English lawyer's reputation was known to\par
every member of the Bar of New York, and there had been and still was a\par
bitter opposition to his admission; but when it became known that their\par
eloquent leader was his champion, many began to feel that after all "the\par
poor fellow ought to be given another chance," and when at the next\par
meeting of the Bar Association O'Gorman in a set oration brought all his\par
splendid eloquence into play the cause was won.\par
\par
Great-hearted O'Gorman had helped this lame dog over the stile, but the\par
dog's heart was not in the right place, and, as my reader will see in\par
the sequel, he soon went lame again. * * *\par
\par
       *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
In the rear room of a somewhat luxurious range of offices in a building\par
on Broadway, facing the City Hall, four men were engaged in discussing\par
what was evidently an exciting topic. The door of the main office bore\par
the sign "Edwin James, Counselor-at-Law and Register in Bankruptcy." He\par
was one of the four. He had failed lamentably in his efforts to secure a\par
practice. The effects of O'Gorman's eloquence had in the gray light of\par
commonplace day faded away, the more so when the ideal his magic had\par
created in the minds of men was in hourly contrast with the man himself\par
and his history. His professional brethren looked upon him with\par
suspicion, and there was a general impression abroad that his escapades\par
were not over yet.\par
\par
He had launched out in his office and home somewhat extravagantly, and\par
now, once again pressed by clamorous creditors, he had once more drifted\par
upon the borderlands of crime, and was here with his companions planning\par
a criminal transaction in order to pay his more pressing debts.\par
\par
One of these four was Brea, who, with a keen eye to business, had\par
married the discarded daughter of a wealthy but not over-respectable New\par
York family, and he had, unsuspected, pulled the wires so that James had\par
been employed as the family lawyer, and in that capacity had drawn the\par
will of the mother. She was an imperious, hot-tempered body, one who,\par
when aroused, was accustomed to use language more vigorous than polite,\par
and who not infrequently went to fisticuffs with her daughters. The\par
husband and father, the creator of the fortune, was dead and the vast\par
family property, in securities, stocks and lands, was vested absolutely\par
in the mother. In the old lady's will Brea's wife, the second daughter\par
of the house (there were no sons), was down in the very first paragraph\par
for the magnificent sum of "one dollar lawful currency," and her name\par
nowhere else appeared in the lengthy document. The old lady was such a\par
termagant and so implacable in her hatreds that it was a moral certainty\par
she would never relent and change her purpose toward her daughter. But\par
James had also drawn up a second will of his own and Brea's\par
concoction, and a precious piece of villainy it was, in which the wife\par
was down for legacies amounting; to $750,000. The genuine will James\par
kept in his own possession, ready to destroy the very moment word came\par
that the old lady was an immortal, while the spurious will was kept in\par
the vaults of the Safety Deposit Company, there to remain until the\par
death of the testatrix, when, of course, it would in due time be\par
produced.\par
\par
[Illustration: BANK OF ENGLAND PARLOR.]\par
\par
Brea had been introduced to the other three men, and cultivated their\par
acquaintance in the belief that they would some day be useful to him. He\par
had a few days before introduced them to James. As a matter of\par
precaution he had concealed from them all knowledge of the will. At the\par
same time he gave them a hint that there was something in the wind, but\par
that some way must be found to secure at once a few thousands, enough\par
for a year or two, until the good time came when fortune was to lavish\par
her favors on them all with a liberal hand. But money must be had at\par
once, for Brea and James were in sore straits, particularly James, who\par
had been threatened with arrest, and was so far involved that he always\par
entered and left his house at night in order to escape importunate\par
creditors. This was James' second interview with the men, and the first\par
time he had been alone with them. He saw at once that he had to do with\par
able, clear-headed men, took them into his confidence, and, in order to\par
excite their hopes and to bind them to him as well, he confided to them\par
the plot of the forged will, producing the genuine for their inspection.\par
He assured them that it was a sure and speedy fortune, as the lady was\par
old and frail in health, and he also promised they should share between\par
them $100,000, provided they would stand by to give a hand in the\par
somewhat improbable event of the other heirs disputing the will, but\par
above all, if they would devise some means to furnish him at once\par
$10,000, or at least $5,000. Money he must have, and he could no longer\par
do without it.\par
\par
The result of our conference in James' office was that the very next day\par
an office downtown was engaged under a fictitious name, and a simple,\par
unsuspicious fellow hired as porter and messenger. After some little\par
negotiation, we obtained particulars of parties banking with the then\par
great firm of Jay Cooke & Company, corner of Wall and Nassau streets.\par
Briefly told, the result was that four days later a messenger walked\par
into their banking house with a check for $20,000, purporting to be\par
signed by another firm, who banked with them. Along with the check went\par
a letter bearing a signature well known to the cashier, asking him to\par
pay the check to bearer. The result of all being that five minutes\par
thereafter we were walking unconcernedly up Broadway, and sending a\par
message to James to meet us at Delmonico's, corner of Broadway and\par
Chambers street, we sat down awaiting his arrival. He had anxiously been\par
looking for news, and almost before we had seated ourselves he entered,\par
eager and anxious-looking; but, when he glanced at our faces, a happy\par
expression came over his own, and without a word he put out his hand.\par
After a warm greeting, I produced the roll, and, to his delight, I\par
handed over to James ten five hundreds. On the morrow I went to the\par
office, and, paying my messenger a week's wages, besides making a small\par
gift, told him he need not come any more.\par
\par
With this twenty thousand coup we fondly thought all our troubles and\par
all our unlawful acts were ended. We now had a few thousands, sufficient\par
to last until the $5,000 we had invested in the will case should bring\par
in a dividend that would mean a fortune for us all. So we took things\par
easy about town, and altogether thought ourselves pretty good fellows,\par
and this world a very good sort of place to be in.\par
\par
Thus the Winter passed by and the Summer was at hand. Our thousands of\par
the year before had dwindled to hundreds, and the old lady whose heirs\par
we had constituted ourselves seemed to have renewed her youth, and\par
threatened to outlive us all.\par
\par
Besides this there had grown up a repugnance in our minds to the\par
business, and when one day my friend Mac remarked it was a scoundrelly\par
business to rob the heirs of an estate, and they women, George and I\par
heartily acquiesced; and we vowed we would take no part in the matter,\par
and then and there resolved we would throw both James and Brea over, but\par
first to use Brea and James for our own purposes. Once more we found\par
ourselves planning a coup in Wall street. Talking the matter over, we\par
three soon had a plan, and, being dowered with intense energy, it\par
promised a successful termination. Audaciously enough we determined the\par
lightning should strike once more in the same place--that is, to make\par
Jay Cooke & Company again the victims. Irving and his honest fellows\par
were to co-operate by watching everything, and, if any arrest\par
threatened, to be on hand to make it themselves; and then let the\par
prisoner escape. Most important of all, when the bankers drove up in hot\par
haste to Police Headquarters to give information, James, Honest James,\par
would be on hand to receive them, would call in his two trustys to get\par
with him full particulars of the robbery and a description of the men.\par
Then the bankers would be sent away with assurances that "we know the\par
men and will have them," but at the same time warning them to keep the\par
matter a secret in order better to enable them to catch the villains.\par
\par
If successful, the detectives were to receive 25 per cent. between them.\par
Our plan required James to play an important part, and, although no\par
confederacy could be fixed on him, yet he would hardly escape\par
questioning and a very considerable degree of suspicion, so much so that\par
it probably would put an end to any lingering remnants of character he\par
had on hand or in stock. But he was tired of America, and determined to\par
go to Paris with his share of the plunder. Our visits to James had\par
always been in his private office, and his clerks had never seen either\par
of us or Brea.\par
\par
Our plan was to make use of James' office in a way that will appear\par
later. As related, he was suspected by his profession, but the general\par
public thought him a very great man. He had appeared as (volunteer)\par
counsel in two or three murder cases and had delivered powerful\par
addresses which had attracted considerable notice in the papers.\par
\par
One day, soon after our plan was matured, Brea went to Philadelphia,\par
and, by a mixture of audacity and finesse, procured from Jay Cooke\par
himself (the parent house of the New York firm of Jay Cooke & Co. was in\par
Philadelphia) a letter of introduction to the manager of the New York\par
firm. He wanted the letter ostensibly in order to consult the manager\par
about certain investments which he, as executor of an estate, desired to\par
make for his wards.\par
\par
The transaction was made to appear as one of considerable magnitude, in\par
which there would be large commissions paid. With the grand send-off of\par
a letter from Jay Cooke to his subordinate in New York, the speculation\par
opened well--so well that we at once decided what we would do with the\par
money when we got it--a case in point for the old proverb. We had\par
ascertained the name of a Newark manufacturer who had recently failed in\par
business. I will call him Newman. On the morning after his return from\par
Philadelphia, Brea presented himself at James' office--it being arranged\par
that James himself be out, so Brea told the clerk that his name was\par
Newman, that he had lately failed in business, and intended to employ\par
Mr. James to put him through the bankruptcy court. The clerk told him to\par
come again at 12, and he would find Mr. James in. At 12 he came; the\par
clerk introduced him. James kept the clerk conveniently near, that he\par
could hear the conversation. Brea, as Newman, told James he had used in\par
his business $240,000 belonging to his wife and her mother, and that in\par
scheduling his assets he proposed to use enough to make those amounts\par
good, intending to conceal the fact from his creditors. He determined to\par
invest the amount in bonds--so ran his story--and was going to deposit\par
the money in the bank that very afternoon, at the same time producing\par
his letter of introduction from Jay Cooke. All of this, of course, being\par
for the eye and ear of the clerk, who might be required as a witness of\par
his employer's good faith.\par
\par
[Illustration: "MAC AND GEORGE WERE WITHOUT, AND WERE STRICKEN WITH\par
CONSTERNATION, FOR A MINUTE'S OBSERVATION OF THE GATHERING CROWD AND THE\par
RUSHING INTO THE BANK OF EXCITED PEOPLE CONVINCED THEM SOMETHING UNUSUAL\par
WAS IN THE WIND, AND THEY KNEW NOYES MUST BE IN DEADLY PERIL. MAC RUSHED\par
INTO THE BANK IN HOPE "TO WARN OR TO BE OF HELP."--Page 236.]\par
\par
Brea-Newman also paid James, in presence of the clerk, a retaining fee\par
of $250, which was privately returned. James banked in Jersey City, and\par
when Newman said, "Introduce me at your bank, as I want a small credit\par
handy," James said, "My bank is in Jersey City." The clerk's brother was\par
paying teller at the Chemical Bank, and, as was expected, he at once\par
spoke up, saying: "Let me introduce Mr. Newman in the Chemical Bank," so\par
down went Newman and the clerk, and in ten minutes our man had the\par
Chemical Bank checkbook in his pocket and $5,000 to his credit in the\par
bank. The same afternoon he presented his letter of introduction at Jay\par
Cooke & Co.'s, and was cordially received. He, of course, told a totally\par
different story there. In this case a relative, lately deceased, had\par
left him an estate of great value. He was, he said, realizing on his\par
real estate, and buying bonds as fast as his money came in, and he\par
wanted to invest a million in various railway bonds. At present he had\par
$240,000 on hand, which he wanted to invest in Government bonds. He then\par
left for the time being, leaving a good impression, which his refined\par
manner and appearance confirmed.\par
\par
So far all was well; that is, all was well from our point of view. The\par
next two or three days Brea paid several visits to the Chemical Bank,\par
getting small checks for $500 and $1,000 certified, and now had his\par
account drawn down to $1,000. The day before he had called on Jay Cooke\par
& Co. and told them he would take $240,000 in seven thirties, "Bearer"\par
bonds, and that he would call the next day and pay for them. At the\par
same time he got them to give him a proforma bill for them.\par
\par
The eventful day had come, and James, to get his head clerk out of the\par
way, sent him to the Admiralty Court to take notes of the evidence in a\par
case going on there.\par
\par
At 10 o'clock Brea sent a messenger with a note to the bankers,\par
requesting them to send the bonds to Edwin James' office, and he would\par
pay for them on delivery. He could not come himself, as he was in\par
consultation with the executors of the estate.\par
\par
In the mean time a check for the full value of the bonds, $240,000, had\par
been made out. It was drawn on the Chemical Bank, and was, in fact,\par
similar to those always given between bankers on bond transactions.\par
\par
Brea had drawn his own check for $240, and had it in his hatband with\par
the $240,000 dummy check. The plan is palpable enough. When the\par
messenger brought the bonds Brea, or Newman, was going to say: "All\par
right, I have the check here; bring the bonds and we will go to the\par
Chemical Bank and get them to certify my check." Then when at the bank\par
he would take out both checks, letting the messenger only get a glimpse\par
of one, and that would be the small $240 one, which Brea would pass in\par
through the window with a request to have it certified. This would be\par
done, and when handed out, of course, Brea was to change it and hand the\par
messenger the big one of home manufacture.\par
\par
It seemed impossible for the scheme to fail, and success in it meant on\par
the surface comparative wealth for us all, with, perhaps, in the not\par
distant future an entrance through the McAllister-guarded portals of the\par
Four Hundred.\par
\par
But here we have a vivid instance of how easily an elaborate scheme can\par
by the merest accident fall to pieces.\par
\par
The night before the expected coup we met James for a final full-dress\par
rehearsal for the morrow, and after everything was settled adjourned to\par
the uptown Delmonico's for supper. It so happened that Detective George\par
Elder was there. This Elder was a bright fellow, was in a ring--but not\par
in our ring--and, of course, had his bank account, diamond pin and\par
turnout for the road. He had had some acquaintance with me, but the rest\par
of the party were strangers. I did not see him at the time, but it would\par
seem he was curious, even suspicious, from some scraps of conversation\par
he overheard. However, neither his curiosity nor suspicion would have\par
been of any consequence or concern to us had it not been that, in going\par
out, Brea left on the table with some papers the memorandum or pro forma\par
bill of the bonds given him the day before by the bankers. Strangely\par
enough, the body of the bill alone was intact. The heading bearing the\par
name of the firm and purchaser had been torn off and destroyed.\par
\par
Elder picked it up and, having some vague suspicions of a plot\par
somewhere, he determined to go around among the hundred or more bankers\par
and brokers in and around Wall street and investigate quietly, without\par
making any report to his superiors, his immediate superior being, of\par
course, our honest friend, the worthy chief of the detective force, who\par
was anxiously looking for his percentage of the deal. The whole force\par
was split up into cliques, each intensely jealous of every other, each\par
with its own stamping grounds, and each strictly protected his own\par
preserves.\par
\par
At 9:30 the next morning Elder started around carrying the fragment of\par
the memorandum he had picked up from bank to bank and from one broker to\par
the other. He had spent over an hour making inquiries, and walked into\par
Jay Cooke & Co.'s office just as the messenger was leaving with the\par
bonds for James' office. Fifteen minutes more and the game was ours!\par
Elder produced the memorandum, and they at once recognized it as their\par
own. Elder asked them if they knew their man and were sure it was all\par
right. They said it was perfectly right, that Mr. "Newman" had been\par
introduced by the head of the firm in Philadelphia, and was also a\par
client of Edwin James; but then it was strange the bill should be\par
mutilated. Elder averred his belief that a fraud was intended, and\par
suggested that he and the manager should accompany the messenger with\par
the bonds. This alarmed the manager, and he directed Elder and the\par
messenger to await his return. Seizing his hat, he started for James'\par
office to investigate. James was there, and Brea (the pseudo Newman) was\par
in the private office with the two checks ready, anxiously awaiting the\par
arrival of the messenger with the bonds.\par
\par
Myself and all the other members of our party were nearby, watching and\par
awaiting developments. The manager, considerably perturbed, entered the\par
office, and James saw at once the business was a failure, for he knew,\par
of course, that any suspicion as to good faith would be fatal to the\par
success of the plot. Brea, hearing the voices and supposing it was the\par
messenger with the bonds, opened the door of the private office and was\par
vexed to see the manager, who, shaking him by the hand, told him the\par
bonds would arrive soon, at the same time saying: "I suppose you will\par
pay currency for the bonds?" To which Brea replied: "I will go to my\par
bank with you now and get my check certified for the amount and give it\par
to you, or leave it until the messenger comes with the bonds."\par
\par
This offer, along with Brea's coolness, apparently disarmed all\par
suspicions, and he said: "Oh, all right, the messenger will go to the\par
bank with you." He left the office, but stopped in the hall for a\par
moment, then turned and hastily re-entering, said: "By the way, Mr.\par
Newman, please draw the currency from the bank, and pay the notes to the\par
messenger upon delivery of the bonds."\par
\par
So the grand coup had failed, ignominiously failed, and through what\par
appeared a trivial accident. More such "accidents" at critical periods\par
will appear before this history is ended.\par
\par
The dummy check was still in our hands, and was at once destroyed, so,\par
with nothing to fear, we coolly walked up Broadway to dinner, and talked\par
of the future over a bottle of wine. At last we fixed upon a definite\par
plan. Clinking our glasses, we drank to "Eastward, Ho!"\par
\par
[Illustration: MERCHANTS EXCHANGE, ILLUMINATED.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XI.\par
\par
"CRACK THE LAWYER'S VOICE THAT HE MAY NEVER MORE FALSE TITLES PLEAD, NOR\par
SOUND HIS QUILLETS SHRILLY."\par
\par
\par
The Eastward Ho was a hint of a project we had frequently talked over as\par
a possible speculation. Here we see how men are led on step by step from\par
bad to worse when once they set out on the Primrose Way.\par
\par
In returning from Europe with the $10,000 commission in my pocket, I\par
vowed never again to engage in any unlawful speculation. I was through!\par
No criminal life for me! Then came the day when we struck for the\par
$20,000 and won, and we were all happy in the thought that our last\par
unlawful deed was over.\par
\par
Then we took the third step we had vowed never to take, and had\par
discussed the $240,000 project. We had spent money on it, had laid our\par
plans cunningly and deep, and were confident of success. We had even\par
planned how to invest our thousands in an honest business, and so win\par
the esteem of all good men, and, of course, in some happy future would\par
make restitution. But that is a future which never comes in the history\par
of crime. These three wrong steps had been taken only after convincing\par
ourselves that the circumstances justified each separate act.\par
\par
Such is the contradiction of human nature that even when planning crime\par
we not only intended to make restitution, but despised all other\par
wrongdoers and reprobated their crimes. Each wrongful act of ours was\par
to be the last, and it was with something like despair that we began to\par
realize that there was no stopping place on the dangerous road we were\par
treading.\par
\par
My $13,000 commission from the European trip had melted away. Our share\par
of the $20,000 got from Jay Cooke & Co. was fast going. Our deep-laid\par
plot to win $240,000 had miscarried, and now the necessity was upon us\par
of engaging in another illegitimate operation if we would continue in\par
our life of ease and luxury.\par
\par
For the next few days we did little but dine and plan. Discussion\par
followed discussion, and through them all we clung to the general\par
proposition that we would not do any more in our particular line in\par
America. At last we resolved to go to Europe and realize the fortune\par
that seemed to elude our grasp at home.\par
\par
We resolved to tell Irving in a general way that we were going to Europe\par
to make some money, and would pay him and his two fellows their\par
percentage. Then we could (apparently) work with impunity; for, of\par
course, if we committed a forgery in Europe and were recognised as\par
Americans--as probably we would be--the foreign police would report the\par
case to the New York police--that is, to Irving--and we should be safe\par
in New York.\par
\par
Edwin James and Brea had dropped out of our lives for good, but as my\par
readers will be curious to know of their fate in after times, I will\par
relate it in this chapter.\par
\par
The morning our scheme on Jay Cooke & Co. fell to pieces, as soon as the\par
manager left the office, telling Brea he was to pay cash for the bonds\par
in place of the check, it was recognized at once that the game was up,\par
and the only thing remaining was to shield James as much as possible. So\par
Brea left the office, but first instructed the clerk to tell the\par
messenger when he came that he had gone for the money, and would call\par
for the bonds. This was done, the messenger arrived, being accompanied\par
by Detective Elder all the time, and took the bonds back again.\par
\par
At 2 o'clock James went down to the bankers, where he was well known,\par
and inquired for Mr. Newman. Being told he was not in, he said he had\par
made an appointment to meet him there. Invited into the inner office,\par
the manager asked him if he had any personal knowledge of this Mr.\par
Newman, and James said no further than that he had called and given him\par
a retaining fee of $250, and had engaged him as legal adviser, etc. Then\par
the manager produced a telegram he had received in answer to one he had\par
sent to the Philadelphia house, inquiring about Newman, and asking if\par
his letter of introduction was genuine or not. James read the reply; it\par
said the letter was genuine, but that they knew absolutely nothing about\par
the man, and warned him to be cautious. James pretended astonishment,\par
and feigned to be very indignant, declaring that if Mr. Newman did not\par
put in an appearance within half an hour he should begin to fear a fraud\par
had been attempted. When the closing hour came at 3 o'clock, the manager\par
announced to James that he should give the whole matter to the press,\par
but would keep his name out of it.\par
\par
So they parted with warm congratulations over their escape, the manager\par
pretending to believe James was an innocent tool, but no doubt with a\par
shrewd suspicion that he intended to have a finger in this pie, had the\par
pie ever been baked and divided. Had the bankers been victimized they\par
would have striven with all their power to keep the fact a secret and\par
forbidden their employees to breathe a word about it to any one. But now\par
the case was different. All the morning papers had long accounts of the\par
transaction. They were absurdly inaccurate, but all agreed as to the\par
extreme cleverness of the manager, and noticed how he had suspected,\par
etc., while poor Elder, who both expected and really deserved all the\par
glory, was not even mentioned in the newspaper accounts. However, his\par
feelings were soon after solaced, as Irving informed us that Elder had\par
stood in on a deal that paid him well.\par
\par
The $5,000 we gave James eased up matters for a time. Practice he had\par
none, but managed to hold on in the hope of realizing on the Brea will\par
matter, but getting deeper and deeper in debt. One night, four years\par
later, the old lady, Brea's mother-in-law, had a more than usually\par
furious outbreak of temper, and fell to beating the three daughters\par
still living with her. Before it was over she had attacked and seriously\par
injured the eldest, and then flew to her room in a passion. Not\par
appearing at breakfast the next morning her daughter went to her room,\par
but she was not there, and the bed was undisturbed. Going to the room\par
that served for office and library, they found the door, as usual,\par
locked. Bursting it open the poor old maids found their mother huddled\par
in a corner of the room dead.\par
\par
Truly a happy relief for the daughters. Poor girls, theirs had been a\par
hard life. Every suitor who tried to cultivate their acquaintance had\par
been driven from the door by the mother, who never spent a dollar on\par
their education, and her death found them all unused to the ways of the\par
world. The result was that all became victims of fortune-hunters, and\par
the unhappy ladies only changed the tyranny of an unnatural mother for\par
the tyranny of a husband, who in each case wedded for wealth alone, and\par
all three husbands were uncultured men. What an experience! Two of the\par
three still live. How sweet the rest of the grave will be to them!\par
\par
The genuine will was destroyed and the "family lawyer," James,\par
immediately after the funeral, produced and read "the last will and\par
testament" of the dead woman. The four sisters and a host of poor\par
relations were present at the reading. When Sarah, Brea's wife, heard\par
her name read as chief heir of the vast estate, she was stunned, but if\par
she was stunned, the rest of the family were paralyzed. Legacies were\par
left to many, small in amount, save in the case of the other three\par
sisters, who were to have a certain tenement and land in Harlem and\par
three thousand a year for life out of the estate. None of those present\par
thought for a moment of questioning either the genuineness of the will\par
or the validity of the testaments, save only a poor relation, a nephew,\par
whose name was down for $500. He was indignant with the old lady and\par
loudly declared that he would not put up with it. The next day he\par
employed a briefless lawyer, one that had wit and brass enough and who\par
had his way to make in the world, and was determined to make it.\par
\par
Without waiting for the will to be probated or having legal authority to\par
do so, Brea and his wife, the very day of the funeral, moved into the\par
house and took possession. But before the week was out he had persuaded\par
the three old maids that they would be happier if away from the scene of\par
their parent's death, so he had them installed in their own house at\par
Harlem, he remaining in undisturbed possession, waiting only for the\par
will to be probated in order to take possession of upward of $200,000 in\par
cash and bonds still in the custody of the old lady's bank. He had full\par
possession of the house, and with entire confidence waited to be put in\par
legal possession of all. But little did he dream that at that moment\par
there was one poor torn sheet of foolscap in the library, casually\par
thrust in a book, lying completely at his mercy to destroy, if he could\par
only have known it, which was going to tear all his wealth from his\par
grasp and drive him forth a foiled plotter, to become an adventurer and\par
ultimately to perish a miserable outcast.\par
\par
The executors of the will (the same in the forged will as in the\par
genuine) were two simple shopkeepers living near. Eagan was the name of\par
the nephew, and to the surprise of the executors his attorney notified\par
them he should contest the will on behalf of his client, and warned them\par
to dispossess Brea of the house until such time as the law decreed it\par
to be his wife's property. The attorney knew the standing of James in\par
his profession, and, being capable of pretty sharp practice himself, he,\par
by some extraordinary intuition, boldly asserted his belief that the\par
will was a forgery. The three sisters declared they would not contest\par
the will, and had Brea acted wisely by fixing it up to give the attorney\par
a liberal fee, and Eagan a paltry thousand dollars, it would have ended\par
there. But, feeling perfectly secure, no doubt he thought an appearance\par
of firmness would strengthen his position still more, and he was so rash\par
as to denounce the attorney as a shyster and blackmailer.\par
\par
The attorney's blood was up; he frightened the sisters into supporting\par
him in disputing the will, and had Brea and his wife ousted from the\par
house and the sisters reinstalled. Brea then attempted negotiations with\par
the attorney. Cautious as he was, he said enough to convince the lawyer\par
that for some reason he did not want the case to come before the courts;\par
still the attorney was half inclined to join hands with Brea. In the\par
mean time Ezra (this was the name of the man of law) had acquired great\par
power over the sisters, and they all looked to him both as champion and\par
protector. He resolved to be protector to one, at least, paying\par
assiduous court to Jane, the youngest. Although past 30 and without\par
education or accomplishments, she was warm-hearted and extremely\par
sentimental, and a thrill went through her tender heart when it became\par
evident that Ezra's attention pointed at her. She quickly made him a\par
hero, and invested the thin-shanked, narrow-chested, waspish attorney\par
with a thousand tender attributes, and when, after one month's\par
acquaintance, she found herself alone with him in the poky little parlor\par
and he asking her to be his wife, her woman's heart overflowed, and\par
telling him she had loved him from the first hour they met she threw\par
herself into his arms, crying she was the happiest and most favored\par
woman in the world. In the midst of the happy lovers' talk she ran to\par
the shelf, took down a book, and, opening it, revealed a soiled sheet\par
of paper and asked her lover what it was. His love had given him a gift,\par
indeed. His trained eye recognized it at once as a draft of a new will,\par
in the handwriting of the deceased mother, and dated the very night of\par
her death. It was a rough draft, but across the bottom was drawn the\par
bold, masculine signature of the old lady. There were no signatures of\par
witnesses, but Ezra was lawyer enough to know it would stand and that it\par
revoked all previous wills. Calling in the two elder sisters he read the\par
will to their amazed ears, and then and there wrote out a full statement\par
as to the circumstance under which it was found. All four attached their\par
signatures to the document, and when Ezra kissed his love a tender good\par
night and went home, he hardly felt the paving stones under his feet,\par
for he had carefully tucked away in the inside pocket of his vest, just\par
over his heart, the little soiled piece of paper which told him in\par
unmistakable terms that his fortune was made, and the wedding ceremony\par
once over, that it was beyond all chance of change.\par
\par
It would seem that the old lady, after her quarrel with her daughters,\par
went to the library in a rage and made the draft of a new will. The\par
chief change in it, as compared with the old genuine will which the\par
conspirators had destroyed, was that it was more favorable to Jane,\par
Ezra's wife to be. But what gave Ezra the greatest satisfaction was the\par
fact that Brea's wife was down by name in the new will for one dollar\par
lawful currency. The will was promptly filed and probated. Ezra gave\par
bonds and was appointed one of the executors, and he had what to him was\par
the immense satisfaction of denouncing Brea to his face as a forger and\par
villain.\par
\par
Before the discovery of the new will, while it was believed that Mrs.\par
Brea was an heiress and her credit good, she and her husband had made\par
use of the fact, and had incurred debts to a large amount. Brea got his\par
wife to indorse his note for $10,000, and he borrowed that sum from the\par
bankers, but as soon as the true state of the case was known, his\par
creditors became clamorous and had him arrested on civil suits. Unable\par
to give bonds, he was locked up in Ludlow Street Jail, and there he\par
remained six months, until, acting upon Ezra's advice, the sisters\par
agreed to pay all his debts and give him and his wife $1,000 each if\par
they would live west of Chicago. This they were forced to accept, and\par
went to Montana. Brea opened a saloon at Butte City, but he never\par
recovered his spirits again. He became his own best customer, and that,\par
of course, meant ruin, but what, after all, killed him was the knowledge\par
that he had been for more than a score of days in full possession of\par
that old house and had spent scores of hours alone in the old library,\par
and yet had not discovered and destroyed the new will lying there at his\par
mercy.\par
\par
The Sheriff soon sold out his saloon, while his wife eloped with his\par
best friend. Ruined in pocket, health and character, poor old Brea was\par
left bare to every storm that blew. One morning, as the sun was rising\par
over the town, surprising half a dozen belated gamblers in Ned Wright's\par
saloon as they were getting up to leave, they found lying across the\par
threshold the body of a man, ragged, emaciated, forlorn. It was Brea.\par
\par
As soon as James had read the will he insisted upon having $5,000 from\par
Brea at once, and he got the money. But when that thunderbolt of the new\par
will fell on the two men, James sadly recognized that fortune and he\par
would shake hands no more, so far as this world is concerned, and he\par
resolved to chance returning to London before the whole of the $5,000 he\par
had from Brea was gone. To London he went; he lived a few years in\par
extreme poverty, driven to all manner of miserable shifts, and at last\par
died. This man died who ought to have been buried in Westminster Abbey,\par
so adding one more brilliant name to the long line of illustrious Lord\par
Chancellors from Thomas a Becket and Cardinal Wolsey down; but he,\par
hating his own soul, took the first step in wrongdoing, and, instead of\par
resting in the mighty Abbey and bequeathing his dust as a precious\par
legacy to succeeding generations, perished forlorn and alone, and was\par
buried in a pauper's grave.\par
\par
[Illustration: GARRAWAY'S.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XII.\par
\par
RESTEZ ICI, MES ENFANTS.\par
\par
\par
We all landed in Liverpool in the highest spirits, and at once took the\par
train for London, enjoying the novelty of everything.\par
\par
It was settled that George should pursue the venture alone in France,\par
while I should go with Mac to Germany to act as his second there. To\par
keep entirely clear myself, but at the same time to watch everything, to\par
exchange the German notes he obtained and to be ready to help if any one\par
should attempt to detain him.\par
\par
Therefore, after completing certain preparations which required skill\par
and considerable business knowledge, we departed to execute this new\par
and, of course, last shuffle for fortune.\par
\par
We had selected Berlin, Munich, Leipsic and Frankfort as the scenes of\par
our operations in Germany. In France we sought to operate in Bordeaux,\par
Marseilles and Lyons. At 8 p.m. Saturday we all crossed to Calais\par
together, where George said good-bye, and, leaving us to take the train\par
eastward to Berlin, he started west to Bordeaux. We were not to meet\par
again until after our hurried rush through the Continent and our return\par
to London with the proceeds. Before I give an account of Mac's adventure\par
and my own for the next three days I will here give George's narrative\par
in his own language, as related to us when we all met again in London:\par
\par
After saying good-bye to you I arrived in Paris in due time, and\par
sauntered about for two hours until the train left for Bordeaux, where I\par
arrived at 8 o'clock Monday morning, and went at once to the Hotel\par
d'Orient, and after a bath and breakfast repaired to the bankers. As\par
soon as I presented my letters of introduction they received me with the\par
greatest consideration, lavishing every attention upon me, inviting me\par
to dinner and to a drive through the city afterward. I thanked them, and\par
explained that I was obliged to decline, as my agent was waiting for me\par
at Bayonne, where I had purchased some real estate, and, having been\par
recommended to their firm, I should feel obliged if they would cash my\par
draft for L2,000 and indorse it on my letter of credit. The manager\par
replied that it was the custom of the French bankers to require\par
twenty-four hours' notice before drawing a check, and asked me if the\par
next day would not answer. "We shall be happy to assist you," said he,\par
"in passing the time pleasantly." This was a new custom to me, but I\par
answered instantly, expressing regret that the nature of my business\par
precluded delay, it being necessary that I should reach Bayonne that\par
night. "I suppose," continued I, "that your bankers will not mind your\par
checking out a small sum without the usual notice. However, if it\par
occasions any embarrassment or inconvenience, I can easily procure the\par
money elsewhere." One of the partners replied that their bank would\par
without doubt honor their check, and the matter should be attended to at\par
once. I sat down for a half hour, conversing on a variety of topics. Of\par
course, this was a most trying period to me; the least show of haste or\par
anxiety might have betrayed me to those lynx-eyed, experienced men of\par
business. In the midst of our conversation an undercurrent of thought\par
kept running through my mind thus: "Who knows but they have sent a\par
dispatch to the Union Bank of London, merely as a matter of business\par
precaution, and that they are delaying me to get a reply? In that case I\par
shall have a good opportunity to learn the pure French accent while\par
passing my days in the Bagnio at Toulon." At last, however, the amount\par
was paid over to me in French bank notes. I deliberately counted them\par
and took leave, lighter in mind and heavier in purse by 50,000 francs.\par
\par
[Illustration: THE RIGHT HONORABLE SIR SIDNEY WATERLOW, Lord Mayor of\par
London in 1873, in official costume.]\par
\par
I had arranged that I would send all the money I obtained to the Queen's\par
Hotel, London, by post at the earliest possible moment after receiving\par
it, that in the event of any accident to myself the money should be\par
safe.\par
\par
After receiving the money I inclosed it in a large envelope, addressing\par
it to the hotel in London. I also wrote on the envelope: "Echantillons\par
de papier" (i. e., samples of paper), after which I threw it into the\par
postoffice.\par
\par
As I wished to reduce the risk as much as possible (the train for\par
Marseilles not leaving for three hours), I took a carriage and told the\par
driver to take me toward the next station on the way to that city. After\par
we were fairly out in the country I got outside and sat with the driver,\par
chatting with him about the country we were driving through, arriving in\par
the village about half an hour before the train from Bordeaux was due. I\par
dismissed my driver at a small village cabaret (tavern), walked to the\par
station, got aboard the train, and early the next morning was in\par
Marseilles. I breakfasted at the Hotel d'Europe, and looked over the\par
papers to see if the Bordeaux fraud had been discovered. As I could see\par
no indication of it, about 10 a.m. I took a carriage and went to call on\par
Messrs. Brune & Co.\par
\par
On making myself known I was, as usual, received with the utmost\par
courtesy, began to talk business, and one of the firm got into my\par
carriage and rode with me to his bank to effect the sale of my draft on\par
London for the sum of L2,500. Arriving at the bank I took a seat in the\par
front office, while Mr. Brune went into the manager's room to introduce\par
the transaction; the clerks eyed me, as I thought, suspiciously, but\par
doubtless only curiously, because they perceived I was a foreigner.\par
Another thing which I noticed sent a shiver through me. After Mr. Brune\par
had been a few minutes in the manager's room, the bank porter stepped to\par
the outer door, closed and locked it. It being but 12 o'clock, I\par
imagined the precautionary measure must be due to my presence. "The\par
Bordeaux affair is discovered and has been telegraphed all over France,"\par
was my first thought; "all is over with me. I am a candidate for a\par
French prison, sure."\par
\par
These and a thousand other thoughts flashed through my mind during the\par
quarter of an hour preceding Mr. Brune's reappearance with his hands\par
full of bank notes. I could hardly believe my eyes. I had suppressed all\par
signs of the internal hurricane which raged during those prolonged\par
moments of suspense.\par
\par
Now the revulsion of feeling was so great that I nearly fainted.\par
However, by a mental effort, I recovered my self-possession and\par
effectually masked all inward convulsions.\par
\par
Mr. Brune placed in my hands 62,000 francs, in notes of the Bank of\par
France, and we then descended to the carriage and drove to my hotel,\par
where we parted. I paid my bill, and at once made preparations to start\par
for Lyons, which was to be the next and last scene of my operations in\par
France.\par
\par
As my train did not leave for three hours, I got into a carriage at some\par
distance from the hotel and was driven toward the next station, located\par
on the beautiful bay a few miles from Marseilles.\par
\par
After driving along the shore of the bay for some miles I remember we\par
met two women, dressed in the quaint costume common to that part of the\par
country, each carrying a basket of eggs. I stopped the carriage and\par
endeavored to enter into conversation with the pair, but could not\par
understand a word of their patois. I then took a couple of eggs, handed\par
out a silver franc piece, and drove on, leaving two astonished women\par
standing in the road, gazing alternately at the piece of money and at\par
the back of my carriage. Arriving at the station I found it would be an\par
hour and a half to train time, and driving to a hotel on the shore I\par
ordered dinner to be served in the upper room of a two-story tower\par
overlooking the bay, with Marseilles in the distance. After dining I\par
strolled along the beach, looking at some queer fish not found north of\par
the Mediterranean, their colors vying in brilliancy with the plumage of\par
tropical birds. Returning to the station I took a ticket for Lyons,\par
stopping off at Arles about sunset, as I wished to see the amphitheatre\par
and other relics of the Roman occupation.\par
\par
I remained in Arles till midnight, then took the train, arriving in\par
Lyons at 9 the next morning. Repairing to the Hotel de Lyons I had\par
breakfast, and on looking over the papers became satisfied that as yet\par
no discovery had been made. Therefore, I resolved to carry out my third\par
and last financial enterprise and then return to London with all speed.\par
\par
I called a carriage and drove at once to the establishment of Messrs.\par
Coudert & Co. I sat near the desk, conversing with the head of the firm,\par
and opened a dispatch I sent from Arles, and, after reading, handed it\par
to him, saying: "I see that I shall have use for 60,000 francs, and must\par
ask you to cash a draft on my letter of credit for that amount." He\par
immediately stepped to the safe, took out a bundle of 1,000 franc notes,\par
and counting out sixty, gave them to me.\par
\par
As it was almost certain that the Bordeaux fraud would soon be\par
discovered, I determined, now that my risky work was completed, to\par
attempt an immediate escape from France by way of Paris and Calais. I\par
did not, therefore, take the train direct from Lyons to Paris, but\par
engaged a carriage and drove back to a junction toward Marseilles. Here\par
I took a train which intersects further to the northward with another\par
road leading through Lyons to Paris. After going the roundabout route\par
above described, I was back at the Lyons station at 9 p.m. in a train\par
bound for Paris, where I arrived without further incident.\par
\par
The next morning (Sunday) as I left the railway station I thought\par
detectives were watching me, but, in all probability, it was only the\par
imagination of a guilty conscience. I was then wearing a full beard, and\par
as a precautionary measure I, that morning, had all shaved off save the\par
mustache. Not daring to leave Paris on the through express, which\par
started at 3 o'clock p.m., nor to purchase a ticket to either Calais or\par
London direct, I went to the station and took the noon accommodation\par
train, which went no further toward Calais than Arras, a town some\par
thirty miles from Paris. I arrived there about 1 p.m.\par
\par
As it would be a couple of hours before the express train was due, I\par
went to a small hotel and ordered dinner. To while away the time I took\par
a stroll through the main street, where were many mothers and nurses\par
with children, nice black-eyed French babies. As I was always a devoted\par
lover of children and other small creatures, I stepped into a shop and\par
bought a package of confectionery, which I distributed among the little\par
ones and their smiling nurses, receiving therefor, almost invariably,\par
the grateful exclamation, "Merci, Monsieur!" I gave some to children 8\par
and 10 years old, a crowd of whom soon gathered about me. Perceiving\par
that I was attracting too much attention, it was clear that I must get\par
rid of my young friends as soon as possible, or the police might also be\par
attracted, and their presence would lead to unpleasant results in case\par
the frauds had been discovered and inquiry was being made for an\par
Englishman. Purchasing a second supply of candies I hastily gave them\par
out, and with a "Restez ici, mes enfants," I passed through them and\par
continued my walk up the street. Quite a number followed at a\par
respectable distance, and I was cogitating how to double on them when I\par
came to the gateway of the town cemetery, through which I hastily\par
entered. The children remained outside and watched me as I walked up the\par
slope and disappeared. At the rear of the cemetery I observed an old\par
man at work in the adjoining field. I climbed upon the stone wall, which\par
instantly crumbled away, and I was landed on the old Frenchman's domain\par
without leave, amidst a pile of stones. Startled by the racket, he\par
looked up from his digging, and, seeing a stranger uprising from the\par
ruins of the fence, began consigning him to "le diable," with a volley\par
of vigorous French expletives delivered in peasant patois. I listened to\par
him, much amused for a moment, and then held up a five-franc piece. As\par
soon as he beheld it a wondrous change came over him. He eagerly seized\par
the silver and straightway showed me to a lane which led almost directly\par
to the railway station. I purchased a ticket for Calais and took the\par
Sunday afternoon express, and here I am.\par
\par
[Illustration: OLD EDINBURGH STREET.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XIII.\par
\par
WE TALK OF THE STARS AND DO THE OTHER THING.\par
\par
\par
After we saw George off to Paris on the train Mac and I walked up and\par
down the platform outside of the station, star-gazing. Mac, with his\par
brilliant scholarship, elegant speech, logical force and fiery\par
enthusiasm, made a most fascinating companion.\par
\par
The study of mankind is man, the old proverb says, but like many other\par
proverbs there is a full measure of unreality in it. It takes a good\par
amount of arrogance and conceit for one to fancy he is going to study\par
and understand men. No man can understand himself, and by no amount of\par
experience or study will he ever come to understand that subtle thing he\par
calls his mind or understand the motives that sway him.\par
\par
I only wish one of those scientists who amuse themselves by pretending\par
to study and understand human minds and motives could have sat in Mac's\par
brain that night, have thought his thoughts and heard his speech, while\par
remaining ignorant of our history and mission. Mac's mind was a\par
storehouse of erudition, his memory a picture gallery, whose chambers\par
were gilded and decorated with many a glowing canvas. As a child he was\par
familiar with the Bible, the Old Testament particularly, and, improbable\par
as it seems, was still a diligent student of Holy Writ. His mind was\par
completely saturated with Bible imagery, yet there we were with our\par
pockets full of forged documents walking up and down that platform\par
star-gazing, while he talked with intelligent enthusiasm of those silver\par
flowers in the darkened sky, of stellar space, how in its infinity it\par
proved the presence of Deity. That with him there was no great and no\par
little. That a thought sweeping across the God-given mind of an infant\par
was as wonderful and as much an evidence of power as the millioned arch\par
of radiant suns in the milky way. While speeding through Belgium on our\par
way to the Rhine, he continued until the sun shone upon the horizon. It\par
was something to stir one's enthusiasm to see his sublime faith in the\par
mighty destiny of man, and to listen to him tell of the dignity and\par
grace of every human soul and his sure faith that all would be garnered\par
in the mighty plains of heaven, and he meant and felt it all; yes, meant\par
all he said, believed all he said, believed that he himself was a potent\par
factor in the Divine economy, and, furthermore, believed it behooved\par
every man to do all things, to be all things good and true, yet on this\par
Sunday morning we were fast speeding to the scene of our contemplated\par
schemes, and with light hearts looked forward to a speedy return to\par
London, fairly well laden with plunder.\par
\par
We talked the whole night through, or rather Mac talked and I listened,\par
and it was a treat to be a listener, he being the speaker.\par
\par
A period was put to his oration by the train stopping at Luxemburg, we\par
being summoned to breakfast.\par
\par
On resuming our journey we took a nap, and when we awoke we found\par
ourselves nearing the Rhine; about noon we arrived at Cologne, and going\par
to Uhlrich platz, drank a bottle of Tokay in a famous wine cellar there,\par
then hurrying back to the station we traveled across the sandy plain\par
that stretches from near the Prussian border to the capital. We arrived\par
soon after dark, and Mac went at once to the Hotel Lion de Paris and\par
registered. I waited across the street in the shadow of the Empress\par
Palace. Mac soon came out, and we went to dine in a large cafe. We\par
enjoyed the novelty of the scene, and were never tired of marveling over\par
the all-predominant militarism. Soldiers everywhere, all with good lungs\par
and loud voices. We spent the evening seeing the town; at midnight we\par
parted to meet and breakfast together at the cafe at 8. I then went to\par
an obscure hotel and soon was in the land of dreams. In the morning I\par
awoke with an anxious feeling, and found myself wishing it were night.\par
At 8, the appointed time, I met Mac. He may possibly have felt some\par
anxiety; if so, it was invisible.\par
\par
When an honest man makes a mistake he has not only sympathy, but can\par
always pick himself up again. With a rogue a mistake may easily be and\par
almost always is fatal. We feared the unseen and the unexpected. Above\par
all, our imagination magnified the danger while tormenting us with\par
needless fears. In Germany the banks open at 9 o'clock, and we knew they\par
would receive soon after 8 the letter we had deposited in the mail in\par
London. We decided that it would be best for Mac to enter the banker's\par
at five minutes after 9. We had discovered the night before the location\par
of the firm. During breakfast Mac went carefully through his pockets,\par
taking out every scrap of paper and turning everything over to me; then\par
taking out from among the others in our bag the letters of credit and\par
introduction we made our last scrutiny of them. We had not settled upon\par
the amount he should ask for, but agreed that it should not in any case\par
be less than 25,000 gulden ($10,000). If everything seemed favorable\par
then Mac was to use his own judgment and demand any sum under 100,000\par
gulden ($40,000). His letter of credit was for L10,000, and we did not\par
want to leave it behind. Of course, if we drew any less sum than the\par
amount the credit called for, the sum we drew would be indorsed on the\par
letter, and it would be returned to Mac and be instantly destroyed. So\par
with the documents in his pockets and giving me a smile, out he went,\par
and I followed after, keeping him in sight, and very anxious I was. We\par
were on Unter den Linden. Walking one square and turning to the left\par
half a block away were the bankers--Hebrew, by the way. I saw Mac\par
saunter up the steps and disappear from view. Outside of America money\par
transactions are carried on with the utmost deliberation; to an American\par
with exasperating slowness; so I thought it possible he might remain\par
invisible for a whole half-hour, and a long half-hour it would be to me.\par
In order to have my anxiety shortened by even a half minute we had\par
arranged that when he came out if he had the money he was to stroke his\par
beard as a signal. If it was all right, but delayed, he was to put his\par
handkerchief to his face, but if everything was wrong he was to clasp\par
his hands across his breast for a moment.\par
\par
[Illustration: "BOYS, THAT IS THE SOFTEST MARK IN THE WORLD."--Page\par
145.]\par
\par
In that event I was to keep a lookout to see if he was followed; if so,\par
I was to give him a signal, when he would go straight to his hotel--in\par
passing through would dispose of his tall hat, and put on the soft hat\par
he had in his pocket--then pass out the back entrance and hasten to a\par
certain hat shop, where I would meet him, and take a cab to a little\par
town six miles away, called Juterbock, where all trains going south,\par
west and east stopped. While driving out, we would settle on some plan;\par
but this emergency did not arise. I had stationed myself in a little\par
shop across the street, and from that vantage ground was watching for\par
Mac's reappearance, and just as I had settled myself for a weary watch\par
out he came, smiling and stroking his beard. A moment's glance satisfied\par
me he was not followed. I hastened after, and, coming up with him as he\par
turned the corner, he merely said 2,600 pounds ($13,000). It seemed too\par
good to be true, and I said: "I don't believe you." He replied: "It is\par
all right, my boy; here it is," at the same time thrusting a big package\par
containing gulden notes into my hand. We instantly separated, I\par
hastening to different but near-by brokers' offices, buying for nearly\par
the full amount French bank notes and gold. We went straight to the\par
hatter's and bought one of those broad-brimmed German student hats,\par
which, when he had placed it on his head, put on a pair of spectacles\par
and parted his flowing beard in the middle, made such a transformation\par
in his appearance that I myself would have passed him unrecognized. In\par
the mean time I had picked out a cabdriver, a stupid-looking,\par
conservative-appearing old fellow, and engaged him to drive "mich und\par
meinen freund nach Juterbock." So we entered the cab, an open one-horse\par
affair, and started for that town. Our next objective point was Munich,\par
but as the train did not leave until noon we preferred to spend the time\par
in a pleasant drive, and at the same time make assurance of our escape\par
doubly sure. Around Berlin the country is flat and uninteresting. Our\par
driver was a crabbed old fellow, but we managed to extract some\par
amusement out of him.\par
\par
What pleased us greatly was to see him from time to time take out from\par
under his seat a loaf of black bread and cut off a slice for himself and\par
one for his horse, and then, seeing we were in no hurry, he would get\par
down, and, walking beside the horse, would feed him and himself at the\par
same time. When we arrived at Juterbock we had an hour to spare, so we\par
drove to an inn, and ordering a bottle of Hochheimer for ourselves and\par
beer and pretzels for our driver, we passed the time pleasantly. In the\par
mean time we had touched a match to the letter of credit, and at train\par
time we went by separate routes to the depot. Each purchased his own\par
ticket; to Nuremberg mine was, his to some near-by city, and at 12.30 we\par
boarded the train and were off for Munich and more profit there on the\par
morrow.\par
\par
Late at night we arrived, and after locating the bank we went to a\par
theatre, where a variety show was going on, and found the performances\par
good; quite up, in fact, to similar exhibitions here. When the house\par
closed we separated for the night, each going to a different hotel. Our\par
plan was to secure all the cash we could in Munich in time to take a\par
train that left for Leipsic a little before 10 o'clock, arriving there\par
soon after 1, in time to visit the Leipsic bank the same day; then\par
leaving the city that night we would be in Frankfort early on Wednesday.\par
We would then make all haste to escape from Germany to the shelter of\par
mighty London.\par
\par
Tuesday morning at 7 we met at a restaurant, as agreed, and soon had\par
over again our Berlin experience; but the amount we obtained here was\par
only 12,000 gulden (L1,000), Mac thinking it best to ask for a small\par
sum, Munich not being much of a commercial city. In cashing his credit,\par
although the amount was in gulden, the bank paid him in New Saxon\par
thalers, the thaler being 70 cents. We did not like the new thaler\par
notes, and wanted to change them there, but there was no time if we were\par
to catch the 10 o'clock train. I had Mac's derby hat in a box, and in\par
three minutes he had the hat and spectacles on, and, with his beard\par
again parted, the transformation was complete, and he, a perfect picture\par
of the dreamy German student, sauntered down to the depot and bought his\par
ticket for Leipsic. I followed him, carrying all the cash and documents\par
in my bag. We arrived at Leipsic soon after dinner. Times were brisk,\par
with plenty of bustle there, for the great Leipsic fair was in full\par
blast. Here was an opportunity missed; we ought to have had three or\par
four letters to as many banks. The place was thronged and the banks were\par
paying out and receiving money in thousands. On the train I had sat\par
apart from Mac, but in the same compartment, which was filled. Arriving\par
at Leipsic he left the train, and, walking up the street, entered a wine\par
room, where I joined him. He scrutinized his letters carefully, and,\par
placing them in his pocket, in five minutes was in the bank. Seeing the\par
bank was full of customers, instead of remaining outside to watch, I\par
entered and stood among the crowd, anxious, of course, but letting\par
nothing escape.\par
\par
Instead of waiting or trying to transact his business with a\par
subordinate, Mac demanded to see the head of the firm. He was received\par
at once, and upon the production of his letters was treated with the\par
utmost consideration. He asked for 50,000 gulden ($20,000), which was\par
given him at once. The amount for fair time at Leipsic was not large. In\par
a very short time the business was done. The money being paid in gulden\par
notes, it made a pretty big bundle. As agreed upon, he went direct to\par
the cafe, carrying the money, while I stopped at a broker's office and\par
bought French money, notes and gold, for my new Saxon thalers. There the\par
transformation scene was re-enacted, but we could not leave town until 5\par
o'clock. We spent the time visiting the famous fair. Leipsic overflowed\par
with the fair. It was fair on the brain with every one. This annual fair\par
has been a yearly feature of the old city for four centuries, and draws\par
to it people from all over the European world, even from furthest\par
Russia. Soon after 5 o'clock we were on the train, but, for some reason\par
which I now forget, we did not arrive until 10 o'clock the next day at\par
Frankfort.\par
\par
Frankfort, the home and still the fortress of the Rothschilds.\par
\par
In Frankfort the Bourse opens at 10 a.m., and closes at 2. During those\par
hours the bankers are to be found on the Exchange only, and not at their\par
offices. Many of the offices are then deserted and fast locked. It\par
proved to be the case with the firm to which our letters were addressed,\par
and if we were to do any business in Frankfort we had of necessity to\par
wait until 2 p.m., but as it was now Wednesday and the third day since\par
our affair in Berlin, the first draft drawn on London, if promptly\par
mailed, would probably have been delivered at the Union Bank this\par
morning. Of course, as soon as the manager of the foreign department\par
found a draft for a large sum drawn by a stranger and made payable to\par
their correspondent in Berlin, he would at once surmise that a fraud had\par
been committed and undoubtedly would send a telegram to Germany to that\par
effect. The forgery once known in Berlin, the rumor of it, with a\par
thousand exaggerations, might easily fly to every Bourse in Europe, and\par
I feared that by 2 o'clock the story might possibly become known on the\par
Frankfort Exchange. So far we had $43,000, the result of our two days'\par
operations, but we had from the first great hopes of Frankfort, chiefly\par
because it was the money centre of the Continent, therefore the bankers\par
were used to handling large sums of money, and so long as everything was\par
all right they would hand out any sum, however large. We really ought to\par
have taken in Frankfort first. Had we done so, we probably would have\par
left the town with $50,000.\par
\par
Soon as we arrived we went to a cafe, and, leaving Mac there and all the\par
money and papers in the bag, I hastened to the bankers, hoping to find\par
them open and ready for business. In that case I should have talked\par
business--that is, about having letters of credit, etc.--and I could\par
probably have told by their actions if any rumors of our transaction of\par
the two preceding days had reached the city. Had this been so the\par
bankers would have betrayed it by their looks and questions, and would\par
have been anxious to see my credits. Had such questions been asked, I\par
would have simply said that my letters of credit had not yet arrived\par
from Paris. This would have, of course, thrown them off the track, and\par
given us time to move off.\par
\par
But when I arrived I found the doors locked. I at once returned to Mac\par
and said: "We are through; let us catch the train for Cologne at once."\par
He was anxious to wait until 4 o'clock and make a dash. We both knew the\par
Germans were slow, and might not think of using the telegraph, and we\par
agreed that we had more than an even chance of success; but Mac said:\par
"My boy, you are my manager, and I leave it for you to decide." Then I\par
said we were through, and that he should take no more chances; so we\par
settled it right there, in the little French-German cafe, and taking\par
out all the letters and every scrap of paper we destroyed them.\par
\par
This decision, of course, brought a great relief--for the strain had\par
been greater than either of us had been willing to confess to the other.\par
So, easy in mind, we ordered lunch. Of course, we would have no news of\par
George until we met in London. We had no anxiety about him; we felt\par
certain he would come out all right. While waiting for the train we\par
discussed the future, and took it for granted that he would secure as\par
much as we had done. We counted ourselves possessors of $90,000. Of\par
this, fully $10,000 would go to our three honest detectives in New York;\par
we would spend about another $10,000, leaving us about $23,000 each.\par
Making this calculation, we sat down, and with the cash safe in our\par
hands we began planning for the future. Did we say: "Now we have a sum\par
of money ample to start us in an honest business, and, as we have\par
promised, we will quit?" Nothing of the kind; we simply ignored our many\par
promises and resolutions. Our ideas had grown with our success, and we\par
felt poor; so we quickly came to the conclusion that it was the part of\par
wisdom, since we were already so far in, to secure $100,000 each, and\par
then to call a halt; so there in Frankfort, in the very hour of our\par
success, we found ourselves planning new schemes, and further down the\par
Primrose Way.\par
\par
Soon after the noon hour the train started, but first I took Mac's tall\par
hat to the hatter's and left it to be ironed, this, of course, to get\par
rid of it, and leave no trace behind; then, returning to the cafe, we\par
started. I fell behind and we made our way separately to the depot. Mac\par
had absolutely nothing about him save $2,000 in French paper and gold. I\par
had over $40,000 in notes and some gold in my bag. He bought a ticket\par
for Amsterdam, and I one for Belgium, both taking us through Cologne. I\par
saw him safe into a car, while I sauntered carelessly up and down the\par
station, swinging my bag and staring at everything; as the train was\par
about to start I entered another carriage. The railway from Frankfort\par
to Cologne follows the river bank for the entire distance. We quickly\par
passed Bingen, Mayence, Coblenz, and about dusk reached Cologne. This is\par
an important junction, and here we had to change cars, having twenty\par
minutes to wait. Both of us went direct to the cathedral. It is close to\par
the station, and there we had a few minutes' talk. Here Mac threw away\par
his ticket to Amsterdam and I gave him mine to Brussels. We agreed to\par
take separate cars at the station, but at the first stopping place I was\par
to join him in his compartment, for we had before us an all-night ride\par
to Ostend (the rival port to Calais), where we would embark for Dover.\par
At the depot I purchased a ticket to London via Ostend. We left Cologne\par
all right, and at the first station out I alighted and joined him.\par
\par
We had a pleasant all-night journey, arriving very early the next\par
morning at Ostend. How lovely the sea looked, with the morning sun\par
shining on its restless waves!\par
\par
We got to Dover without accident, and two hours after the express landed\par
us in London, and we drove at once to our appointed rendezvous, the\par
Terminus Hotel, London Bridge. We had no news of George, but that\par
evening, opening the door in response to a loud knock, he walked in,\par
receiving a boisterous welcome.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XIV.\par
\par
I PLAY THE SILVER KING.\par
\par
\par
The next morning we all drove to Hampton Court, the creation of Wolsey,\par
and when tired we went to the Star and Garter. There we talked over\par
matters, and came to the conclusion we must have a hundred thousand\par
apiece before we could afford to settle down at home.\par
\par
We resolved to send off the "percentage" to Irving & Company, and to pay\par
all debts we were owing at home.\par
\par
Mac's heart went out to his father. He longed for a reconciliation, and\par
he determined to send him $10,000 and so make good the money his father\par
had given him to establish himself in New York, at the same time write\par
the old gentleman he had made a big strike in a cotton speculation, in\par
order to explain his having so large a sum to spare.\par
\par
Our accounts were pretty well mixed up, and I hit upon a novel way to\par
settle them and give each of us an equal start. My proposal was that we\par
should pool everything. To put every dollar we had in the world on the\par
table then and there, and let the firm assume all obligations, purely\par
personal as they were, save only the Irving "percentage," and pay them\par
from the general fund, then divide the balance. This was agreed to, and\par
the queerest balance sheet ever made out was soon ready.\par
\par
[Illustration: "THREE OR FOUR SHOTS RANG OUT, OUR TRAIN WAS OFF THE\par
TRACK."--Page 281.]\par
\par
We all had planned certain gifts and presents to friends in America, a\par
considerable sum in the aggregate; all the cost of this was assumed by\par
the firm. The main item was $10,000 to the New York police. When the\par
balances were finally struck nearly $30,000 had disappeared from our\par
cash capital, but on the whole it was a good plan. It drew us all closer\par
together, consequently increased our faith in each other and at the same\par
time prevented all chances of future dispute. This matter settled, we\par
determined to have a little recreation by taking a tour in Italy. After\par
studying guide books and routes we resolved to take a steamer from\par
Southampton to Naples, spend a few days there in seeing the town and\par
visiting Pompeii, etc., then north to Rome.\par
\par
We had made considerable preparation for our tour, when a circumstance\par
arose that not only changed our plans, but in the sequel changed our\par
lives as well.\par
\par
We had been paying another visit to Hampton Court, and in place of\par
dining at the Star and Garter we returned by boat on the Thames and\par
dined at Cannon Street Hotel. Before going to the hotel we took a stroll\par
down Lombard street, and, arriving at the intersection of streets\par
opposite the Bank of England, we came to a halt. While watching the\par
human whirlpool in that centre of throbbing life, I turned to my\par
friends, and, pointing to the Bank of England, said: "Boys, you may\par
depend upon it, there is the softest spot in the world, and we could hit\par
the bank for a million as easy as rolling off a log." No response was\par
made at the time, and the casual remark was apparently forgotten. Well\par
for us if it had been.\par
\par
The next day we went for a drive to Windsor, and were to dine at a\par
famous old roadside inn. On arriving we, of course, visited the castle,\par
and, while viewing the decorations in the stately throne room, Mac\par
stopped us with the remark that something I had said the day before had\par
been sticking in his mind. He went on to say that we wanted a hundred\par
thousand apiece in order to return home in good shape; that the Bank of\par
England had plenty to spare, and it was well for the lightning to strike\par
where the balances were heavy. The bank would never miss the money, and\par
he firmly believed the whole directorate of the fossil institution was\par
permeated with the dry rot of centuries. The managers were convinced\par
that their banking system was impregnable, and, as a consequence, it\par
would fall an easy victim, provided, as we suspected, the bank was\par
really managed by hereditary officials.\par
\par
Here was a picture, indeed. Three American adventurers, two of them\par
barely past their majority, standing in the throne room of Windsor\par
Castle, and plotting to strike a blow at the money bags of the Bank of\par
England!\par
\par
The idea grew on us rapidly. After dinner we sat in the twilight of that\par
old inn and discussed the Old Lady of Threadneedle Street from a point\par
of view from which she had probably never been discussed before. I can\par
imagine with what scorn the idiotically puffed and bepuffed magnates of\par
the bank would have regarded us had they known of our discussion.\par
\par
They afterwards boasted to me, as they had boasted for a century, that\par
their system was perfect, and as a proof that it was so they widely\par
proclaimed they had not changed it in a hundred years. They had\par
proclaimed so loudly and so long its absolute invulnerability that they\par
not only believed it themselves, but all the world had come to believe\par
it as well. "Safe as the bank" was a proverb everywhere underlying the\par
English tongue.\par
\par
In our discussion we speedily came to the conclusion that any system of\par
finance unchanged in detail for a century, belief in the perfection of\par
which was an article of faith not alone with the officials charged with\par
its management, but with the people of England at large, must, in the\par
very nature of the case, lie wide open to the attack of any man bold\par
enough to doubt its impregnability and resolute to attack.\par
\par
What a figment of the imagination this boasted impregnability of the\par
Bank of England was the sequel will show. And as for those masters of\par
finance, those earthly Joves of the financial world who sat serene above\par
the clouds, "the Governor and Company of the Bank of England," they\par
soon had the whole money world shaking with laughter when they stood\par
revealed the Simple Simons they proved to be.\par
\par
We wanted a hundred thousand apiece now, and had resolved to get it from\par
the Bank of England. Such was our confidence that we never thought\par
failure possible. Truly, if there ever was a plan laid in ignorant\par
enthusiasm this was one. Here we were, absolutely without any knowledge\par
of the inner workings of the institution, strangers in London, being\par
under assumed names, without business of any kind, and not only unable\par
to give any references, but unable to stand any investigation.\par
\par
Exactly how we were to manipulate the bank we did not know. We were\par
inclined, now we had some fifty thousand dollars capital, to avoid so\par
serious a thing as forgery, but had an idea for one of us to obtain in\par
some way an introduction to the bank and to use all the money of the\par
party to establish a credit. In the mean time all were to get in the\par
swim in or around the exchange, and use the one who had the account in\par
the bank for reference for the others. If some good chance offered to go\par
into a straightforward business we could drop forever all thoughts of\par
breaking the law again. This was the theory; in practice, we were almost\par
certain to try on the game we had of late played so successfully.\par
\par
In conference it was determined an account should be opened with the\par
bank, anyway; after that was done we could decide what use to put it to.\par
\par
As I had not yet shown up in the previous transactions, I volunteered to\par
go to the front in this; so, telling my two friends to go to the\par
Continent--Italy, if they liked--I would remain in London and manage to\par
get the account started. They took me at my word, and a day or two after\par
sailed from Liverpool to Lisbon, and passed through Portugal to Spain,\par
visiting the chief cities of that country.\par
\par
I was left alone in London and began prospecting at once, setting all\par
my wits at work to see how I could manage to get an introduction to the\par
bank. I had only $20,000 to start the thing with, as we did not think it\par
policy to risk our entire capital in one place. My first idea was to\par
find some solicitor of standing who kept his account at the Bank of\par
England, to give him a retaining fee of L100 to act as my legal adviser,\par
telling him some fairy tales about establishing a branch firm in London,\par
and engage him, as soon as we started, to devote all his time to our\par
business at a fat salary. But there were many objections to having a\par
lawyer to introduce me, they being wide awake and liable to scrutinize\par
too closely. If one should depart so far from his policy of caution as\par
to introduce a new client he might after the introduction easily notify\par
the bank that I was a stranger to him and perhaps advise them to\par
investigate, and investigation was the one thing I must avoid. Of\par
course, one is supposed to give reference, even if introduced. Although\par
I had no acquaintance with this bank's methods, yet I was confident that\par
all those at the top must be a stupid lot of red-tape sticklers, and I\par
resolved to do my business with them alone. I was pretty sure that the\par
routine of an introduction once well over, so as to give me access to\par
the officials, they could be easily satisfied and made to help on the\par
fraud, in place of being obstacles. The result proved my surmise\par
correct, for such a lot of self-sufficient barnacles no institution in\par
the world was ever burdened with.\par
\par
The dry rot of officialism permeated the bank through and through; even\par
the bank solicitors, the Messrs. Freshfields, were merely "highly\par
respectable," and sometimes when that term is applied in England it\par
indicates mediocrity. The Freshfields managed to spend four hundred and\par
fifty thousand dollars of the bank's money in our prosecution. That fact\par
alone would have ruined the reputation of any law firm in America, but\par
the ring of toadies who control that close corporation called the\par
Benchers of the Inn was loud in its praise of this firm for the extreme\par
ability shown in working up the case for the bank.\par
\par
I finally made up my mind to find some old established shopkeeper who\par
kept an account at the bank, and secure an introduction through him.\par
\par
I determined to carry out the plan at once. The thing was first of all\par
to find my man; so at 2 o'clock that afternoon I stationed myself near\par
the bank to watch depositors coming out and then follow them. Four out\par
of five depositors when they take money to the bank come out examining\par
their passbooks. That afternoon I followed several; of these I selected\par
three; one was an optician and electrician, an old-established firm,\par
doing a large business. Another was an East India importing house. The\par
third was Green & Son, tailors.\par
\par
The next day I went to the optician and purchased an expensive opera\par
glass, and had him engrave on it "To Lady Mary, from Her Friend," and\par
paid him for it with a L100 note; then I went to the East India firm and\par
bought a costly white silk shawl and a lap robe fit for a prince, and\par
looked at a camel's hair shawl at one hundred guineas.\par
\par
I had brought from America with me a Western hat, and as I had resolved\par
to play the Silver King, I wore it when going around among the\par
tradesmen. The English had, and still have, absurd ideas concerning that\par
desirable article, "The American Silver King." The stage article they\par
take for the genuine, and devoutly believe that the pavements are thick\par
with them in America, all marching around with rolls of thousand-dollar\par
bills in their pockets, which they throw out to bootblacks and\par
bartenders.\par
\par
Therefore, I resolved to play this role. After my purchase of the shawl\par
and robe, I drove in my brougham up to Green & Son, and entered, smoking\par
a cigar, and with my big hat pulled well down over my eyes. Soon as I\par
saw the elder Green I felt I had my man. Certainly I had hit well, for\par
the firm (fathers and sons) had been depositors in the Bank of England\par
for near a century, and had considerable wealth; but, English fashion,\par
stuck steadily to business. This is a firm of ultra-fashionable tailors,\par
that, like the historic Poole next door, charge for their reputation\par
more than for the fit of their garments.\par
\par
One of the firm and an attendant flew to wait upon me, but, paying no\par
attention to them, I started on a slow march around the establishment,\par
examining the array of cloths, they following at my heels. I went down\par
one side and returned on the other to the door. Arriving there I halted\par
and, pointing first at one roll of cloth and then another, said: "One\par
suit from this, three suits from that, two from that, a topcoat from\par
that, another from that, another suit from that, one from that. Now,\par
show me some dressing gowns." The first shown was twenty guineas. I\par
instantly said that would do. One may be certain the tailor and his\par
assistant flew around, one to measure and the other to write the\par
measurements of this American sheep that Providence had led astray into\par
their shop. When asked my name and address, I gave F. A. Warren, Golden\par
Cross Hotel, and then, for fear I might forget my name, I made a\par
memorandum of it and placed it in my vest pocket. They bowed me out,\par
evidently greatly impressed with my taciturnity, and especially my big\par
hat, confident also that they had hooked a fortune in a genuine American\par
silver king. I entered the brougham and drove directly to the Golden\par
Cross Hotel, Charing Cross, and there registering "F. A. Warren" and\par
securing a room I left for my hotel. This room at the Golden Cross I\par
kept for a whole year, but never slept there. It was the only address\par
the Bank of England ever had of their distinguished customer, Mr.\par
Frederic Albert Warren.\par
\par
I did not trouble any more about the other two store people, but looked\par
about the town, amusing myself. In due time I called and tried the\par
garments on, and, when ready to deliver, I left the cash with the hotel\par
people with orders to pay the bill, which was done. There the matter\par
rested for ten days, when I drove up again, and, remaining in my\par
carriage, the head of the firm came out to me and I remarked: "I must\par
have more garments; duplicate that order," and drove off.\par
\par
A week after I called to have them tried on, and then said that as I was\par
going to Ireland for a few days' shooting with Lord Clancarty, I would\par
send down a portmanteau for the garments and call for it on my way from\par
the hotel to the station. So I bought the most expensive trunk I could\par
find and sent it to the tailor. When the day came for me to call I\par
provided myself with six L500 bank notes, five L100 and about fifty L5\par
notes to go on the bottom of the roll. Before leaving my hotel I had a\par
large trunk put on the cab, and then taking inside of it all the\par
dressing bags, rugs, silk umbrellas and canes in the whole party, I\par
drove to the tailor's, paid my bill with a L500 note and had the\par
portmanteau put on the cab. I turned to go, but, halting at the door, I\par
remarked quite in a casual manner: "By the way, Mr. Green, I have more\par
money than I care to carry loose in my vest pocket to Ireland; I think I\par
will leave it with you." He replied, "Certainly, sir," and as I was\par
pulling the roll out of my vest pocket he said: "How much is it, sir?"\par
"Only L4,000; it may be L5,000;" to which he replied: "Oh, sir, I would\par
be afraid to take charge of so much; let me introduce you to my bank."\par
He ran for his hat, accompanied me to the Bank of England, and, calling\par
one of the sub-managers, introduced me as an American gentleman, Mr. F.\par
A. Warren, who desired to open an account. A check and a pass book were\par
brought and the signature book laid before me for my autograph, and I\par
was requested to sign my name in full, so I christened myself Frederic\par
Albert. I drove to the North Eastern station and telegraphed the boys at\par
Barcelona that the thing was done and they could, if they liked, curtail\par
their excursion and return to England at once.\par
\par
So the first step had been taken and successfully. We talked of now\par
giving up all further idea of breaking the law, and starting in London\par
as brokers and promoters of stock companies. The plan was for me to take\par
the money of the firm, L10,000, place it all in the Bank of England, and\par
begin to buy and sell stock and keep my money moving in and out of the\par
bank. Then George and Mac were to start an office and launch out as\par
promoters and refer to Mr. Warren of the Bank of England. This would\par
place them on a footing at once, and I would gradually drop out of the\par
Bank of England after introducing George and Mac in their right names.\par
This was a grand plan, and had we only carried it out fortune would have\par
been ours, and honor as well, but we were too impatient of any delay in\par
securing wealth and overconfident of our success and cleverness. Above\par
all, we were anxious to get home again. But I have got somewhat ahead of\par
my story.\par
\par
Soon after I had a telegram from George and Mac saying that they would\par
arrive in time for a late dinner, and for me to wait and dine with them.\par
At the time I was living at the Grosvenor Hotel, Victoria Station. We\par
had a pleasant meeting and a good dinner to celebrate it. I exhibited my\par
check book, and they were eager to know all details of my interviews,\par
not only at the bank, but with the tailor, and over the wine I related\par
with great spirit the details of the little comedy. I have to this very\par
day a vivid recollection of the shouts of laughter that arose from my\par
companions during the recital. We laughed then, but we did not laugh for\par
the next twenty years, neither did we partake of any sumptuous banquets.\par
In the world of crime success is failure, and perhaps never had the\par
absolute accuracy of that statement been so fully confirmed as in our\par
own lives.\par
\par
That merriment of ours ended in anguish too deep for words. For twenty\par
years I never looked upon a star, nor saw the face of a woman or of a\par
child; that is to say, from my early years when the heart beats fast\par
and the blood runs warmly in the veins. That fearful gap of time was\par
filled to the brim with the peltings of a pitiless storm, hungry,\par
driven, toiling like a galley slave under the Summer's burning sun, or\par
thinly clad exposed to every blizzard and all the whirling storms of\par
Winter, until my early manhood had vanished and the best years of my\par
prime were all melted away, and at last I came forth from my dungeon,\par
but with the mark of suffering and desolation burned deep upon me, to\par
face a world of which I could not but be ignorant.\par
\par
[Illustration: THE "SUGAR-LOAF" IN THE BAY OF RIO.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XV.\par
\par
PIRATICAL CRUISE IN TROPICAL SEAS.\par
\par
\par
The way to the bank vaults with their treasures had been laid open, but\par
there remained many matters of detail to be carried out before we could\par
enter them. There promised to be a delay of several months, but we were\par
impatient over the prospect of delay of even six months in securing the\par
fortunes we wanted, and which we had come to consider essential to our\par
happiness.\par
\par
Our plan to ease the bank of a million or two of her forty million\par
sterling was, roughly stated, to borrow from day to day large sums upon\par
forged securities, the bad feature of the plan, from our point of view,\par
being the fact that the bank, as a matter of course, would retain these\par
documents, which could be produced at any future time to found a\par
criminal charge against us, provided justice ever had the opportunity to\par
weigh us in her balances.\par
\par
Protected as we were by the police in New York, we felt that the chance\par
of our identity ever becoming known was remote. Still, there was an\par
element of chance we wanted to eliminate entirely. In our recent raid on\par
the bankers of France and Germany we never exhausted our letter of\par
credit, but had the amount of cash we drew indorsed upon it, and brought\par
the actual forged document away and instantly destroyed it. Had we been\par
arrested in Europe, no doubt, under the laws prevailing there, they\par
would have made us suffer upon the verbal statement of the banker; but\par
in America to convict one of forgery the document itself must be\par
produced in court.\par
\par
I paid several visits to the bank, depositing and drawing out various\par
sums of money. I had talks with the sub-manager, and, on various\par
pretexts to get information, I interviewed bankers and money men in the\par
city. Finally, after many conferences, we came to the conclusion that\par
the boasted impregnability of the bank was imaginary, and that the\par
vanity and self-sufficiency of the officials would some day prove a\par
snare to the institution they ruled over.\par
\par
The next conclusion we arrived at was that, easy as it might be to\par
defraud the bank, yet there was an infinity of detail which would\par
require six months of preparations to carry out. Then, again, the word\par
forgery began to look black in our vocabulary. We knew John Bull was an\par
obstinate fellow when he once got his back up, and we began to think it\par
wise to keep beyond his dull weather eye.\par
\par
Finally, as the result of many debates, we resolved to abandon the Bank\par
of England matter temporarily, possibly forever, because it was too\par
dangerous, and the delay would be too great. Our new plan was to go to\par
South America on a buccaneering expedition. There being no cable in\par
1872, and it took, as we ascertained, forty days to send a letter from\par
Rio de Janeiro to Europe and get a reply; so that, if we executed an\par
operation boldly and well, we might hope for anything. We resolved to go\par
to South America, but to leave my account stand in the bank, and if our\par
success was as great as expected, we would let the Bank of England keep\par
the million or two we wanted, and continue her century-long slumber\par
until the time came when some adventurous but unscrupulous mind should\par
accept the temptation she held out to seize some of her bags of\par
sovereigns.\par
\par
Our plan was, in the main, similar to the one we had lately used with so\par
much success in Germany and France. Only in this case we proposed to use\par
the credit of the London and Westminster Bank, and, therefore, obtained\par
the documents required to carry through such an operation successfully.\par
\par
The steamer Lusitania of the Pacific Steam Navigation Company was\par
advertised to sail on the 12th, and we determined to go by her. Our plan\par
was to go on the same steamer, to be ever within supporting distance of\par
each other, and yet pretend to be strangers, or if associating together,\par
to act so as to make all observers think our acquaintance merely casual.\par
\par
Mac had his tickets in the name of Gregory Morrison. He carried letters\par
of introduction to Maua & Co., who had branches in all the coast cities\par
down the coast, including Montevideo and Buenos Ayres on the east coast,\par
and Lima, Valparaiso and Callao on the west.\par
\par
The steamers of the Pacific Steam Navigation Company, leaving Liverpool,\par
touch at Bordeaux, Santander and Lisbon, then are off 6,000 miles away\par
to Rio, never slowing the engines for a moment during the voyage. Two\par
days at Rio to discharge cargo and take in coal, then off again to\par
Montevideo, discharge cargo, and coal again, then away round the Horn,\par
and thousands of miles up the west coast, touching everywhere to land\par
mails and passengers; finally after 14,000 miles of sea travel they\par
reach Callao, then take the home track for Liverpool.\par
\par
Modern buccaneers, indeed, were we, engaged in a nineteenth century\par
piratical descent upon the shores of South America. Instead of the\par
burly, much-beweaponed pirate of other years, we were mild-mannered,\par
soft-spoken, courteous youngsters, yet our steel pen and bottle of ink\par
were more deadly instruments or at least of surer fire and of better\par
aim, than the long toms and horse pistols of the piratical braves of the\par
seventeenth century. Our hopes of gain were high, and we counted on an\par
ample return for the trouble of our adventure. I say trouble, for danger\par
we feared none, so confident were we of our ability to carry off\par
everything with a high hand, and so complete was our faith in each\par
other that we had no anxiety as to the result, but simply regarded our\par
trip as a pleasant voyage into tropic seas--a happy change from the\par
March wind and sombre skies of England to the bright skies and balmy air\par
of the tropical world in the Winter months.\par
\par
I had a balance in the bank of L2,335, and we, as a matter of policy,\par
wanted to have our capital ready at hand. The bank has a rule that a\par
depositor must never have less than L300 to his credit. My friends were\par
somewhat skeptical as to whether the bank did not regard their new\par
customer, F. A. Warren, with some suspicion and as a depositor to be\par
watched. My personal relations with the bank people convinced me\par
everything was all right, but to convince my friends I determined to\par
give them a proof that the bank would break their rule on my account.\par
\par
The Monday before we sailed for Brazil I called at the bank and told the\par
sub-manager that I was going to St. Petersburg and on to Southern Russia\par
for a time to inspect some work I was doing there, and I purposed to\par
withdraw my account. He begged me not to do so, said many flattering\par
things to me, and urged that it would be convenient to have an open\par
account in London.\par
\par
"Well," I said, looking at my passbook, "I see I have L2,335 to my\par
credit. I will leave the odd L35 with you." He instantly acquiesced. Had\par
he said: "No, you must leave at least L300, as our rules require," I\par
should have said "All right," and made it five hundred. I drew out the\par
L2,300 at once, intending to deposit L300 before leaving London, but in\par
the haste of our preparations I neglected it, and my balance at the bank\par
stood L35 for all the weeks I was on our piratical cruise to the Spanish\par
Main.\par
\par
Storing most of our baggage in London, we took the train to Liverpool,\par
and, purchasing tickets for Rio, we went on board the good ship\par
Lusitania, but not the "good" ship, for her first trip, this being her\par
second, had won for her the name of being unlucky, and Liverpool\par
insurance men, no less than Liverpool sailors, do not bank on an unlucky\par
ship--their faith of ill luck following an unlucky ship has been\par
justified in thousands of instances, as it was in the case of the\par
Lusitania. But I am not going to relate the after history of the ship.\par
\par
From the hour of our arrival in Liverpool we were outwardly strangers,\par
and during the voyage no one ever suspected that we were anything else.\par
We soon discovered we had a pleasant company of fellow voyagers, and as\par
we steamed out of the Mersey and headed southward we settled down to\par
have a good time. Boreas was friendly, and away we sped across the Bay\par
of Biscay, rapidly neared the mouth of the Garonne, on an estuary of\par
which is situated the old city of Bordeaux. Arriving there, the ship lay\par
at anchor for some hours, taking in and discharging freight, and\par
receiving emigrants for various parts of South America. When the steamer\par
was about to leave, it was a strange and rather comical sight to witness\par
the farewells and leave-takings from the crowds of friends who had come\par
to see them off. The customary performance appeared to me so peculiar\par
that I will describe it as well as I can after so many years: Two men\par
standing face to face, one clasps the other round the body, the other\par
passive, then leaning back lifts the party clear off the ground once,\par
twice or thrice, probably according to the degree of relationship or\par
amount of affection; then the operation is reversed, the embraced\par
becoming the embracer. In some cases the ceremonial is repeated the\par
second or third time, neither kissing nor crying being the fashion\par
there.\par
\par
The next morning we were off the coast of Spain, watching the silvery\par
gleam from the ice-clad peaks of the Pyrenees--at least those of us who\par
were not engaged in the more disagreeable employment of discharging\par
their debt to Father Neptune. However, by the time the ship arrived at\par
the small port of Santander the passengers were mostly recovering from\par
the mal de mer occasioned by the rough water in the Bay of Biscay. While\par
leaving this tiny landlocked harbor, one of the propeller blades touched\par
the rocky bottom, and broke short off, but our ship continued her voyage\par
with undiminished speed, and within three days was steaming up the Tagus\par
to Lisbon. Here the passengers who wished to avail themselves of the\par
opportunity had a few hours on shore; then we were off for the long\par
diagonal run across the Atlantic.\par
\par
"The Lady of the Lusitania," as she was called, because there was no\par
other lady among the saloon passengers, was the wife of a captain in the\par
British army, who was going out for a few months' hunting on the pampas\par
of Buenos Ayres, and, of course, accompanied by many dogs, with an\par
assortment of guns. There was also a chaplain in the British navy who\par
was going out to join his ship at Valparaiso. A strange character was\par
he; a big, burly man, about 28 years of age, the most inveterate\par
champagne drinker on board, and that is saying a good deal. Whenever he\par
met any of the "jolly" ones of the saloon passengers it was "Come, old\par
fellow, will you toss me for a bottle of fizz?" as he called his\par
favorite wine, and he had no lack of accepters. The majority in the\par
saloon consisted of a party of fifteen young Englishmen, civil\par
engineers, who were going under the leadership of a Swedish colonel to\par
survey, for the Brazilian Government, a railway line across the southern\par
part of Brazil, from the Atlantic to the Pacific. In all there were\par
twenty-five young men, full of frolic and fun, who made things rather\par
lively about the ship. They went in for everything from which any fun\par
could be extracted. At the equinoctial line they roped in the\par
"greenhorns" to look through the field glasses at the line, and having\par
fastened a hair across the field of view, of course, we could all see it\par
plainly. Father Neptune came on board and those of the crew who had\par
never crossed the Equator were hunted out of their hiding places,\par
dragged on deck, lathered with a whitewash brush dipped in old grease,\par
shaved with a lath-razor, and then tumbled unceremoniously backward into\par
a cask of water.\par
\par
After a prosperous voyage of three weeks we arrived within sight of the\par
famous "Sugar Loaf," and were duly disembarked at the Custom House, our\par
baggage passed, and were off to our hotels, each going to a different\par
one, and each registering the name our letters of credit and\par
introduction bore. While in Rio we went by day in the parks or cafes,\par
and spent our evenings together, having a most enjoyable time.\par
\par
This was our first experience of the tropics, and life under the Equator\par
proved as novel and as fascinating as it ever does to the inhabitant of\par
a cold climate. The show of tropical fruits in the markets was\par
magnificent, and, although strangers are warned not to partake of it,\par
yet our health was so good and our digestion so perfect that we\par
disregarded all warnings and gratified our palates without stint, with\par
no bad results following.\par
\par
However, we felt after all that we were there on business; we wanted\par
plunder, in fact, and not pleasure, in Rio. Our pleasure lay in Europe\par
or America, there in the good time just ahead, when, as moneyed men, we\par
returned, and, surrounded by those nearest and dearest, we would enjoy\par
life to the full.\par
\par
Mac was the grand swell of our party, and, wanting to excel us all in\par
his financial successes, was eager to go to the front. Accordingly, we\par
fixed everything so that he could everywhere strike the first and the\par
heaviest blow.\par
\par
Of course, on our twenty-two days' voyage we had ample time for\par
discussion, and before we passed the Equator had settled on our plan.\par
First of all, it was agreed that one of the party should keep his neck\par
out of the noose, to stand by if either of the others came to grief.\par
Very much to my satisfaction, it was again decided that I was the man to\par
stand from under.\par
\par
[Illustration: "AT 5 O'CLOCK ALL HANDS UP AND BREAKFAST READY."--Page\par
290.]\par
\par
The firm of Maua in Rio was the most considerable in all South America,\par
and Mac's introductions were to this firm. The plan was for Mac to\par
present himself to Maua & Co., and to draw within twenty-four hours, at\par
least L10,000, so as to make sure of our expenses, and a day or two\par
before steamer day to arrange for a very large sum, twenty or thirty\par
thousand pounds. As soon as that was obtained, George was to go to the\par
Bank of London and Rio de Janeiro, and secure as much as he thought it\par
safe to ask for, five or ten thousand pounds. This would be paid in\par
Brazilian paper money, which I was to exchange for sovereigns. Then I\par
was to buy a ticket for myself on the steamer going south, take the gold\par
off and stow it away in my stateroom. At the last moment, in the bustle\par
and confusion of sailing, Mac and George were to slip into my stateroom,\par
conceal themselves and sail with the steamer, and when once out of the\par
harbor, to see the purser, explain that they had arranged with a friend\par
to purchase tickets; but, as he had not put in an appearance, they would\par
be obliged to pay a second time. We purposed to go down the east coast\par
and up the west to Lima. Visiting the cities as we went from Lima, we\par
would go to Panama, there catch the steamer to San Francisco, and after\par
a pleasant sojourn in California go overland to New York with a million.\par
\par
This was our plan, but, as all the world knows, there is a vast\par
difference between making plans and carrying them into successful\par
execution.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XVI.\par
\par
"SHOW ME YOUR LETTERS OF CREDIT."\par
\par
\par
Fate, Providence, call it what you will, seldom fails to upset\par
wrongdoing, making it rocky for the wrongdoer.\par
\par
By an irony of fortune we carried with us that which was going to balk\par
all, or nearly all, our fine scheme.\par
\par
In our letters of credit in some mysterious way the name of the\par
sub-manager of the London and Westminster Bank had been omitted,\par
although this was absolutely essential to the validity of the letters.\par
There was also another error, an error of such an extraordinary\par
nature--that of spelling "endorse" with a "c"--that it is enough to make\par
any man contemplating an unlawful act despair of success, since we could\par
be defeated by such mysterious and unforeseen accidents.\par
\par
A few hours after our arrival Mac called at the bankers' and was well\par
received by the manager.\par
\par
He told the manager his letters of credit ran from L5,000 to L20,000\par
each, and that he should want L10,000 the next day. Would they have it\par
ready?\par
\par
The next day he went to the bank, George and myself being posted\par
outside. In ten minutes he reappeared with a square bundle under his\par
arm. He smiled as he passed us, and, turning a corner, entered a cafe,\par
where he joined us. His bundle contained L10,000 in Brazilian bank\par
notes. He assured us that everything was serene at the bank, that he\par
could have L100,000 if he wanted to ask for it.\par
\par
I had already been to the three largest money brokers and arranged to\par
buy gold. So, leaving Mac and George, I got a sole leather bag we had\par
for the purpose, and, hiring a stalwart black porter, went to the\par
brokers. I bought sovereigns for the whole L10,000. It was ten bags with\par
one thousand pounds in each. The weight was 168 pounds. The black fellow\par
put it on his head, and followed me to my hotel, and found it a pretty\par
good load, too. So here we had one big fish landed, and confidently\par
counted on several more.\par
\par
I related above how we had in some incomprehensible way omitted putting\par
on the letter of credit the sub-manager's name. How could we have\par
committed such a blunder? My answer is that this is only another example\par
of the unforeseen "something" ever happening to defeat any anticipated\par
benefit from ill-gotten gains.\par
\par
The next day Mac went to the bankers again, and was requested by the\par
manager to show the letter of credit on which was indorsed the ten\par
thousand pounds he had drawn against it. Looking at the letter, the\par
manager said: "This is singular; there is only the name of Mr. Bradshaw,\par
the manager, on this letter; J. P. Shipp, the sub-manager's name, should\par
be on the credit as well." And then he went on to say that some time\par
since they had been notified by the London Bank that all letters issued\par
by them would bear two signatures.\par
\par
Mac was a man of nerve, but it required all he had not to betray his\par
uneasiness. He said he really could not say how the omission had\par
occurred; he supposed it must have been accidental, but he would examine\par
his other letters as soon as he went back to the hotel.\par
\par
The look of chagrin and vexation on Mac's face when he came out was a\par
sight to see, and one that is as vivid in my memory now as in that far\par
off day in 1872.\par
\par
He went direct to the hotel, and there George and I soon joined him. We\par
sat down and looked at each other. The game apparently was up, and we\par
were a sorely disgusted party. We did not fall out with or reproach\par
each other, but felt we deserved a kicking. We did not ask each other\par
any questions, but I know our faces all wore a sadly puzzled look as we\par
repeated mentally, "How could we have made such an oversight?" But soon\par
another blunder--the misspelled word--was to crop up, that made this one\par
of the omitted name seem as a fly to an eagle.\par
\par
Mac and I thought the game up, and were mentally planning for flight.\par
But George, being a man of extraordinary courage and resource as well,\par
declared we could and would retrieve the blunder. He declared a bold\par
step must be taken, that, as the bankers had only seen the one credit,\par
the name of Shipp, the sub-manager, must be instantly put on the others.\par
We had the genuine signature of J. P. Shipp on a draft, and Mac at once\par
sat down to write it on all the letters. It was a trying ordeal for him,\par
Mac's nerves having had a wrench. He was a temperate man, but under the\par
circumstances we advised him to take a glass of brandy to steady his\par
nerves. Then placing the genuine signature before him and the forged\par
letters, he began to put in the name. The signatures were not well\par
written, but under the trying circumstances they were wonderfully well\par
done. All this had taken place within half an hour after he had left the\par
bank.\par
\par
It was a trying ordeal, but Mac was quite willing to do as George\par
advised. That was that he should take several of the letters and march\par
boldly into the bank and say: "Here are my letters; they are all right.\par
Both signatures are on all my letters but the one, and from that the\par
second signature has been in some way omitted." George's last word to\par
Mac was: "Rely upon us to extricate you from anything. Keep cool. Act up\par
to the character you have assumed. They can never fathom that the names\par
could have been written in so short a time. Boldly offer them more\par
exchange on London, and if there is any hesitation say you will transfer\par
your business to the English Bank of Rio at once."\par
\par
[Illustration: "SURELY THE CLERKS IN THE BANK KNOW HOW TO SPELL."--Page\par
172.]\par
\par
He started on his decisive errand, followed by us, in a miserable state\par
of anxiety. He was not long in the bank, but returned empty-handed. Upon\par
meeting at the designated place, he informed us the manager was\par
evidently agreeably surprised when shown the letters with both\par
signatures, and transferred the indorsement from the letter that had but\par
one signature to one with two. Once more we had matters all right, and\par
the broken place patched up again, but it behooved us not to do so any\par
more. But we did.\par
\par
During our stay in Rio we saw much to interest us. The negro was very\par
much in evidence. Slavery was still the law of the land; all the toil\par
and burden-bearing falls to the poor slave's lot. One day we all three\par
took an early train and alighted at a small hamlet on the border of a\par
stream about thirty miles from Rio, beyond the ranges of mountains that\par
hem in the city. We managed to find some saddle mules and started to see\par
the country. We rode for some miles through a land covered with\par
moundlike hills, no sooner coming to the bottom of one than we were\par
ascending another. These hills are covered with coffee bushes filled\par
with red fruit, about the size of a cherry, each containing two kernels.\par
The coffee was being picked into large flat baskets by slaves, which,\par
when filled, they carried away on their heads to the drying grounds.\par
\par
The roads were bordered with orange trees loaded with luscious fruit, to\par
which we helped ourselves. After a time we turned into a bridle path and\par
rode some miles through a dense forest. We emerged upon the outskirts of\par
a coffee plantation, where the slaves were just on their way to dinner,\par
and another half mile brought us to the planter's residence. Thirty or\par
forty slaves of both sexes and all ages were grouped upon the grass,\par
engaged in eating a black-looking stew out of metal dishes, their\par
fingers serving for knives, forks and spoons. Seeing three horsemen ride\par
out of the forest, they stared in stupid wonder, until one more\par
intelligent than the others went in search of the overseer. Presently a\par
white man appeared, and, in response to Mac's "Parlate Italiano," came\par
the smiling answer, "Si, Signor," proving, as we wagered he would be, a\par
native of beggarly, sunny Italy.\par
\par
The overseer showed us over the place, and explained all the processes\par
of preparing coffee for the market. In one corner of a large, unpainted\par
building was what he called the infirmary, and a comfortless looking\par
place it was. He said there was no doctor employed, and that he dealt\par
out medicine to the slaves himself. After being served with coffee we\par
thanked him for our entertainment and returned to Rio by an evening\par
train.\par
\par
The mail steamer Ebro was advertised to leave Rio for Liverpool on\par
Wednesday of the week following the exciting events narrated in the last\par
chapter. This was the mail that would carry the draft for L10,000 on the\par
London and Westminster Bank, along with a letter from the Rio bank,\par
stating that they had cashed Mr. Gregory Morrison's draft upon the\par
letter issued by them.\par
\par
Twenty-two or three days after the steamer left Rio the London bank\par
would know their correspondents in Rio had been victimized, but 8,000\par
miles of blue water was between them, with no way to bridge it but by\par
steam; so we had at least forty-four days more to gather in our harvest.\par
I ought to say, apparently forty-four more days, for by an amazing\par
blunder we were about to bring a storm upon our heads.\par
\par
The steamer we purposed to load our money on and ourselves, too, was the\par
Chimborazo, advertised to arrive on Tuesday and to leave for the River\par
Plate and the west coast the next day. So it was agreed that on Monday\par
Mac should go to the bank and arrange to cash his letters for twenty or\par
thirty thousand pounds, and go the next day for the money. As soon as\par
Mac came from the bank and announced that all was well, another of us\par
was to call at the Bank of London and Rio and the River Plate Bank,\par
present his letters of introduction and ask in each bank to have the\par
five thousand pounds or ten thousand pounds ready the next day. They\par
purposed to call about 11 o'clock, so as to give me time to exchange the\par
Brazilian bank notes for sovereigns, and to buy my ticket by the\par
Chimborazo, to secure my stateroom and to take the gold to the steamer,\par
and, above all, to get my passport vised by the police.\par
\par
Monday came. We expected a nervous day, not such a paralyzingly nervous\par
one as it proved to be. In fact, a nervous Tuesday followed a nervous\par
Monday. My reader must remember that we were in the tropics, with a\par
blazing sun looking down on us with an intensity that made one long for\par
Greenland's icy mountains to cool us.\par
\par
We went into the public park for our last consultation before our\par
fortune, which never came, was to come.\par
\par
Mac had in the little morocco case in his pocket two letters each for\par
L20,000. Certainly no man in the world, save him, could have carried off\par
such a game played for such high stakes. Handsome in person, faultless\par
in address, cool in nerve, a master of all the languages spoken in\par
Rio--Portuguese, Spanish, Italian and French. Above all, he had a\par
boundless confidence in himself. What an honorable future might have\par
been his but for his youthful follies! Truly he could have achieved a\par
wonderful success in any honorable career. Unhappily for him, he, like\par
thousands of our brainiest youth, had entered the Primrose Way. In our\par
youthful fire and thoughtlessness we saw only the flowers and heard the\par
siren's song, but at last the Primrose Way led us down into a gloom\par
where all the flowers withered and the gay songs turned into dirges.\par
\par
Looking at his watch Mac jumped up, saying: "It is 10.45 and time to be\par
off." So he started for the bank, we following at some distance, our\par
nerves all on the stretch. We felt that our lives and fortunes were\par
trembling in the balance. The minutes dragged like hours. While watching\par
we saw several persons enter or leave the bank, and still our friend\par
delayed his appearance.\par
\par
To our suspicious minds there appeared to be strange movements about the\par
bank that boded ill for us. A thousand suspicions born of our fears came\par
and went through our minds, until at last, unable to endure the\par
suspense, I entered the bank myself, and stood there, pretending I was\par
waiting for some one. I sharply scrutinized every one and everything.\par
Mac was somewhere out of sight in the private offices. The clerks were\par
gossiping together, and that fact to me was suspicious. Then, to my\par
alarm, a bank clerk entered from the street with an eagle-eyed man, a\par
Hebrew, evidently, of about 45 years of age. Both passed hurriedly into\par
the private office, leaving me in an agony of suspense. My only relief\par
at that moment was the thought that George and myself had not as yet\par
compromised ourselves, and could, in the event of Mac's arrest, manage\par
to save him, either by bribery or a rescue.\par
\par
Without appearing to do so, I watched that dingy, mottled door leading\par
into the private office until every crack and seam in it was\par
photographed indelibly on my brain.\par
\par
In the trying periods of one's life, when the heart and soul are on the\par
rack, how strangely trifling details of the objects about one will be\par
noticed and remembered. It seems some cell of the brain, quite separate\par
from the cell of feeling and sensation, works calmly and steadily on,\par
photographing the material of one's surroundings.\par
\par
I can never forget a flower worn by a lady guest at my table, when, in\par
the midst of enjoyment and surrounded by friends, the hand of the law in\par
the form of a burly detective was laid on me in Cuba. In all the misery\par
and humiliation of that scene I remember the peculiar color of the wood\par
of a cigar box standing on the sideboard. Doubtless each of my readers\par
will recall some similar phenomenon in his own life.\par
\par
At last, unable to endure the suspense, above all, the uncertainty, I\par
went to the little door, and, opening it, looked in. To my intense\par
relief I saw Mac sitting there apparently talking unconcernedly with\par
Braga, the manager, and the Hebrew. As I had not attracted attention I\par
closed the door, went out in the street and gave George the pre-arranged\par
signal that all was well. Just then our partner appeared but with\par
telltale face. It was flushed with chagrin and vexation, and there was\par
gone from the contour of his body that indescribable port that tells,\par
better than words, of confidence and victory.\par
\par
We went by different routes to our rendezvous, and I will leave it to\par
the imagination of my readers to picture our state of mind as we\par
listened to his recital of woe--the tale of Priam's Troy over again.\par
\par
Mac had been cordially received by the manager, and had told him he\par
would require L20,000 the next day; would he please have it ready? The\par
manager replied that he did not require any more exchange on London, but\par
that he would send out for his broker, who would sell his bills on the\par
exchange. He (the manager) would indorse the bills of exchange and\par
indorse the amounts on his letters of credit. Of course, Mac could only\par
acquiesce, and Mr. Braga sent a clerk to his broker, Mr. Meyers, to come\par
around. This was the sharp-eyed Hebrew whom I saw enter.\par
\par
The manager introduced Meyers to "Mr. Gregory Morrison," and explained\par
that he was to sell exchange for L20,000 on Morrison's credit, which the\par
bank would indorse. Meyers said: "Please show me your letters." Putting\par
his hand into his breast pocket and pulling out the little morocco case\par
containing the two letters, he handed the case and contents to Meyers,\par
who, probably without suspicion of anything being wrong, unrolled both\par
letters, and holding them in his hands, ran his sharp eyes down one of\par
them and read right through the body of the letter. They came to the\par
"note," which read: "All sums drawn against this credit please endorce\par
on the back, and notify the London and Westminster Bank at once." Here\par
he suddenly halted, turned his hawk's eye on Mac and said: "Why, sir,\par
here's the word indorse misspelled. Surely the clerks in the London\par
banks know how to spell!"\par
\par
Here was a thunderbolt, indeed, that pierced poor Mr. Gregory Morrison\par
through and through, but he showed no sign. He coolly remarked that he\par
did not care to have his bills sold on the exchange, but would go and\par
see the people of the London and Rio and River Plate Banks, as they\par
probably would want exchange and would doubtless let him have what money\par
he required. Meyers said very sharply, "Have you letters to those\par
banks?" "I have," said Mac, at the same time producing two, one to each\par
bank, and each bearing the stamp of their respective banks.\par
\par
That he had these letters was a happy thing, and no one under forty\par
days' time could say for a fact that they were not genuine. The dramatic\par
production of these letters lulled the fast gathering suspicions, and\par
would have called a halt had they purposed any serious action, for the\par
reason that during the forty days it would take to communicate with\par
London the credits could not be proved to be forgeries. That such\par
letters existed at all was due entirely to the foresight which had\par
provided to meet just such a contingency.\par
\par
We all were for a brief few seconds utterly dumfounded, but quickly\par
aroused ourselves to the necessity of instant action to protect our\par
comrade. We saw that we must at once give over all thought of trying to\par
do any more business in Rio, and set all our inventions and energy at\par
work to save the L10,000 and to smuggle our companion safely out of Rio.\par
But how?\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XVII.\par
\par
ONCE MORE WE SAIL THE SEAS OVER.\par
\par
\par
Here in our country we know nothing of the annoyances and humbuggery of\par
the passport system, but now, as in 1872, every person desiring to leave\par
Brazil must be provided with a passport--if a foreigner, from his own\par
Government; if a native, one from the government of Brazil. When ready\par
to leave the country he must take his passport to police headquarters\par
and get it vised, at the same time notifying the police of the steamer\par
he proposes to sail on. Leaving the passport with the agent from whom he\par
buys his ticket, the latter, after ascertaining from the police that the\par
intending passenger is not wanted by the authorities, transmits the\par
passport to the purser of the steamer, who, in turn, hands it to the\par
passenger after the vessel is at sea.\par
\par
It will be seen that these regulations make it difficult for a suspected\par
person to leave Brazil by the regular channels of communication, and\par
there are no back doors of escape in that country. Once in any seaport\par
town you must, if you leave at all, sail out of the harbor mouth, for in\par
the other direction, that is, inland, one is confronted by the mighty\par
tropical forests, the greater portion of which has never been looked\par
upon by the eye of man; and between all the seaports the same\par
impenetrable forest stretches.\par
\par
So, straight out of the harbor between the Sugar Loaf and Fort Santa\par
Cruz Mac had to sail. How he should do so with safety was the problem we\par
had to solve. In this venture it would not do to have any blunders.\par
Without doubt the steamers would be watched for him, and instant arrest\par
and incarceration in the deadly tropical prison would be his lot if\par
discovered in the attempt to slip out of the country.\par
\par
To complicate the matter here it was Monday, and no steamer to sail\par
until Wednesday, so there were forty-eight hours of frightful anxiety\par
ahead of us.\par
\par
The Ebro, going to Europe, was in the harbor taking in cargo and coal.\par
The Chimborazo, going South, was not yet signaled, and we determined at\par
all hazards to get him off by the Ebro. We all had American passports,\par
and by the use of chemicals could alter the names and descriptions on\par
them at will.\par
\par
Of course, the names in our passports were the same as we had in our\par
letters. George went to police headquarters, and giving a douceur to an\par
attendant, had the "vise" put on his passport at once. Then going to the\par
passenger agent he bought a ticket to Liverpool by the Ebro, and by\par
paying ten guineas extra had a stateroom assigned to him alone. After\par
this he took a boat and went out to the steamer, carrying with him two\par
bags of oranges and stowed them away under the bottom berths.\par
\par
To make the escape a success it was decided prudent for George as Wilson\par
to get the agent well acquainted with his face and appearance, so if the\par
question was asked, "Who is this Wilson?" the police would see by the\par
description it was not the man they were looking for. For the next forty\par
hours George made the agent very tired. At one time he would want to\par
know if he could not get some reduction in the passenger rate, or if the\par
Ebro was seaworthy, or if there was any danger of her engines breaking\par
down, etc., until the agent got not only to know "Mr Wilson," but wished\par
him at the bottom of the sea.\par
\par
When George started for the police office he left Mac and me alone in\par
the park.\par
\par
[Illustration: "POINTING TO THE GOLD, MAC SAID: BOYS HELP\par
YOURSELVES."--Page 244.]\par
\par
It was absolutely essential that Mac should put in one more appearance\par
at the bank. It was an ordeal, but one he had to undergo. He even\par
dreaded to return to his hotel, but go he must; so, just before the bank\par
closed, he called in and casually informed the manager that he should\par
start the next morning for S. Romao, a town in the interior of Brazil,\par
to be absent a week. He was then to go to the Hotel d'Europe, pay his\par
bill, at the same time stating that he was to leave Rio by the 4 o'clock\par
train the next morning, for San Paulo. As Mac had two trunks and other\par
impedimenta befitting a man of his importance, it was necessary to take\par
a carriage to the station, which was nearly a mile distant. It would be\par
unsafe to go in a carriage belonging to the hotel; therefore, he was to\par
say that a friend would call for him. As it was still two hours to\par
sunset, I suggested that after he had arranged matters he should saunter\par
out, walk about the streets until dark, then return to the hotel and be\par
ready when George should call for him at 3 o'clock the next morning.\par
\par
After these arrangements we separated, George and I following to\par
ascertain if he was being watched or shadowed by detectives. When he\par
entered the hotel we remained in view of the entrance. It was not long\par
before he reappeared and walked leisurely along the street. A few\par
seconds after we saw another man come out, cross the street, and go in\par
the same direction. I followed him, and was soon satisfied that he was\par
keeping Mac in view. This sort of double hunt was kept up until dusk,\par
when Mac returned to his hotel, unconscious that a moment later his\par
"shadow" entered the place also. Here was a complication, indeed, though\par
it was no more than we had anticipated among the possibilities; still, I\par
had indulged in the hope that the bank would rely entirely upon the\par
passport system, and take no further steps for a day or two, which was\par
all the time required to carry out our plan. Though Mac had good nerve,\par
it was already somewhat shaken, and surely the situation would have\par
unnerved most men. Therefore, fearing that the certain knowledge of\par
imminent danger might still further confuse him and cause some false\par
move, we determined to keep our discovery to ourselves.\par
\par
George next proceeded to an obscure part of the town, and stopping at a\par
small but respectable looking tavern, he engaged a room for the next\par
day, also a carriage, with an English-speaking driver, to be in\par
readiness at 3 o'clock the next morning. Promptly at the hour he was at\par
the livery stable, where he found the carriage ready, and was driven to\par
the Hotel d'Europe. Sending the driver up to the office on the second\par
floor, Mac soon appeared and informed him that he had promised to take\par
to the station a man who was stopping at the hotel. "He is going to S.\par
Romao by the same train," continued Mac, "and seems a good fellow, for I\par
had a long talk with him last night." Upon seeing signs of disapproval\par
in my face, he explained: "Well, you know, he said he could not get a\par
carriage at so early an hour in the morning, and I thought it could do\par
no harm to take him in, and he is waiting upstairs."\par
\par
Here I joined them, and it would be difficult for the reader to imagine\par
the effect of this surprising communication upon our minds, for it was\par
clear enough that this was the very person who had been "shadowing" Mac\par
the day before, and had skillfully ingratiated himself into his new\par
friend's confidence. I could but admire his nerve in asking a\par
contemplated victim for a ride to the station. I said to Mac: "What in\par
the world can you be thinking of? Don't you see you are blocking our\par
whole plan? Go up and tell him your carriage is loaded down with\par
luggage, and express your regrets that you cannot accommodate him."\par
\par
During this time the baggage was being placed in the carriage, and as\par
soon as Mac had dismissed his "passenger," who for some reason did not\par
show himself, we started rapidly for the station. On the way I requested\par
him to avoid making any new friends until he should find himself well\par
out at sea. I said:\par
\par
"It might be fatal to attract the attention of any one, or to let any\par
one see you leave the train. Of course, this new acquaintance of yours\par
is only a countryman, but it is not possible to foresee what disaster\par
the least mistake or want of caution might originate. These cars are on\par
the English system, divided into compartments. You must go into the\par
station, stand near the ticket office until your new acquaintance comes,\par
then observe if he buys a first-class; if so, you take a second, and\par
vice versa. Pay no attention to him, and let him see you get into your\par
compartment, but keep an eye on his movements. In case he comes to get\par
in where you are, despite the different class of the tickets, tell him\par
the compartment is engaged. Everything depends on how you carry yourself\par
through the next twenty minutes. A single false step, a word too little\par
or too much, will surely prove fatal to all, for if anything happens to\par
you, we remain in Brazil."\par
\par
In accordance with our pre-arranged plan, I stopped the carriage\par
opposite the station, it being still dark. Mac alighted, went straight\par
inside, and in a few minutes saw his "passenger" come puffing in, nearly\par
out of breath. Unquestionably supposing Mac's baggage to be already on\par
the train, he purchased a ticket, and after seeing his intended victim\par
enter a compartment, got into another himself just as the train began to\par
move. This was the vital moment for which Mac had been waiting, and,\par
quickly opening the door on the opposite side, he stepped off on that\par
side, hastily crossed to the other platform of the dimly lighted\par
station, and made his way unnoticed into the street. While this was\par
passing, I sat in the carriage, and it was not many minutes before I had\par
the satisfaction of seeing Mac coming back. But for the benefit of the\par
driver we then had a dialogue somewhat as follows:\par
\par
"It is too bad. Our friends have not arrived. What shall we do?"\par
\par
"Well, I suppose we must go back to the hotel and wait for the afternoon\par
train," I answered.\par
\par
"But I have paid my bill there," said Mac, "and do not care to go back."\par
\par
"Then," I replied, "meet me at the station, and I will look after the\par
luggage."\par
\par
In case they recovered the trail, the information obtained from the\par
driver would cause confusion and delay sufficient, I hoped, to enable us\par
to get Mac out of Rio.\par
\par
I then told the coachman to drive me into the city. It was not yet\par
daylight, but after a while I saw a sort of eating house and tavern\par
combined, and had the carriage halted there. Alighting, I entered and\par
said to the person in charge that I did not wish to disturb my friends\par
at so early an hour, and would pay him for taking care of my baggage, as\par
I wished to discharge the carriage. The offer was, of course, accepted,\par
the baggage housed and the carriage dismissed. In the mean time Mac was\par
waiting for us in an appointed place not far away, where I joined him,\par
and we went to the obscure tavern where the room had been engaged.\par
George was awaiting us.\par
\par
So far our plan was successful. Mac was safely hidden away, while his\par
clever friend was speeding miles away on a wild goose chase. There was\par
only one train a day each way, and we knew the detective could not get\par
back to Rio until late. We felt certain that when he found Mac was not\par
on the train he would think his intended victim had slipped off at some\par
way station--possibly with a view of making his escape into the\par
interior; even if he sent a dispatch to the bank--an unlikely thing for\par
a Brazilian to do--it would doubtless be to the effect that his quarry\par
had left Rio on the early train that morning with him.\par
\par
[Illustration: VIEW OF MONTEVIDEO.]\par
\par
We passed some trying hours together. Then George left to take Mac's\par
baggage off to the steamer. He engaged two stalwart porters; they stand\par
on every corner busily engaged in plaiting straw for hats while\par
waiting for a job. Dividing the baggage between the two he had it\par
carried to the wharf, and, taking a small boat, quickly had it stowed in\par
the hold and the small articles carried to the stateroom. Soon after he\par
joined us on shore.\par
\par
It was but 10 o'clock when he came, and it was with something like\par
dismay that we realized that the whole day was before us. Until the day\par
before, when Mac was in the bank, I had never known how long an hour\par
was, but this day we all came to know how long a day could be.\par
\par
The Ebro was anchored out in the bay. Her coal was all stowed, but\par
strings of barges laden with sacks of coffee were alongside. She was\par
advertised to sail sharp at noon.\par
\par
I went out once or twice to the bank and police headquarters, hanging\par
about for a few minutes to see if there was anything suspicious, but\par
there was nothing, and each time I hastened back to Mac.\par
\par
Our presence cheered him up, and he could not brook our absence. At last\par
the long day drew to an end and the shadows, to our intense relief,\par
began to darken in our little room, where we were holding our watch. The\par
tropic night closes quickly in. Soon the city was shrouded in darkness,\par
and we sallied out to the beach at the head of the bay to find relief in\par
movement. The time passed quicker then, and at last we sat down on some\par
wreckage there and watched the tropic night as it revealed its wealth of\par
stars, and sitting there we began to philosophize, moralizing upon the\par
destiny of man and his relations to things seen and unseen, upon\par
spiritual force; most of all upon divine justice, which in the end evens\par
up all things. But like so many other philosophers who write the style\par
of the gods and make a pish at fortune, we failed to make a personal\par
application of our philosophy.\par
\par
Near by there was a boat stand from which we had resolved to embark for\par
the steamer about two miles away. The night was lovely as a dream, and\par
we knew that midnight would find a large number of passengers on deck,\par
many of whom would pass the night there. Forward was all the bustle and\par
confusion inseparable from receiving and stowing cargo.\par
\par
At 9 o'clock I left them to go and get the remainder of the gold not yet\par
on board--some four thousand pounds. The street cars passed near by, and\par
within half an hour I returned with the gold in a bag swung from my\par
shoulder by a heavy strap. I also had with me a woman's wrap and a silk\par
shawl. We sat for an hour longer, and then securing a boat with two\par
negro rowers, we pulled for the ship. Three or four small boats were\par
fastened to the companion ladder, and our arrival attracted no\par
attention. Two officials in uniform--probably custom officers--stood at\par
the companion way. It was an anxious moment, but we slipped through the\par
dimly lighted cabins and passages, and were soon safely in the\par
stateroom. Bidding both good-bye, and promising to be on board again at\par
8 in the morning, I went ashore and straight to bed, and soon was\par
dreaming of starlit seas, of tropic woods and Summer bowers, white and\par
sweet with May blossoms. My health then, as now, was perfect, and I\par
awoke fresh and hopeful. After breakfasting on a dish of prawns and\par
another of soft-shelled crabs, I was off across the bay. Soon after 8 I\par
knocked softly at the stateroom door, was admitted and presented the\par
lunch I had brought. They gave me a warm greeting, but neither had\par
slept. The room had been hot and stuffy, and the noise of stowing cargo\par
had helped to banish sleep. Both were unnerved somewhat, but I had just\par
come off shore confident and cheerful, and my confidence and spirits\par
proved infectious.\par
\par
I knew by sight the chief of police and those just under him. I also\par
knew Braga, the bank manager, by sight. They, of course, did not know\par
me, and I could, unsuspected, be a looker-on in Vienna. Soon the\par
passengers, their friends and many idle visitors came off in boatloads,\par
while I, of course, scrutinized every boatload as it came up the side of\par
the ship.\par
\par
At 9.30 I saw a boat coming, which, when half a mile away, I recognized\par
as containing the chief of police and several of his subordinates; ten\par
minutes after Braga and one of the bank officials came, the only\par
passengers in their boat, and at once joined the police on the after\par
deck and stood with them waiting and watching the boats as they arrived.\par
In the mean time babel reigned around the ship. About three score boats\par
surrounded her, the owners selling to the passengers everything from\par
oranges to monkeys, snakes and parrots.\par
\par
I determined to conceal from George and Mac that Braga and the police\par
were on the ship, and about every twenty minutes I would slip down and\par
report "All's well;" but soon after 10 o'clock the enemy were joined by\par
the ticket agent from shore, and I could see they were contemplating\par
some movement. Slipping down to the cabin, I said: "Boys, everything is\par
all right; keep perfectly cool. Braga and the police are pulling to the\par
ship and may search it; if so, it will take half an hour to get here. I\par
will keep everything in my eye and give you ample notice." I then\par
returned on deck and stood among the officials. They conversed in\par
Portuguese, which was Greek to me; soon the agent dived below and\par
reappeared with the manifest of the passengers, and an enormous heap of\par
passports. After some conversation they sent the passports back; then,\par
headed by the agent and purser, manifest in hand, they began to verify\par
the list and scrutinize the passengers in the staterooms. Once more I\par
hurried below and reported.\par
\par
Mac was naturally very dignified, but divesting himself of coat, vest\par
and dignity at the same time, he planted himself under the berth. Very\par
close and very hot quarters he found it, and we put the bags of oranges\par
in front, disposing of them so as to make it appear as if they filled\par
the whole space, when in reality they were a mere screen.\par
\par
Then we opened the door to the fullest extent. We had taken off our\par
coats--it being frightfully hot--and with a bottle of claret and a bowl\par
of ice standing on the little washstand and two glasses all in full\par
view, we awaited the arrival of our friends, the enemy.\par
\par
Our door was flat against the partition, giving a full sweep of the room\par
to the eye of the passerby, and George and I waited confidently for the\par
inspection we knew was inevitable. I sat on the foot of the lower berth,\par
smoking and swinging my feet. George sat on a folding camp-stool, with\par
his face toward the door, but not obstructing the view. Soon the\par
procession arrived, with the ticket agent in front. When he saw George\par
he at once recognized him as the Mr. Wilson who had bought the ticket,\par
and he simply said: "How do you do, Mr. Wilson?" and passed on without\par
looking in the room. Braga and the police followed, casually glanced at\par
us two, and were gone. I put on my coat and followed the procession, and\par
at 11.30 they went up on the after deck, evidently satisfied that their\par
man was not on the ship, and contented themselves with watching new\par
arrivals. I flew down, gave them the good news that the search was over,\par
and poor Mac, half-roasted, came from behind the bags of oranges.\par
Declaring he was roasted alive and dying of thirst, he finished the\par
bottle of iced claret.\par
\par
Ten minutes before 12 the bell was rung and all people for shore were\par
warned to leave. Soon we heard the pleasant sound of the steam winch\par
lifting the anchor, and at noon precisely, to our relief, the screw\par
began to revolve at quarter speed, and the Ebro to respond by forging\par
slowly ahead. All boats fell off but ours and the police boat. At last,\par
after giving a good look up and down the bay, Braga and the police\par
entered the boats, and, casting off, soon were left behind. Once more\par
and for the last time I flew down to the cabin. They saw the good news\par
in my face; then, shaking Mac's hand in hearty farewell, we ran to the\par
upper deck, down the ladder into our boat, and a moment later the big\par
ship, putting on full steam, left us astern, we ordering the boatman to\par
pull hard after the ship. Mac soon appeared on the after deck, and waved\par
his handkerchief to us in farewell. We gave him three cheers, and,\par
excited and happy, with our long anxiety over, we returned to the shore.\par
\par
With Mac sailing northward ho! with Wilson's passport and ticket in his\par
pocket, and all our money save two thousand pounds in his trunk, our\par
buccaneering expedition on the Spanish Main was over and all but a\par
failure when comparing the L10,000 we had captured with our magnificent\par
expectations.\par
\par
Here was a gigantic and well-conceived scheme which had almost collapsed\par
through trifles, which, to an honest enterprise would have been light as\par
air, but which to us and to our plans were of crushing force, built up,\par
as all schemes of wrong doing are, on foundations of sand.\par
\par
To conclude very briefly the narrative of this expedition, I will here\par
add that the day after Mac's departure, altering his passport to fit\par
George's description, we sailed on the Chimborazo south to Montevideo.\par
Upon our arrival we, with all other passengers for the town, were\par
promptly put in quarantine for ten days in a vile little island called\par
in irony the Isle of Flowers; but the mails were fumigated and sent\par
through, as were two additional mails arriving from Europe and Rio. When\par
our quarantine was over we were permitted to enter the city. We found\par
that some advice or rumor had reached there, and we feared to venture\par
our letters of credit for money. So, destroying all documents save our\par
passports, we paid a visit to Buenos Ayres, and then we embarked on a\par
French steamer for Marseilles, arriving there without any particular\par
adventure, and the next day had a happy meeting with Mac in Paris.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XVIII.\par
\par
LITTLE FISHES WRIGGLING THROUGH GREEN WAVES.\par
\par
\par
Once more together and our adventures since we separated related, the\par
question arose: What next?\par
\par
We determined to abandon our dangerous business, for we had capital\par
sufficient to start in an honest career, and resolved to do so. For a\par
long time our attention had been turned to Colorado, and we had\par
frequently talked over a project of going to some growing city there,\par
starting a bank and building a wheat elevator and stockyards. Fifty\par
thousand dollars would start our bank, and $10,000, with some credit,\par
the elevator and yards. This sum we had, with an additional $10,000 to\par
pay our way until profit came in from our investments. Here was another\par
great and honorable scheme--one easily carried out had we only gone on\par
with it. What a success we might have made, particularly so when\par
considered in the light of the development of Colorado since 1872 and\par
our energy and knowledge of business.\par
\par
In Paris we all stopped at the Hotel Meurice, Rue Rivoli, and spent much\par
time sightseeing. We were particularly interested in viewing the\par
battlefields around Paris--so interested, in fact, that we read up the\par
whole history of the mighty struggle with Germany, which ended in\par
throwing France into the dust. We, like most of the world here, got our\par
ideas of the war and the battles from the current news of the day, as\par
published in the newspapers, and we had a general idea that the\par
Frenchmen had not made much of a fight. That conclusion could only be\par
arrived at by a superficial knowledge such as had been ours.\par
Investigation upon the spot and a study of impartial authorities soon\par
opened our eyes to the fact that France only succumbed after a mighty\par
and most heroic struggle. The first few weeks of the war saw her entire\par
regular army captive, and transported prisoners across the Rhine. That\par
army had made a brave but unfortunate fight. Badly commanded, with the\par
transport and subsistence utterly demoralized, they were no match for\par
the mighty hosts that Germany poured across the Rhine. Perfectly\par
equipped, matchless in discipline since the palmy days of Rome,\par
commanded by the foremost military intellects of the age, they met the\par
French, overmatching them at every point of contact; enveloping their\par
columns with masses of infantry, or sweeping them with murderous storms\par
of shot and shell, or launching a magnificent cavalry at them, against\par
which French valor--ill directed as it was--proved futile, and that\par
splendid array of 480,000 men had to ground their arms, surrender their\par
colors, and, to their own unspeakable shame and humiliation, become\par
captive to their foes, leaving their beloved France defenseless. But the\par
loss of their army, no more than their thronging foes, dismayed France.\par
The heart of the nation was stirred, and from the Rhine to the Atlantic,\par
from the Channel to the blue Mediterranean, France rose as one man. They\par
saw the entire military force of Germany encamped on their soil, and in\par
their undisciplined valor, hurled themselves against it, and gave to\par
their astounded foes an exhibition of Titanic force and determined valor\par
whose story, when known, will become the admiration of all generations\par
of men.\par
\par
It was against the decree of Heaven that France should win in the\par
struggle, but she fell only to rise the higher for the fall. The year\par
1871 saw France in the dust, with the armies of her foe encamped over\par
more than half her soil, with robber-like demands for huge sums of gold\par
ere the modern Goths would march home again. To-day she stands the\par
marvel of the world. Twice the France of 1870, with the busy hum of\par
industry through all her borders, an overflowing treasury, a contented\par
people and an army and many which are the awe of Europe. To-day the\par
enemy that flung her to earth twenty-four years ago, seeks safety from\par
her attack in defensive alliances with all the nations of the Continent.\par
\par
We resolved to see Europe before returning to America, so the next few\par
weeks were spent in a pleasure jaunt.\par
\par
In the course of it we visited Vienna, remaining there some time and\par
bringing away many and pleasant memories of that music-loving old city\par
on the Danube. We finally all returned to Wiesbaden together and visited\par
the Casino, watching the play and players with an interest that never\par
flagged. Here we saw such vast sums of money ever changing hands that we\par
almost insensibly began to think the thousands we had were as nothing,\par
and when divided up, the sum coming to each seemed almost beggarly.\par
\par
Gradually we began to speculate as to the desirability of doubling our\par
capital once or twice at least, before we threw up our hands and gave up\par
the game. I need hardly tell the reader that what at first was a\par
philosophical speculation, an airy theory of a possibility, rapidly\par
crystallized into steadfast purpose and determinate resolve, and soon\par
our brains were working, and readily brought forth a new scheme. For was\par
not there the Bank of England, with uncounted millions in her vaults,\par
and was not I, as Frederick Albert Warren, a customer of the bank, and\par
as such were not the vaults of the bank at our disposition?\par
\par
We rated our powers high and fondly thought that, speaking in a general\par
way, honesty was the best policy, yet in our case there was an exception\par
to the rule. We felt and acknowledged we were doing wrong, but since the\par
wrong (apparently) profited us, we would do wrong that good might come\par
thereby.\par
\par
Finally we resolved to go on with our postponed assault upon the money\par
bags of the Bank of England, at the same time evolving a plan that\par
seemed to promise unbounded wealth and complete immunity for us all.\par
\par
So we packed our baggage, bade farewell to Wiesbaden, and one early June\par
morning in 1872 saw us all once more in smoky London, resolved to rouse\par
that Old Lady called the Bank of England from her century-long slumber\par
spent in dreaming of her impregnability.\par
\par
In Frankfort there are several firms, Fischer by name, all bankers, and\par
as soon as we determined to return to London, Mac wrote a letter in\par
French to the Bank of England and signed it H. V. Fischer, which, of\par
course, would leave the manager to suppose his correspondent was one of\par
the Fischer bankers. In the letter he said his distinguished customer,\par
Mr. F. A. Warren, had written him from St Petersburg, requesting him to\par
transfer to his account in the Bank of England the small balance\par
remaining to his credit on his (Fischer's) books, therefore he had the\par
honor to inclose bills on London for L13,500, payable to the order of\par
the manager, said sum to be placed to the credit of Mr. F. A. Warren.\par
\par
I took this letter to Frankfort, and, having purchased bills of exchange\par
on London to the amount named, inclosed them and mailed the letter. A\par
day or so after I received a letter at Frankfort from the manager of the\par
bank, acknowledging the receipts of the drafts, and announcing that the\par
proceeds of the same had duly been placed to the credit of F. A. Warren.\par
So I had over $67,000 to my credit, and had now been a depositor for\par
five months.\par
\par
George took up his residence at a private house in the west end of\par
London, while Mac and I went to the Grosvenor Hotel.\par
\par
This hotel was one of the very few then in England which were allowed by\par
the aristocrats of London society to be what they called highly\par
respectable, that is, exclusive, and, therefore, a fit dwelling place\par
for their dainty selves. In Dublin there is one of these highly\par
respectable hostels, the Gresham, on Sackville street. This hotel was a\par
type of all of the sort I mention. I once stopped at the Gresham for a\par
week and became one of the "nobility and gentry" that frequent these\par
hotels. The waiters all wore full-dress suits, faultless in cut and fit,\par
and the chief event in their daily existence, the serving of the table\par
d'hote, wore white kid gloves. The bewildering changes of varied colored\par
dishes (I mean crockery ware), was something to make one stare. Course\par
number one brought on a soup dish of pale violet color, quite a work of\par
art, but its contents was a watery compound with an artistic name.\par
Course number two consisted of a unique plate, light green in color,\par
with little fishes wriggling through green waves, but bearing on it a\par
small insipid portion of a genuine inhabitant of the deep; and so on,\par
course followed course, each on a different colored plate. If the dinner\par
was intended for an exhibition of crockery, each one of the seven I had\par
there was a success, but, however gratifying to the eye the dinners\par
might be, they were lamentable failures so far as stomach and appetite\par
were concerned; but when I came to pay my bill I found the white kid\par
gloves and the fancy china again; they were all in it, and many more\par
things as well. The bill was more than a foot long, filled with such\par
items as soap, sixpence; one envelope, one penny; one sheet note paper,\par
one penny; bath, two shillings; extra towels and soap for same,\par
sixpence, and so on through the line.\par
\par
We found the Grosvenor another Gresham. However, as we wanted to stop at\par
a swell hotel, we concluded--so long as we were there--to remain; but\par
after a few days we found the cuisine "highly respectable;" that is, for\par
dinner one could get roast--either beef or mutton. As for vegetables, we\par
were strictly limited to turnips, cauliflowers, cabbage and potatoes,\par
and, for dessert, the famous apple tart of England, more deadly even\par
than our mince pie.\par
\par
[Illustration: SOME NATIVES I MET IN TAWNY, SPAIN.--Page 290.]\par
\par
The proprietor of a certain popular restaurant in New York has a fad for\par
hanging elaborately got-up Scripture texts--exhortations mostly--around\par
the walls of his restaurant. Interspersed with these are advertisements\par
of his eatables--also exhortations--such as, "Try our buckwheat cakes,\par
10 cents;" "Try our doughnuts and coffee;" between the two exhortations,\par
a third bidding one flee from the wrath to come; but the most fetching\par
of all are two companion cards. On the one is the legend, "Try our hot\par
mince pie;" on the other is displayed the apropos warning, "Prepare to\par
meet thy God."\par
\par
So we resolved to sleep at the Grosvenor, but to avoid the apple tart.\par
We soon discovered a good restaurant near by, where we dined, and, as I\par
am on the subject of dining, I will finish this chapter with a little\par
narrative, the moral of which I will leave my readers to find: We were\par
now settled down in London, prepared to devote all our attention to that\par
Old Lady--The B. of E.--and, in accordance with a habit of ours, we\par
began to look for some safe place--hotel, cafe or restaurant--where we\par
could meet, run in at any time for consultation, or to write notes.\par
Three things were requisite--nearness to the money centre of the city, a\par
room where we could be secluded from people coming and going, and a\par
proprietor clever enough not to be inquisitive, with a genius for\par
minding his own business. A man who has a genius for that thing always\par
carries it in his face, just as his opposite--the busybody--carries\par
the traces of his restless inquisitiveness in the face and manner.\par
\par
That same day we discovered, in a small street leading off Finsbury, a\par
shop with a sign over the door bearing the legend: "Licensed to sell\par
spirits and caterer." It had canned and potted meats, along with bottles\par
of wine, in the window, but was evidently fast going to seed. We pushed\par
our way in and found a bright, fresh-looking young Englishman,\par
evidently a countryman, but intelligent and civil, much like a\par
gamekeeper. We knew at once we had our place and man.\par
\par
After some weeks we observed, now and then, a couple of sharp-looking\par
customers hanging about the place.\par
\par
We feared being watched, and began to think it time to change, so\par
suddenly ceased calling at mine host's snuggery and took up new quarters\par
in a private house not far away. About two months later I happened to be\par
near and called. He received me warmly, and told me we had saved him\par
from bankruptcy. He had been a gamekeeper on a nobleman's estate, and\par
his wife had been a housemaid there. They married against the wishes of\par
their master, but they had five hundred pounds, and, coming to London,\par
started business on that. Custom was poor, and soon they were at the end\par
of their rope, when, happily for them, we came along and spent money\par
enough in his place to set him on his feet again.\par
\par
[Illustration: BANK OF ENGLAND BULLION VAULTS.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XIX.\par
\par
WITH NO REGRETS, WITH NO TORTURING REMORSE.\par
\par
\par
Although I had the very respectable balance of $67,000 at the bank, I\par
had not as yet, since my arrival in London, paid it a visit. This was in\par
pursuance of our plans. So far I had only done business with the\par
supernumeraries, and none of the people at the top had ever even heard\par
of me. But we determined that they should not long remain in ignorance\par
of the great American contractor, F. A. Warren.\par
\par
Three months had elapsed since our departure from London on our\par
piratical tour to the Spanish Main. In all nearly five months had\par
elapsed since Green had introduced me to the Old Lady whose impregnable\par
vaults we had now at last determined to loot. That in itself was a\par
favorable circumstance, as it would give me a chance to flourish in a\par
grandly indefinite way to the effect that I had "for some time" been a\par
customer of the bank, and none of the officials would probably take the\par
trouble to ascertain how very brief, in fact, my acquaintance had been.\par
\par
I left London by the night mail from Victoria Station for Paris, the\par
first of many hurried trips I took to the Continent on the business we\par
had entered upon. Truly, we worked hard, spent money lavishly, brought\par
all our power and genius to work--for what? To have the lightning fall\par
on us.\par
\par
Upon my arrival I drove at once to the Hotel Bristol, Place Vendome, a\par
swell hotel, where none but the great sirs o' the earth could afford to\par
stop.\par
\par
Here I registered as F. A. Warren, London, and at once sent off the\par
following letter:\par
\par
     P. M. Francis, Esq., Manager Bank of England, London.\par
\par
     Dear Sir: I am a customer of the bank, therefore I take the liberty\par
     of troubling you in the hope to have the benefit of your advice.\par
\par
     Will you kindly inform me what good 4 per cent. stocks are to be\par
     had in the market, also if the bank will transact the business for\par
     me? I remain very truly yours,\par
\par
     F. A. WARREN.\par
\par
By return mail came a letter wherein I was advised to invest in India 4\par
per cents or London Gas. I wrote an immediate order to have the bank\par
purchase ten thousand pounds of India stock and sent my check for that\par
amount, on his own bank, payable to the order of the manager. I received\par
the stock, instantly sold it, and replaced the money to my credit, and\par
the next day sent off an order for ten thousand pounds gas stock, and\par
repeated the operation until I had made the impression I wanted to make\par
on the mind of the manager, so that when I returned to London for my\par
decisive interview and sent in my card he would at once recognize the\par
name, F. A. Warren, as the multi-millionaire American who had been\par
sending him ten thousand pound checks from Paris.\par
\par
All the time of my stay in France I had nothing to do but enjoy myself,\par
and I entered upon a systematic sightseeing in and around Paris. There\par
are some strange contrasts in that old town. One day I made one of a\par
coaching party to Fontainebleau, twenty-one miles from the city. Every\par
foot of the road there is classic ground, and I had assiduously studied\par
day by day the history of France. That Paris is France is nearly a\par
truth, and I had in my mind a tolerably clear view of the history of the\par
country and of the men who made its history. I was right there on the\par
scene of the history-making, and I found an intensity of interest in my\par
excursions such as I had never experienced before. The driver of the\par
coach was an Englishman by the name of Nunn. I mention this here, as he\par
eventually became my servant, and will appear again in the narrative.\par
\par
To the Parisian hotel proprietor and shopkeeper the American visitor is\par
truly a providence. "Mine host" looks to him for loaves and fishes, and\par
is never deceived. The antics of our rich countrymen in Paris are\par
portentous in their amazing prodigality, and I fear we are the laughing\par
stock of the shopkeepers there.\par
\par
At the Cafe Riche and Tortoni's I have seen extravagances in ordering\par
expensive wines and viands by my countrymen that made me regret that the\par
fools who were being served were not forced to toil for the mere\par
necessaries of existence. Certainly they were unworthy stewards of the\par
wealth heaven or the other place had bestowed on them by inheritance. I\par
remember one boy there throwing away in vice and dissipation the fortune\par
his father had through years of a long life spent toilsome hours in\par
accumulating. I sat at a table near him on several occasions, when,\par
after his banquet was half over, he used to reward the waiter with a\par
five-hundred franc note ($100), but the proprietor was ever close at\par
hand and would instantly despoil the garcon of his prize. He was\par
companioned by a member of the demi-monde, who, when arrayed in male\par
attire, as she was nightly, would cut up enough monkey tricks in one\par
night at the Valentino or Mabille to have made the fortunes of all our\par
comic paper artists had they been on the spot to catch her antics with a\par
kodak and then lay them before an admiring public.\par
\par
The fortune this boy had inherited was unfortunately too vast and too\par
well-invested by his overfond and madly foolish father for the son to\par
run through it entirely. A very few years left him an imbecile in body\par
and mind, to become the prey of a parcel of sharks who, dressing in\par
purple and fine linen and faring sumptuously every day, held him in a\par
state of abject slavery and fear. One day, aboard his own yacht, off\par
Naples, they married him to a notorious woman. Under the guardianship of\par
his wife and her villain paramour he wandered like a spectre amid the\par
scene of his former riot.\par
\par
For long at Monte Carlo he lingered like a ghost, and at last died in\par
Florence. The American colony attended his funeral in a body, while his\par
widow, dissolved in tears, refused to be comforted. Although many dark\par
stories were whispered, the Americans there forgave her all, for her\par
grief and sorrow were so overpoweringly evident that it would have\par
seemed a crime to doubt her tender love for the departed. After having\par
the body embalmed, she embarked with her dead love for America, and\par
to-day his ashes rest in that mighty city of the dead, Greenwood, under\par
a Greek cross of white marble, bearing the date of birth and death. I\par
went to see it last Easter week. The grave was strewn with flowers, and\par
the pedestal bears this inscription:\par
\par
  "Too good for this world,\par
  The angels bore him to heaven,\par
  Leaving his heartbroken wife\par
  To mourn her unspeakable loss."\par
\par
Unopposed she succeeded to her husband's estate. It was large then;\par
to-day it has grown to enormous proportions. She is not, but easily\par
might have been, one of the Four Hundred.\par
\par
At Saratoga last August I saw her sitting on the balcony of the United\par
States Hotel--fat, wrinkled, vulgar-looking, covered with diamonds.\par
Nemesis appears to have postponed her visit to the lady. Her life from\par
her own standpoint has been a tremendous success. She has been\par
philosopher enough to appreciate what an immense factor mere eating and\par
drinking is in the sum of human enjoyment. Born with a cold heart, a\par
constitution of iron, and the digestion of an ostrich, happily for her\par
peace of mind she was absolutely without imagination.\par
\par
[Illustration: "IN MY DREAM I WAS ON A SHORELESS SEA."--Page 286.]\par
\par
To fill the sum of human happiness (from her own standpoint) she only\par
required one other thing, a good bank account, and that, she said,\par
heaven had put in her way, so her life has been filled full of joy, and\par
of the only sort she cared for or could appreciate. In her early years,\par
when her passions were strong, lover and paramour followed in rapid\par
succession. When her blood grew cold she found her delight in the\par
pleasures of the table, and keeping the same cook, who was an expert,\par
for twenty years, and exercising freely, 1894 found her at 60 with a\par
strong pulse, a perfect digestion and a keen enjoyment of sport, racing\par
in particular, and, on the whole, enjoying life as well as any woman in\par
the universe, with no regrets, no torturing remorse, but with a serene\par
faith that when done with this world she--never having done anything\par
very bad here--will have a pretty good time in the world to come.\par
\par
[Illustration: ENTRANCE TO BULLION VAULTS, BANK OF ENGLAND.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XX.\par
\par
DETAILS NECESSARY, IF TEDIOUS.\par
\par
\par
After the events narrated in the last chapter, I returned to London. I\par
arrived early in the morning, and, meeting my companions, we had a long\par
and anxious talk over my near-approaching and all-important interview\par
with that great Sir of the London world, the manager of the Bank of\par
England. Happy for us if in that interview the manager had asked for the\par
customary references, or had used ordinary business precaution and\par
investigated me, or, indeed, had acted as any ordinary business man\par
would have done under ordinary circumstances. Our own conclusions were\par
that the fact that I was already a depositor, together with the\par
impression made by the letters and my L10,000 checks, would put the\par
thing through. Yet we, of course, felt that a thousand things could\par
arise to block our way effectually. A look, a word too much, a shadow,\par
or a smile in my face might ruin all; but still, after providing so far\par
as possible for every contingency, after planning what was to be said or\par
left unsaid at the interview, after my companions filling me full of\par
advice, we felt after all that everything must be left to my discretion,\par
to say and to act as I thought best under the circumstances.\par
\par
This council of war was held in my room in the Grosvenor. I had arrived\par
from Paris at 6 o'clock. Mac and I breakfasted together at 8. George\par
joined us at 9, and we talked until 10, then we set out together for the\par
bank. Arriving there, they remained outside, watching for my\par
reappearance. Entering the bank, I sent in my card (F. A. Warren) by a\par
liveried flunkey, and was immediately ushered into the manager's parlor.\par
He has long since gone over to the majority, so here I will not so much\par
as name or describe him. Sufficient to say, that as soon as I set eyes\par
upon him I thought that we would have no particular difficulty in\par
carrying out our plans, save only so far as details went.\par
\par
The manager, who had been told that I was a railroad contractor,\par
expressed himself highly gratified to have me do my business through the\par
bank, and said they would do all in their power to accommodate me. I\par
told him that, of course, I was financing large sums, and would require\par
more or less discounts before the year was out. Then I came away, and\par
meeting my two friends outside of the bank, in answer to their eager\par
inquiries as to what had transpired, I told them that, so far as the\par
bank officials were concerned, our way to the vaults of the bank was\par
wide open.\par
\par
So ended the last scene of Act I.\par
\par
The next day I went to the Continental Bank, in Lombard street, and\par
bought sight exchange on Paris for 200,000 francs, paying for it by a\par
check on the Bank of England. I was given a note of identification to\par
the Paris agent of the bank.\par
\par
That night I left Victoria Station for Paris. At 10 the next morning I\par
had my money, and, going to the Place de la Bourse, near the Exchange, I\par
commissioned a broker, who was a member of the Exchange, to purchase\par
bills on London for L8,000. I cautioned him to buy bills drawn only on\par
well-known banking houses. About 3 o'clock he had the bills ready. I\par
paid him the amount, along with his commission, and, examining the\par
paper, found that he had purchased for me about what I wanted.\par
\par
I will explain, for the benefit of any reader not conversant with\par
financial transactions, that if John Russell, cotton broker in Savannah,\par
ships a thousand bales of cotton to a firm in Manchester, England, the\par
firm in Manchester authorizes him to draw a bill of exchange on their\par
firm, payable at some London bank at three or six months' time, for the\par
value of the cotton. We will say the price is L10,000. Russell draws ten\par
bills for L1,000 each, say payable at the Union Bank of London. He gives\par
these bills to a money broker in Savannah, who sells them on the\par
Exchange and gets for them whatever the rate of exchange may then be on\par
London. The president of the Georgia Central Railroad may have ordered a\par
thousand tons of steel rail in England for his road, and to pay for them\par
he orders a broker to buy for him bills on London to the amount of the\par
cost of the rails. He purchases the Russell bills, and these bills of\par
exchange he sends in payment to the steel rail manufacturers in England,\par
so, as a matter of fact, the president of the Georgia Central pays\par
Russell for his thousand bales of cotton, but has the bills of exchange.\par
So, in place of L10,000 in gold being freighted twice across the ocean,\par
the ten pieces of paper cross only once. These ten bills for L1,000\par
each, drawn on the Union Bank of London at six months, in due time are\par
presented, duly accepted and paid at maturity by the bank.\par
\par
Instead of commercial notes or bills they are now known as acceptances,\par
and are just as good as a bank note. Therefore, if the owner--no matter\par
who it is--wants the money at once, any bank will discount all or either\par
for the face value less the interest. In every commercial centre of the\par
world these accepted bills are being discounted by banks and moneyed\par
corporations for enormous sums, but by no bank in the world in such huge\par
amounts as by the Bank of England. Their daily discounts run into the\par
millions.\par
\par
What our plan was will be made clear later.\par
\par
[Illustration: A BILL OF EXCHANGE.]\par
\par
The evening of the day of my arrival in Paris found me on the express\par
speeding to Paris. Two hours past midnight I was on the miserable little\par
passenger steamer that plies across the chopping channel, and which I\par
suppose has seen more of human misery than all the fleets that sail the\par
Atlantic, for the channel has stronger counter currents, and wind,\par
tide and currents seem ever to be in violent opposition, and here\par
\par
  "E'er across the main doth float\par
    A sad and solemn swell,\par
  The wild, fantastic, fitful note\par
    Of Triton's breathing shell."\par
\par
And Triton (old Neptune's t'other name) makes all passers over this part\par
of his realm pay ample tribute for "his fantastic, fitful notes."\par
\par
The Paris night express lands one at early dawn in London, nearly always\par
weak on the legs, however. I breakfasted with Mac, and after that took\par
the bills to the various banks on which they were drawn, and leaving\par
them for their acceptance, I called again the next day and received them\par
back, bearing across the face, the magic words:\par
\par
  "London, Aug. 14, 1872.\par
\par
  "Accepted for the Union Bank of London.\par
\par
  "E. Barclay, Manager.\par
\par
  "J. Wayland, Assistant Manager."\par
\par
Then I hurried to the Grosvenor, and we all looked at them with\par
curiosity, for it was upon the imitation of just such acceptances that\par
our whole plan was based. I intended to present this and many more\par
batches of genuine bills for discount at the bank until the officials\par
should become accustomed to discounting for me. In the mean time, as\par
fast as I got genuine acceptances and bills, we kept on making\par
imitations of them for future use, only leaving out the date until such\par
time as we should be ready to put them in for discount. Of course, the\par
success or failure of our whole plan turned upon this point. Is it the\par
custom of the Bank of England (in 1873) to send acceptances offered for\par
discount to the acceptors for verification of signatures?\par
\par
This is always done in America, and had this very requisite precaution\par
been used by the Bank of England our plan would have been fruitless and\par
we should have been a few thousands out of pocket; but, if not, then we\par
could throw into the hopper enough acceptances of home manufacture so\par
that through the red tape routine of the bank millions of sovereigns\par
would be ground out into our pockets.\par
\par
Taking my deposit book and the genuine bills, I went to the bank and\par
left the bills for discount. This was at once done and the amount placed\par
to my credit. I drew L10,000, and that night found me once more one of\par
500 unfortunates paying tribute to Neptune. This time I landed at Ostend\par
and took the train for Amsterdam. There I repeated the Paris operation,\par
securing L10,000 in genuine bills. I returned to London, and as before\par
left them for acceptance. Then my companion manufactured a lot of\par
imitations and put them away with those previously manufactured, to be\par
all ready when the day came to use them. The genuine bills were then\par
discounted. Again and again I went to the Continent, repeating the\par
operation, until at last my credit at the bank was firm as a rock, and\par
we were ready to reap our harvest. But these operations, simple as they\par
seem, lasted over a period of six months, and had been made at heavy\par
cost. Our ordinary living expenses were not less than $25 a day for the\par
three, while our extraordinary expenses were enormous. I probably\par
traveled 10,000 miles over the Continent in my bill-buying expeditions\par
to Paris, Amsterdam, Frankfort and Vienna.\par
\par
Another source of expense was the commissions paid to brokers for buying\par
bills on the exchange. Then we had many expenses purely personal, and,\par
enormous as it seems, the sum total from the day of our return from\par
Brazil until the day of our operations against the bank began to bring\par
us in cash were quite $500 a week, so that we had invested $15,000 in\par
preparation, not to speak of our hard work--and it was hard work, and\par
trying, too, for there were a multitude of details to be worked out.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXI.\par
\par
THE EGYPTIANS PASS OVER THE RED SEA AND THE HEBREWS ARE DROWNED THEREIN.\par
\par
\par
All the details of events leading through the long Summer and Autumn\par
days of 1872 up to the hour when the golden shower began to fall on us\par
are of intense, almost dramatic, interest. I will not, however, lengthen\par
the narrative by giving here any further account of them, but will\par
merely relate the story of the last five days before the actual\par
presentation of our home-brewed acceptances.\par
\par
The bank had been discounting for weeks comparatively large sums for me.\par
Many thousand pounds of the genuine article discounted had matured and\par
been paid, and more thousands were still in the vaults, awaiting\par
maturity, and would fall due, while our home-manufactured bills would be\par
laid away in the vaults, there to remain for four or five months until\par
due. Of course a full month or two months before that we could pack our\par
baggage and be on the other side of the world; I on some hacienda in\par
Mexico, George and Mac at some fashionable resort in Florida. They soon\par
to knock at the gates of the Four Hundred, I to spend a year or two in\par
Mexico, playing "grand senor," until, under the skillful management of\par
our friends, Irving, Stanley and White, at Police Headquarters in New\par
York, the affair had blown over, and they invited me to return.\par
\par
But, as the sequel will show, the reality took on a different complexion\par
from the ideal.\par
\par
[Illustration: BOW STREET POLICE STATION.]\par
\par
My credit at the bank was solid as a rock. That means I had gone through\par
the red-tape routine. It only behooved us to use circumspection enough\par
to avoid making mistakes in our papers, and fortune was ours. I knew\par
everything was all right, but George, being a thorough business man\par
himself, could not comprehend that it could be quite right, and he\par
insisted upon one supreme test. Any single bill of exchange is seldom\par
drawn for more than L1,000, rarely for L2,000, and one of L6,000 is\par
almost unheard of. If a party in Bombay wanted exchange on London for\par
L100,000, his broker would probably furnish him with one hundred bills\par
for L1,000 each. But George had made up his mind that as a test, and to\par
make an impression upon the bank manager, I should go to Paris and get a\par
bill on London from Rothschilds drawn to the order of F. A. Warren\par
direct. Could this be done it would, of course, make it appear that I\par
had intimate relations with the Rothschilds, and as a minor\par
consideration we could use the Rothschild acceptance--a pretty nervy\par
thing to do, as Sir Anthony de Rothschild, the head of the London house,\par
whose name we proposed to offer, was a director of the Bank of England,\par
and would have to pass his own paper for discount--that is, paper\par
bearing his name, manufactured by ourselves.\par
\par
We tried to talk George out of this notion, which Mac and I regarded as\par
a freak, unnecessary in the first place, and impossible anyhow. But he\par
was persistent, and I had to start out and try. I expected an expense of\par
$1,000 and a delay of two weeks, but fortune or the devil favored us.\par
So, purchasing at the exchange broker's in London 200,000 francs in\par
French paper money, once more I left Victoria Station for Paris. Once\par
more, an unwilling victim, I heard the "wild, fantastic, fitful note of\par
Triton's breathing shell." At Calais I took my place in what the French\par
call a coupe; that is, the end compartment on a car, which, by paying\par
ten francs extra, you can occupy alone. It is unlike the other\par
compartments in that there are no arms dividing it into seats; so one\par
can lie full length on the cushion.\par
\par
Before this night I speak of I had cherished a theory as to what I\par
should do in the event of an accident happening to any train whereon I\par
was a passenger. In such a case I proposed to catch on to some object\par
and hold on, leaving my body and limbs to swing freely. My theory ever\par
since that night has been that I will go just wherever the breaking\par
timbers and flying furniture send me. I had fallen into a sound sleep\par
before the train started, and was aroused from it to find myself hurled\par
about the compartment much as a stout boy would shake a mouse in a cage,\par
and quite as helpless.\par
\par
Our train was off the track. My carriage was near the engine, and the\par
momentum of the long train forced the car in the rear of mine up on end,\par
and it appeared as if it would fall over and crush me. I thought my hour\par
had come, and I cried out, "At last!" There was no fear or terror in it,\par
but merely the thought that after many months of almost incessant\par
travel, and necessarily of peril, "at last" my fate had come. It had\par
not. How good heaven would have been if it had sent me to my doom then\par
and there!\par
\par
The accident had occurred at Marquise, a small town sixteen miles from\par
Calais and four from Boulogne, the first stopping place of the express.\par
It was a very long train, but the carriages were all empty except two. A\par
heavy excursion train had left Paris, and the cars were going back\par
empty. What lessened the number of passengers was the fact that it was\par
Sunday night. The English do not travel on Sundays as a rule. So,\par
fortunately, a great loss of life was prevented. However, two were\par
killed and half of the remaining passengers injured. My own injuries\par
were slight and consisted of trifling cuts on the face and hands from\par
flying glass. But, far worse than that, I had received a nervous shock,\par
which took some weeks to wear off, and during the rest of my journey to\par
Paris and return to London I was as nervous as a timid woman. I stayed\par
at Marquise until noon, when the express passing at that hour made a\par
special stop to pick me up.\par
\par
In our glorious and free country the killing or mangling of a few\par
persons more or less is of no particular concern to any one beyond the\par
friends of the victims, least of all to the railway magnate or to his\par
servant. But in France an accident which results even in the wounding of\par
a passenger is a very serious matter to the road where it occurs and to\par
its officials. They always hasten to take the fullest responsibility,\par
and if attention or the more solid matter--cash--can comfort the\par
sufferer, he will have no occasion to mourn long. If one life be\par
lost--even a servant of the road--a strict judicial inquiry takes place\par
upon the scene of the accident, by a high official of the State, advised\par
by experts, not as in this country, by some drunken country loafer or\par
ward heeler, who, all ignorant of the law, has been "elected" county\par
coroner, and one who is more anxious to procure free passes on the road\par
than he is concerned for the victim murdered by the neglect or parsimony\par
of inefficient railway officials.\par
\par
The road from Paris to Calais is known as the Chemin de Fer du Nord, and\par
Baron Alphonse de Rothschild, head of the Paris Rothschilds, is the\par
president of the road. This fact occurred to me within a few minutes of\par
the accident, and I thought I might make use of the affair as a means to\par
help me in my business at Paris. I arrived about dark, went to the Grand\par
Hotel, and to bed at once. My nerves were so shaken that I was timid,\par
even when in the elevator, but I slept well and awoke at daylight\par
feeling better.\par
\par
At 10 o'clock, limping badly and leaning on a cane, I entered a carriage\par
and drove to the Maison Rothschild, Rue Lafitte. The banking house might\par
well be called a palace. The various offices open upon a courtyard,\par
while the whole architecture of the building would suggest the residence\par
of an officer of State or nobleman rather than a building devoted to\par
finance. But the currents which centre there are potent and\par
far-reaching, and come richly laden with tribute from the four quarters\par
of the world. To win that tribute slaves toil, and, toiling, die, in\par
Brazilian diamond mines, and thousands of coolies, entrapped by agents\par
in China and India, enter into perfidious contracts which commit them to\par
hopeless slavery and send them to wear out their lives in despairing\par
toil amid the pungent and murderous ammoniacal fumes of the guano\par
islands of Chili and Peru. The Rothschilds, too, own the Almaden\par
quicksilver mine and others.\par
\par
They control the quicksilver industries of the world, and to swell their\par
abnormal hoard, portentous in its vastness, other poor wretches,\par
condemned under form of law, are doomed to days of wearing toil, and,\par
their bones rotting from quicksilver absorption, to nights of racking\par
pains. So, too, far Siberia contributes its quota of human misery that\par
the golden stream of interest on century-old loans may have no\par
interruption, but pour on unceasingly into the vaults of the\par
Rothschilds.\par
\par
Alighting from the carriage and mounting the steps with difficulty, I\par
entered the English Department, and, seating myself, awaited the\par
manager's presence. He came, and expressing great concern when he\par
learned I was a victim of the Marquise disaster, asked what he could do\par
for me. I replied I wanted to see the Baron. He disappeared into a range\par
of offices, and no doubt told Baron Alphonse I was some important\par
personage, doubly important because injured on his road.\par
\par
Soon a slight, sallow man of about 43 appeared, wearing an old-fashioned\par
stovepipe hat and a shabby suit of snuff-colored garments. The look of\par
the attendants testified that the deity was before me. Taking off his\par
antiquated chapeau he began a profuse apology for the accident,\par
explaining that accidents were most unusual events in France; that he\par
would order his own physician to attend me, that I should have every\par
attention without the slightest charge or expense to myself, etc., etc.,\par
and ended by saying I was to command him if he could serve me. In return\par
I told him since he was so distressed over the accident and my plight, I\par
should say no more about either, but as I was too badly shaken to\par
complete the business on which I had come to Paris I should request him\par
to instruct his subordinates to aid me in transmitting the funds I had\par
brought from London back again. He called the manager and told him to\par
accommodate me in anything, then, shaking hands and with many\par
expressions of regret, he withdrew.\par
\par
I told the manager I wanted a three months' bill on London for L6,000.\par
He informed me that the house of Rothschild was not issuing time bills,\par
but since the Baron's order suspended the rule in my case, he would\par
procure me six bills for L1,000 each. These really were just as good for\par
our purpose as one bill for L6,000, but I had come to Paris on George's\par
demand that I should procure one bill for this unusual amount, so\par
perforce I had to say "No," that I wanted one bill only.\par
\par
The manager began to remonstrate, saying it was unusual, and wanted to\par
explain the nature of a bill of exchange, but I cut him short, bidding\par
him recall the Baron at once. The thought of recalling that Jupiter to\par
repeat an order was enough to send a thrill through the entire staff,\par
and he instantly said: "Oh, sir, if you wish the L6,000 in one bill, you\par
shall have it, but it will involve some delay." So paying him 150,000\par
francs on account, I ordered the bill sent to me at 2 o'clock precisely\par
at the Grand Hotel, and drove off to the Louvre, where I spent two hours\par
in the picture galleries. At 2 o'clock I was at the hotel, and an\par
attendant came with the bill, and, pointing to a signature on it,\par
informed me it was that of a Cabinet Minister, equivalent to our\par
Secretary of the Treasury, certifying that the tax due the government\par
on the bill was paid. He explained the revenue stamp required upon a\par
bill of exchange was one-eighth of 1 per cent. of the face of the bill,\par
making the tax on my single bill 187 francs, or about $37. All bills are\par
stamped in a registering machine, which presses the stamp into the\par
paper; but there were no registering machines for a stamp of so high a\par
denomination as 187 francs either in the branch revenue office in the\par
Rothschild bank or at the Treasury, so the Baron had taken the bill to\par
the Treasury himself and got the Cabinet Minister to put his autograph\par
on it--probably the first and only time in history that such a thing had\par
been done. I wanted very much indeed to keep that bill as a curiosity,\par
but then the necessity of the time was on me, and I was not then a\par
collector of curios.\par
\par
I had been only eighteen hours in Paris, and by a happy fluke the\par
business was done over which I had counted upon spending a good part of\par
the month.\par
\par
When I left London I was all at sea as to how I should carry out the\par
objects of my visit to Paris. One plan was to procure an interview by\par
strategy with the Baron Alphonse and try to cajole him, but without\par
reference, and devoid of all business relations or acquaintance in\par
Paris, it was at best a questionable expedient, and I probably would\par
have had a take-down. But the accident at Marquise came and smoothed the\par
apparently insuperable difficulties in my way. But I have found that\par
something unusual does come to help a man on his way to the devil when\par
he is anxious to get there, which he is pretty sure to do, if he is only\par
diligent and careful to improve his opportunities.\par
\par
What diligence and strict attention to business do men exhibit when they\par
start out to wreck their own lives and break the hearts of those near to\par
them! In a play by a modern writer, one scene presents Satan flying at\par
midnight over one of our cities, while the drunken songs and joyous\par
shouts of some gilded revelers rise in the night. The merry songs and\par
laughter are music to the ears of Lucifer. He pauses in his flight to\par
listen, and as the songs and shouts increase in volume he looks down on\par
the revelers and with a bitter sneer soliloquizes thus of them:\par
\par
  "Ye are my bondsmen and my thralls,\par
  Your lives I fill with bitter pain."\par
\par
And that sums it up pretty well; but we must look straight away from the\par
entrance of the Primrose Way to the exit.\par
\par
Well, I had successfully played my trump card on the Rothschilds, and,\par
not seeing the end, thought I had won, and cleverly won; so before\par
sitting down to dinner I went to the telegraph office and telegraphed to\par
my partners:\par
\par
     "The Egyptians all passed over the Red Sea. But the Hebrews are\par
     drowned therein."\par
\par
Thinking this rather witty, I went to dinner well satisfied. An hour\par
past midnight the moon looked from behind a cloud and saw me, one of\par
many miserables, leaning over the bulwark of that wretched Dover\par
steamer, again paying tribute to Neptune.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXII.\par
\par
"ACCEPTED. LIONEL ROTHSCHILD."\par
\par
\par
When George and Mac received my telegram they, knowing the difficulties\par
of my mission, deemed it incredible that I had succeeded within a day,\par
so when my telegram came they thought I was attempting some jest. Upon\par
my arrival in London, walking into Mac's room--he being still in bed--I\par
announced that I had in my pocket Rothschild's bill for L6,000, drawn on\par
the London house. He flatly refused to believe me, but when he, and\par
later George, saw the bill, they were forced to believe. I at once took\par
it down to St. Swithin's lane, and, leaving it for acceptance, called\par
the next day, when I found scrawled across it in thin, pale ink the\par
mystic words "Accepted. Lionel Rothschild."\par
\par
The bill itself was drawn on cheap, blue paper, on the same form as the\par
blank bills to be had at the Paris stationers', where I had bought some.\par
From Rothschilds' I went direct to the hotel where we had our\par
rendezvous, and the acceptance was so simple and easy that Mac had it\par
copied on another bill in ten minutes. The business methods of the bank\par
were so loose that there was no necessity for imitating signatures, but\par
as a precaution this was done to some extent. I then proceeded to the\par
Bank of England for my last personal interview with the manager. I must\par
halt here for a brief space in the narrative, in order to enlighten my\par
reader upon some new developments, also to introduce the new member we\par
at this time brought into our firm.\par
\par
[Illustration: "NOYES ESCORTED BY AN ANGRY MOB TO NEWGATE."--Page 379]\par
\par
There was a friend, a very old friend, of mine residing in Hartford,\par
Edwin Noyes by name. We had known each other from our schoolboy days,\par
and there was a warm friendship between us. Our paths in life had been\par
wide apart, but we maintained a frequent correspondence and often met.\par
He knew nothing of my primrose life, but supposed, of course, from the\par
style of my living that I was the possessor of a handsome income from my\par
business, which lay, as he imagined, in that mysterious precinct known\par
as "The Street," which, of course, meant Wall street, and that my\par
business was speculating in stocks.\par
\par
He was a trifle older than myself, of a steady, reserved nature, and a\par
discreet and safe friend. This was the new member of our firm. How he\par
came to be so I must explain. Up to this time, as the reader will have\par
noticed, I was the only one of the party known at the bank, and, of\par
course, was the only one who seemed to be taking any risk. Even in the\par
event of discovery it would apparently be necessary for me only to take\par
flight. George and Mac, not being known in connection with the fraud,\par
could remain in London until such time as they chose to go home. To make\par
matters absolutely safe for me as well we got up this scheme.\par
\par
I told the manager of the bank that I had bought an immense plant and\par
shops in Birmingham to manufacture railway material, and that I should\par
be there superintending the work a good deal; therefore I might\par
occasionally send any bills I had for discount from there by mail. I had\par
sent two or three lots of the genuine bills in that way. If I could send\par
the imitation bills the same way, Mac and George could carry on the\par
business through the mail in my name and I could be at the other side of\par
the world while the actual operation was going on, so that, far from my\par
ever being proved guilty, there would be proof of my innocence, for how\par
could I be guilty of a crime committed in England at the very time I was\par
on a pleasure jaunt in the West Indies and Mexico? Thus it was\par
arranged. Mac and George could do everything and remain in the\par
background themselves, provided we had a safe man whom I could introduce\par
at the bank as my clerk or messenger, also to represent me in different\par
places where I could introduce him as my messenger before I left\par
England.\par
\par
The reader will see the extreme artfulness of the plot, but in all\par
wrongdoing there is sooner or later a slip up. Be the plot ever so\par
artful, or however safe the wrongdoing may appear, the unforeseen\par
something will happen.\par
\par
Of course, Mac and George not being known at the bank need not care, but\par
it might easily be serious for me.\par
\par
When the explosion came, fifty people in and about the bank would\par
remember my face. But if I brought Noyes on the scene to act as my clerk\par
I need only introduce him to the paying teller of the bank, and to Jay\par
Cooke & Co., the American banking house, where I proposed to buy\par
enormous quantities of United States bonds, paying for them in checks on\par
the Bank of England. Of course, the bonds being all bearer bonds, would,\par
with our knowledge of finance, be as good as so much cash.\par
\par
So, knowing Noyes, if he would embark in the enterprise, had plenty of\par
nerve and could never be bribed or bought into betraying us should he by\par
any failure of our plans happen to be arrested, we determined to send\par
for him. A short time before we arrived at this conclusion I had sent\par
this precautionary letter to him:\par
\par
                                                  "Grosvenor Hotel,\par
                                             "London, Nov. 8, 1872.\par
\par
"My Dear Noyes: You will be surprised to hear from me from London, but\par
the fact is I have been here with George and a friend of ours for a\par
year, and have made a lot of money from several speculations we have\par
embarked in. In fact, we have been so successful that we have determined\par
to make you a present of a thousand dollars, which find inclosed. Please\par
accept the same with our best wishes.\par
\par
"We may be able to give you a chance to make a few thousands, if you\par
would care to venture across the ocean. Perhaps we can make use of you.\par
If so, I will send you a cable. If I do, come any way, as we will pay\par
all your expenses should you determine not to go in with us on the deal.\par
Be cautious and preserve absolute secrecy when you leave home as to your\par
destination. Will explain the reason for this when we meet. Keep your\par
weather eye open for the cable. It may come any hour after you have\par
this.\par
\par
      "Hoping you are quite well, I remain," etc., etc.\par
\par
       *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
A few days later we sent him this cable (it was afterward produced in\par
court in evidence against him): "Edwin Noyes, New York. Come by Atlantic\par
on Wednesday; wire on arrival at Liverpool; meet at Langham."\par
\par
He arrived ten days later, and at a little dinner given in his honor we\par
told him our plot. He was astounded, and for the remainder of the\par
dinner, and for the day, too, for the matter of that, he acted like a\par
man in a dream, and we three were amazed that he did not instantly fall\par
into our plan.\par
\par
Here was the dramatic representation of the poisonous effect of\par
wrongdoing. We three had by degrees become accustomed to look upon a\par
fraud committed by ourselves with equanimity. I say by degrees.\par
Insensibly we had been sinking deeper and deeper, until, our moral\par
senses blunted, we found excuses to our own consciences. But here was my\par
companion and friend; he was no Puritan, but, like ourselves but a few\par
brief months before, regarded crime with detestation, and now, when the\par
men he trusted proposed he should become a party to a crime, his mind\par
revolted in horror. Well for him had he yielded to the prompting of his\par
own conscience and fled from us and the fearful temptation of sudden\par
wealth. At last he said he would consider it. After a day or two of\par
silence he began to question us as to our mode of operation, then his\par
mind became more and more familiarized to the thought, until at last,\par
fascinated by our association, he acquiesced, saying: "I will do it. I\par
want money badly. The Bank of England, after all, will not miss it. So\par
I'll go in for this once."\par
\par
By our direction he went to an obscure hotel in Manchester square, and\par
then purchased clothes more suitable for his new position than the\par
fashionable tailor-cut suit he wore from New York.\par
\par
On several occasions I had gone to Jay Cooke & Co. in Lombard street and\par
purchased bonds under the name of F. A. Warren and giving checks in\par
payment upon the Bank of England. So one day I went there with Noyes and\par
purchased $20,000 in bonds, giving my check for them. I then introduced\par
Noyes as my clerk, directing them to deliver any bonds I bought to him\par
at any time. The next day he called and they gave him the bonds which I\par
had given my check for the day before, so there was no necessity any\par
longer for me to come in person to make purchases. Noyes could appear\par
there any day, give an order for bonds, secure a bill for them, and in\par
half an hour bring a Warren check for the amount of the bill,\par
pretending, of course, that he had got it from me, but really getting it\par
from Mac, leaving the check for collection and to call the next day for\par
the bonds.\par
\par
The same day that I introduced him to Jay Cooke & Co. I took him to the\par
Bank of England at a busy time of day, and while drawing L2,000, I\par
casually introduced him to the paying teller as my clerk, requesting the\par
teller to pay him any checks I sent. Then for the next few days I had\par
Noyes take checks to the bank and had him order two or three small lots\par
of bonds from Jay Cooke & Co., so that they became familiarized with\par
seeing him come on my business.\par
\par
[Illustration: "I DEMAND A GUARD AND SHELTER FOR MY WIFE, THE\par
DUCHESS."--Page 282.]\par
\par
The plan was complete at last. Everything was ready to carry out our\par
scheme in perfect safety to all, and, as related in the beginning of the\par
chapter, I was now on my way to the bank for my last visit, with the\par
Rothschild bill in my hand. Many accounts were given of this famous\par
interview in the English press just after the discovery of the fraud\par
and prior to my arrest, also when the details transpired at the trial.\par
The facts were simply these: I presented myself at the bank, and,\par
sending in my card to the manager, was ushered at once into his parlor.\par
After a few remarks upon the money and stock market, I produced the\par
bill, remarking that I had a curiosity to show him which had been sent\par
me by a correspondent in Paris. It was certainly a curiosity; it was a\par
thing entirely unknown in the history of the bank to have a bill of\par
exchange bearing the signature of a Cabinet Minister certifying that the\par
internal revenue tax had been paid on it. This, along with the\par
circumstance that the bill was made payable to myself, evidently made\par
considerable impression on the manager and confirmed him in his good\par
opinion of his customer. The unusual features of this bill of exchange\par
led him to relate some of the inner events of the bank's history, during\par
which I asked him what precaution the bank took against forgery. He told\par
me a forgery on the bank was impossible. But I asked: "Why impossible?\par
Other banks get hit sometimes, and why not the Bank of England?" To that\par
question he gave a long reply, ending with the assertion that "our wise\par
forefathers have bequeathed us a system which is perfect." "Do you wish\par
me to understand you have not changed your system since your\par
forefathers' time?" I said. To which he emphatically replied: "Not in\par
the slightest particular for a hundred years." In conclusion I told him\par
I should be fully occupied looking after my different business\par
interests, but would give him a call if I found time. I also said I\par
would have the bill discounted and take the cash away with me, instead\par
of having it placed to my credit. He called an attendant, gave the\par
necessary order, and the cash was handed me. Bidding the manager\par
good-bye, I repaired to our meeting place and showed the notes for the\par
discounted bill. Even George was satisfied that my credit at the bank\par
was good for any amount of discounts on any sort of paper.\par
\par
Everything now was ready for my departure from England. For some weeks\par
my partners had been busy preparing for the completion of the operation.\par
\par
The first lot of bogus bills were ready to go into the mail at\par
Birmingham as soon as I was out of the way--it having been decided that\par
I should then be out of the country. So one Monday late in November I\par
packed my baggage, and, after many warm hand shakings, I bade my friends\par
adieu. We had had many talks about the happy future. We had planned\par
pleasant things in the future, and spoken confidently of our\par
four-in-hands, our Summer cottages at Saratoga and Newport, of our town\par
house, fine suppers and our boxes at the opera. After that I saw them\par
for a brief hour on the coast of France and once more said adieu. When\par
we met again it was in Newgate. I need hardly say that for the next\par
twenty years we had no boxes at the grand opera, no four-in-hands, nor\par
yet any fine suppers, but all that which was merely external passed\par
away, consumed in that fierce flame, but all that was manly and true\par
remained; that is, our devotion and courage and our high resolve to\par
conquer fate and live for better things.\par
\par
Before leaving London we had squared up our cash account. It was\par
something to make one stare to see how our money had melted away. It was\par
arranged to send in the first lot of bogus bills on Thursday, giving me\par
two full days out of the country. Here I made a fatal mistake in\par
determining to go to the West Indies, then on to Mexico. As George had\par
planned I should have gone at once to New York, stopped at the best\par
hotel in the city and registered in my right name. By taking this course\par
I should have been safe and could have laughed at any attempt of the\par
bank authorities to extradite me, for the first lot of bogus bills could\par
have been held back until I had actually arrived in America. Then there\par
could not have been found a single particle of evidence against me.\par
\par
I say "if I had come to New York." But there is some mysterious spell\par
over men embarked in crime that blinds their eyes to the plainest\par
dictates of common sense or prudence. This has been proved in a thousand\par
dramatic instances, but never more forcibly than in our own. It would\par
seem as if clever, daring men do almost impossible things with ease, but\par
there is a Nemesis which blinds them to trifles, fatal if overlooked,\par
causing them to make mistakes of which a schoolboy would be ashamed.\par
\par
When we first got our combination together I thought we had found a\par
short cut to fortune, and never doubted of our success to the very end,\par
and amid many mishaps, that either crippled or ruined our schemes and\par
lengthened this short cut to fortune, I maintained my confidence until\par
on that day down in blazing Rio, when the letter "c" in lieu of the "s"\par
in indorse came to the front to crumble our "sure thing" into ruin. I\par
remember that in the stupefaction which for a few minutes settled down\par
on us, I felt we were really fighting against fate. A fate that like the\par
fiat of Deity says "Thou shalt not," to all wrongdoing.\par
\par
For some time after that "indorce" takedown a feeling took possession of\par
me that such short cuts to fortune were risky, and that if success did\par
come the success would in the end prove a failure. But there is so much\par
in companionship and such magnetism in human association that when we\par
all three met in Paris and went in and out together, then, under the\par
stimulus of our union, I forgot all my forebodings and began to think\par
the unforeseen fatal something would not happen, and that we could\par
conquer fortune whether she would or no, and by any method on which we\par
chose to enter. But, as will be seen in the sequel, when reveling in an\par
unheard-of success, literally loaded down with wealth, Nemesis appeared\par
and by means even more simple than our error in Rio stripped us of our\par
wealth and dignity and left us naked to every storm that blew.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXIII.\par
\par
SHOWERS OF GOLD FALL--AND THEN?\par
\par
\par
I shall try and condense into a single chapter the narrative of events\par
in London from the time of my departure until the day, some months\par
later, when our scheme exploded and all took to flight when Noyes was\par
arrested.\par
\par
Our expenses had been so enormous that we were anxious to make enough to\par
recoup them, so it had been agreed that the first batch of bogus bills\par
should not exceed the amount paid out since leaving Rio.\par
\par
I left for Paris on Monday. On Wednesday, Noyes went to the bank and\par
drew out all the money to my credit, except three hundred pounds. The\par
same day he went to Birmingham and mailed lot number one of\par
home-manufacture bills representing L8,000.\par
\par
The next twenty-four hours was an anxious time for my friends. The bills\par
would be delivered by the early mail on Thursday, and if all went right\par
the proceeds would be placed to my credit by 12 o'clock, and the bills\par
themselves would be stowed away in the vaults until they were due some\par
months ahead. George and Mac waited with the greatest anxiety until 2\par
o'clock. They had everything packed for instant flight, when at that\par
hour they sallied out of Mac's lodging and started for the bank to make\par
the test. They had filled out two Warren checks, one for L2,300 payable\par
to Warren, another for L4 10s., payable to bearer.\par
\par
Noyes went on ahead, the others following, and took his stand on the\par
steps of a hotel in a side street not far from the bank. Keeping his\par
eye out for a suitable appearing party he finally stopped a uniformed\par
messenger, and, telling him to take the L4 10s. check to the bank, bring\par
the money to him there, and he would be paid for his trouble.\par
\par
Of course, as soon as the messenger had turned his back Noyes bolted\par
around the corner to a place agreed upon, while Mac followed the\par
messenger to the bank and saw he was paid without question. He gave the\par
pre-arranged signal to George, who went with all haste to notify Noyes,\par
and when the messenger arrived with the cash, he found him standing on\par
the steps as cool and unconcerned as possible. Paying the messenger, all\par
three started to the bank, Mac on the way giving Noyes the L2,300 check,\par
which he presented. Nodding good day to the cashier he asked for L2,000\par
in gold and the remainder in notes, which were handed him at once, and\par
three very happy men sat down that evening to dinner, because the day's\par
operations had conclusively proved that the Bank of England methods were\par
fallible.\par
\par
The next morning Noyes went to Jay Cooke & Co. and ordered $75,000 in\par
United States bonds, giving a check for them on the bank. The same\par
afternoon he went to Birmingham and mailed another letter, this one\par
containing L15,000 in bills, and later drew L2,000 in gold from the\par
bank. On Monday he went after the bonds, and the $75,000 was handed over\par
to him without questions. The whole operation was a repetition of these\par
tactics, but with an ever-increasing volume in the amounts of the bills.\par
On some days the mail brought to the bank letters with bills for\par
$100,000, sometimes for more, sometimes for less. So November and\par
December passed away, and the bank continued day by day and week by week\par
laying away in its vaults the worthless collateral of Mr. F. A. Warren in\par
exchange for its gold.\par
\par
But why not be satisfied and stop while it was all right? That is the\par
question of a wise man, but who ever knew any man who wanted to do a\par
thing, whether he did it or not, who could not find half a hundred good\par
reasons why he should do it. But as Christmas came near Mac began to\par
long for home. He had repaid his father every penny of the large sum he\par
was owing him; there had been a reconciliation by mail, and each steamer\par
that came bore many long letters from parents and sisters, all speaking\par
of their joy over the happy turn of events that was going to bring the\par
absent member of the flock home within its walls again. The father's\par
heart, long estranged, grew very tender toward his boy, and with pride\par
he thought his eldest had thrown off the follies of his youth, and in\par
manful strength was making ample atonements for the thoughtlessness and\par
the wanderings of his youth. He and they were all destined to a terrible\par
awakening. For soon the press of the world was to teem with accounts of\par
his son's arrest and incarceration for participation in a gigantic\par
fraud. When the blow fell it came with crushing force on that home, and\par
a shadow deep as night settled down on the household; all joyousness and\par
even hope itself fled when the cable bore the news that their boy had\par
been condemned to life imprisonment in a foreign dungeon. And one by one\par
the members of that family passed away from a world that held no more\par
for them since their good name had been tarnished.\par
\par
In London the boys talked of spending Christmas at home, but the\par
argument to stay--and it prevailed--was that since the money came in so\par
easily and in such amounts it was a pity to run away from it. Then,\par
again, by obtaining an enormous sum and putting it in a place of\par
absolute security, the bank would be glad to compromise the matter in\par
consideration of receiving a million or two back again.\par
\par
So they spent a pretty merry and an exceedingly expensive Christmas in\par
London, but later in February they determined to pack up and leave.\par
\par
Everything smiled upon them. The gold and bonds they had, meant fortunes\par
for all. I was away in tropic islands leading an idle life with my\par
bride amid the cocoanut and palm trees. Mac and George had never\par
appeared in the transaction, and as for Noyes, not a soul in all America\par
knew he was in Europe, and in all Europe only three or four people had\par
seen him, and knew him as representing Warren.\par
\par
The business was finished. All three laden with money were going to\par
leave England, leaving the bank to slumber on for weeks until the first\par
bills became due before there could be a discovery. By that time the\par
cash would have been safely stowed, and how or where or to whom could\par
anything be traced?\par
\par
So in council they had decided to be content with the enormous amount\par
they had. The last batch of bills was in the mail. Only one day more and\par
the strain on the nerves would be over. That day Noyes bought bonds and\par
drew cash for more than $150,000. At 3 o'clock they sat down to lunch,\par
their last in London, and then went direct to Mac's apartments in St.\par
James' place. All the material for making fraudulent bills was there,\par
and what could be burned was to be thrown into the grate, and the rest\par
to first be filed into blank nothings and then thrown into the Thames.\par
The three were there and they were happy. They had engineered a gigantic\par
scheme, had struck for wealth and won. The short cut to fortune in\par
defiance of fate had been traversed and now they set about a grateful\par
task--that of getting themselves and their rich argosy out of England.\par
Mac being the artist of the party, and having executed the actual\par
writing, drew the sealed box containing the unused bills up to the fire\par
and began throwing them in one by one. In doing so he occasionally would\par
throw some bill more elaborate than the common run on the floor beside\par
his chair. He had finished his task and took from the floor those he had\par
thrown there, looked at them for a moment, then crumbling them together,\par
raised his hand to throw them in the fire, but as the devil always\par
forsakes his friends at the critical moment, he stopped, smoothed out\par
the bills and turning to the others, said: "Boys, these are perfect\par
works of art; it is a pity to destroy them." From our point of view it\par
was, since it was only necessary to drop them into the mail and they\par
would coin us thousands. Then George said: "Suppose we send them in."\par
The others said "All right," and our doom was sealed.\par
\par
There were in the lot nineteen bills of exchange for L26,000. A date had\par
been left off one of them! They failed to note it! Poor fools, we had\par
sold ourselves.\par
\par
Was this an accident? No, it was Nemesis; it was anything you want to\par
call it, but it was not an accident.\par
\par
So a letter was written, the bills, with memorandum, inclosed, the\par
envelope directed and stamped, and the three fools went to Birmingham,\par
mailed the letter, and then laughed over their success in the fight\par
against society, facilitated themselves that they had discovered the\par
undiscoverable, that they had safely traversed the short cut to fortune.\par
There is no short cut by wrongdoing to fortune, Boss Tweed and the long\par
list of robber barons to the contrary!\par
\par
The bills were mailed on Monday. As that fatal letter slipped from their\par
fingers into the mail-box the last act of the deadly tragedy began. When\par
it ended the curtain fell upon us descending from the dock into the\par
chill dungeons of Newgate, never, so far as the sentence was concerned,\par
to emerge again.\par
\par
On Tuesday morning the letter with the bills arrived at the bank.\par
Following the routine, they went to the discount department, were\par
discounted and placed to my credit. As I had a balance of L20,000, when\par
the proceeds of the bills were added to it, it brought up the whole to\par
the handsome sum of L46,000.\par
\par
[Illustration: "THE DAY OF MY DESTINY IS OVER."--Page 304.]\par
\par
When the bills arrived at the bank a strange thing occurred. The fatal\par
omission was made on an acceptance of Blydenstein & Co., a great banking\par
firm in London. The discount clerk noticed the omission of the date of\par
acceptance, but this being a mere formality, he thought it a clerical\par
error on the part of the bookkeeper of Blydenstein & Co. He made no\par
report of the matter, and it was discounted along with the other\par
eighteen, which were put away in the vaults with the batches that had\par
preceded it, while he laid this one aside until the next day, which was\par
Wednesday. At half past ten he gave it to the bank messenger, telling\par
him when he went his regular rounds to take the bill to Blydenstein's\par
and request them to correct the omission.\par
\par
At 2 p.m. on Tuesday Noyes went to Jay Cooke & Co. and ordered $100,000\par
in United States bonds, and gave them a check on the Bank of England for\par
the amount. He was to call for the bonds next day, of course, after the\par
check had gone through the Clearing House and had been paid.\par
\par
As soon as the bank opened on Wednesday, in order to test if everything\par
was all right, Noyes sent in a messenger with a small check, and the\par
money was thrown out as at all other times without remark. And that was\par
a complete demonstration that everything was all right. So it was then,\par
but within thirty minutes from that second the messenger was going to\par
start with the bill to Blydenstein's for correction.\par
\par
This was 10 o'clock Wednesday. The bills had been twenty-five hours in\par
the possession of the bank, had been discounted and the proceeds placed\par
to my credit for twenty-four hours.\par
\par
Who with intellect less than an archangel's could have divined the true\par
combination? First of all, that men brilliant and clever, gambling with\par
their lives, could have made such an omission, damning, fatal. Second,\par
if made, that the great Bank of England, thought absolutely infallible\par
by the whole world, conservative, supposedly cautious, would have\par
discounted a bill for L20,00 with the date out of the acceptance, and\par
having done so, hold the bill well on into the second day, without a\par
discovery, and that, too, when the firm whose acceptance was a forgery\par
was not 100 yards away! So when at 10 o'clock on Wednesday Mac saw the\par
small check paid without question to the messenger it seemed he had an\par
assurance doubly sure and a bond of fate that all was well, and that the\par
last batch of bills was packed safely away for another three months in\par
the vaults of the bank.\par
\par
So Noyes went at once to Jay Cooke & Co., and as the check had been paid\par
at the bank they handed over, as in so many other occasions, the\par
$100,000 in bonds to him.\par
\par
Mac and George were outside. George took the bonds and gave Noyes a\par
L10,000 check, and one minute from his leaving Jay Cooke & Co., Noyes\par
was at the counter of the bank. The cashier counted out the $50,000 to\par
him. He walked out of the bank with a lighter heart and more buoyant\par
step than ever before, for was not the danger all over and the long\par
strain on the nerves at an end, the transaction complete and fortune\par
won? He was never going to the bank again.\par
\par
They had arranged to meet at Garraway's Coffee House in Exchange alley.\par
This is the Garraway's that became so famous at the time of the South\par
Sea Bubble, and its fame continued down to the end of the wars of\par
Napoleon. Then its glory departed as a centre of speculations, but its\par
renown as an old-fashioned chophouse remained till 1873. Everywhere in\par
contemporary English literature, from Swift and Addison to Goldsmith and\par
Johnson, one meets references to Garraway's.\par
\par
The Dean immortalized it in his well-known lines on 'Change Alley:\par
\par
  "There is a gulf where thousands fell,\par
    Here all the bold adventurers came,\par
  A narrow sound, though deep as hell,\par
    'Change Alley is the dreadful name.\par
\par
  "Subscribers here by thousands float\par
    And jostle one another down.\par
  Each paddling in his leaky boat,\par
    And here they fish for gold and drown.\par
\par
  "Meantime secure on Garraway's cliffs\par
    A savage race by shipwreck fed,\par
  Lie waiting for the foundered skiffs\par
    And strip the bodies of the dead."\par
\par
Dickens also makes it the scene of the writing of the famous chops and\par
tomato sauce letter from Mr. Pickwick to Mrs. Bardell.\par
\par
One can imagine the elation of my friends as they sat around that little\par
table at Garraway's. It was only 10:35. Their income that morning had\par
been $150,000. And many more such days had gone before. All danger was\par
over, wealth was won. They saw themselves back in America, among the\par
Four Hundred, possessors of a fortune, however wrongfully obtained, yet\par
obtained in a way that would leave behind no ruined widows and orphans\par
to linger out the remainder of their blighted lives in poverty and\par
misery. That was a point which added zest to their enjoyment of the\par
prospect.\par
\par
"I am never to go to the bank again. Come, shake hands on that," said\par
Noyes. And in their excitement and wild delight they shook hands again\par
and again.\par
\par
But they would have moderated their joy had they known that at the very\par
moment the bank porter, pale and frightened, was rushing past the room\par
where they sat, carrying the news to the bank that the two-thousand\par
pound bill was a forgery. Instantly all was confusion and excitement in\par
the bank. Telegrams were at once sent to the detective police, and at\par
that moment swarms of them were pouring out of the Bow street and\par
Scotland Yard offices.\par
\par
That already stories of gigantic frauds, multiplied a thousand fold by\par
rumor, were flying everywhere that every bank in London was victimized.\par
In ten minutes the story reached the Stock Exchange and a scene of\par
terrific excitement ensued, and, through it all, our three innocents sat\par
on in that dingy old coffee-house, serenely unconscious of the fearful\par
storm that was rising. Still they were safe. Everything was confusion in\par
the bank. The terrified official, frantic with fear, could only describe\par
a tall young man, an American, who said his name was Warren.\par
\par
Had my three triumphant friends only known what was up they might have\par
sat where they were the day through and drank porter out of the pewter\par
mugs in safety. There were a hundred thousand men in London who would\par
answer any description the bank could have given of Noyes, Mac and\par
George had never appeared in the transaction, and I, the F. A. Warren\par
they were looking for, was living quietly with my young wife in a lovely\par
isle in the tropic sea.\par
\par
Surely then, these three high-toned financiers still had the game in\par
their own hands. They had nothing to fear. They had wealth. There was no\par
clue to their identity and the world was before them--a world which lays\par
her treasures and pleasures at the feet of him who commands wealth.\par
\par
But that mighty Something had decreed otherwise, and a subtle spirit\par
under whose power they were but purposeless puppets inspired them to\par
commit an act of folly which was to hurl them from the fools' paradise\par
wherein they were reveling down to the pit of despair.\par
\par
Upon Mac casually remarking that they had still a balance of $75,000 to\par
Warren's credit, Noyes spoke up and said: "Boys, that is too much money\par
to leave John Bull; suppose you make out a check for L5,000. I will run\par
over and get the cash, and it will do for pocket money." And the two\par
others, triumphant in success, became idiots and assented. Making out a\par
check for L5,000, Noyes started for the bank, check in hand, and\par
entering, instantly found himself with a hot and angry swarm of hornets\par
about him.\par
\par
[Illustration: A NEWGATE SCENE.--DON'T WANT HIS PICTURE TAKEN.]\par
\par
There were twenty-five detectives in and around the bank. Special\par
messengers had summoned the affrighted directors. The great bank parlor\par
was packed with a host of stockholders and directors, who were\par
questioning the manager and clerks. And excitement rose to fever heat\par
when, with twenty hands holding him, poor Noyes was hustled in among\par
them. They rushed at him like a pack of wolves. Had that been a bank\par
parlor in festive Arizona, they would not have endured the delay\par
incidental to procuring a rope, but would have ended it and him by\par
gunnery at short range. Noyes could not be shaken; his nerve never\par
failed. He said a gentleman had hired him as a clerk, and that was all\par
he knew. He had left him at the Stock Exchange; if they would let him\par
go, he would try and find him and bring him around to the bank. J. Bull\par
is gullible, but not so much so as to swallow that yarn.\par
\par
So they held tightly to him, and a committee of indignant Britons\par
escorted him to Newgate.\par
\par
[Illustration: A SENTRY.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXIV.\par
\par
POINTS FOR JUSTICE TO PICK UP.\par
\par
\par
Mac and George were without, and were stricken with consternation, for a\par
minute's observation of the gathering crowd and the rushing into the\par
bank of excited people convinced them something unusual was in the wind,\par
and they knew Noyes must be in deadly peril. Mac rushed into the bank in\par
hope to warn or to be of help. Everything there was in confusion.\par
Unobserved in the excitement, he made his way into the parlor and there\par
saw what made his heart stand still--Noyes surrounded by an angry crowd\par
of officials. With great presence of mind and great nerve he pushed\par
through toward Noyes, who saw him and knew he was there to help if he\par
had a chance to bolt from his captors; but there was no chance. As they\par
were about starting for Newgate, Mac slipped outside and told George\par
what had befallen Noyes, and discussed the possibility of a rescue when\par
on the way to Newgate with him. While they were waiting in the entrance\par
Noyes came out in custody. He saw and recognized them. They joined in\par
the crowd and were within arm's reach of him every rod of the short\par
distance to Newgate, but the crowd was packed so tight that one could\par
hardly move, and a rush for escape was hopeless. Arrived at Newgate, Mac\par
in his desperation was entering with the escort, when George pulled him\par
away, and as they got out of the crowd they heard the newsboys crying:\par
"Great forgery on the Bank of England by an American; L10,000,000\par
obtained." That afternoon Lionel Rothschild, president of the Board of\par
Directors, called on him at Newgate, and offered him his liberty and\par
L1,000 reward if he would tell all he knew; but Noyes' nerve was not to\par
be shaken. He said a gentleman, an entire stranger, had hired him as a\par
clerk and messenger, and he knew nothing about Mr. Warren nor his\par
business.\par
\par
[Illustration: "NOYES WAS SURROUNDED BY AN ANGRY CROWD OF\par
OFFICIALS."--Page 236.]\par
\par
All this time the $150,000 drawn that morning was in a stout bag behind\par
the counter at Garraway's.\par
\par
Little did the barmaids dream of the treasure that was in the bag at\par
their feet. When Mac went for it, one of the barmaids asked him if he\par
had heard of the great bank robbery. He drove to St James' place, and\par
soon George joined him there.\par
\par
Here again was enacted the scene we had in Rio; as there, so here, they\par
looked at each other in helpless stupefaction. Why had they not been\par
satisfied? Why had they let Noyes go for a paltry L5,000? Why had they\par
not understood the meaning of the evident excitement in and around the\par
bank?\par
\par
In Rio there was only a suspicion aroused. Here our companion was a\par
prisoner in Newgate. Scarcely an hour had passed since he was free and\par
without a fear had joined in the congratulatory scene at Garraway's. Now\par
ruin was threatened. Upon cool reflection they came to two conclusions.\par
First, that Noyes not only would never betray them, but that he could be\par
depended upon to keep so close a mouth that no clue could be pumped from\par
him; and next, that he could never be convicted of the forgery.\par
\par
He might, of course, be subjected to a few weeks of Newgate life. That\par
was very awkward, of course, but it would come all right.\par
\par
So they resolved for the present to remain in London and await\par
developments.\par
\par
That night the cable flashed the news of the forgery over the world,\par
dwelling particularly upon the fact that the perpetrator was an\par
American. The next morning the London press overflowed. Every prominent\par
paper gave a leader in the editorial column, and when the weeklies and\par
monthlies came out they followed suit. These editorials make now to us\par
who were on the inside amusing reading. They were full of Philistine\par
talk and amazement, and generally conceded that Noyes was an innocent\par
dupe, and all more or less doubted if his principal, the mysterious Mr.\par
F. A. Warren, would ever come back to say so.\par
\par
Day after day went by, and Mac and George hung around London reading the\par
accounts of the affair and of the examination of Noyes before the Lord\par
Mayor.\par
\par
They had communicated with him through his solicitor, and he sent them\par
word to leave England at once. In the mean time they had been sending\par
away the cash, and so entrenched were they in the belief that by no\par
possible chance could their names become mixed up in the affair that in\par
every instance but two they sent the money or bonds to America in their\par
right names.\par
\par
In the mean time the bank very wisely sent a cable to their legal agent,\par
Clarence A. Seward, in New York, asking him to set the American\par
detective force on the alert. He was a man of the world and understood\par
quite well what sort of men then ruled at Police Head quarters. So he\par
sent at once for Robert A. Pinkerton and gave him entire charge of the\par
American end of the line. Eventually they unearthed the whole plot,\par
secured the evidence that convicted us and recovered the greater part of\par
the money. The first step taken by the private inquiry men was to have\par
our friends, the detectives at headquarters, led to believe that they\par
had the case entirely in their own hands and to strengthen this\par
Pinkerton had the Bank of England agent in New York go to headquarters\par
every day and pretend to consult with Irving.\par
\par
After the continental raid, on our return to London we sent Irving\par
$3,000 in greenbacks in a registered letter, but in order to have a\par
hold on our three honest friends at headquarters in case of any possible\par
treachery in the future we put the money in the envelope in the presence\par
of a magistrate and had his clerk register it and make it a part of the\par
court record. The envelope was simply addressed "James Irving, Esq., 300\par
Mulberry street, New York," and of course the officials in London\par
supposed it a private address.\par
\par
When we returned from Rio we sent another $3,000, $1,000 each for\par
Irving, Stanley and White, and took the same precautions.\par
\par
Soon after the floods of money coming to us in London Mac sent $15,000\par
to Irving in another registered letter, without any precautions,\par
however. Irving & Co. did not know what game we were playing, but were\par
very happy over the dividends past and to come. But when they read the\par
cable dispatches in the press about the bank forgeries, their bliss was\par
ecstatic. Each in fancy saw himself decked out in a magnificent diamond\par
pin and ring, spinning along Harlem lane behind a particularly fast pair\par
in a stylish rig. This was their day vision. At night each saw himself\par
in certain resorts ordering unlimited bottles, or seeing New York by\par
gaslight at the rate of $100 a minute, and the Britishers paying for it\par
all. But the lawyers and the Pinkertons between them played Irving and\par
headquarters for fools and knaves. Day after day one of the lawyers\par
visited Mulberry street, and, being tutored by Pinkerton, gave deceptive\par
points to Irving, who, with his two chums, was completely hood-winked\par
and never suspected the game being played on them.\par
\par
But as I have got somewhat ahead of events in London I will return there\par
and very briefly narrate what was taking place there. Nearly every day\par
Noyes was brought before the Lord Mayor and officially examined, but,\par
acting under advice of his lawyer, he was strictly non-committal. The\par
detectives and officials were convinced he knew all about it, and tried\par
by both threats and promises to make him talk. Baron Rothschild and\par
others of the directors visited him again, but our friend was deaf, dumb\par
and blind, and they were foiled. In time two Pinkerton detectives had\par
arrived in London, and by a series of lucky hits soon began to let in\par
some light on the business.\par
\par
In searching Noyes the English police had found his garments were made\par
by a certain London tailor who had several establishments. They brought\par
the foremen and salesmen down to see him, and none could identify him;\par
but the American detectives went over the ground again, and discovered\par
that the London officers had missed one branch store. This was the one\par
Noyes had patronized. They remembered him as a customer who had, when\par
ordering garments, given the name of Bedford. This in itself was a bad\par
point against Noyes, and the New York men wanted very much to make him\par
talk, and had they been permitted to adopt the vigorous American methods\par
they might have succeeded.\par
\par
A salesman remembered seeing Noyes or Bedford one day walking in Mayfair\par
with a gentleman who really was Mac, of whom he gave a good description,\par
and taking the clerk the detectives started out to make a house-to-house\par
investigation. Now, No. 1 Mayfair, the first house they entered, was the\par
residence of a famous London doctor by the name of Payson Hewett, and\par
Mac had been a patient of his. But Hewett knew absolutely nothing about\par
him save only his name and the address he gave, Westminster Palace\par
Hotel. The detectives were elated, and flew to this hotel, but as Mac\par
had never been a guest they could learn nothing; still they had cause\par
for rejoicing. Here was Noyes giving a fictitious name to a tailor and\par
in company with an elegantly dressed American, who gave a fictitious\par
address to his surgeon. And they were well satisfied that whenever the\par
matter was dug out it would be found that the elegantly dressed\par
stranger, as well as the clerk, had a hand in the business. Payson\par
Hewett stated that Mac said he was a medical graduate from an American\par
university, and said that, no doubt, he spoke the truth, as he had a\par
perfect knowledge of medical subjects.\par
\par
Here they were getting matters down pretty fine, and cabled all the\par
facts to America with orders to look Mac up, also his friends. This\par
information was the fruit of hard work--many blind trails had been\par
followed that ran nowhere.\par
\par
In the mean time George and Mac had determined to return to America. The\par
last thing Mac did before leaving his lodgings in St. James' place was\par
to roll up in three rolls $254,000 in United States bonds and send the\par
trunk containing them by express to Major George Mathews, New York. He\par
wrapped them in a nightshirt belonging to me, which in some way had got\par
into his baggage. Then he bought a ticket to Paris and sent his baggage\par
over, waiting in London a day or two longer before going himself.\par
\par
George determined to go to Ireland, and to Ireland he went, and I shall\par
let him in a later chapter tell in his own language the stirring events\par
in Ireland and Scotland that finally ended in his arrest in Edinburgh\par
some weeks later. Mac, before sending his baggage away, had intended to\par
sail from Liverpool by the Java of the Cunard line, and he cabled Irving\par
at Police Headquarters to meet him on the arrival of the steamer. Mac\par
went to Paris, stopping at the Hotel Richmond, Rue du Helder, under his\par
right name, never for a moment thinking he could possibly come under\par
suspicion.\par
\par
In the mean time the Pinkerton men continued their house-to-house\par
visitation of the fashionable lodging houses to hunt out Mac. This, in\par
huge London, was a Titanic task, but they exhibited a marvelous activity\par
in tracing out clues. In a lucky moment for the Pinkertons, a\par
subordinate inquiring at every number in St. James' place if an\par
American gentleman was lodging or had lodged there was informed by one\par
landlady that Mac had been a lodger, but had left a few days before. As\par
soon as this important report arrived they flew to St. James' place and\par
found the landlady a warm friend of the man they were looking for. The\par
detectives were forced to tell her their business. She was indignant\par
that any one should so wrong Mac, and ordered them out of the house.\par
\par
They brought the bank solicitors and other important people to see her\par
before she would consent to be questioned; when she did, her information\par
was important indeed. She had seen very little of George, but much of\par
me, though she had never heard my name, but still the detectives knew\par
from her description that the man she described was the F. A. Warren\par
they wanted, and whom to get meant fame and comparative fortune for\par
them.\par
\par
The rooms had been unoccupied since Mac left and a careful search was\par
made for clues, but nothing was found until she was asked for the\par
waste-paper basket. The basket proved to be a bag, and when turned out\par
some pieces of blotting paper appeared, which, held in front of a\par
mirror, of course would reflect the writing the same as on the written\par
sheet, and on holding the last of the lot to the glass they were\par
thrilled through when the Pinkertons saw reflected there:\par
\par
  Ten Thousand......................Pounds Sterling.\par
  F. A. WARREN.\par
\par
which, when compared with a canceled check of mine, then in the\par
possession of the bank, exactly fitted it. Here was a piece of evidence,\par
which, if it could be brought home to Mac, was a chain to bind him fast\par
and sure.\par
\par
Pinkerton and his man started at once for Paris, and going to the\par
American bankers, where most Americans register on arrival, they found\par
Mac's name as large as life, registered at Andrews & Co.'s as stopping\par
at the Hotel de Richmond.\par
\par
Pinkerton was not long in reaching Rue du Helder, and learned that Mac\par
had left for Brest the night before. In short order he was at the Paris\par
agency of the steamship company, and found that Mac had purchased a\par
ticket to New York by the Thuringia, which was due to sail that very\par
hour from Brest. He did not let the grass grow under his feet between\par
the ticket and telegraph offices, and there he telegraphed the\par
authorities to arrest Mac, but he had a speedy reply that the Thuringia\par
had sailed half an hour before his telegram came. On second thought he\par
quite possibly was not sorry Mac had got off to New York, as it would\par
lengthen out the bill and scatter some of the bank's money in New York.\par
\par
He therefore cabled to his office in New York particulars as to Mac's\par
departure, and then he turned all his attention to discovering who this\par
F. A. Warren could be. Mac had cabled Irving that he was coming by the\par
Thuringia. Pinkerton, feeling that there was no secrecy required about\par
his man being on the steamer, gave the fact to the press, and Irving\par
discovered, very much to his chagrin, that all the world shared with him\par
his secret as to Mac's whereabouts, and that if he would save his\par
reputation he would have to be on hand, not as a friend and confederate,\par
but in his official capacity and make a genuine arrest--that is, unless\par
he could arrange to have Mac taken off the steamer in a small boat as\par
soon as she came into the lower bay and before the police boat, with its\par
load of officials, came alongside. This Irving and his two subordinates\par
resolved to attempt, so he took into his counsels a great chum of his\par
and a well-known burglar by the name of Johnny Dobbs. To him was given\par
the job of getting Mac off the steamer, but he made a serious blunder.\par
Instead of hiring and manning two boats, one to relieve the other, he\par
got only one. For a day or two they came within hailing distance of all\par
incoming steamers, but were ashore on Staten Island, taking a rest, when\par
bright and early one morning the Thuringia slipped into the harbor.\par
There was a man in the boat with Dobbs who knew Mac, and the plan was to\par
meet the steamer, and as Mac was sure to be on deck on the lookout, to\par
shout to him to jump overboard and they would pick him up and make for\par
shore. Once ashore and warned they would not have seen him again.\par
\par
After the Thuringia came into the harbor, Irving kept the police boat\par
waiting over an hour. Then, supposing his friend was safe ashore, he\par
boarded the ship. There were five United States Marshals on the police\par
tug, the bank lawyers and some of the private inquiry officials.\par
\par
Irving, accompanied by White and Stanley, jumped aboard the big ship,\par
after giving orders to the captain of the tug not to let any one off\par
until he gave permission. Mac saw the tug and recognized his three\par
friends, but was in no way alarmed until Irving, shaking hands with him,\par
hurriedly explained the state of affairs. Mac took them to his cabin and\par
gave them $150,000 in bonds, $10,000 in greenbacks, which he had bought\par
of the brokers in London, besides English bank notes and two or three\par
valuable diamonds. Then taking out several bags of sovereigns he said:\par
"Now, boys, help yourselves. Load yourselves down and keep them from the\par
enemy." What a picture those fellows loading up with that golden store\par
of sovereigns would have made! They knew the marshals and detectives\par
they held entrapped aboard the tug would be furious, and morally sure\par
that Irving & Co. had plucked their bird. Therefore any appearance of\par
pockets bulging out might lead to disgrace, so, while they hated to\par
leave any, for their fingers itched for all, yet they were forced to\par
that cruel self-denial.\par
\par
One amusing piece of impudence on Irving's part occurred when looking\par
with greedy eyes on the eight-carat diamond Mac wore on his finger, he\par
said: "My God, Mac, I wish I had brought along a paste diamond. You\par
could wear the ring and give me yours in exchange." The ring having been\par
seen by so many he feared to chance taking it. No doubt his enforced\par
denial for long sat heavy on Jimmy's soul. What a penchant all our\par
honest detectives have for gems, and where do they get them?\par
\par
In the mean time a storm was raging among the rival officers, who did\par
not relish being duped, and finally by threats forced the captain to\par
bring the tug alongside the steamer. Then they rushed on board to find\par
Irving & Co. with their prisoner awaiting them.\par
\par
The marshals went to the cabin and found some L4,000 or L5,000 in\par
sovereigns, but when Mac was searched nothing was found on him but $20\par
in greenbacks. He was turned over to the United States officials and\par
landed in Ludlow Street Jail, pending an examination before the United\par
States Commissioner with a view to his extradition.\par
\par
How the Pinkertons unearthed the $254,000 wrapped in old clothing in\par
Mac's trunk at the European Express Office, 44 Broadway, would take too\par
much time to tell here, or how circulars were sent out to the banks and\par
trust companies warning them to hold all funds deposited by any of our\par
party, or how Pinkerton and his men recovered large sums in various\par
places, must all be passed over here. Suffice it to say that the fatal\par
piece of blotting paper was produced in New York along with many lesser\par
points of evidence, and after a hard legal fight Mac was finally ordered\par
to be given up to the English Government to stand his trial for\par
complicity in the great bank forgery.\par
\par
The legal proceedings before the commissioner lasted three full months.\par
The array of counsel on both sides made it a forensic contest between\par
giants, in which all past history was invoked for precedents. This\par
extradition case attracted wide attention.\par
\par
After United States Commissioner Gutman had finally decided to surrender\par
him to the demand of the British Government, appeal was made to the\par
United States Circuit Court, Judge Woodruff, then to the Supreme Court,\par
Judge Barrett, before whom Mac was brought by writs of habeas corpus;\par
but the commissioner's decision was sustained. Mac was sent to Fort\par
Columbus for safe-keeping while counsel were vainly arguing on new writs\par
of habeas corpus and certiorari, but before any conclusion could be\par
reached, he was hurried away by his custodians. He had scarcely time to\par
bid good-bye to his counsel, when with a United States officer he was\par
hurried into a carriage in Chambers street, guarded by Chief Deputy\par
Marshal Kennedy and Deputies Robinson and Crowley, and driven rapidly\par
down Broadway to the Battery, so that the large crowd who gathered to\par
witness his departure from the metropolis had very little time to feast\par
their eyes.\par
\par
He was transferred from the Battery to Governor's Island by a tugboat\par
and subsequently handed over by the deputy marshals to the charge of\par
Major J. P. Roy, who had him escorted to Fort Columbus.\par
\par
The following morning United States Marshal Fiske, with Deputies Crowley\par
and Purvis; Mr. Peter Williams, solicitor of the Bank of England; Sergt.\par
Edward Hancock, a London detective; Deputy Marshal Colfax and others,\par
boarded the steam tug P. C. Schultze at the Battery and steamed across\par
to Governor's Island. At 10.30 o'clock Capt. J. W. Bean, on post at the\par
fort, received an order to deliver him over.\par
\par
Capt. J. W. Bean then delivered him over to United States Marshal\par
Fiske's charge, with whom he descended the steps from the balcony of the\par
fort, and marched, with a deputy at either side, through tiled pathways\par
and groved and shaded avenues, to the wharf at the other end of the\par
island, where the Schultze was awaiting his arrival. A large crowd of\par
spectators, soldiers and civilians lined the wharf, lingering anxiously\par
to see him off. But he walked very leisurely, smoked, laughed and\par
appeared in a state of unaccountable good humor.\par
\par
It was nearly 11 o'clock when the Schultze steamed away from Governor's\par
Island wharf and whistled and rattled down the Bay to await the arrival\par
of the Minnesota, which lay at anchor during the forenoon near Pier 46,\par
North River, and did not sail until some minutes after 12 o'clock. The\par
Schultze meantime waited, steaming around the lower bay until the\par
Minnesota arrived. The steam tug neared the bulky and huge vessel, and\par
Mac was finally taken on board by United States Marshal Fiske and Deputy\par
Marshals Robinson, Crowley and Colfax, and given into the custody of the\par
English detectives, Sergts. Webb and Hancock, who in return gave the\par
usual receipt to Marshal Fiske.\par
\par
For the present, I leave Mac on the Atlantic, sailing swiftly eastward,\par
to meet his terrible doom.\par
\par
[Illustration: DRAWING STONE.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXV.\par
\par
THE IRONY OF FATE.\par
\par
\par
In this chapter I give in his own words George's account of his flight\par
from London and his arrest.\par
\par
"Without the remotest suspicion that my right name was known or that\par
anything had been discovered to show my connection with the fraud, I\par
resolved to take the steamer Atlantic of the White Star line at\par
Queenstown for New York. Knowing that all the railway stations in London\par
were being watched, and that any man buying a ticket for America might\par
have to give an account of himself, I sent a porter to purchase a ticket\par
for Dublin via Holyhead. I intended taking the 9 p.m. mail train, and,\par
as a precaution, I waited until the last moment, after the passengers\par
were on board, and the waiting-room doors shut. As the mail was being\par
transferred from the wagons to the train, I took the opportunity to walk\par
through the big gate unobserved amid the rush and confusion. The car\par
doors were all locked, but on showing my ticket to a guard (conductor)\par
he let me into a compartment, no doubt supposing that I had obtained\par
admission to the station from the waiting-room and had been loitering\par
about. The same was probably the case with the two or three other men\par
looking out of the waiting-room window at the platform, whom I judged to\par
be detectives. The train rolled out of the station, and soon I was\par
leaving London behind at the rate of fifty miles an hour. After midnight\par
we took the steamer at Holyhead and arrived at Dublin about 7 a.m. I\par
should not have felt so comfortable throughout this night's journey had\par
I known that the telegraph was flashing in all directions five thousand\par
pounds reward for my capture.\par
\par
"A whole column regarding myself and my supposed movements was published\par
in the Dublin papers of that morning. Not suspecting they contained\par
'news' regarding me, I neglected purchasing one, and, remaining ignorant\par
of my imminent danger, took the train for Cork, where I arrived about 4\par
p.m. I had two or three London papers of the previous day in my hand as\par
I left the station. I had never been in Cork until then, and as I passed\par
into the street two detectives, who were watching the passengers, turned\par
and followed me. A few yards from the station one of them stepped up by\par
my side and said:\par
\par
"'Have you ever been here before?'\par
\par
"I slightly turned my head toward him, gave a haughty glance as I\par
replied: 'Yes,' then looked straight ahead and continued my slow gait,\par
paying no further attention to him. He continued walking by my side for\par
a few steps, as if irresolute, then dropped to the rear, rejoining his\par
companion. I did not dare to look around or make inquiry as to the\par
location of the wharf from which the tugboat started to convey mail and\par
passengers to the New York steamers, which waited in the outer harbor.\par
Therefore I continued my walk along what appeared to be the main\par
business street, perhaps for a quarter of a mile, then turned into a\par
druggist's and called for some Spanish licorice. This was done to enable\par
me to ascertain if the detectives were still following. In a moment they\par
passed the shop gazing intently in and saw me leaning carelessly against\par
the counter with my face partially turned to the street. As soon as I\par
had paid for the licorice I continued my walk in the same direction, but\par
saw nothing of the men, they having evidently stopped in some place to\par
let me get ahead once more. In a short time I approached an inclosure\par
over the gate of which was a sign that informed me I had come by\par
accident direct to the wharf of the New York steamers. Entering I found\par
the place crowded and the tugboat ready to convey the passengers to the\par
steamer Atlantic. Before attempting to step aboard the tug I took a\par
covert look around and saw my two detectives standing back in one corner\par
with their eyes fixed upon me, all but their heads being concealed\par
behind the crowd waiting to see their friends off for America.\par
Apparently unconscious of their presence, I threw my papers, one by one,\par
down among the passengers; and as the deck of the boat was eight or ten\par
feet below, the detectives could not see to whom they were thrown. I\par
stood leaning on the rail a short time gazing at the scene, then left\par
the wharf not even glancing in the direction of the detectives. I felt\par
that any attempt of mine to embark would precipitate their movements,\par
therefore I at once abandoned all ideas of taking passage from\par
Queenstown.\par
\par
"Now mark the irony of fate! That was the last passage ever made by the\par
magnificent steamer Atlantic! Some magnetic influence deranged her\par
compass so that she ran twenty miles out of her course, striking on the\par
coast of Nova Scotia, at Meager's Head, Prospect Harbor, broke in two,\par
then rolling into deep water sank in a few minutes. Out of 1,002 persons\par
on board 560 perished, including most of the saloon passengers and all\par
the women and children. The elegant cabins and staterooms became their\par
tombs--and one might have been mine. But not for me such favoring fate;\par
a moment's struggle ended their sufferings, while I was left to undergo\par
the pangs of a thousand deaths!\par
\par
[Illustration: A CORRIDOR OF THE TOMBS, NEW YORK.]\par
\par
"I continued my walk up a hill among the private residences of the city,\par
and, hailing a cab, told the driver to take me back to the station.\par
Eager for a job, he asked to drive me a mile beyond on the railway.\par
Thinking I might elude the detectives at the Queenstown station, I\par
acceded, and he made his little Irish horse rush along at a pace\par
which brought us to the stopping place just before the train arrived.\par
\par
[Illustration: INTERIOR OF AN ENGLISH CONVICT PRISON.]\par
\par
"I purchased a ticket and hastened into a carriage, where, lo and\par
behold! sat the two detectives. A few minutes brought us to Cork again.\par
I was not yet aware they were in possession of my right name and the\par
knowledge that a reward of L5,000 was offered for my capture, nor that\par
their hesitation was occasioned by doubts as to my identity, which the\par
first false step on my part might remove. I did not suppose they were\par
looking especially for me, but for any one in general whose actions and\par
appearance might indicate that he was one of the operators in the bank\par
forgery. Under this erroneous belief I crossed to the Dublin station,\par
which was a quarter of a mile from that of the Cork and Queenstown. As I\par
entered the waiting room I saw my two detectives standing at the other\par
side. 'Well,' I thought to myself, 'this is very strange; I left the\par
Queenstown station ahead of them and here they are again, all alive!' I\par
walked away into the most thronged streets of the business part of the\par
city; turning a corner I glanced backward and saw them following at some\par
distance in the rear. As soon as I had fairly turned the corner I\par
started at a fast walk, turning the next before they came in view, and\par
after three or four such turnings I went into a small temperance hotel\par
and took lodgings for the night. There was but a single commercial\par
traveler in the sitting room--a special room set apart in every English\par
hotel, sacred to the 'drummer' fraternity. In the course of the evening\par
he handed me a small railway map of Ireland, which, in my subsequent\par
flight through the country, proved of incalculable service to me.\par
\par
"The next morning I went out and purchased a handbag, a Scotch cap and a\par
cheap frieze ulster. My night's cogitations had not enabled me to solve\par
the detective problem, but I felt confident that something was decidedly\par
wrong. I then hired a covered cab, driving past the postoffice to\par
recoinnoitre, and saw one of the detectives standing in the doorway.\par
This sight deterred me from going in to ask for a letter. Dismissing my\par
cab, I took another and drove to the place where I had made my\par
purchases, taking them into the cab and going through a by-street which\par
brought me close to my hotel.\par
\par
"From the commercial room in the second floor front I looked out and\par
marked the farthest house I could see to the left on the opposite side.\par
Stepping to the desk I wrote an order directing the postmaster to\par
deliver any letters to my address to the bearer. This I gave to a\par
cabman, instructing him to drive to the postoffice and bring my mail to\par
the house I had marked, returning myself to the commercial room to\par
watch. In a few minutes I saw the cabman drive to the house, and seeing\par
no one waiting there, he turned and drove slowly down the street past\par
the hotel, holding up at arm's length a letter to attract my\par
notice--which it did to my two detectives walking along a short distance\par
behind him, on the hotel side of the street, with noses elevated and\par
eyes peering everywhere.\par
\par
"'Well,' I thought, 'this is getting to be hot, and it is time for me to\par
leave Cork.' I was now fully aroused to a sense of my danger. No one\par
happening to be in the commercial room for the moment, I left my hat on\par
the sofa, and wearing the Scotch cap, slipped downstairs just as they\par
were past the hotel, following them until I came to where the cab was\par
waiting with my luggage. I ordered the driver to take me to a canal-boat\par
wharf, where I dismissed him; then, with bag in hand, I walked across\par
the canal bridge, stopped in a small shop and hired a smaller boy to go\par
for a jaunting car, and a few minutes later I was rolling to the\par
northward.\par
\par
"On the road I threw some small coins to poor-looking people, who then,\par
as now, comprised among their numbers the most honest patriots and the\par
truest-hearted sons of Erin.\par
\par
"Seeing me throwing the pence to the poor folk, cabby took it into his\par
head that I must be a priest--a good criterion of the estimation in\par
which the benevolence of the fathers is held by their own people. And I\par
may here remark that all the Catholic priests I have known, occupying\par
the post of chaplain, were without exception faithful and entirely\par
devoted to the duties of their holy calling. I had no intention of\par
traveling as a priest, and when I told the driver as much he would not\par
believe it, but insisted that I was really a priest traveling incognito;\par
therefore, when we stopped at a small wayside tavern, about twelve miles\par
from Cork and two to Fermoy, he privately informed the mistress that I\par
was a priest who did not want the fact to become known. Accordingly the\par
good woman treated me with marked attention during my short stay. It was\par
then nearly sunset, and as I did not wish the cabman to get back to Cork\par
until late at night, I kept him eating and drinking until dark, when I\par
paid the bill and started him homeward, uproariously rejoicing. I then\par
started for Fermoy station, about two miles distant, taking the hostler\par
along to carry my bag. When within half a mile of the village I let him\par
return. While passing through the village I went into a shop and\par
purchased a different Scotch cap, the 'Glengarry.'\par
\par
"Arriving at the station, I noticed a man near the ticket office who\par
appeared to be watching those who were purchasing tickets. This made me\par
change my plan--instead of taking a ticket to Dublin, I bought one for\par
Lismore, the end of the road in the opposite direction. The exclamation,\par
'Well, are you going to stay all night?' was the first intimation I had\par
of our arrival at that place. I rubbed my sleepy eyes, and saw with\par
dismay that all the passengers were gone and one of the porters was\par
putting out the lights. At the platform I found a cab, and by 9 p.m. I\par
was at the Lismore House. After eating supper I entered the sitting\par
room, finding a single occupant whom I took to be a lawyer, and judging\par
by his conversation and manner, in the light of later events, I do not\par
doubt that he surmised who I was. He was reading a newspaper, which he\par
once or twice offered to me; but, not dreaming of the interesting nature\par
of its contents, I declined to take it from him. About 10 o'clock the\par
gentleman retired, leaving his paper on the table. I carelessly picked\par
it up, and the first thing that caught my eyes was a displayed heading\par
in large type, offering L5,000 reward for my arrest.\par
\par
"A thunderbolt, indeed! For a few minutes I stared at the paper in blank\par
dismay. It was fortunate for my temporary safety that there were no\par
witnesses present. 'Well,' I thought to myself, 'this is a predicament!\par
How did they obtain any clue to me? I thought we had covered up the\par
whole affair so deep in mystery that not a clue to our personality could\par
ever be obtained!'\par
\par
"I sat for an hour alone in this Lismore Hotel, utterly dumfounded,\par
bewildered, paralyzed. I had experienced some shocks, some 'take-downs,'\par
in my time, but never one to compare with this.\par
\par
"Arousing myself from a state of mental stupefaction hitherto unknown, I\par
began to realize the necessity of immediate action if I wished to avoid\par
falling into the merciless jaws of the British lion. I put the paper\par
into the fire, and retired to the room allotted to me.\par
\par
"Before daylight in the morning I had decided upon the first step, and\par
as the lawyer had asked me if I intended to remain over Sunday, I\par
resolved to be as far away as possible before he was out of bed. While\par
it was yet dark in the house, I left my bag in the bedroom and crept\par
gently down the stairs to the basement, where the porter-hostler was\par
sleeping in a box of rags. I suppose the poor wretch had not long\par
finished his multifarious duties, for I could arouse him only to a state\par
of semi-consciousness, and could get no information from him. I then\par
went up to the front door, carefully turned the key and stepped out on\par
the piazza which ran along the front of the hotel. Another shock was in\par
store for me. A man posted on the other side of the street was watching\par
the hotel!\par
\par
"It was now quite light, and I sauntered carelessly up the street,\par
apparently taking no notice of the man over the way, and endeavoring to\par
show by my actions that I was out for an airing before breakfast.\par
\par
"As I turned the next corner and glanced back, I saw him following. I\par
noticed a place where jaunting-cars were to be let, but passed on, at\par
each turn glancing back to see my follower the same distance in the\par
rear. I now took a circuit around by the hotel, but instead of going in\par
I hastened and turned the next corner beyond--he, when reaching the\par
corner near the hotel, not seeing me, doubtless thought I had gone in,\par
and planted himself in his old position. I thought Lismore to be getting\par
rather hot, and hastening to the livery stable, found the hostler just\par
getting up. He informed me that all the horses were engaged for the day\par
except one, the fastest they had, but as this was engaged for a long\par
journey on Tuesday, they were letting him have a rest. I said: 'But, my\par
good fellow, I must have a horse, and at once, with you to drive, and\par
there will be a half sovereign for a good Irishman, such as I see before\par
me.' My 'blarney' began to do its work. Scratching his head, he finally\par
said: 'Well, I will waken up my master, and you can talk with him.' So\par
he rapped at a window, and soon a night-capped head appeared, and after\par
some parley the master consented to let me have his equipage. In a few\par
minutes from the time I had lost sight of my follower we were rattling\par
out of the town of Lismore at the full speed of a blooded Irish horse. I\par
had left my bag behind, taking only the Scotch caps and ulster with me\par
from the hotel. I found, by reference to the small map and railway\par
guide, that Clonmel was less than thirty miles distant, and connected\par
with Dublin by a branch line. When I engaged the jaunting-car I had\par
told the owner that it was uncertain what part of the day I should\par
require it, and after we were about five miles from Lismore I said to\par
the driver:\par
\par
"'You say that you are going to Clonmel on Tuesday for a passenger.\par
Well, now, as I must go there before I leave this part of the country,\par
you may as well continue in that direction, and I can return with you on\par
Tuesday.'\par
\par
"This pleased him, and we drove on till about noon, when we stopped at a\par
country grocery about five miles from Clonmel. As we drove up to the\par
door, the words of an old Irish song went jingling through my brain:\par
\par
  "'At the sign of the bell,\par
  On the road to Clonmel,\par
  Pat Flagherty kept a neat shebeen.'\par
\par
"The rain poured down in torrents. I gave my driver a lunch of bread and\par
cheese, which--of course, there--included whisky. I also gave him a\par
sovereign, telling him to pay his master for the horse-hire and keep the\par
change for himself; then started him back, brimful of delight and the\par
'craythur,' receiving his parting salute:\par
\par
"'Yer 'onor is a jintleman, and no mistake.'\par
\par
"I arranged with the storekeeper to let a boy take me in his car to\par
Clonmel.\par
\par
"The Green Isle! Well, I found out that day what keeps the grass green\par
in Ireland. My Irish frieze and every thread on me were water-logged,\par
yet the Irish lad, my driver, took the 'buckets-full' as a matter of\par
course. Amid this deluge of rain we arrived in Clonmel and stopped at a\par
'shebeen,' kept by the boy's uncle--driving into the back yard through a\par
gate in a board fence fifteen feet high, which shut it in from the\par
street.\par
\par
[Illustration: "I AM JOHN CURTIN OF THE PINKERTON FORCE."--Page 332.]\par
\par
"I went into a room in the rear of the sale room, the door of which\par
stood open so that I could see all that passed within, and, as I stood\par
drying my clothes by the turf fire, I saw how thirsty souls on the\par
'ould sod,' evaded the Sunday liquor law. The proprietor stood in the\par
shop in a position whence he could covertly keep an eye on the policeman\par
patrolling the street, and as soon as he was out of sight a signal was\par
given, the backyard gate thrown open, when a dozen men rushed in, and\par
the gate closed. Coming hilariously through the dwelling into the shop,\par
these were soon busily drinking their 'potheen.'\par
\par
"It was now 2 o'clock p.m., the rain had ceased, and starting out, I\par
walked along a main street until I saw a sign 'cabs to let.' I went into\par
the house and was shown into an inner room, where the proprietress sat\par
crooning over a turf fire. She motioned me to a seat beside her, and\par
when I told her I wished for a conveyance to take me to Cahir, a place\par
eight miles distant, she asked me several questions, among others, how\par
long I wished to be gone, and if I were not an American. To all of which\par
I replied to the following effect: That I was going to visit some\par
friends who were officers stationed in the fort at Cahir; and as to her\par
mistaking me for an American, the ancestors of the 'Yankees' went from\par
about Norfolk County, England, to America, of course, taking the accent\par
with them, and I being from the former place, (Norfolk) of course had\par
the same accent.\par
\par
"This explanation appeared to satisfy the old lady, and she became quite\par
confidential; and, anxious to remove from my mind any trace of offense\par
at her unusual questioning, she drew closer to me and said:\par
\par
"I can see that you are all right; but the fact is that the captain of\par
police sent an order that I should notify him at once in case any\par
stranger wished to hire a vehicle, especially if I thought him an\par
American. But I do not care for the curs; they are nothing but a parcel\par
of spies and informers in the pay of the English Government; so even if\par
you were the one they are looking for they will wait a long time for me\par
to inform them, and you shall have my best horse and a good driver.'\par
\par
"I heartily thanked the good old Irish lady--for I have found true\par
ladies and gentlemen among the poor and humble, as well as the wealthy,\par
especially in Ireland--and in a few minutes I was bowling gayly along\par
toward Cahir.\par
\par
"This is a small, ancient, walled garrison town, the nearest railway\par
station being at Clonmel. This miniature city has been the scene of many\par
a heart-stirring event in the distant past. Here Cromwell was for a time\par
held at bay, and his fanatical hordes made their Celtic opponents pay in\par
blood for their patriotic and desperate defense of their homes and\par
firesides.\par
\par
"Driving through the town gate, I saw in the main street a grocery store\par
with a blind down, and telling the driver to halt there, I paid him and\par
sent him back. I then went into the grocery, and after taking a lunch of\par
bread and cheese, continued my walk up the street. I saw a hotel just\par
ahead, but not wishing to attract attention to my movements, I crossed\par
to the opposite side, and while doing so glanced back and saw a car come\par
through the same town gate I had just entered, and dash furiously up the\par
street, pulling up at the walk a few yards behind me. Just as they\par
sprang out I turned to the left in a narrow lane in which I saw a\par
gateway to the fort, just within the entrance of which a sentry was\par
pacing, there being opposite several roofless cottages. The soldier's\par
back being turned, quick as thought I sprang unseen within one of these,\par
and in a moment I heard some men run around the corner and interrogate\par
the soldier, who stoutly declared that no one had entered. The men then\par
demanded to see the captain, were admitted, and after a short time I\par
heard them come out and depart. I stood in that ruin two mortal hours\par
until dusk, then walked out unseen by the sentry, and turning to the\par
left, came into a narrow street lined with small dwelling houses."\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXVI.\par
\par
"EXCUSE ME, SIR, FOR QUESTIONING YOU."\par
\par
\par
"Crossing the narrow street in Cahir, referred to at the close of the\par
last chapter, I went in haphazard at the first door, without knocking,\par
and saw a family eating their humble supper. As I walked in I addressed\par
the family at the table thus:\par
\par
"'Good evening. Pardon my intrusion, and do not disturb yourselves; but\par
by all means finish your supper.'\par
\par
"'Good evening, sir,' was the reply from the man, whom I will call\par
Maloy. 'We are glad to see you; will you sit by and have pot-luck with\par
us?'\par
\par
"'No, thank you,' I answered. 'I am an American--and it is my custom\par
when traveling in any country to make unceremonious calls like this, in\par
order to see the people as they really are at home.'\par
\par
"After supper was over I related to Maloy and his family several stories\par
and incidents concerning the Fenians and their doings in America, which,\par
of course, interested them greatly. When it was fairly dark I arose to\par
go, and Maloy went outside with me. He had previously informed me that\par
he was employed by the government in the civil service. I will not state\par
in what capacity, for, although so many years have elapsed, the\par
true-hearted Irishman may still be earning his bread in the same humble\par
employment, and the knowledge that he assisted one whom he supposed to\par
be a Fenian leader in 1873 might even now cost him dearly. When we were\par
outside the door I said:\par
\par
"'The fact is, Maloy, I am a Fenian leader, and the police are after me!\par
I have been dodging them for two days, and they are looking for me now\par
in Cahir! I have important papers for prominent Fenians in various parts\par
of Ireland, and it would delay our plans if I am obliged to destroy\par
them. But I fear I must do so at once unless you can help me. I would\par
almost sooner forfeit my life than to lose these papers, and I shall\par
fight to my last breath rather than let them fall into the hands of the\par
police, for it might be the ruin of several good men! My plan is to\par
double back to Clonmel, and I want your assistance to get me out of\par
Cahir!'\par
\par
"'Oh, sir,' he replied, 'it is too bad you did not let me know a little\par
sooner, for the mail car is gone; it starts at 6 o'clock.'\par
\par
"Just as he had finished speaking, a car came rumbling past and he\par
exclaimed joyfully:\par
\par
"'We are in luck! There goes the mail car to the postoffice! Come with\par
me!'\par
\par
"We hastened through a narrow, dark lane to the gate--the same I had\par
entered from Clonmel--walked through and at a hundred yards beyond\par
waited for the mail car, which soon came along. Maloy being well\par
acquainted with the driver, hailed him, saying that a friend of his\par
wanted a ride to Clonmel.\par
\par
"After shaking hands warmly with Maloy, I climbed upon the car, and the\par
next instant I was whirling along--into fresh dangers--in that unique\par
vehicle, an Irish jaunting car.\par
\par
"Arriving near Clonmel I saw a tavern, and ascertaining from the driver\par
that it was near the railway station, I left the car and entered the\par
place, only to find that the best, and, in fact, the sole food to be had\par
for supper was eggs. Having been on the move since dawn, after a\par
sleepless night, and almost without food, I hesitate to divulge how many\par
eggs I disposed of that evening, for the statement might tend to throw\par
distrust on the general veracity of my narrative. Having dried my wet\par
clothes and put myself into a presentable condition, I went to the\par
railway station to take the 11 p.m. train to Dublin. Seating myself on a\par
bench outside, I handed some money to a porter and sent him for a\par
ticket, which he obtained. There were but a few waiting about, so I\par
stepped into the small waiting room and sat down near three other men.\par
The one nearest, whom I at once put down for a local policeman in\par
private clothes, turned and spoke to me. I replied with civility to his\par
questions until finally he said: 'But are you not an American?' I\par
replied to his startling question in such a manner that he appeared\par
satisfied.\par
\par
"'You must excuse me, sir, for questioning you,' he explained, 'but\par
there has been a great forgery in London, and it is said some of the\par
parties are in Ireland, and I am anxious to get a claim on the L5,000\par
that is offered for each one of them.'\par
\par
"I told him that instead of being offended I was greatly pleased to see\par
the zeal he exhibited in the execution of his duties, and expressed the\par
hope that he might be successful in securing at least one of the\par
forgers, which would give him not only the L5,000, but undoubtedly\par
promotion. I got on the train all right, resolving that I would not\par
speak another word of English while in Ireland, and forthwith turned\par
into a Russian, who could speak 'une veree leetel Francais,' confident\par
that I should not be in danger of exposure by encounter with any one who\par
could speak the Russian language. I threw away the ordinary Scotch cap I\par
had been wearing, and put on the Glengarry. When I arrived at the\par
Maryborough junction, the train on the main line from Cork was late, and\par
I walked up and down on the platform, well knowing that the detectives\par
would scrutinize more closely those who appeared to shrink from\par
observation; therefore, I affected the bearing of a Russian prince as\par
nearly as I knew how.\par
\par
"I got on the train unmolested, and arrived in Dublin at 1 a.m.\par
\par
"There appeared to be some special watching of those leaving the train,\par
but I passed out unchallenged and took a cab. Not knowing the name of\par
any hotel, I told the driver I would direct the route as we passed\par
along, and he drove away at a great pace. Very soon I noticed another\par
cab following at an equal speed. I had mine turn a corner, but the one\par
behind came thundering after; and though I bade my driver to turn at\par
nearly every corner still I could not shake off my supposed pursuer\par
until, after apparently being followed about two miles, the stern chaser\par
turned off in another direction, much to my relief. We soon approached\par
the Cathedral Hotel, where I alighted about 2 a.m., rang up the porter,\par
and was shown to a room.\par
\par
"At 7 o'clock in the morning I sent for my bill, left the hotel, went\par
direct to the 'Jew' quarters, and purchased a valise and some\par
second-hand clothes. Noticing the old Jewess' looks of curiosity at\par
seeing one of my appearance making such purchases, I remarked: 'A Fenian\par
friend has got himself into a scrape, and the police are after him; so I\par
am going to get him out of the country, and wish to let him have some\par
things that do not have too new a look.' At hearing those (in Ireland)\par
magic words, 'Fenian,' 'police,' she became all smiles, let me fill the\par
valise with old garments at my own price, and at starting said: 'God\par
bless you! May you have good luck, and get off safe to America!'\par
\par
"I then went to a more pretentious locality, where I procured a silk hat\par
draped with mourning crape, put the Glengarry in my pocket, and became a\par
Frenchman. At this moment I discovered that I had left in my room at the\par
hotel a large silk neck-wrapper on which were embroidered my initials. I\par
immediately stepped into a shop and left my new purchases, resuming the\par
Scotch cap, and started for the hotel (where I had given no name), to\par
secure the dangerous article left behind. Coming in sight of the hotel,\par
I saw a man stationed opposite, leaning on a cane, who appeared to be\par
watching the house. As I approached nearer he kept his eyes covertly\par
fixed upon me; therefore, instead of entering the hotel, I walked past\par
it and turned the next corner, glancing backward as I did so, and, to my\par
dismay, saw the man following me. I now adopted the same plan of action\par
that succeeded so well at Cork, and in half an hour I had shaken him off\par
and returned to the place where I had left my new silk hat and valise.\par
Donning the hat, with valise in hand, I was soon seated in an Irish\par
jaunting car, on my way to a station about ten miles out on the railway\par
to Belfast.\par
\par
"Upon reflection I was satisfied that the chambermaid had found the silk\par
wrapper and taken it to the hotel office. There the initials, together\par
with the knowledge of my arrival at so unusual an hour, without baggage,\par
and my early departure, had aroused suspicion, and the police had been\par
notified. At about 11 o'clock I arrived at the station, and going into a\par
store paid my Dublin cabman and called for lunch. About five minutes\par
before the train was due from Dublin I walked into the empty station,\par
presented myself at the ticket office, and said: 'Parlez vous Francais,\par
Monsieur?' and received the reply, 'No.' I then said in a mongrel of\par
French and English that I wished for a ticket to Drogheda--not daring to\par
purchase one through Belfast. Supposing me to be a French gentleman, he\par
was very polite and ordered the porter to take my baggage to the\par
platform. There I found myself the solitary waiting passenger. As the\par
train approached I saw a pair of heads projecting from the carriage\par
windows, eagerly scanning the platform. Two men jumped off, and,\par
hastening to the station master began to talk to him in an excited\par
manner, all the time glancing toward me. As I passed near the group to\par
get on the train, I heard the agent say: 'He is a Frenchman.' No doubt\par
he informed them that I had purchased a ticket to a way station only--a\par
fact that would naturally allay suspicion. At the next stopping place\par
they actually arrested a man, but went no further.\par
\par
"I afterward ascertained that twelve men were arrested on that and the\par
preceding day, among the number being a fraudulent debtor trying to\par
escape by the same steamer, the Atlantic.\par
\par
"The following extracts from contemporary newspapers will give the\par
reader some idea as to what a 'hot' place Ireland was for me:\par
\par
\par
\par
                          "(By Cable to the New York Herald.)\par
                                                     "London.\par
\par
     "Three shabbily dressed men, who, from their accent, are believed\par
     to be Americans, were arrested in Cork, Ireland, this morning while\par
     attempting to deposit $12,000 in that city.\par
\par
     "They are supposed to be the parties who recently committed the\par
     frauds on the Bank of England."\par
\par
                       "(From the London Times of same date.)\par
\par
                                         "To Editor of Times.\par
\par
     "Sir: The case of Dr. Hessel has been so lately before the public,\par
     and so much has been written both in the English and German papers\par
     against the English police, that probably a little evidence upon\par
     the procedure of the German (or, I ought probably to say, the\par
     Bavarian) may not be uninteresting at the present moment. Myself\par
     and son, a sub-lieutenant, R. N., made a great attempt to reach\par
     the grotesque old city of Nuremberg on Saturday last, arriving\par
     there about 7 o'clock. We were asked to put our names in the\par
     stranger's book, as usual, which we did, and retired to bed.\par
     Imagine our surprise, on rising on Sunday morning, at receiving a\par
     visit from one of the chief police officers, requesting us to\par
     'legitimize ourselves.' I asked him his object for making this\par
     demand, when he replied that a man named Warren was wanted by the\par
     English police.\par
\par
     "In vain I showed him an old passport and letters addressed to me,\par
     showing that my name was Warner; he informed me that I could not\par
     leave my room, and placed two policemen at the door. At 1 o'clock\par
     I remembered an influential inhabitant of the town who knew me, and\par
     I sent for him. He at once went to headquarters and gave bond for\par
     me to a large amount, and at 6 o'clock in the evening myself and\par
     son were released. You will remember that in the case of Dr. Hessel\par
     four persons swore to his identity before he was deprived of his\par
     liberty. In my case a similar name to that required was sufficient\par
     to deprive me of mine.\par
\par
     "I have since received, thanks to the strenuous and prompt action\par
     of the British Minister at Munich, a very ample apology in writing\par
     for the blunder that had been committed. It was signed by the\par
     Burgermeister of the city, and as the intelligence of this worthy\par
     seems to be equaled by his simplicity, he sends me a safe pass to\par
     protect me in my further travels, in case Warner should again be\par
     considered the same as Warren. I remain, sir, your obedient\par
     servant,\par
\par
                                             "CHARLES W. C. WARNER,\par
                                  "Ex-Sheriff, London and Middlesex\par
\par
\par
\par
"I now return to my narrative. In the second-class compartment where I\par
sat were two burly, loud-talking, well-informed farm proprietors, one of\par
whom had imbibed a little too freely of the native distillation. The\par
sober one had just finished reading a column article on the 'Great Bank\par
Forgery' to his lively companion, who at length turned and addressed me.\par
I answered him politely in broken French, and he then went on to give\par
his opinion of the bank affair, as nearly as I can remember, as follows:\par
\par
"'You, being a Frenchman, don't understand about our great bank; but I\par
tell you those Yankees did a clever thing when they attacked that\par
powerful institution. The one they have got penned up here in Ireland\par
can't possibly escape; indeed, according to the newspapers, he is\par
already in the hands of the police. I am almost sorry to hear it, for in\par
getting the best of that bank so cleverly the rascal deserves to get\par
off; and see, here is a description of him.'\par
\par
"I looked at the paper and saw that it was a fair general outline of my\par
appearance, even to my ulster which I had with me in the valise, and\par
the Scotch cap which was in my pocket. Before we reached Drogheda I had\par
explained to one of my new friends, in broken French, that, owing to my\par
ignorance of the English language, I had purchased a wrong ticket, and\par
being liable to make a similar mistake, should feel obliged if he would\par
take the trouble to procure me a ticket at that station. He readily\par
assented, and by this means I procured it without exposing myself. The\par
hunt for me was becoming so extremely hot that I dared not show myself\par
again at a ticket office; and if I should be found on a train ticketless\par
that fact might lead to closer scrutiny--the rule in that country being\par
that every passenger must be provided with a ticket before entering a\par
car.\par
\par
"The train arrived in Belfast at 9 o'clock, and I at once took a cab to\par
the Glasgow steamer. It was very dark, and I went on board unobserved,\par
two hours before the time of departure. Going down into the saloon\par
cabin, I saw the purser sitting near the entrance, to whom I said:\par
'Parlez vous Francais?' He shook his head. I then asked in jargon for\par
'une billet a Glasgow.' Surmising what I wished, he gave me a ticket,\par
putting on it the number of my berth.\par
\par
"Expecting to be followed, I had taken that instant precaution of\par
impressing on the purser's mind that I was a Frenchman. I passed into\par
the washroom, just opposite where the purser sat, washed myself and\par
brushed my hair. Just at this moment I heard steps descending the cabin\par
stairway, then the words:\par
\par
"'Purser, a cab just brought a man from the Dublin train. Where is he?'\par
\par
"'Oh, you mean the Frenchman,' replied the purser; 'he's in the\par
washroom.'\par
\par
[Illustration: ONE WHO HAS BEEN ROBBED IDENTIFYING THE THIEF AT\par
NEWGATE.]\par
\par
"While this was passing I had put on my silk hat and taken up my valise,\par
and was standing before the glass (a la Francais) taking a final view of\par
my toilette, and snapping off some imaginary dust and lint, as the\par
two detectives stepped in, and after looking me well over went out, and\par
I saw them no more. That proved to be the last ordeal through which I\par
passed in Ireland. After being convinced that they had left the steamer\par
I went to my berth, and being thoroughly exhausted I fell asleep in an\par
instant, not awaking until the steamer was entering the harbor of\par
Glasgow.\par
\par
"After my arrest a month later in Scotland, during the transfer to\par
London and afterward to Newgate, while awaiting trial, the detectives\par
told me that they were in Cork three hours after I had left, and one of\par
them related their adventures substantially as follows:\par
\par
"'We arrived in Cork Saturday afternoon and were not long in finding the\par
temperance hotel where you stayed on Friday night, and the hat you left\par
behind. After a long hunt we ascertained that a jaunting car had left\par
the stand some hours previously and was still absent.\par
\par
"'We had a good laugh at those blunder-heads, the Cork officers, letting\par
you slip through their fingers, and then showed them how we do things.\par
After some delay we traced the cab across the bridge to the shop where\par
you got the boy to go for it. The shopwoman was quite voluble about you,\par
saying she knew all the time that you were an American by the accent,\par
and described the bag and ulster which we had ascertained were in your\par
possession. Of course, we were now satisfied that we were on the right\par
scent, but could get no further trace or the direction taken by the cab.\par
We therefore sent dispatches to all the telegraph stations within fifty\par
miles to put the police on the watch and sent messengers to the outlying\par
places, but somehow you slipped through our meshes, and nothing turned\par
up until the car man returned at about 11 p.m., as drunk as a soldier on\par
furlough. After putting him under a water tap until he was half drowned\par
we got him sober enough to tell where he had left you; but he swore you\par
were a priest, and his evident sincerity caused us all to roar with\par
laughter. This angered him, and he said: "Ye may twist me head an'\par
dhroun me intirely, but I wull niver spake another wurrud about the\par
jintelman at all, at all," and sure enough we could get nothing more out\par
of him.\par
\par
"'We had a carriage ready, and, jumping in, we were at the wayside inn\par
by midnight and terrified the old woman half out of her wits in arousing\par
her out of bed. After a while she gathered them sufficiently to show us\par
that you had six hours the start of us. The boy who carried your bag\par
could give us no points, but we concluded you intended taking the branch\par
line at Fermoy for Dublin. We drove right on, arriving at the Fermoy\par
station at 1 p.m., but, getting no trace we telegraphed to all the\par
stations along the line to Dublin, and there as well to be on the\par
lookout. Who would ever have thought of your taking the opposite\par
direction, penning yourself in at the end of a branch line, at a small\par
inland town like Lismore? Why, you were, as we discovered the next\par
morning, at that moment sleeping quietly at the Lismore Hotel, and only\par
about ten miles from where we were working so industriously for that\par
L5,000! Well, you "done" us fine that time!\par
\par
"'After you so cleverly threw us off the trail, we could get no trace\par
until Sunday morning, when we received a dispatch from Lismore, stating\par
that a man had come on the last train, stayed at the hotel and left at\par
daylight without paying his bill. "Hello!" said I, as soon as I read the\par
dispatch, "we never suspected Lismore; he has been there all night and\par
is off again!" We telegraphed to Clonmel, Waterford and other places;\par
then left for Lismore, where we arrived, paid your bill and took the bag\par
with us. Surmising that you might make for Clonmel, we looked for and\par
found the place where you got the car, but no news as to what direction\par
you had taken. It would have made you laugh, as it did us, to see the\par
old livery man stamp about and tear his hair when he found how easily\par
he could have made the L5,000--if he had "only known."\par
\par
"'Starting on the way to Clonmel, we soon had news which satisfied us we\par
were once more on the right track. Shortly after we met, sure enough,\par
the cab you had sent back from the country store. Arriving there we took\par
the boy, who had just returned from driving you to Clonmel, with us,\par
and, feeling sure that we should soon come up with you, we made our\par
horses spin toward that town. Arriving there, we saw the inspector, who\par
informed us that he had sent a constable in pursuit of a man who had\par
hired a car to go to Cahir.' (This must have been one of the men in the\par
car whom I escaped by dodging into the ruined cottage.) 'It being then\par
sundown we drove to Cahir with all speed, arriving there just after\par
dark, passing the Clonmel mail car inside the gate; but it contained no\par
one but the driver.\par
\par
"'We soon found the constable sent from Clonmel, who said you had\par
disappeared into the fort, where a friend must have concealed you, and\par
that you must be there still. He then took us to the fort, which was\par
closed for the night. As soon as my eyes lighted on the ruined cottages\par
I asked him if he had searched them and received an answer in the\par
negative. "Why," said he, "they are, as you see, all open to the day,\par
without roof, doors or windows, and no one would think of hiding in\par
them." "You are a fool," I replied. "Give me your lamp and come with\par
me." After a look around and seeing how easily any person could stand in\par
a corner out of sight, I remarked to him emphatically that he was the\par
biggest specimen of a goose I had ever seen in my line. "I think," said\par
I, "you had better go home and play pin. Here is where he dodged you,\par
and now he is off again, with an hour or more start." We worked until\par
after midnight and gave Cahir such a "turning over" that the inhabitants\par
won't soon forget, but could not get hold of the least trace, except at\par
one place (Maloy's), where a woman said a stranger came in at supper\par
time, who said he was an American seeing the people in their homes. We\par
cross-questioned the man, but could get nothing out of him more than\par
that you had departed.\par
\par
"'At last we gave it up, went to the hotel to get some sleep, which we\par
needed badly, and the next day went to Dublin, heard about the finding\par
of your neck-wrapper at the Cathedral Hotel, and knocked about Ireland\par
for some time. During this time we arrested several persons, but soon\par
discovered none of them was the right party, and we never obtained a\par
genuine trace until you were discovered later in Edinburgh.'"\par
\par
[Illustration: MARKET CROSS, EDINBURGH.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXVII.\par
\par
THE FLOWERS IN THE PRIMROSE WAY ARE SWEET.\par
\par
\par
As narrated in an earlier chapter, I left England two days before the\par
first lot of forged bills were sent in. I left serene and confident of\par
the future. My departure was a happy event in a double sense. All my\par
negotiations had been carried on at a considerable expense of nerve, and\par
in leaving I left everything in such trim that success seemed certain,\par
with all chance of danger eliminated from the venture. I felt that the\par
trying toil was now all over, with nothing for me to do but to reap the\par
harvest, and that without effort or care on my part.\par
\par
So, when the late November sun looked down on me--I crossed by daylight\par
this time--standing on the deck of that same wretched Channel steamer,\par
it looked on a happy man. I did not know then that success in wrongdoing\par
was ever a failure. The anxious toil of the London and Continental\par
negotiations was a thing of the past. Was I not young; wealth was or\par
soon would be mine; was I not in perfect health, body sound and\par
digestion good, and, above all, was not the woman I loved awaiting me in\par
Paris, to give herself to me, in all her youth and beauty, and then\par
somewhere across the Western waters would I not find in some tropic seas\par
a paradise, which gold would make mine, where I could bear my bride, and\par
there, turning over a new leaf, live and die with the respect of all\par
good men mine?\par
\par
Here was a stately structure I was going to erect, but how rotten the\par
foundation! I, in my egotism, fancied, in my case, at least, the eternal\par
course of things would be stayed, and that justice would grant me a\par
clean bill of health. She did give me that, but it was long years after,\par
and only when she had had from me her pound of flesh to the very last\par
ounce.\par
\par
I joined my sweetheart and her family at the Hotel St. James, Rue\par
Saint-Honore. She was an English lady, and for a whole year our\par
courtship had been going on, and now, our wedding day being fixed a week\par
ahead, we all set out sightseeing and having a good time generally. I\par
now engaged the coachman I had met before as my valet, and a very good,\par
all-around, handy man he proved to be. Of course I was anxious to hear\par
that the first coup on the bank had succeeded, but I was tolerably\par
confident it was all right. Had it fallen through it would have proved\par
awkward for me. In that event the Paris climate would have been too warm\par
for me, and I would have had to find a score of excuses to hasten our\par
marriage and leave for the Western World as speedily as possible.\par
\par
I had a four-in-hand coach, and we drove everywhere in and around Paris,\par
once to Versailles and on to Fontainebleau, where we dined, a merry\par
party. What a strange world is this, what a stage it is, ever crowded\par
with tragedies, too! How absolutely in the dark we are as to the motives\par
and actions of men.\par
\par
There I was, the centre of merry pleasure parties in gay Paris. A young\par
dude, driving my four-in-hand, and yet a criminal, waiting in hourly\par
expectation a telegram announcing success in a great plot which, when it\par
exploded, was destined to startle the business world, and to hurl me\par
from the summit of happiness, where I was reveling, apparently free from\par
care, to the misery of a dungeon, banishing the happy smiles from my\par
face and the joyous ring from my voice, leaving in place of the smiles\par
the sombre gloom of the prison, and in place of the snatches of song and\par
eager accents I was wont to speak with, the hushed voice subdued to\par
prison tones.\par
\par
Late one morning, on opening my eyes, my first thought was: It will be\par
hit or miss at the Bank of England within the next sixty minutes. We had\par
engaged for a coaching party to Versailles and were to dine there. I\par
left for the drive that day with a dim fear that before the sun set I\par
might be under the necessity of leaving Paris in a hurry.\par
\par
When starting for Versailles I left my servant behind to wait for the\par
expected telegram, and to bring it to me by rail. We were at dinner, and\par
I was just raising a glass of champagne to my lips when I saw my valet,\par
Nunn, crossing the esplanade. He entered the room and handed me a\par
telegram. Tearing open the envelope I read:\par
\par
"All well. Bought and shipped forty bales."\par
\par
That meant the first lot for $40,000 had gone through safely. It was\par
certainly a great relief. The next day I received $25,000 in United\par
States bonds, from George in London, my first share of the proceeds. I\par
sold the bonds in Paris, receiving payment in French notes.\par
\par
On Thursday, the day before our marriage, I had a telegram from Mac and\par
George to meet them in Calais, and to Calais I had to go. I arrived\par
there at midnight, just before the Dover steamer got in, and was on the\par
pier to meet them. We exchanged warm greetings; as we did so Mac placed\par
a small but very heavy bag in my hands, and they began laughing over my\par
surprise. It contained L4,000 in sovereigns, and was stuffed with bonds\par
and paper money. We went to a hotel near by, and there they counted out\par
to me the very nice sum of $100,000 in gold, bonds and French money. As\par
they were going back on the same steamer, and I was to return to Paris\par
by the train carrying the passengers of the steamer just arrived, we had\par
only a brief half hour's talk. After giving me the money we went out\par
and sat down on the pier, and that conversation and scene are forever\par
impressed on my memory. I shall make no attempt to describe either, but\par
could both be put on the stage, with the audience in possession of a\par
full knowledge of the enterprise we were embarked in, there would be\par
seen a picture of human life such as the novelist or playwright never\par
had the imagination nor the daring to depict. To the earnest student of\par
human life it would have been a revelation.\par
\par
There we were, three earnest, ambitious young men, enthusiastic for all\par
that was good and noble. I about to wed a pure-souled woman, who thought\par
me an angel of goodness, and about to fly with my plunder and bride to\par
Mexico. My two companions were returning to London to continue carrying\par
out a giant scheme of fraud against a great moneyed institution, but\par
there we were, with $100,000 in plunder at our feet, sitting under the\par
stars, listening to the dash of the waves, and talking not at all like\par
pirates and robbers, but much more like crusaders setting out on a\par
crusade, or like pilgrims going on a pilgrimage.\par
\par
I told my friends I should go to the City of Mexico for a year or two,\par
and then meet them somewhere in America where we would unite our wealth\par
to inaugurate some scheme that would benefit thousands in our own\par
generation and millions in the generations to come. We would hedge\par
ourselves about with kindly deeds, so live as to win the respect of all,\par
and when under the sod live in the eyes and mouths of men.\par
\par
Too soon the whistle sounded, and we had to say good-bye, which we did\par
in an enthusiasm that told how deeply we felt. We were walking in the\par
Primrose Way, its flowers and songs were sweet, and we thought their\par
perfume and melody eternal.\par
\par
I again arrived in Paris at daylight, but early as it was, my\par
sweetheart, escorted by my servant, was waiting my arrival. It was our\par
wedding morning. During our drive to the hotel, radiant with joy, she\par
told me the separation had been a cruel one, and she was so happy to\par
know we should never be separated again!\par
\par
At 4 o'clock that afternoon we were married at the American Embassy.\par
\par
I had told every one I was going to leave the next day for Havre, to\par
embark for New York. Our baggage was all packed and placed in a van,\par
which I accompanied to the Havre station, and had stored there. Sunday I\par
purchased one ticket to Bayonne, one for Madrid and one to Burgos, each\par
from different agencies. On Sunday morning I took a van to the Havre\par
station, and transferring our baggage to the road into Spain, checked\par
all of it to Madrid.\par
\par
My purpose was to sail by the Lopez & Co. steamer El Rey Felipe from\par
Cadiz to Mexico, which was advertised to sail ten days later.\par
\par
We were married very quietly on Friday, and our friends, wisely\par
recognizing the fact that young married people like to be alone, the\par
next day said good-bye and returned to Normandy. We spent a quiet and\par
happy Saturday and Sunday, and on Sunday night we left--my wife, servant\par
and self--for Cadiz, via Madrid. My wife, like all English people, knew\par
little of geography, and had such hazy notions of America that she\par
thought it quite the thing to go to such an outlandish and far off\par
quarter of the globe as America via a Spanish port. Columbus, she knew,\par
had gone that way, and why should not we?\par
\par
We had an all-night ride to Bayonne in one of those antiquated\par
compartments used in railway carriages all over Europe, but the ride was\par
not tedious, nor was the night long. This little earth had no happier\par
couple, and, talking of the happy years that lay before us, the night\par
rushed by like a fairy dream.\par
\par
Where was my conscience? Why, my dear reader, I had sung it such a song\par
that it was delighted with the music, and had, I was going to say, gone\par
to sleep, but it had not. It was wide awake, and we were good chums. We\par
both--conscience and I--had persuaded ourselves it was a virtuous deed\par
to do evil that good might come. My conscience was perhaps as old as the\par
sun, but I myself was young and too inexperienced to see the fallacy of\par
the argument, since I myself was the doer of the wrong; but, of course,\par
I should have hotly denounced any other such philosopher as a villain\par
and rogue.\par
\par
The night flew by, and to our surprise we found 240 miles had slipped\par
away and we were in Bayonne. Thirty minutes more and we were speeding\par
south, and soon crossed the Bidassoa, the boundary between France and\par
Spain. Then my wife saying, "Now I will sleep," laid her head on the\par
shoulder of the happiest man in or out of Spain, and in ten minutes her\par
regular breathing told me she was in the land of dreams.\par
\par
The Pyrenees, in dividing France and Spain, stand between two distinct\par
peoples, and as the centuries go by the streams of national life meet,\par
but only to repel each other, never to mingle. One has but to cross the\par
bank to realize that he is among a different race. Dress, food and\par
cooking--social life, religious devotion, modes of thought--are all\par
different. To us here in America it is difficult to realize that so\par
slight a thing as a mountain barrier, easily traversed, crossed by many\par
defiles and good roads, should continue to separate two distinct\par
peoples. But so it is. Stranger still, for nearly all time the\par
inhabitants of the Spanish mountains have been more or less opposed to\par
the people of the Spanish plains, and every century has seen several\par
insurrections among the mountaineers. In 1872 and '73 the Carlists held\par
the mountains and more or less fusillading was going on. The possibility\par
of my way being blocked by the Carlists never entered into my\par
calculations.\par
\par
The railway from Bayonne to Madrid is owned in Paris, and it seems that\par
the directors were paying blackmail to Don Carlos, ostensibly to him,\par
but really to several marauding bands who plundered under the name of\par
fighting for the Don, upon the understanding that the railroad was not\par
to be meddled with. The directors had been paying 100,000 francs a\par
month. As will be easily believed, there was a difficulty in the\par
distribution of the money among so many greedy and inartistic robbers,\par
and the discontented determined to hold up the railroad itself and stop\par
all trains. Unluckily, the train we were on was the one they proposed to\par
experiment on first, and they proposed drastic measures, too--in fact,\par
had blown up or down a short tunnel, and torn up the rails in front of\par
our train. As we crossed the frontier a French gendarme and Spanish\par
civil guard appeared, demanding passports. It was, of course, a sure\par
thing that I had them all right. It is a safeguard under the protection\par
of which the man who has anything to fear slips through the fingers of\par
frontier guards and police, while the honest man quite frequently\par
neglects the necessary formalities and is detained.\par
\par
Our train crossed the bridge over the Bidassoa and we were on Spanish\par
soil. Soon we entered the gorges of the Pyrenees, and while speculating\par
whether I should awaken my wife to see the magnificent scenery all\par
necessity for awakening any one on that train was over. Three or four\par
musket shots rang out, our train was off the rail, and after a crash or\par
two came to a sudden stop, and then a babel arose, while the train was\par
surrounded by armed men. It was laughable. It seemed like an opera\par
bouffe, the real thing, this motley array of brigands, all trying to\par
maintain under difficulties the grave Spanish exterior.\par
\par
One monkey of 18 or 19 years, armed, came to our compartment, and,\par
pointing to my chain, said he wanted it and my watch. None of us\par
understood Spanish, but we all comprehended his meaning readily. I\par
refused to make him a gift, and got rid of him easily.\par
\par
We were all ordered to alight and our captors seemed inclined to be\par
ugly. Myself and party were about the only well-dressed people on the\par
train, and, seeing a priest close by, I went up to him, and ascertaining\par
he could speak French, I began, in very bad French indeed, to threaten\par
with very dire consequences Don Carlos and every band of Carlists who\par
dares to annoy an English Duke and Duchess, and demanded instant shelter\par
and a guard for my wife, the Duchess. We could hardly keep from\par
laughing, it was so very like a melodrama. My wife thoroughly enjoyed\par
the situation, and I should have done so too, had I not had such strong\par
reasons for quick passage through Spain to blue water on the South, for\par
I desired to speedily put some leagues of Neptune's domain between\par
myself and the Old World.\par
\par
The priest, although a sallow, sombre fellow, was a very good one, and\par
seemed to realize the gravity of the situation, for, calling the chief\par
to him, he warned him to be careful. That gentleman came up, and drawing\par
himself up said very proudly: "Sir, we are soldiers, not robbers." I\par
said I was very glad to know it, and demanded to be informed if I was a\par
prisoner or not, and was told I was not, but with the same breath he\par
said he would be obliged to detain us for a few days. There was a fonda,\par
or inn, close by, and leaving my wife there, I finally managed by a\par
liberal use of money to secure an ox-cart, and by virtue of great\par
generalship on the part of myself and servant, got all our baggage out\par
of the wrecked train and safely up to the inn.\par
\par
Spaniards are provokingly slow, but by riding mule-back five miles away\par
I succeeded in seeing the local commander of the Carlist forces, and he\par
promised to send me the next day a pass through the lines, going either\par
south or north. I got him also to include in the pass my fellow\par
passengers. I did this because there was a Portuguese family who had\par
tickets for South America. They were then on their way to embark at\par
Lisbon, and the old gentleman, the head of the family, was very weak and\par
ill.\par
\par
My safe plan would have been to return to France, make my way to Brest\par
and embark from there to New York, and that would have been my course\par
had I had any conception of the slowness of the Spanish officials and of\par
the fierce storms and snows that dominate the passes of the Pyrenees in\par
Winter.\par
\par
We were informed by many officials, railway guards, Custom House\par
officers, Carlists, etc., that by crossing thirty miles south we would\par
pass the lines and get to a little town on the railway where trains left\par
frequently for Madrid. The Spaniards about the place would never have\par
let us start out on that perilous trip had it not been for the money\par
there was in it. I had secured at a round price three century old\par
bullock carts, and in the afternoon of the second day we got off. I had\par
all the women and the sick Portuguese in one cart, with the two other\par
carts ahead heaped with luggage. Thus there were eight bullocks, four\par
mules and (unlucky number) thirteen men engaged.\par
\par
I had very misty notions as to our destination, but took it for granted\par
the baker's dozen of natives I had with me knew what they were about.\par
Snow was everywhere, and we were mounting up, up, up, on wheels, but I\par
supposed the highest altitude was only four or five miles away, and that\par
the down grade would be easy until we reached some snug inn where we\par
would find shelter for man and beast. Then an early start by daylight\par
and our novel jaunt would come to an end in civilization and a railway.\par
But I did not know Spaniards, their country, the Pyrenees, nor what\par
blizzards can blow in sunny Spain.\par
\par
Myself and my servant Nunn trudged on alongside the cart with the women.\par
It took an hour to get out of sight of the fonda, and then we struck a\par
fine, wide military road that wound in and around the mountains, but\par
always up and deep in snow. Three, four o'clock came and still no sign\par
of the summit, but with the road winding in and out for miles ahead. The\par
sky began to darken, and without warning down came the snow. Then\par
frequent halts of the caravan to rest the cattle. Deeper grew the snow,\par
and as the darkness began to settle down I realized the responsibility I\par
had unwittingly taken on my shoulders. I had four delicate women in my\par
forlorn party and found myself fast in the midst of a snowstorm, in a\par
wild pass of the Pyrenees. I recognized one blessing, however, and was\par
profoundly grateful--the air was calm--and though the snow fell thick\par
and fast it was not driven by a storm.\par
\par
Nunn proved to be thoroughly reliable, helpful and full of cheer.\par
Between us we kept up the spirits of the party. But all hands began to\par
grow hungry. Fortunately I had in my baggage a large pate de foie gras.\par
That is a fat goose liver pie, and it was fat, happily so, as it went\par
the further. Then I got rugs and wraps out of my trunks for the women\par
and a couple of bottles of brandy, and administered liberal doses all\par
round. I soon had them happy and full of courage. It was certainly\par
better to have them full of Dutch courage in a fool's paradise than to\par
have them awake to their position, for I quite expected it would end in\par
a night camp-out in the snow and sending an empty cart for supplies. Two\par
hours after dark we came to a dead halt, and my guides--they were\par
beauties--said they could go no further; the oxen could not pull the\par
carts. There was a fonda, they said, two miles away, but did not show\par
any disposition to help to get there, and for that matter did not seem\par
to care whether we did or not. I ordered them to leave the middle cart\par
behind and divide the teams, one team to be added to the front cart and\par
one to be hitched in front of the mules. Our interpreter was one of the\par
Portuguese women, but we did not get on very well, the Spaniards\par
objecting to anything being done, all of them apparently waiting for\par
the Virgin or some of the saints to come to our aid; but as neither did,\par
Nunn and I were exasperated, and finally took the matter into our own\par
hands. By my orders, despite the energetic protests of the drivers, he\par
unhitched the oxen from the middle team, and between us we got them to\par
the mule cart, hitched them in front of the mules and pulled out and\par
past the other carts. Here the Spaniards halted us, and after an angry\par
altercation in the dark--and it was dark--they agreed to go on. So,\par
taking a yoke of oxen from our cart, they were put in front of the four\par
of the first cart, and off we started. Nunn volunteered to stand by and\par
guard the stranded cart; so giving him two blankets and a little brandy\par
we drove off in the darkness. But not until, in sight of all, I had\par
given him a revolver, and each of the unlucky thirteen a good nip of\par
brandy. My anxiety about serious results was over as soon we started,\par
and in an hour and a half we halted in front of a wretched mountain inn,\par
patronized by muleteers, with the first story for a stable, but none of\par
us were disposed to be particular. A supper of Spanish beans was soon\par
ready, and then a bed was made up on the floor, and the women were soon\par
asleep. After seeing that the mules and oxen were fed, I took half an\par
hour's nap. Then with two drivers we started back, taking three yoke of\par
oxen. What a tramp I had back through the snow and storm! I was very\par
happy, however, for I knew my wife and party were safely sheltered, and\par
the excitement of action kept one from being gloomy.\par
\par
In due time we found our stray, hitched to and started, but it was hard\par
pulling and the exhausted oxen had to come to frequent halts. At last,\par
just as I was beginning to feel tired, we came to the fonda.\par
\par
The snow had slackened, but the wind was beginning to blow, so Nunn and\par
I carried all the luggage and traps into a corner of the stable below,\par
and tumbling down into the hay we were soon in the land of dreams. In my\par
dreams I was on a shoreless sea in a bark that silently and swiftly\par
circled around. Dark clouds closed in on all sides, while my boat sailed\par
between ever-narrowing walls, the clouds still closing in, until a giant\par
hand grew out from a ragged edge of the cloud wall, which, seizing the\par
prow of my boat, pulled it into the gloom and darkness. I felt the\par
clouds brushing my cheek. I heard the roar of falling water, and felt\par
that my doom was sealed. I thought of my wife, and, trying to call her\par
name, was dumb. I looked behind. Far off and far up there was a glow of\par
rosy light, and within the aureole was her face, full of sorrow, looking\par
at me with pity in every feature. As I looked, her face was slowly\par
eclipsed by a cloud. Then with one cry I plunged into the sea--and\par
awoke.\par
\par
That dream would easily have joined the long procession of forgotten\par
dreams, but it was recalled many a time during many years. And, try as I\par
might, I felt it to be a portent and a prophecy.\par
\par
When I awoke in the morning I was dumfounded to find a blizzard blowing\par
that the cattle could not face, and with every appearance of\par
continuance. In reply to my inquiries I learned they sometimes blew in\par
those altitudes for a week. This was unpleasant news for me, and the\par
prospect made me nervous. It was now Thursday, the fourth day since our\par
departure from Paris. And what might have happened in London in that\par
time! Here was I as completely isolated from the outside world and from\par
all news about my companions in England as if on a desert isle. For all\par
I knew discovery might have been made, and full details of the fraud\par
might be blazing in the press of Europe. I began to fear I had run into\par
a trap. To make matters worse, the steamer El Rey Felipe was advertised\par
to sail Monday from Cadiz, and to miss her seemed danger indeed.\par
\par
[Illustration: PRISONERS WAITING TRIAL, AT NEWGATE, RECEIVING VISITORS.]\par
\par
I was a prisoner in a wretched inn in a defile of the Pyrenees, with a\par
civil war raging, and no telling what might arise to detain us. Our\par
objective point was only some thirty-five miles away, but with roads\par
deep in snow, with wretched cattle and more wretched Spaniards for\par
drivers, there was poor prospect of making headway. I felt it would\par
never do for me to suffer longer detention.\par
\par
I determined to leave my wife and baggage in charge of Nunn, to put the\par
$120,000 I had in a bag and start back to the French frontier, cross\par
into France and catch the Saturday steamer from Havre to New York,\par
explaining to my wife that important business demanded my presence in\par
America, that she could follow on the next steamer and that I would meet\par
her on arrival.\par
\par
In the mean time my unlucky thirteen were happy. For were they not\par
sheltered, with plenty of food and high wages, all out of the pocket of\par
the great lord the Virgin herself must have sent to them? In fact, they\par
were winning from me what to them was a fortune. I was paying each man a\par
dollar a day and $5 for each team and cart.\par
\par
From my experience I must give the Spaniards a good name for honesty. Of\par
course, they were charging me cut-throat prices, but they were poor,\par
and wealthy lords did not often come their way. Aside from that they\par
were very honest. Many things, such as rugs, shawls, lunch baskets,\par
dressing cases, etc., that must have seemed of value to them, lay around\par
everywhere, but not a single article was missing during the entire trip.\par
\par
All day long the blizzard blew. It was a novel situation, and how I\par
should have enjoyed it had I only possessed that greatest of all\par
blessings--a good conscience! As it was, I was in misery, and could find\par
no peace, not even in my wife's smiles and evident content to be\par
anywhere with me.\par
\par
I saw that the cattle were well cared for and that the men had both food\par
and wine. Then my servant skirmished around and decapitated sundry\par
chickens he found. So we had roast chicken three times a day, and as I\par
had a case of brandy in my luggage, we did not suffer. Nunn roasted the\par
chickens, made the punch, got the Spanish men and women to dance for our\par
entertainment, and made himself generally of service. About midnight the\par
storm broke up, and to my great satisfaction the stars came out. That\par
night I slept in the same room with the women, with a sheet hung between\par
us.\par
\par
At 5 o'clock I had all hands up and breakfast under way. I ordered the\par
drivers and hangers on to have the teams hitched up and ready at\par
daylight. They all ate breakfast heartily enough, but were not zealous\par
about starting out. They made all sorts of pretexts and excuses to avoid\par
leaving their comfortable quarters. Certainly the road was not an\par
inviting prospect, there being quite eighteen inches of snow, but I was\par
determined to start one way or the other, either south with the party or\par
north alone. After long argument they, thinking they had me at their\par
mercy, refused to hitch up the cattle to make the attempt. I at once\par
paid and dismissed them all. Determining to set out immediately alone\par
for the French frontier, carrying only a small bag slung over my\par
shoulder, and concealing the bonds and paper money on my person, I would\par
leave the greater part of the gold in charge of my wife. I knew Nunn\par
would be a trusty guard to her.\par
\par
I had not given her any intimation of my purpose, but got my bag ready,\par
and, secreting about me the bonds and paper money, I took my wife into a\par
room, and, first telling her she must be very brave, explained my plan,\par
pointing out I must not miss the Saturday steamer. She should follow on\par
the next, and I would leave her $20,000. But she pleaded to go with me,\par
said she would be no encumbrance, would ride mule-back to the railway,\par
no matter how far away. I then called Nunn and told him I should leave\par
him in charge of the baggage, and that we were going to set out at once.\par
I praised his fidelity, and informed him I would make him a present\par
when he arrived all safe in New York with the baggage. But when the sick\par
man and his family were told we were going they raised a howl. The women\par
all hung on me crying and imploring me not to leave them to despair and\par
death. They would all perish, etc.\par
\par
[Illustration: Henry Hawkins, Esq. Q.C. Hon. Sir. J. Kellog, KKT Judge\par
of the Queen's Bench Rt. Hon. Sir R. J. K. Cockburn, Chief High Justice\par
of England]\par
\par
\par
[Illustration: The Lord Chancellor. Sir C. Russell J.C. Queen's\par
Counsel.]\par
\par
\par
[Illustration: Witnesses. Clerk of The Old Bailey. "I object My Lord".]\par
\par
I had secured a good saddle mule, but with a man's saddle, and my wife\par
was sensible enough not to make an outcry over the prospect of a ride\par
man-fashion. She came out warmly clad and mounted the mule, and I\par
strapped some rugs and a bundle of lunch behind the saddle. The owner of\par
the mule was at his head, halter in hand, ready to lead off. The entire\par
population were out staring open-mouthed. I delivered a speech to my\par
lucky-unlucky thirteen, telling them in the best way I could that I was\par
going in order to deliver them all over to the vengeance of the military\par
chief of the district. That I should accuse them as robbers and thieves,\par
and that they might look for anguish that would wring their hearts and\par
souls.\par
\par
They were greatly moved, and, pulling out my watch, I informed them by\par
pantomime and bad Spanish that if they got the teams in harness and the\par
luggage all packed on the carts in twenty minutes I would take them into\par
my favor and resume our journey southward.\par
\par
Spaniards are proverbially slow. But these Spaniards were not slow, and\par
a very few minutes saw us all once more mounted on our cart, with the\par
two baggage carts following, and on our rocky way southward.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXVIII.\par
\par
FEAR SAYS "NO" TO HAPPINESS.\par
\par
\par
We passed during the day a military post and several squads of armed\par
men. Poor fellows! they were wretchedly equipped, so far as garments\par
went. They all examined us curiously, but did not offer to stop or\par
question us while I marched on ahead of the cavalcade like a drum major,\par
giving the military salute to each party as we passed. I ought to have\par
been fatigued, but I was not. After about five miles of uphill work we\par
began to descend. The road was a masterpiece of engineering, and well it\par
might be, for it was one of five military roads the great Napoleon\par
ordered to be constructed across the Pyrenees, and it was done in a\par
thoroughly workmanlike manner. It wound in and out and along defiles of\par
stern beauty.\par
\par
We halted for rest and refreshment at noon, and again at 4 o'clock for\par
an hour. At the last place we found some Carlist officers, one a young\par
Englishman, who was a good fellow and most attentive. He was an\par
aide-de-camp on Don Carlos' staff. He told me there was no chance of his\par
side winning, but he was in it for the fun of the thing and in hope of\par
seeing some fighting. He had taken part in a number of skirmishes, and\par
was by no means satisfied yet. He volunteered to escort us through the\par
lines, and was evidently more than pleased to meet an English lady in\par
the person of my wife.\par
\par
It was beautiful to see him order about my muleteers and bully them up\par
hill and down dale, not hesitating to use his whip on them. About 5\par
o'clock we started off in great shape, having some twenty miles to go to\par
the little town on the railway south of the Pyrenees. We had two\par
lanterns and a number of torches; it was a picturesque caravan in the\par
darkness. The young officer rode beside the first cart, conversing with\par
my wife, while I walked in the rear. We had reason to congratulate\par
ourselves over our escort, he being a brave and brilliant fellow and\par
evidently a person of importance. He little thought whom he was\par
escorting. I was pleased on my wife's account, as he was company for\par
her, and, altogether, she thoroughly enjoyed the novelty of the whole\par
situation.\par
\par
We had made a fine bed of hay and blankets for our sick man.\par
Nevertheless, he was a source of much anxiety and trouble. At last, to\par
the intense relief of all, we heard far away the shrill whistle of a\par
locomotive. It was sweet music to my ears, for I realized the peril of\par
the delay. We had now arrived at the base of the southern slope of the\par
Pyrenees and the plain stretched out before us. We had just passed\par
through an intrenched camp that guarded the entrance to the valley. Our\par
escort had ridden ahead, and not satisfied with smoothing the way for\par
us, had turned out the guard to do us honor. We halted for a few\par
minutes, and several uniformed officers came forward and were introduced\par
to my wife and me. It was a picturesque scene. The mantle of snow\par
covering all, the strange-looking mountaineers, the eager-faced, boyish\par
officers--French, English, Austrian--all soldiers of fortune, who, in\par
the dearth of great wars, were seeking fame in the inglorious civil\par
contest; our torches casting fantastic shadows until the forest-covered\par
mountain, dark and frowning, though snow lay everywhere, seemed peopled\par
with hosts of men--all made a picture never to be forgotten by some of\par
the observers.\par
\par
Another mile and our escort had to leave us, but the town, standing dark\par
against the snow, was in plain view. By his advice I went ahead on foot\par
with two men, in case any of "the enemy" were prowling around, but found\par
none until we arrived in the town; then a scene of great excitement to\par
the townspeople arose.\par
\par
We were examined and cross-examined, and our statements taken down in\par
writing and sworn to by all hands. In the mean time I had made beds for\par
our sick man and the ladies in the waiting room of the station, and\par
about 2 o'clock I went to sleep. The station was fortified and full of\par
soldiers, but I did not care, being told the Madrid train would start at\par
daylight; if so, I would be in time for El Rey Felipe, and would be\par
sailing out of Cadiz harbor on Monday over the blue water, westward ho!\par
\par
After a two hours' nap I was up, paid off my lucky thirteen, giving them\par
a present in addition to their due, with a written paper certifying that\par
they were honest and brave, and had delivered me and mine in safety.\par
\par
The weather continued very cold, and when the train, consisting of two\par
passenger and one baggage car, arrived we found there were no heating\par
arrangements, and we shivered at the thought of an all-day's ride\par
without fire or heat across that windy plain. I determined to have a\par
compartment to ourselves, for my wife and I had not had a moment's\par
privacy since the smash-up of the train. So we fixed up a bed on the\par
floor of a compartment for our sick man, and I put his family in to look\par
out for him. When the train left we found ourselves, very much to our\par
satisfaction, alone. I had telegraphed ahead to Burges to have hot water\par
cases, then the only mode of heating cars in Europe, ready on our\par
arrival.\par
\par
The engineer of our train was an Englishman. As it was so important that\par
I should not be delayed I gave him a sovereign and his stoker another,\par
and asked him as a favor to make time. He said he would and kept his\par
word. But arriving at Burgos we found that the train from Santander\par
going south was two hours late, so my wife and I started out to see the\par
famous town.\par
\par
After a short view we made our way to the Cathedral, and it was a sight!\par
It is one of the many sacred edifices which the piety of former ages\par
bequeathed our own. One of these sacred buildings--like the Strasbourg\par
and Cologne Cathedrals, in the construction of which generation after\par
generation of pious souls--pious according to the fashion of their\par
times--had given their days to the building and decoration of the\par
cloister or church where their lives were lived, and all was done with\par
loving and patient care.\par
\par
We in our day may sneer at the monks and brothers of the Dark Ages, but\par
in those times of rude violence all gentle hearted, scholarly souls\par
found in the sanctity and quiet of the cloister the only refuge open to\par
them, and they did good work, both in the domain of mind and in the\par
world of material things. Much that was "piety" and much that was\par
"faith" in their day is termed superstition in ours; but who will deny\par
that the simple piety and credulous faith of their day was a million\par
times better than the restless skepticism and sad unrest of ours?\par
\par
At Burgos I tried to get an English paper, but none was to be had and no\par
one there had ever seen one.\par
\par
But here some startling news came flashing over the wires. Nothing less\par
than that there had been a revolution at Madrid, the capital. Amadeo,\par
the lately elected king, had suddenly resigned, and a republic had been\par
proclaimed with Castelar at the head.\par
\par
I began to see more and more what a fool I was to let myself be caught\par
at such a time in such a land, but still had so much confidence in my\par
good fortune that I felt I would be on time for the steamer on Monday.\par
\par
It was now 3 o'clock Friday. We were all aboard for Madrid and just\par
pulling out of the station. We would be due there the next morning. From\par
Madrid to Cadiz there is only one through train in twenty-four hours,\par
and that leaves seven mornings a week; but, as it runs only fifteen\par
miles an hour, and is seldom on time at that, one must figure on taking\par
an entire twenty-four hours for the journey. Still, as we would be due\par
Saturday morning, I had a big margin for delay.\par
\par
At last we were off. On the train and in every group we passed there\par
were signs of subdued excitement. Between Royalists and Republicans\par
sharp lines were evidently drawn which soon were to culminate in bloody\par
conflict.\par
\par
Soon after 10 o'clock we arrived in the walled town of Avila, about\par
eighty miles from the famous Escurial built by the second Philip, and\par
about 150 miles from Madrid. Here we got an excellent dinner and good\par
coffee. But dinner was spoiled for me by the disastrous intelligence\par
that martial law had been proclaimed and that the Government had seized\par
the roads running north from Madrid to transport troops.\par
\par
Here was a pretty pickle! I was enraged. I saw the chief of the railway\par
at Avila, but he was a fool, and under the unwonted state of affairs had\par
lost what little head he ever had.\par
\par
So once more our baggage was all piled out of the train, and once more\par
we had to go into camp on the floor of the station, with a terrific din\par
around us.\par
\par
I arose early, and looking up the telegraph clerk and railway chief, I\par
made them both rich by the present to each of five escudos.\par
\par
Then I telegraphed Castelar and the Minister of War that I was an\par
Englishman, that I had my family with me, and having important business\par
in Madrid I must not be detained in Avila. I demanded that he should at\par
once direct the military officials to send me on to Madrid by special\par
train. I also sent a telegram to Hernandez, president of the road in\par
Paris, offering 5,000 francs for a special train. Another urgent message\par
was sent to the superintendent in Madrid repeating the offer for a\par
special train, the same sum to himself if he expedited the train. I also\par
authorized him to spend a similar amount if necessary in bribing the\par
military authorities.\par
\par
[Illustration: TRIAL OF THE FOUR AMERICANS AT THE "OLD BAILEY," LONDON.]\par
\par
At 11 o'clock I had a long telegram from him saying a train would be\par
made up at Avila. But an hour having passed away, I sent him a message\par
to order up an engine and one car from Madrid. Another message arrived\par
at 12 o'clock, and down came an engine and car.\par
\par
Our baggage was hustled into the three front compartments. I put Nunn\par
and the Portuguese party in one and my wife and I occupied the rear\par
compartment. Thank Heaven! once more alone together. The soldiers and\par
inhabitants flocked around, and we were the observed of all observers.\par
\par
The local railway chief was more than anxious to see us off, as I added\par
another five to the five escudos already given. Just then the telegraph\par
operator flew out with an order for our train to await the arrival of\par
the train from Madrid.\par
\par
I stormed. I kept the wire hot with messages of protest to officials.\par
Two messages came from Madrid saying the delay was but temporary. So\par
there I sat in that musty compartment, my wife by my side and with a\par
heart full of bitterness, for I saw the precious hours slipping away,\par
and with them my chance of taking the Sunday morning train so as to\par
catch the Cadiz steamer. To miss it, I thought, meant ruin.\par
\par
Hour after hour passed by, and there we sat. My secret cause of unrest\par
had to be kept locked in my breast, while my young wife, all\par
unsuspecting, was merry and happy, chanting little snatches of song and\par
telling me a hundred times she was the happiest of women. She did not\par
care for revolutions, nor for delays. Was she not with me! The sun\par
began to go down the sky, and the shadows fell. Still we sat on,\par
expecting every moment an order to proceed. The suspense was terrible.\par
\par
At last about 6 o'clock an order came to have everything ready to pull\par
out for Madrid at 7, so very reluctantly we dismounted to take supper in\par
the station, and once more got into the car. But no order came. The\par
hours dragged on, and I saw fate closing her hand on me.\par
\par
The night wore on, when suddenly, toward midnight, the operator rushed\par
out of his office and, shouting to the engineer, flew up to our\par
compartment, said good-bye and in a minute we were off. After that long\par
and terrible day it was happiness to be moving.\par
\par
I had given the engineer a tip; he put on steam, and as we flew over the\par
road hope returned. I felt we were safe. At the rate we were going I\par
should have two or three hours to spare. We soon were at the Escurial.\par
As fate would have it we found here an order to run us on a side line\par
and to keep the track clear for a train going north. For two miserable\par
hours we waited and no train. Then I set the wires in motion again, and\par
just as the eastern skies grew gray we started.\par
\par
Soon after midnight I telegraphed to the railway authorities at Madrid\par
to hold the train going south to Cadiz until my arrival, offering $100\par
an hour for every hour's detention.\par
\par
Madrid is situated on a high sandy plain, storm-swept in Winter worse\par
than any plains in Northern Europe. We had a wheezy engine. Four miles\par
out it broke down, and then I gave up the struggle.\par
\par
At 4 o'clock Sunday afternoon, nine hours too late for the Cadiz train,\par
we arrived at Madrid, too late to reach Cadiz by a special train. Not\par
too late could the train have been started off as soon as ordered, but\par
in Spain a special train is an unheard-of thing.\par
\par
Mine from Avila was an innovation, only possible because there was so\par
much money behind it to all concerned at both ends of the line. No\par
Spaniard was ever known to be in a hurry, and no particle of matter\par
between his chin and his sombrero holds any lurking suspicion that\par
anything born of a woman could be in a hurry or have any reason for any\par
such insanity.\par
\par
Here I was at last in the much-longed-for Madrid, but not on time, and I\par
had nothing to do but to put in execution some new plan. Had I even at\par
that late date resolved to go to New York, I could have returned to\par
France by the Eastern route, via Barcelona, and all might have been\par
well.\par
\par
I telegraphed to Lopez & Co. to Cadiz inquiring if they would hold the\par
El Rey Felipe for twenty hours. They replied they were under contract\par
with the Government and had to sail on time. So I said good-bye to that\par
plan.\par
\par
On consulting my memorandum I saw there was a French steamer sailing\par
from St. Nazaire, on the west coast of France, for Vera Cruz, Mexico,\par
which would touch at Santander on Saturday for mails and passengers, and\par
I resolved to go by her; this, of course, meant retracing our way\par
through the hated Avila to Burgos, and changing there for Santander.\par
\par
Here we saw the last of the Portuguese family with their sick member.\par
They said good-bye with every expression of gratitude, and in truth I\par
was glad to see them off. We were all very tired of them, and they had\par
been a serious expense. That is, might have been serious, but as I paid\par
that expense out of the Bank of England's cash I naturally could be\par
liberal in the extreme, and gave a salve to my conscience by reflecting\par
what a good-souled, charitable young man I was in looking out for these\par
strangers and putting my hand freely in my pocket in their behalf.\par
\par
As soon as breakfast was over I hurried to the English Embassy, and\par
there securing files of the London papers looked eagerly and nervously\par
through them. To my intense relief I saw there was nothing in them.\par
Therefore, I knew all was serene in London and that the Old Lady was\par
without doubt giving out sovereigns by the tens of thousands for us.\par
\par
Very much relieved in mind I returned to the hotel, and we set out to\par
see Madrid.\par
\par
[Illustration: A DETECTIVE IDENTIFYING OLD OFFENDERS AT NEWGATE.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXIX.\par
\par
I WATCH THE PYRENEES SINK IN THE SEA, THEN SAIL O'ER GREEN NEPTUNE'S\par
BACK.\par
\par
\par
It was 11 o'clock when we started. The streets were thronged, and the\par
throngs moving in one direction. That was to the street lined on both\par
sides with churches, whose doors were flung wide open to the surging\par
masses. We went with the current and entered a famous church which was\par
crowded with the pious, their souls rapt in their devotion. Like all\par
European churches, there were no seats, but the audience, closely\par
packed, knelt or stood. We joined the worshipers, but looked around with\par
curious eyes. When the prayers were ended the street was one living mass\par
of people, all moving toward the outskirts of the town. We went with the\par
tide, and with the tide entered the arena, where a bull fight was\par
on--curious transition from church to arena. It was a great sight--I\par
mean that of seeing the people--there were 15,000 present in that\par
amphitheatre. It looked just like the old Roman arena, and to us was in\par
all its details intensely interesting.\par
\par
On Monday we visited the picture galleries and museums, and on Tuesday\par
we got our baggage down to the depot once more, and purchasing our\par
tickets we were off for Santander. I was too anxious to enjoy the\par
scenery. We were a day and a night on the journey, and arriving on\par
Wednesday I still had before me three days of anxiety.\par
\par
Being thoroughly sick of Spain, I longed to be on blue water with our\par
good ship's prow pointed to the Western World. Then I felt I could begin\par
to enjoy life. I had a charming wife--delightful companion--and once up\par
anchor all my haunting fears would die, and life's pleasures would be\par
mine to the full. But there in Santander the time dragged wearily. To be\par
sure, I had the English papers, but they were nearly a week on the way,\par
and a bad conscience finds many a cause for fear. I was aching to be\par
aboard. Saturday came at last, and going early down to the headland at\par
the harbor's mouth, with my field glass I anxiously scanned the Bay of\par
Biscay to see if I could discern anywhere on the horizon the smoke of\par
the approaching steamer. Lingering there until the dinner hour, I\par
hastened to the hotel.\par
\par
My wife was merry and happy. I was glad to see her so, and found it\par
difficult to conceal my solicitude. Going both together to the headland\par
we spent most of the afternoon there. Night and then midnight came, and\par
no steamer's lights flashed in the dark waters of the bay. Heartsick and\par
anxious I went to bed, half resolved to take my wife into my confidence,\par
tell her in some measure the truth, and point out to her the necessity\par
of my taking flight, leaving her to follow at her leisure. It would have\par
been a terrible shock to her, but I began to fear that the truth would\par
come to her ears some time.\par
\par
Early the next morning my servant awoke me, asking me to look out of the\par
window. I ran to it, and looking out, there in the bay, just in front of\par
the hotel, lay a steamer of the largest size and magnificent in her\par
beauty. It was a happy sight for me.\par
\par
Nunn hired a boat for our luggage and a second for me, and then, after a\par
hurried breakfast, we boarded the steamer, Nunn following with the\par
baggage. Among other things I had a favorite dressing case, and had\par
given the servant strict orders to keep it under his eye, but as soon as\par
he came aboard he inquired in great agitation if I had brought it off\par
with me. Upon my saying no he was quite overcome, at the same time\par
explaining that he had laid it on top of the baggage in front of the\par
hotel, and some one had stolen it. While he was speaking a passenger\par
came walking by with the identical case in his hand. Nunn flew at the\par
man and seized both him and the bag, and sure enough he had the thief,\par
but I ordered him to let the man go, and he went away shamefaced enough.\par
He little thought when stealing the bag that the owner was going on the\par
same steamer. At last we were afloat, and now I was all eagerness to\par
hear the steam monkey start to bring the anchor a-peak. It is simply\par
amazing how a bad conscience "moldeth goblins swift as frenzy's\par
thought." Even as I stood there I was not at rest, but was impatient and\par
suspicious of every movement from the shore. As the long day dragged\par
slowly on and 4 o'clock came, preparations for getting under way were\par
going rapidly forward. I took my field glasses, stationed myself on the\par
after deck and anxiously scrutinized every boat leaving the shore.\par
Suddenly a boat started out from the head of the bay, pulled steadily by\par
eight rowers, and my conscience told me it meant danger, but the boatmen\par
pulled down along the shore, then suddenly stopped, and I could see that\par
they were passing a bottle around, taking a drink. Soon I discovered a\par
heap on the stern, which on closer inspection proved to be nets, and my\par
fears boiled down showed me they were simply fishermen and I an ass and\par
somewhat ashamed of myself. I felt I had really no cause for fear, even\par
had the steamer remained in harbor for a week. Just then, with a mighty\par
throb, the screw gave a turn, and it was music to my ears. Then the\par
waters of the bay were churned into yeasty waves. The city and shores\par
seemed to glide by and our prow was pointed direct to the blue sea\par
rolling beyond. Soon the joyous billows were toying with our ship, and\par
huge as it was were tossing it as lightly and easily as a child a toy.\par
\par
But, still ill at ease, I walked the deck restless and unhappy.\par
\par
I no longer feared arrest, was confident that never would hand of human\par
justice be laid on me, but I dimly felt that there was a divine justice\par
which would exact retribution. I felt that if there was mind behind this\par
frame of matter we see, then He who made the natural law and decreed a\par
penalty for every infraction must have made an infallible decree for\par
every violation against the moral law. If so, where could we poor\par
insects go or hide, or how scheme or dodge to escape the divine\par
vengeance?\par
\par
But as I stood on the deck that night and watched the mountains sink\par
into the sea I felt this all dimly, and tried to shake off the feeling.\par
I stood fascinated, with many conflicting emotions sweeping through my\par
mind, sadly watching the receding shores of Spain, and just as the\par
highest mountains were sinking in the sea my servant appearing at my\par
side informed me that dinner was ready and my wife waiting. Sending him\par
away and turning my face to the land, I strained my eyes through the\par
gathering gloom to discern the distant shore. Then with a bitter feeling\par
in my heart I set out for the saloon, but stopped and quoting these\par
lines--\par
\par
  "The day of my destiny is over,\par
  And the star of my fate hath declined"\par
\par
--went below.\par
\par
Soon, under the warming influence of wine, forgetting all my forebodings\par
and looking into my wife's face beaming with love and content, I could\par
not refrain from saying to myself: I am a fool to doubt that happiness\par
is mine. Am I not Fortune's favorite? With love, youth, enthusiasm,\par
health and wealth on my side, what else save happy days and nights and\par
long years filled with content can be mine?\par
\par
So, shaking off my forebodings, the eighteen days of our voyage over\par
green Neptune's back were ideal, and we became objects of envy to all\par
the passengers.\par
\par
Our ship was the Martinique, with French officers and crew, and a fine,\par
manly lot of men they were. The passengers were mostly colonial people\par
returning home to the French colonies in the West Indies. They were\par
nice, refined people, but we were rather reserved and kept to ourselves.\par
One of the passengers had a dozen Spanish fighting cocks, and they\par
afforded us much amusement. There were frequent mains on the after deck\par
and sometimes on the dinner table. These were very popular, particularly\par
with the ladies, who were continually asking to have the cocks brought\par
on after dessert. A space would be made in the centre of the table and\par
two cocks placed on it. How they loved fighting! They certainly enjoyed\par
it far better than the spectators. There were four long tables, all\par
crowded, but when the main was started the other tables were deserted\par
and the passengers packed around ours.\par
\par
Our opposite neighbors were two Sisters of Charity who were on their way\par
to the City of Mexico to fill a gap that death had made in the ranks of\par
their order there. They were simple, sainted souls and had never known\par
any life other than the religious, and never emerged from the cloister\par
save only to do deeds of mercy in the country town outside. They had\par
been selected by lot to go to Mexico. We were favored to become fast\par
friends of theirs, and I was glad to have them accept such attentions as\par
we could give. It was delightful to meet such simple, unsophisticated\par
people under circumstances when, they being travelers, the rules of the\par
Church permitted them to throw off their reserve, to associate with\par
strangers and to live--so far as food and drink were concerned--like the\par
people they were associated with for the time.\par
\par
My wife and I grew to like them well, and I was never tired of getting\par
their views of men and things. Truly their lives were a thing apart from\par
the world and the ways of men. They told me with a kind of rapture that\par
the average life of one of their order in Mexico was only five years,\par
and they thought heaven had been very gracious in selecting them, that\par
they might give their lives to the Church and so become members of the\par
mighty army of martyrs who were honored in heaven by looking upon the\par
face of the Virgin and her Son and serving them.\par
\par
They knew nothing of wines and did not suspect the costliness of those\par
which during the entire voyage they drank at my expense.\par
\par
The dinners were rather formal affairs and occupied an hour and a half,\par
and between the good sisters and us two we always finished a bottle of\par
claret and two of champagne, and about a like quantity between dinner\par
and bedtime. I don't believe that up to the hour they left the world\par
they ever quite understood why they were so happy and merry on that\par
voyage.\par
\par
We used to visit the steerage forward nearly every day. There was an\par
unmistakable lady so unfortunate as to be a passenger there. She\par
appreciated our visits, and eventually confided the story of her life to\par
my wife, and what a story it was of woman's love and man's perfidy!\par
\par
I had an electric battery which I frequently took into the steerage to\par
astonish the natives. When I first put a silver piece in a basin of\par
water and told them the man taking it out could keep it, what a rush\par
there was! There was one would-be clever clown who was perfectly willing\par
to test the power of the battery, but was so clever he never would take\par
hold of both handles at once. He dodged around for two or three days\par
greatly pleased with his sharpness, but I determined to have him some\par
day and have him hard when I got him. So one morning when dancing about\par
as usual he happened to be barefooted. Apparently by accident, I upset\par
the basin of water over the deck, making it a good conductor, then\par
accepting his offer to try the machine by holding one handle, I dropped\par
the other on the wet deck and gave him the benefit of the whole power\par
of the battery. He let one terrific yell, then stood rooted to the deck\par
speechless for a moment; then gave vent to a series of whoops that would\par
have made the fortune of a Comanche Indian. When freed from the current\par
the clever fellow made a break for the steerage and never appeared again\par
at any of my electric seances. All those ignorants insisted that my\par
battery was surely el diablo.\par
\par
After eighteen days we cast anchor in St. Thomas harbor, and pleasant as\par
our voyage had been we were glad to see land. We were to stop a day for\par
coaling.\par
\par
Taking the two sisters, we went ashore in one of the many boats\par
surrounding the ship, all manned by scantily robed black fellows. The\par
town, with its hordes of gaudily dressed and noisy blacks, was most\par
interesting. I had hired the boat for the day, so the three black\par
fellows accompanied us around the town. Each wore a stovepipe hat. The\par
remainder of their furniture consisted of cotton shirt and trousers. The\par
men were barefooted, of course.\par
\par
My wife was the typical blue-eyed, golden-haired Englishwoman, and was\par
the observed of all observers in that black mob. I myself was all in\par
white, from canvas shoes to white umbrella. So, between the two sisters\par
in their black robes and white bonnets and our attending boatmen, along\par
with a mob of half-naked black boys that followed, we formed quite a\par
circus and created a commotion in the town.\par
\par
First I took the sisters to the cathedral. Both were grateful and knelt\par
at the altar for a full half hour while we waited. Then after visiting\par
several stores to make some small purchases, we went to a circus showing\par
there that week. I bought ten tickets for my party. Everything they saw\par
in the town was marvelous and strange to them. When we entered the\par
circus tent the sisters were perplexed and thought it must be a new sort\par
of church. But words would fail to express their amazement when they saw\par
the clown and bespangled horseman enter the ring and the performance\par
begin. They were in a new and hitherto undreamed-of world, and gazed in\par
childlike wonderment on the scene, and, like children, only saw the\par
glitter of the spangles and thought both men and women performers were\par
angels of beauty. Even after the thing was over the magic and witchery\par
of it all rested on them. Their hearts were deeply stirred and their\par
thoughts were with the performers. To please them we sat until the\par
audience had dispersed, and, when going out, one of them, speaking of\par
the performers, told my wife they must be "very near to God."\par
\par
Then we went to the hotel. I dispersed my cortege and ordered a room for\par
ourselves and one for the sisters, and we all took a nap until evening.\par
Then we had some negro singing and dancing for our amusement in the\par
courtyard of the hotel, and at 9 o'clock we went out for a moonlight\par
walk under the tropical sky. About 10 we found we had had enough of it\par
and were glad to betake ourselves to bed.\par
\par
We all breakfasted together in the courtyard the next morning and soon\par
after went aboard. At noon up came the anchor and we were off for\par
Havana, our next stopping place, twenty-four hours' sail away. The\par
steamer after one day's detention to take in cargo would continue her\par
voyage to Vera Cruz. It was my intention to go on to that port, and from\par
there across the country to the capital, the City of Mexico. There was\par
no cable to Mexico in 1873, and things there were in rather a primitive\par
condition. Of course, I never anticipated pursuit beyond New York, and\par
took it for granted that my friends at Police Headquarters would squelch\par
it there. But once in Mexico there would have been no danger for me. To\par
be in Mexico was like being in the centre of darkest Africa. There was\par
no extradition treaty, no railroads and no telegraph; above all, I had\par
plenty of cash.\par
\par
I intended to buy an estate near the capital, and settle down for two\par
or three years, and by a liberal expenditure of money secure the\par
friendship of the government officials and the chief people of the\par
country. Official and social morals being not of the best, if my history\par
transpired I would probably become the lion of society, as they would\par
all esteem it a creditable thing to any man to secure a few millions\par
from the English, whose enormous wealth is the plunder of India and all\par
the world for centuries.\par
\par
The next morning I found we were sailing along the Cuban coast, quite\par
near the land, which looked so inviting that I made up my mind to go\par
ashore and stay a month in Havana, so I had my baggage got on deck. Soon\par
after dinner the engines were stopped for some hours for repacking, the\par
captain informing me that it was doubtful whether we should arrive in\par
Havana in time to go ashore that night. At 6 o'clock the sunset gun is\par
fired, the custom house closes and no more debarkations are allowed that\par
day. If I went ashore the next day I must be up and off at an early\par
hour, as the ship sailed at 7.30, so I told the captain if he arrived\par
before 6 o'clock I would go ashore and wait for the next steamer, but if\par
we were late I would go on to Vera Cruz with him.\par
\par
Once having made up my mind to go ashore, I was all eagerness to push\par
matters. To do so I even asked the captain to tell the engineer to force\par
the engines a little if possible. It was well on to 6 o'clock when we\par
steamed past Moro Castle and dropped anchor in the harbor. I engaged two\par
of the boats alongside, our baggage was hurried into them, my wife went\par
down the ladder, and speaking some hurried farewells I ran down after\par
her and sprang lightly into the boat. That instant the sunset gun was\par
fired. Two minutes later and the custom house officers on board would\par
have forbidden my leaving the steamer. I say two minutes, but it was\par
less than half a minute. Half a minute! Thirty seconds changed my\par
destiny.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXX.\par
\par
"HAPPINESS AND I SHAKE HANDS FOR A TIME."\par
\par
\par
Cuba! What a productive and fertile island it is, with its charming\par
climate and lovely scenery! But, as in so many of the green spots of\par
this world, man has blasted and spoiled all that indulgent nature has\par
lavished here. From the days of Columbus the story of Cuba has been one\par
of wholesale murder of natives, of revolutions--later of insurrections,\par
and deadly civil strife, which have ruined whole provinces once covered\par
with large sugar, coffee and tobacco plantations.\par
\par
Slavery now, as in all her past Christian history, is everywhere.\par
Previous to 1861 40,000 slaves were yearly imported in slave ships into\par
the harbor of Havana.\par
\par
Perhaps all men are cruel when they are absolute masters of the lives\par
and fortunes of their fellows and amenable to none for their acts.\par
Certainly the white Cubans, as a rule, are cruel masters in all their\par
dealings with their slaves.\par
\par
Probably to-day, certainly in 1873, most of the large plantations\par
witnessed scenes of cruelty never surpassed in the long annals of human\par
servitude.\par
\par
During my stay I was invited to visit many plantations, but visits to\par
two were enough for me, there being too many signs on the surface of the\par
brutality that lay beneath. I could easily give cases that I saw or\par
heard of, but refrain from doing so here.\par
\par
One day's stay in Cuba convinced us we could spend a month very happily\par
on the island, and, discovering that Don Fernando, the proprietor of\par
the hotel, had a furnished house in a lovely situation to let, we\par
resolved to remain, renting the house for a month at a fixed rate per\par
day. This rate included the ten servants--slaves--in the house, he to\par
furnish good horses and everything except wine. The service proved good,\par
and the cooking exquisite. This was rather expensive, but certainly a\par
handy kind of housekeeping, taking all worry and household cares from my\par
wife's shoulders.\par
\par
There were a large number of American visitors on the island, lovers of\par
and seekers after sunshine and warmth, which they found in abundance\par
while swinging in hammocks under the palm or cocoanut trees, or in\par
strolling along the white strand, with its innumerable sunny coves,\par
while the Winter storms and blizzards were raging in the Northern\par
States. Here we formed many pleasant acquaintances, and, throwing off\par
much of the reserve maintained during the voyage, we mingled freely in\par
the nice but gossipy society which winters there.\par
\par
Our house was on a lovely slope in full view of the Gulf of Mexico, and\par
in the midst of what was more like a tropical plantation than a garden.\par
\par
I made the acquaintance of Gen. Torbert, our Consul, and was introduced\par
by him to the Spanish officials, including the colonel of police. I\par
assiduously cultivated the acquaintance of the latter, and frequently\par
had him out to the house to dinner and lunch, and felt pretty confident\par
that if any telegrams came about me he would certainly bring them to me\par
at once for an explanation. Even if my presence became known, and\par
telegraphic orders for my arrest should arrive, no speedy action would\par
be taken and ample time given me to escape. In all the assemblies,\par
picnics and balls I was gratified to find my wife very much sought after\par
and admired. It was well she had a few happy days; enough misery lay not\par
far ahead.\par
\par
In the mean time I had no word from my friends in London. In fact, they\par
did not know where I was. When I bade them good-bye at Calais they told\par
me not to inform them of my destination until I had got there, and then\par
to do so through some relative.\par
\par
Every day I watched the New York papers to see if there had been any\par
explosion in London, but the silence of the press told me my friends\par
were having an amazing success, and we might expect two or three months\par
more to elapse before there would be any discovery.\par
\par
We had been some weeks in Havana.\par
\par
It was well into the month of February when one day, being in my hammock\par
on the veranda, with my wife sitting near me, my servant rode up with\par
the papers, and, handing me the New York Herald, I leisurely opened it,\par
while chatting with my wife, but could not suppress an exclamation when\par
my eyes fell upon an Associated Press dispatch from London, in staring\par
headlines. They read:\par
\par
  AMAZING FRAUD UPON THE BANK OF\par
  ENGLAND!\par
\par
         *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
  MILLIONS LOST!\par
\par
         *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
  GREAT EXCITEMENT IN LONDON!\par
\par
         *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
    L5,000 REWARD FOR THE ARREST OF THE\par
    AMERICAN PERPETRATOR, F. A. WARREN.\par
\par
    "London, Feb. 14, 1873.\par
\par
    "An amazing fraud has been perpetrated upon the Bank of England by a\par
    young American who gave the name of Frederick Albert Warren. The\par
    loss of the bank is reported to be from three to ten millions, and\par
    it is rumored that many London banks have been victimized to\par
    enormous amounts. The greatest excitement prevails in the city, and\par
    the forgery, for such it is, is the one topic of conversation on\par
    the Exchange and in the street. The police are completely at fault,\par
    although a young man named Noyes, who was Warren's clerk, has been\par
    arrested, but it is believed that he is a dupe.\par
\par
    "The bank has offered a reward of L5,000 for information leading to\par
    the arrest of Warren or any confederate."\par
\par
[Illustration: "I FIRED POINT BLANK, AND DOWN HE WENT AS IF FELLED BY\par
LIGHTNING."--Page 334.]\par
\par
I took a long walk on the beach to think over the situation. I was\par
alarmed over the arrest of Noyes, which I knew ought not to have\par
occurred if the proper precautions had been taken, but I concluded that\par
at the worst his arrest only meant for him a brief incarceration.\par
\par
I knew that no human power and no fear could ever make him betray us.\par
Two things never entered my calculations at all; that is, that my right\par
name would ever transpire, or that George and Mac would ever, by any\par
possibility, be brought into question for the fraud.\par
\par
So I came back from my walk with my plans outlined. It was to remain\par
quietly where we were for a fortnight longer, then take the steamer to\par
Vera Cruz, go to the City of Mexico and there buy an estate, as I had\par
originally proposed. Then, after a few months, leave my wife there and\par
travel incog. through Northern Mexico and Texas, meet Mac and George and\par
afterward return to Mexico.\par
\par
Not a soul in all Europe knew I was in Cuba, and so long as my name did\par
not transpire I was as safe in Cuba as if in the desert.\par
\par
Consequently I determined to go on in the same way since our landing. In\par
the mean while I would watch the papers, and if any signs of danger\par
appeared I could take instant measures for my safety.\par
\par
As the days passed the cable dispatches appearing in the papers\par
increased in volume, and the papers everywhere had editorials, which, as\par
a rule, were humorous or sarcastic, poking fun at the Britishers in\par
general and the Old Lady of Threadneedle Street in particular. Then the\par
comic papers took it up, and from week to week published cartoons\par
intended to be funny.\par
\par
One of the funniest of these came out in one of the New York comics,\par
which appeared after the mail arrived from London with the particulars\par
of the simplicity of the bank officials in their dealings with the\par
mysterious F. A. Warren. This full-page cartoon represented a young dude,\par
seated on a mule, riding down a steep declivity.\par
\par
At the bottom the devil stood, holding in the fingers of his extended\par
hands a quantity of thousand-pound bank notes tempting Warren, and John\par
Bull stood behind the mule, belaboring it with an umbrella and driving\par
Warren down to the devil.\par
\par
I tried to keep the papers from my wife, but one day she came home from\par
a visit with a flushed face and eager to talk, and began telling me\par
about some daring countryman of mine "who had the audacity to rob the\par
Bank of England," and "who ought to have a whipping." On several\par
occasions Americans there asked my opinion as to who the party could be.\par
\par
I always told them he was some clever young scamp, with plenty of money\par
of his own, who did it for the excitement of the thing and from a wish\par
to take a rise out of John Bull.\par
\par
The next French steamer for Mexico was advertised to land at Havana for\par
passengers and mails for Vera Cruz in a few days, and I determined to\par
sail by her. Soon after my arrival I had formed the acquaintance of a\par
wealthy young countryman of mine from Savannah by the name of Gray. We\par
soon became fast friends, and I had him out to dinner nearly every day.\par
He had a warm friend in Senor Andrez, a rich young Cuban planter, and\par
had accepted an invitation to visit his coffee plantation in the Isle of\par
Pines, the largest of all that immense body of islets and keys of the\par
south coast of Cuba in the Carribean Sea, one of the loveliest tropical\par
isles imaginable, and Gray insisted upon my making one of the party.\par
\par
It was proposed to spend a week on the island, and to take three days in\par
going and coming. But if I went then I would be unable to sail on the\par
steamer of the 25th, and would have to wait another week.\par
\par
One day Gray brought Senor Andrez to dinner, along with a common friend,\par
a Senor Alvarez. All three joined in imploring me to make one of the\par
party, promising sport as novel as good; said the wild boars were\par
plentiful; that we would have two days' shark fishing, turning turtles\par
and hunting their eggs, and could vary it by a slave hunt, the jungle\par
and some of the smaller islands being "full of runaways," and as they\par
were by law wild beasts we might be lucky enough to shoot a few of\par
them--shoot, not capture, as the planters knew that a runaway slave who\par
had tasted the joys of freedom if caught was useless as a slave. So, as\par
a matter of sport, as well as a warning to other slaves, they organized\par
yearly hunts to bag a score or two. But so great is the depravity of the\par
human heart that these wretches, in their desperate wickedness, objected\par
to being shot, and at times were guilty of the enormity of shooting back\par
again. History records how, on certain occasions, they did so with such\par
good effect that the hunted became hunters; but these were rare events.\par
\par
After long urging I consented. At the time there were only two short\par
railways in all Cuba. We were to cross the island to the south coast,\par
and there embark for the Isle of Pines in a boat owned by our host,\par
which would be in waiting. The railway would take us to the little\par
hamlet of San Felipe, some forty miles south, and there we were to take\par
horses to the seaport town of Cajio. We were to start on Saturday, two\par
days ahead. My wife did not relish my going, and I disliked it more than\par
she did, but for totally different reasons. Mine were that, as a matter\par
of prudence, I ought to recall my consent and remain in Havana until\par
steamer day, and then sail without fail to Mexico. But fearing the\par
ridicule of my friends, I went, persuading myself that there could be no\par
danger and that everything in London was buried in so dense a fog bank\par
that the detectives would struggle in vain to find a way out of it or\par
any clue to our identity.\par
\par
Had I known of the clever work of the Pinkerton brothers in London and\par
the discoveries in Paris I should have been ill at ease; but had I known\par
that Capt. John Curtin--then a member of the Pinkerton staff in New\par
York, but now (1895.) of San Francisco--had with perfectly marvelous\par
intuition and rare detective skill let daylight into the whole plot, and\par
had reported to his chief that whenever F. A. Warren was discovered he\par
would prove to be Austin Bidwell; I say if I had known this, instead of\par
going off on a ten days' pleasure jaunt into an isolated corner of the\par
world I should have taken instant flight, leaving Cuba, not by the usual\par
modes of departure, but by sailing boat, and alone, for one of the\par
Mexican ports.\par
\par
Capt. Curtin had been detailed to work on the New York end of the case,\par
to look for clues. It seemed a hopeless task. He is a warm friend of\par
mine now, after twenty years, and has long forgiven me for the bullet I\par
lodged in him in 1873. A few years after arresting me in the West Indies\par
he went to San Francisco and started a private inquiry office of his own\par
at 328 Montgomery street. When, after twenty years' incarceration, I\par
arrived there one lovely May in 1892, he was waiting for me at the\par
ferry, and gave me warm greetings, and as hearty congratulations, too,\par
as any man could give another; then introduced me to his friends\par
everywhere, and, in fact, from the hour of my arrival until my\par
departure, three months afterward, was never tired of doing me a service\par
and forwarding my business, so that by his kind offices I made a great\par
success out of what, by reason of the great financial depression, might\par
otherwise have proved a failure. But as Capt. Curtin, after effecting my\par
arrest, having recovered from his wound, was one of the four who took me\par
to England, I will wait until a later chapter to tell how it was he\par
discovered my name and located me in Cuba.\par
\par
On Saturday morning our party of four, accompanied by a following of\par
black fellows and half a dozen dogs, set out by train. Before reaching\par
San Felipe our bones had a shaking. The roadbed was execrable, the\par
trucks of the cars were without springs, and to me it seemed as if we\par
must leave the rails at any moment.\par
\par
In Havana we regarded Don Andrez as a good fellow, but upon our arrival\par
at San Felipe he had grown into a man of importance. When we came to\par
Cajio he had grown into a person of distinction, and at the island he\par
had swollen into a local Caesar. At San Felipe, a mere hamlet, horses\par
were waiting for us and mules for the baggage, but before setting out we\par
went to a nearby hacienda and sat down to what was simply the best lunch\par
of which I ever partook.\par
\par
The town was chiefly remarkable for the number of its fighting cocks. At\par
the hacienda there were dozens, each in its separate\par
compartment--regarded the same as horses and game dogs are in England\par
and America--and half the black boys we met were carrying game birds.\par
\par
At last, starting for Cajio, the road soon degenerated into a mere\par
track, which led through some barren hills with scanty growths of a\par
species of oak without underbrush, and here and there a sprinkling of\par
cacti, and in the lower reaches between the hills grew dense green walls\par
of Spanish bayonet.\par
\par
We were crossing Cuba at its narrowest part, and from San Felipe to\par
Cajio was only some thirty miles. After fifteen miles we came into the\par
fertile coast belt and passed a number of deserted sugar plantations\par
where tropic vegetation was trying to cover up the work of ruin wrought\par
by man. Residences and sugar houses destroyed by fire were very much in\par
evidence. To my surprise I learned that bodies of insurgents--who then\par
held and had held for six years nearly the entire eastern province of\par
Santiago de Cuba and Puerto Principe, and part of the extreme western\par
province of Pinar del Rio--had only a few weeks before landed by night\par
at the port La Playa de Batabano, fifteen miles away, and with the cry\par
of "Free Cuba and death to the Spaniard!" had blotted out the town and\par
then marched into the heart of the country, burning houses, killing the\par
whites and calling upon the slaves to join them in freeing Cuba. Many\par
did, and terrible were their excesses, and terribly did they pay for\par
these. The Spanish soldiers and loyal Cuban volunteers closed in upon\par
them, and at the little hamlet of San Marcos, where we halted and\par
examined the too evident signs of the battle and massacre that followed,\par
they made their last stand, but were no match for their well-armed and\par
disciplined foes. After a desperate struggle they were overpowered, and\par
every surviving soul was butchered by the infuriated soldiers. It was\par
better so. Had they been spared it would have only been for the moment,\par
for by official decree of the Captain-General of Cuba, indorsed by the\par
Madrid Government, every inhabitant within the insurrectionary line,\par
without regard to age or sex, was doomed to death without form of trial.\par
\par
At San Marcos we made a halt to view the scene of the fight and examined\par
the heaps of ashes where the fires were kindled which consumed the\par
bodies of the slain. Two or three were my countrymen. At the time it was\par
quite the thing for venturesome Americans to go and join the rebels and\par
help the fight for "Cuba libre." For some years every few days notices\par
would appear in the press about some Americans having been shot for\par
joining or attempting to join the rebels. This went on until the affair\par
of the steamer Virginus, when her crew and passengers, to the number of\par
150, were shot, the steamer having been captured close to the shore and\par
about to land men and guns. Then our Government awoke and forbade\par
Spanish officials to shoot Americans without trial.\par
\par
As I stood there curiously examining the marks of the conflict, or\par
examining some part of an unconsumed bone, I little thought that in a\par
very few days I myself would be a fugitive, creeping through jungles and\par
over tropic plains, seeking to join the comrades of the men on whose\par
ashes I was then treading, to aid their fight for free Cuba.\par
\par
Perhaps my subsequent fate made me ponder over my happy life in Cuba,\par
and compare the horrible misery of my prison life, with its hardships\par
and degrading detail, with the brightness of those days, when love,\par
obedience, wealth and luxury were mine.\par
\par
But in those long years, when in their gloom and depression I was\par
fighting to keep off insanity by ignoring the dreadful present and\par
dwelling on the past, no incident of all my life on the island haunted\par
me more than this at San Marcos. Every detail was photographed on my\par
brain, and as I recalled that blackened spot strewn with ashes soddened\par
by tropical rains, soon to be all the greener for the fertilizing\par
tragedy, many a thousand times I said, "Would to God my ashes were\par
mingled with the dead there."\par
\par
Soon after leaving San Marcos, striking into the jungle, the road became\par
so narrow that we had to go single file. I found the silence of the\par
tropical forest impressive, and think it had its effect on us all--even\par
the negroes and dogs moved on, making no sound. Although novel scenes,\par
yet I was glad when 5 o'clock came and we emerged from the jungle on to\par
the coast road. It was sandy, but well traveled. Another mile and we\par
were in Cajio, and the Caribbean, blue and lovely as a dream, lay spread\par
before us, with hundreds of palm crowned islets and coral bays, all with\par
sandy beaches of dazzling whiteness.\par
\par
Senor Andrez had a house here, and as they had notice of our coming\par
everything was prepared for our reception. Entering the house, we were\par
served with black coffee and thin rice cakes fried. Gray and I wanted a\par
swim before supper in the waters, which looked very tempting, but it\par
would have been a breach of etiquette to indulge then--and, by the way,\par
there is a strange repugnance to water inherent in the Spanish nature,\par
there being no bathhouses in Spain, they say, and I believe it. Gray and\par
I, during the next few days, were in and out of the water at all hours,\par
but could never persuade any one else to try the experiment of a swim in\par
the warm water of the Caribbean. At the house, or when out in boats, we\par
frequently invited some of the company to join us in a plunge, but none\par
ever accepted the invitation. We are told on good authority that "our\par
virtues depend on the interpretation of the times," and one might add\par
"on the interpretation of our nation." The Anglo-Saxon loves soap and\par
water and plenty of it; the Spaniard does not. But this contrast may\par
mean nothing in our favor; there may be a reason for it, racial\par
probably, but possibly climatic.\par
\par
Supper came, and it was a treat. Gray and I noted that in suitability of\par
material to the purpose intended, and in cookery, it excelled anything\par
in our experience. Cafe Riche and Tortoni's were not in it. We were\par
curious to see the cook. She was ordered in for our inspection, a sober,\par
sad-faced negress, angular, bony, and, strangely enough, knew only a few\par
words of Spanish, her language being some African dialect, Africa being\par
her natal place, as it, indeed, was of most of the slaves.\par
\par
What views of life, what views of the Christian world most of these\par
slaves must have! Torn from their homes, leaving their slaughtered\par
family on the ashes of their homes, and carried off to toil and wear out\par
the only life nature will ever give them--for what? To toil amid hunger\par
and abuse too foul to name in order that the Christian robber may have\par
gold to gratify his desire.\par
\par
[Illustration: "ANOTHER SECOND WOULD HAVE ENDED MY LIFE."--Page 371.]\par
\par
She was evidently alarmed over the summons--it might mean anything--she\par
was unused to the coin of compliment; but we gave it freely, however,\par
and the next morning each of us did better, and when departing placed a\par
sovereign in her hand and made Senor Andrez promise to be good to her.\par
\par
Our host grew his own tobacco and made his own cigars. These were famous\par
even in Havana, and Gray and I enjoyed them that evening. A number of\par
grass-woven hammocks were swung under a roof in front of the house. It\par
was delightful lying there watching the phosphorescent waves rippling or\par
breaking on the beach under the light of a full moon and listening to\par
the chatter or the songs of the black fellows who swarmed around while\par
smoking cigars worth the smoking. The negro children, shrill-voiced and\par
loud, were very much in evidence.\par
\par
The air was delightful, and following the custom of the country we slept\par
in the hammocks without undressing.\par
\par
The next morning, under a sunrise sky, which in its glowing colors\par
looked like the New Jerusalem, Gray and I made a break for the glorious\par
water that rippled on the beach. What a swim we had! We were the only\par
humans visible. All other unfeathered bipeds were asleep, and we varied\par
our bath by wandering around the beach in a state of nature, viewing\par
things generally, but a turtle pond held us fascinated. Stakes had been\par
driven down inclosing a space, and upward of twenty great turtles were\par
prisoners, waiting apparently with the greatest of patience to be\par
devoured--that being, so far as I can see, the ultimate destination of\par
all life--that huge procession to the stomach. The rocks tell us that it\par
began a good while ago, and it has kept up with crowded ranks ever\par
since. When the missionary landing in Fiji anxiously inquired of the\par
boss cannibal gentleman where his predecessor might be sojourning, he\par
was promptly informed that he had "gone into the interior." To "go into\par
the interior" is the decree fate writes in her book of doom and copies\par
on the birth certificate of all the breathers of the world.\par
\par
[Illustration: SUGAR LOAF MOUNTAIN, View from Rio de Janeiro.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXI.\par
\par
THE PHILISTINES ARE UPON THEE, SAMSON.\par
\par
\par
I was very fortunate in my servant Nunn, he being devoted to me, a\par
resolute fellow as well, and thoroughly trustworthy. He felt very badly\par
over my leaving him behind in Havana. Nor would I have done so under\par
ordinary circumstances.\par
\par
The day before leaving on the trip, taking him aside, but not wishing to\par
actually disclose anything, I talked in a very impressive, grave way,\par
instructing him to leave Havana secretly after telling his mistress that\par
I had ordered him to go to Matanzas, a city forty miles east by rail. He\par
was to bring all the New York papers, meet me at Cajio and not let a\par
soul know his destination, but be there awaiting my arrival from the\par
Isle of Pines the following Sunday week. If in the mean time anything\par
unusual, no matter what, happened, then he was instantly to depart for\par
Cajio, there hire a boat and crew and come after me, not to mind expense\par
and not to lose a moment's time. Nunn was one of those wise men who know\par
how to obey orders without self-questionings as to the whys and\par
wherefores.\par
\par
I had secured gun licenses from the authorities, and, giving them to\par
Nunn, ordered him to bring a breech-loader and a brace of revolvers with\par
him.\par
\par
During my stay in the Isle of Pines I would be out of reach of the\par
outside world. If on meeting Nunn I found from the papers he brought\par
that there was any sign of danger I would not return to Havana, but\par
would secure a boat, provision it, set sail alone for some port in\par
Central America and send my servant back after my wife.\par
\par
At 10 o'clock our party set out in an open-decked cargo boat from Cajio\par
for San Jose, seventy miles across the water and on the west coast of\par
the island. San Jose was one of the half-dozen plantations belonging to\par
our host, the chief product being coffee, and on this one there were 130\par
slaves.\par
\par
We had a motley cargo. Twenty black fellows, dogs, turtles, fighting\par
cocks, two trained pigs, a good-sized snake that answered to the name of\par
Jacko and had the run of the ship. Ship, men, women and young darkies,\par
trained pigs and everything except we three guests were the absolute\par
property of our host.\par
\par
We were passing through the gate of the Gulf of Matamano. The bottom was\par
so white and the water so clear that we could see distinctly all the\par
wondrous marine life beneath. Ashore in the thick forests all seemed to\par
be dead, but here in the water and beneath the surface all was teeming\par
with life. Flocks of sea fowl were in the air or whitened the rocks\par
which everywhere rose above the waters, and innumerable little islets\par
rested like lovely pictures in the blue setting of the sea.\par
\par
At one of the loveliest, called Cayos de Tana, with a wide fringe of\par
white beach, we landed; that is, our boat ran toward it until the keel\par
stuck in the sand, when a dozen black fellows sprang over into the\par
water, and, taking us white trash on their shoulders, carried us ashore.\par
Once there we set out to find turtle eggs, and soon found heaps of sand\par
which, when scraped away, revealed the eggs in dozens. We took away\par
about a bushel, but they had a rancid flavor, so Gray and I backed out\par
of our promise to eat them, as did Senors Andrez and Mondago.\par
\par
The man in charge of the boat was a skillful sailor, and, having a fine\par
breeze, we rushed through the water at a great rate. At last, after a\par
day of novel enjoyment, just as the short twilight of the tropics was\par
fading out, we ran alongside of the little pier of San Jose and were\par
welcomed with loud shouts and gun shots from about a hundred gaudily\par
attired slaves, who were excited and seemingly glad over the return of\par
their master, this being Sunday and a holiday.\par
\par
Did any of my readers ever think what the rest of Sunday is to the\par
toilers of the earth? If Christ left no other legacy to the Christian\par
world but that happy day of rest, then must we still bless and praise\par
him as the Mighty Benefactor of the world, the Saviour and glorious hero\par
of the workingman. For nineteen years I toiled, exposed to every storm\par
that blew, and was sustained through all the six days' misery by the\par
blessed knowledge that Sunday, with its rest, was never far off. And\par
when the Sunday morning dawned and the happy consciousness filled my\par
mind that for one day at least I was free from toil, my heart filled\par
with gratitude to the Galilean carpenter, who, by his gracious deeds and\par
genius, had so impressed the hearts of men that for his sake they had\par
taken the seventh day of the Hebrew and bequeathed it as a day of rest\par
to all the toiling generations of the sons of men. The Roman Empire,\par
which overshadowed the world and held the nations in subjection, knew no\par
day of rest, and to-day the toiling millions of China never wake to say:\par
"This is a day of rest on which I can turn my thoughts to other things\par
than toil."\par
\par
I must not here enter into details of that week of rare sport and keen\par
enjoyment in the Isle of Pines. We went shark fishing by day and tipping\par
turtles in the moonlight by night, when they came ashore to deposit\par
their eggs in the sand. One never-ending source of enjoyment to the\par
Cubans was the battles of the fighting cocks. I had got over some of my\par
repugnance to the sport, and enjoyed it almost as well as the cocks\par
themselves. How soon one learns to do in Rome as do the Romans!\par
\par
The week had come to an end, and, although importuned by my host to\par
delay my departure, my anxiety as to the state of affairs in the outside\par
world was too great to postpone my return to the mainland. So, after a\par
rousing send-off from every one on the plantation, I departed. Just as\par
the sun was flinging its dyes over the clouds and waters, one week from\par
the Sunday of my arrival at San Jose, I was sailing into the little bay\par
of Cajio. Gray was to remain another week, and I was returning in a\par
small sloop manned by two of Senor Andrez's men. I found Nunn waiting\par
for me on the beach. He handed me a letter from my wife and said\par
everything was well at home. Opening the letter I found an earnest\par
appeal to return at once. Going to the hacienda near by I took the\par
bundle of New York and London papers Nunn had brought. I went to my\par
room, and, opening the Herald I was amazed to see the storm over the\par
Bank of England business and the great desire to discover the mysterious\par
Warren.\par
\par
I felt that the time had come when it would no longer be prudent for me\par
to live under my right name. It was an easy matter to invent a name and\par
live under it, and I determined to do so, for a time at least, until\par
after I saw how matters developed. But I could not do this in Havana,\par
for in case of using an alias it would be necessary to take my wife into\par
my confidence. She was sure to discover the matter sooner or later, and\par
it was better for her to learn the miserable truth from my own lips than\par
to leave the discovery to come to her through the public press.\par
\par
In Mexico I should really have nothing to fear, even if it was known I\par
was there. So, after some cogitation, I determined to return to Havana,\par
say good-bye to all our friends and embark as soon as possible for Vera\par
Cruz. I was impatient to set off at once, but it was both dangerous and\par
difficult work to go through the jungle by night, so telling Nunn to be\par
ready to start at sunrise I went to bed.\par
\par
At dawn we set out and did not halt until we reached San Marcos, with\par
its gloomy memorial of human savagery. After an hour's halt we set out\par
and arrived at San Felipe in time to catch the train to Havana. On\par
arriving there at dusk I sent my servant to inform his mistress of my\par
safe arrival while I called on Don Fernando at the hotel. His frank and\par
hearty reception told me at once that he had heard nothing, and he knows\par
pretty well everything going on in the town. From the hotel I drove to\par
the police barracks and called on the colonel of police, with the same\par
result, which satisfied me beyond all doubt that however the storm blew\par
in London or New York there was not a single cloud on the horizon in\par
Havana. But it was soon to blow a hurricane. I had a very happy meeting\par
with my wife, and found her the picture of health and happiness.\par
\par
As I looked in her face, beaming with confidence and faith, I realized\par
how hard it would be to tell her the terrible truth, and what a shock it\par
would be to her when she discovered the husband she believed the soul of\par
honor stood in danger of a prison. Yet I was tolerably certain she would\par
forgive me upon my promise never to do wrong again.\par
\par
She had sent out invitations to dinner for Thursday to twenty friends.\par
There was then a steamer in the harbor advertised to sail in two days\par
for Mexico, and I had thought of going by her. Had we, this book would\par
never have been written.\par
\par
As invitations were out for Thursday, I concluded to wait for Saturday's\par
steamer, but determined to sail on that day without fail.\par
\par
Under our system of housekeeping a dinner party was a simple thing. We\par
merely had to notify our landlord how many guests we expected and the\par
thing was done, so far as we were concerned. Don Fernando would send his\par
hotel steward down to the house with reinforcements of cooks and\par
waiters, and my wife had simply to usher the guests into the dining room\par
and out again. Don Fernando's supernumeraries did the rest. On the day\par
of our dinner I was strongly tempted to give some hint to my wife that I\par
was in some way entangled in a web, but as she was so happy I could not\par
do it, but resolved to wait until we were settled in Mexico, and then to\par
tell her a little, but not all the truth.\par
\par
My wife, all unconscious of the frightful calamity impending, entered\par
upon the last half day of happiness she was to know for many long years.\par
The same statement would be true of myself. As the guests were arriving\par
I was in a happy vein, and in the same happy frame of mind sat down to\par
dinner. Twenty happy mortals, but not one divined the termination of\par
that dinner party, least of all the proud and happy hostess. It was a\par
great success, and at 8 o'clock was drawing to a close. The long windows\par
were open, while the warm breeze from the nearby gulf was pouring\par
through the room. The clock had just chimed the quarter when there came\par
a sudden rush of feet over the veranda and through the hall. All eyes\par
were fixed on the open door leading to the hall, when an eager,\par
resolute-faced man, evidently an American, stepped with a firm pace into\par
the room, followed by a dozen civilians and soldiers. With a quick\par
glance over the company his eyes rested on me, and coming direct to my\par
chair, while my guests gazed in amazement, he bowed and said in a low\par
voice: "Mr. Bidwell, I am sorry to disturb your dinner party or to annoy\par
you in any way, but I am forced to tell you I have a warrant in my\par
pocket for your arrest upon a charge of forgery upon the Bank of\par
England. The warrant is signed by the Captain-General of Cuba,\par
everything is in due form, and you are my prisoner. I am William\par
Pinkerton."\par
\par
[Illustration: BENEATH OLD BAILEY COURT ROOM--COURT ADJOURNED FOR\par
LUNCH.]\par
\par
Every man who enters the arena and joins in the struggle of life has\par
more or fewer takedowns in his history. But my wish is that between\par
this hour and my last I may have no more takedowns so near the freezing\par
point as this was. I shall never forget the look on my wife's face.\par
First she gazed at the intruders with indignation, then turned to me\par
with a look of eager expectation, as much as to say: "Wait till my\par
husband raises his arm and you will all go down." But instead of seeing\par
me rise, indignant and angry, driving the intruders out, she saw me\par
talking quite calmly to Curtin. Then her face grew deadly white. None of\par
the guests heard Pinkerton's words, but, as will be easily imagined,\par
there was a painful silence, which I broke by standing up and saying\par
that there was some unhappy mistake, that I was arrested upon the charge\par
of furnishing arms to the insurrectionists in the eastern provinces. I\par
requested my friends to withdraw at once, and everything would be\par
explained on the morrow.\par
\par
[Illustration: TRANSFERRED FROM DARTMOOR TO WOKING PRISON]\par
\par
There were five soldiers present, Mr. Crawford, the English\par
Consul-General, Pinkerton and Captain John Curtin, my servant Nunn being\par
in custody of the latter. It was a strange and unhappy scene, and every\par
one felt extremely awkward and ill at ease, especially the writer. In\par
the rear of the dining room was a large sitting room, where I kept my\par
valuables in trunks and did my writing. I turned to Mr. P., and said:\par
"Will you come in the other room?" "Certainly," he replied, without the\par
slightest hesitation. The room was brilliantly lighted. Motioning him to\par
a seat, I said:\par
\par
"Will you have a glass of wine?"\par
\par
"Yes, but I never drink anything but Cliquot," replied Mr. Pinkerton,\par
pleasantly.\par
\par
A servant brought in a bottle and glasses, and I turned the conversation\par
upon the subject of money. The captain, being a stranger to me, guided\par
by former experience with Irving & Co. I fancied he might be bribed.\par
Sometimes the police are susceptible to this form of temptation, and I\par
was at bay and desperate. I intended to offer him a fortune for a bribe.\par
If he refused to take it I resolved to shoot him and dash out of the\par
window, for at my elbow was an open drawer, with a loaded revolver ready\par
at my hand.\par
\par
I said: "You know the power and value of money?"\par
\par
"Yes, and I need and want plenty of it."\par
\par
Pointing to a trunk I said: "I have a fortune there. Sit where you are\par
ten minutes, give no alarm, and I will give you $50,000."\par
\par
Then a scene ensued that if put upon the stage would be deemed\par
farfetched, if not incredible. When I said this the captain never moved\par
a muscle, but looked at me seriously, earnestly, then dropped his eyes\par
to the bottle. As he did so I placed my hand on the revolver. He took\par
the bottle up, filled his glass, and, looking steadily at me, drank it\par
off, and, replacing the glass on the stand, coolly remarked:\par
\par
"Why, sir, that is $5,000 a minute!"\par
\par
"Yes, and good pay, too," I said.\par
\par
"But I won't have it!" he interjected, and sprang to his feet as he saw\par
me make a movement; but I was too quick for him.\par
\par
I fired point-blank, and down he went as if felled by lightning.\par
\par
I rushed to the window, when the Venetians were torn violently down, and\par
one of Curtin's subordinates, revolver in hand, sprang from the outer\par
darkness through the window into the room, and the others came with the\par
soldiers. My wife, too, white faced, rushed in from the dining room. A\par
lively struggle followed, in which Curtin, having risen from the floor,\par
joined. The struggle was soon over, leaving me a prisoner under close\par
guard.\par
\par
My bullet had struck the captain, breaking a rib and glancing off, but\par
he was game, and when we shortly after departed for the city he rode\par
with me in the same carriage. I tried to soothe my wife's fears, but it\par
was attempting the impossible, so we drove away to the city in three\par
carriages, Mr. P. assuring my wife that I would sleep at the hotel.\par
\par
By the time we arrived the news had spread among the American colony,\par
and as the hotel was a sort of American club delegations of my\par
acquaintances speedily arrived. All were loud in the denunciation of the\par
outrage. Of course, they saw things on the surface only. Soon our\par
Consul-General Torbet arrived, and assured me he would see that I should\par
be treated with every consideration until such time as the unfortunate\par
mistake was corrected.\par
\par
That night I slept at the hotel with Curtin and his two companions for\par
roommates. Mr. P. took his wound and close call very good naturedly, and\par
said he did not blame me at all, but felt taken down to think I had got\par
the drop on him. Early the next morning my friend, the chief of police,\par
Col. Moreno de Vascos, called on me, indignant and angry that I should\par
suffer such discourtesy. He was particularly indignant over the insult\par
to himself in not being consulted, so that he could have sent me a note\par
to call on him and explain. Then he turned to Pinkerton and told him to\par
liberate me, as he would be responsible for me whenever wanted. But the\par
captain knew what he was about, and knew his business too well and the\par
backing he had to pay any attention to Col. Vascos. I claimed the\par
protection of our Consul, but Torbet regretfully told me that on account\par
of the orders Pinkerton bore from the State Department at Washington he\par
was forced to consent to my detention, but he would not permit me to be\par
kept in the ordinary prison. So about 12 o'clock next day I was\par
transferred to the police barracks, and put into the lieutenant of\par
police's room and a guard of soldiers placed over me.\par
\par
The New York Herald of the next day contained the following:\par
\par
     (Editorial, New York Herald, Feb. 26, 1873.)\par
\par
     "CUBAN AFFAIRS--BIDWELL'S IMPRISONMENT.\par
\par
     "The special telegraphic advices which we publish to-day in\par
     reference to the arrest and imprisonment at Havana of Bidwell, one\par
     of the parties accused of the recent forgeries on the Bank of\par
     England, are very interesting, touching the jurisdiction of the\par
     Island authorities in this matter. It appears that Bidwell was\par
     arrested at the request of the British Government on the\par
     supposition that he was a British subject; but it is represented\par
     that he is a citizen of the United States of America, and that his\par
     arrest in Cuba is not justified by any extradition treaty with\par
     England, nor by any authority, except that of the Captain-General,\par
     whose will over the Island is the supreme law. If it can be\par
     established that Bidwell is a citizen of the United States his case\par
     certainly calls for the intervention of the Secretary of State. The\par
     prisoner, it seems, desires a transfer to New York, which is\par
     perfectly natural, but we suspect that the international\par
     difficulties suggested touching his detention in Cuba will not\par
     materially improve his chances of escape. Such proceedings could be\par
     carried out in no other country than Cuba, where the\par
     Captain-General does not always act in accordance with law.\par
     Distinguished lawyers and judges of that city, in conversation with\par
     the Herald correspondent, denounced the act as being utterly\par
     illegal and without precedent."\par
\par
\par
     (Cable dispatch to the London Times, March 3, 1873.)\par
\par
                                         "Havana, Cuba, March 2, 1873.\par
\par
     "Great efforts are being made by the lawyers and prominent citizens\par
     here to obtain the release of Bidwell, supposed to be Warren.\par
     To-morrow the American Consul will demand his release on the ground\par
     that he is an American citizen. The British Consul-General, E. H.\par
     Crawford, is doing everything in his power to counteract these\par
     efforts. There is great excitement here over Bidwell's arrest and\par
     the popular sympathy is with him."\par
\par
\par
     (By cable from Havana to New York Herald, March 31, 1873.)\par
\par
     "Bidwell, the alleged Bank of England forger, whose arrest caused\par
     so much excitement here, escaped by jumping from the second story\par
     balcony of the police barracks late last night in the presence of\par
     his guards. He was partly dressed at the time. Bidwell and his wife\par
     are greatly liked here, and no doubt his Havana friends, seeing the\par
     impossibility of counteracting by legal means the efforts of the\par
     British Consul to secure his extradition, planned the affair.\par
\par
     "It is the general opinion that John Bull has seen the last of\par
     Bidwell, there being dozens of planters in the district ready and\par
     willing to shelter him, which they can do effectually."\par
\par
[Illustration: MAT-MAKING AT PENTONVILLE PRISON.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXII.\par
\par
NIGHTLY IN MY DUNGEON THE MAGICIAN MEMORY WOULD UNROLL THAT SCENE.\par
\par
\par
So at last justice had laid hold of me, but I thought it a very shaky\par
hold--so much so that I was confident that I could break away from her,\par
so that she could never weigh me in her balance.\par
\par
I will not enter into the details of events in Havana for the next few\par
days--briefly told, I was nominally a prisoner; actually so, as regards\par
leaving the barracks. The commander, Col. Vascos, was a warm friend,\par
and, living in the barrack, he wanted me to dine at his table, but as I\par
was already planning an escape, I deemed it best not to accept.\par
\par
My wife spent many hours with me daily. All my meals were brought from\par
the hotel. Nunn was kept a prisoner for two days, then liberated. I took\par
him into my confidence, telling him I was going to escape, and directed\par
him to make all outside arrangements for that event, and he was greatly\par
rejoiced when I told him he should accompany me in my flight.\par
\par
Pinkerton, was awake to the danger of losing his man, and had lodged a\par
written protest with the English and American Consuls against my being\par
confined in the police barracks.\par
\par
The only result was that Col. Vascos issued an order to keep him and his\par
men out of the barracks.\par
\par
I had a great many visitors, including officers of the army and navy,\par
and all were loud in protestation and indignant at my arrest. None\par
seemed to care whether I was guilty or not, but all demanded my\par
liberation, as there was no extradition treaty and no law to surrender\par
me. Even my lawyer, the most influential in Cuba, assured me there was\par
not the slightest danger of my surrender, but I knew that the bankers\par
Rothschild would ask Spain to give me up, and to an impecunious\par
Government like that of Spain the word of a Rothschild was more potent\par
than that of a king.\par
\par
Then I knew such bright men as William A. Pinkerton (who had arrived)\par
and his lieutenant, Capt. John Curtin, would never have made the mistake\par
of coming to Cuba without full powers; therefore, feeling confident that\par
my surrender would be only a question of time I resolved to escape.\par
\par
At my request Col. Vascos had sent a guard of soldiers to my house and\par
brought to the barracks two of my trunks. I had $80,000 in cash and\par
bonds, besides many valuables as well, in them. I gave my wife $20,000\par
and my servant $1,000 in gold and $5,000 in Spanish bank notes. Curtin\par
had in vain tried to seize my luggage, but the Spanish law stood in his\par
way.\par
\par
All this time the rebellion in the island was in full blast, the\par
insurgents--consisting of native Cubans, mulattoes and negroes\par
(ex-slaves)--held possession of the greater part of the Eastern\par
provinces--that is, the whole eastern end of the island, and the western\par
end, called Pinar del Rio. They had kept the flame of rebellion alive\par
for six years and were still making a desperate and fairly successful\par
fight to maintain themselves. The sympathies of the American people were\par
with them, and they looked to our country for arms and recruits. The\par
former were smuggled into the island as opportunity offered by a Cuban\par
committee in New York. Not many, but yet some, recruits went, for it was\par
death to be caught going or returning, and few ever returned. The civil\par
conflict was murderous, neither side giving quarter. The spirit of\par
adventure was strong upon me, and I resolved, if I escaped, to make my\par
way to the Western Province and join the insurgents for a year, then\par
make my escape by crossing the narrow body of water between Cape San\par
Antonio and the mainland of Central America.\par
\par
Once among the rebels all pursuit of me was at an end, as army after\par
army had been sent from Spain to crush the rebellion, and each had in\par
turn melted away before the valor of the rebels or the deadly climate.\par
\par
Nunn volunteered to accompany me, and I gave him $2,000 to send to his\par
wife in Paris, that his mind might be easy on that score. No one knew my\par
real destination save Nunn and my wife. It was hard to obtain her\par
consent, but at last it was given. I arranged with her that she was to\par
leave Havana as soon as she knew I was off, cross to Key West, wait one\par
month there, and, if she then heard nothing of me, she was to telegraph\par
my sister to meet her in New York, take the steamer to that city and\par
live with her until I rejoined her.\par
\par
Among other things, Nunn, by my orders, procured good maps of the\par
country. A Spanish gentleman, a warm friend, but whose name I will not\par
mention, was my counselor in the plot. He advised me to go to the Isle\par
of Pines, as Senor Andrez had promised to keep me safely from all\par
pursuit. I let my friends think that was my destination. I proposed as\par
when on my visit to embark from Cajio, but to take a westward course\par
along the coast, and when well off Pinar del Rio and night fell to put\par
about and steer to shore under cover of the darkness. Once ashore, to\par
get as far inland as possible before dawn. Then to keep a lookout for\par
any body of rebels and join them as a volunteer in the cause of "free\par
Cuba." We were sure of a welcome, particularly as we would come well\par
armed.\par
\par
[Illustration: BLACK MARIA CONVEYING THE FORGERS THROUGH LONDON IN\par
CHAINS.]\par
\par
I had made it a practice to give the sentinels in the police barracks a\par
bottle of brandy every day and a box of cigars every second day during\par
my stay, besides what were to them valuable presents, so I was highly\par
popular in the barracks. We had fixed on the night of March 20 for the\par
venture.\par
\par
My room was in the second story of the barracks, but I was allowed to go\par
freely through all the rooms on that floor, followed more or less by a\par
guard. None of the windows opened on the street. There was a room\par
leading to an open window, but the door was kept locked. It was arranged\par
to have it unlocked with the key on the inside at 10 o'clock that night.\par
I was to walk about as usual, and, when the hour came suddenly step\par
through the door, lock it behind me and then bolt through the window\par
into the street. Nunn and my friend were to await me outside of the\par
window with orders to shoot any man (not a native) who attempted to stop\par
me, as I feared Curtin or his men might be on guard in the street, and\par
once in the street I did not propose to go back again alive.\par
\par
The guns and two extra revolvers had been made into a bundle and left at\par
the station. At a nearby room were disguises for Nunn and myself,\par
consisting simply of cloaks and whiskers. We intended to board the 10:30\par
train going South, and once well out of the station would dispense with\par
all disguise but the Spanish cloak each of us wore.\par
\par
The day for the venture came. I had previously instructed my wife to\par
send word she was indisposed, and to remain at the hotel. She had very\par
bravely offered to be on hand and with me up to the moment I disappeared\par
through the door, but fearing that in the excitement some of the\par
soldiers might say or do something insulting, I forbade her being on the\par
scene. I had had an unusually large number of visitors during the day. I\par
felt but little anxiety over the result, save only on the side of\par
Pinkerton. I had a sort of suspicion or presentment that, once fairly\par
outside of the barracks, I would run against him. The day passed rapidly\par
away, and 6 o'clock came, and all the civil officials, with the horde of\par
hangers-on, departed, leaving the usual evening solitude in the\par
barracks. Soon Nunn came with my supper and cautiously produced a\par
revolver and belt. I strapped the belt around me under my vest, placing\par
the revolver under a pile of clothing. Nunn reported everything all\par
right. He had seen Curtin that day as usual around the hotel and\par
apparently unsuspicious of anything unusual going on.\par
\par
The window I was to jump out of opened on the public street, and the\par
street would be jammed full of people at the hour I was going. Of course\par
there were a good many chances of failure, chiefly so because all the\par
police from top to bottom knew me by sight, and if one of them happened\par
to be one of the half hundred witnesses of my jump he might have wit\par
enough to seize me.\par
\par
Nunn and my friend were to be under the window ready to act according to\par
circumstances. Above all, to be ready to seize hold of any one who\par
manifested any intention to detain me. Nunn was full of courage and\par
hope. At 7 o'clock he went away, not to see me again until we met\par
outside the barracks. I called the guard and three or four idle soldiers\par
into my room and served them out liberal doses of brandy. Unluckily\par
enough, however, the one on duty would drink but lightly. Soon after 8\par
Consul-General Torbet came in to smoke a cigar and have a chat. He\par
remained until nearly 10, and then departed. Then I felt the hour had\par
indeed come. I thrust the revolver inside my shirt, and rolled up a cap\par
and put it in the same place. Then calling the sentry, I gave him a\par
drink and a cigar, and stepping out into the hall, I began my usual\par
march around through the upper rooms of the barracks. I was to go out of\par
the window at precisely 10. It wanted ten minutes of that time. It was a\par
long ten minutes to me, but I marched around puffing my cigar\par
unconcernedly, with an eye on the door I was to slip through. At the\par
hour I had my watch in my hand, and was in the room farthest from the\par
door of exit into the room opening on the street. I walked swiftly\par
through the two intervening rooms and so was for a brief four or five\par
seconds out of sight of the slow following sentinel. I reached the door,\par
opened it, stepped through and instantly locked it. In a moment I was\par
through the open window into the little iron balcony outside. One swift\par
glance showed me the street thronged with people, but hesitation meant\par
failure and death. I climbed lightly over the railing and hung suspended\par
for an instant from the bottom; the crowd below made a circle from\par
under, and I dropped easily to the ground, bareheaded, of course. Nunn\par
was there, and instantly clapped a large straw hat on my head. The\par
strange incident did not seem to attract the least notice, for in a\par
moment we were lost in the crowd. I had my hand on my revolver, and had\par
so strong a belief I should every second be confronted by Curtin that I\par
was strangely surprised when I saw no sign of the gentleman. In less\par
time than it takes to tell it, I was down into an open hallway and then\par
into a room. I and Nunn, who were smooth-faced, were given bushy\par
whiskers and a cloak. In the mean time, I paid an agent in waiting\par
$10,000 in French and Spanish notes, then we hurried out of the rear\par
into a cab and were driven to the station, arriving just in time to\par
catch the 10:30 train.\par
\par
The cab ride and train ride that night were happy rides. I had been a\par
captive and now was free. The sights and sounds all around me took on a\par
deeper purpose and a more significant meaning than they had ever borne\par
before.\par
\par
I had for a few brief days been a captive, shut out from nature's sights\par
and sounds, and that brief deprivation awoke in me a feeling of\par
appreciation for the feast that is everywhere around us spread with a\par
lavish hand. My mind was in a tumult of delight, and I almost forgot I\par
was a fugitive; fortunately the Spaniard is not a suspicious animal, and\par
no notice was taken of us; and so we bumped slowly on southward through\par
the tropic night.\par
\par
Seven o'clock on the morning of the 11th found us at Guisa, a small\par
station on the railroad about ninety miles from Havana and west from\par
Cajio some twenty miles. Our friend here procured us horses, and,\par
bidding him good-bye, Nunn and I started on our ride to Cajio. We were\par
both greatly elated over the success of our adventure. Our friends had\par
procured for us police passports and gun permits under the names of\par
Parish and Ellis.\par
\par
I had a chronometer, several valuable diamonds, a revolver and gun. Nunn\par
carried a canvas bag containing, among other things, 250 capital cigars,\par
tobacco, matches and 300 cartridges. Then we had good maps of the island\par
and current charts of the Gulf of Mantabano, with its hundreds of rocky\par
inlets, spreading everywhere along the south coast. But, armed as we\par
were, it would never do to be picked up by any Spanish boat or patrol\par
anywhere near the rebel border. It probably meant death if we were\par
captured.\par
\par
I think on the whole it would have been the wiser plan to have gone to\par
Senor Andrez's plantation at San Jose. The fear in that case was that if\par
an order arrived from Madrid to deliver me up I might not be safe even\par
in the Isle of Pines. At Cajio I resolved to lose myself so far as the\par
Spanish authorities were concerned, and only travel by night. If we\par
remained on land this would be necessary, as soldiers were everywhere\par
and our police passports would not hold good if we were found traveling\par
in the direction of the rebel lines.\par
\par
I proposed going by sea, and then all our voyaging would necessarily be\par
by night, for there were Spanish gunboats everywhere patrolling around\par
the shores, but there were innumerable small inlets where we could draw\par
up our boat, lay perdu during the day and spy out the next island to\par
sail to at night.\par
\par
[Illustration: CASTS OF THE HEADS OF NOTORIOUS CRIMINALS.]\par
\par
We arrived in due time at Cajio, and here our passports were demanded by\par
a little yellow monkey of a sergeant. I did not quite like having\par
passports scrutinized and determined to try and avoid any more of it.\par
\par
We found no boat at Cajio, nor could we buy, or, if we bought, could not\par
manage one alone. The only thing we could do was to charter one with a\par
crew of four men. During my stay in Cuba I had been studying Spanish. I\par
had become a tolerably proficient speaker, so I had no great difficulty\par
in associating with the natives.\par
\par
I found my idea of joining the rebels by sea impracticable, and as to go\par
by land was perilous in the extreme, I made up my mind to send Nunn back\par
to Havana and to make the venture alone. I did not care to chance his\par
life, and I also felt that it was safer for one than for two.\par
\par
Forty miles away was the last fortified post on the Rio Choerra, at the\par
small town of Voronjo. Once across that small stream I would be on\par
neutral ground, liable at any time to fall in with a rebel band.\par
\par
Nunn was very plucky and most devoted. He by no means wanted to go back,\par
but at last consented.\par
\par
I determined to chance traveling on the beach by night. So at 12 o'clock\par
the day after our arrival at Cajio we mounted our horses and announced\par
that we were returning to Havana. Two miles away, at the small hamlet of\par
Zoringa, we put our horses out and struck for the beach about four miles\par
west of Cajio. Then we went a few yards into the jungle and sat down for\par
our last talk and to wait for the darkness. We were no longer master and\par
servant, but friends. The hours went slowly by; we did not say much, but\par
felt strongly. We had good cigars and smoked almost incessantly.\par
\par
I told him to see Curtin, to give him my regards and laugh at him in a\par
nice way, and to tell my wife that I would limit my stay with the rebels\par
to a year. I told Nunn to send for his wife to join him in New York, and\par
my wife would take her into service so that they could be together.\par
\par
I did not dare to keep the gun we had, but retained the revolvers in a\par
belt around my waist. They were rather old-fashioned, and, as the sequel\par
proved, the ammunition was not waterproof or else was defective. I had\par
two bottles of water, a hundred cigars in my pocket, 300 cartridges,\par
four pounds of dried beef and a loaf of bread. I wore a soft hat and had\par
on a fine pair of English walking boots, an important article for the\par
tramp ahead of me. I wore my chronometer tied by a stout string. I sent\par
my wife all my valuables save three diamond studs, $700 in gold and\par
$5,000 in notes, mostly Spanish bank notes, and I kept $10,000 in bonds.\par
\par
Nunn cut me a stout ironwood cudgel as a handy weapon.\par
\par
At last the night came, and still we waited, loath to say good-bye. We\par
had come out of the jungle and were sitting in the still warm sand\par
talking in low tones and watching the stars. At last when my watch told\par
me it was 10 we rose, and, shaking hands warmly, parted, he going east\par
to Cajio, I west toward Pinar del Rio and the rebel camps.\par
\par
Of course, my great danger lay in meeting soldiers who would stop me.\par
Indeed any one who met a stranger and a foreigner heading west would\par
either stop him or give an alarm, and if once arrested (passports so\par
near the enemy's camp were useless) it meant death, or what was quite as\par
bad, incarceration in a filthy prison until my case was reported on to\par
the Captain-General in Havana. That, of course, meant my return to\par
Havana and possibly to England.\par
\par
Everything is very primitive in Cuba. The common people--that is, the\par
whites and free people--live in mere huts or cabins, and sleep in\par
hammocks under roofs open on two sides. All go to bed soon after sunset,\par
so there was no danger in night traveling, save only in meeting the\par
sentries or running on some detached post of soldiers.\par
\par
In case of meeting these, I had resolved to plunge into the tropical\par
jungle which came close down to the beach.\par
\par
Neither night traveling nor the situation had any terrors for me. I\par
felt my only danger lay in stumbling upon some outpost or sentry who\par
might perceive me before I saw him and so cover me with his rifle before\par
challenging, but I knew from observation since my arrival in Cuba that\par
the discipline among the Spanish soldiers was very slack, and I had a\par
pretty firm belief that isolated sentries usually took a nap while\par
waiting the relief.\par
\par
After leaving Nunn I started out at a quick pace, alert and confident.\par
The moon had gone down, but the Caribbean Sea was lovely in the\par
starlight, and between watching the phosphorescent ripples of the waters\par
and listening to the night noises of the jungle I soon discovered I was\par
enjoying my jaunt and found myself anticipating the pleasure of the\par
free, open life ahead of me when once beyond the Spanish outposts and a\par
soldier of fortune. I thought what a story of adventure I would have to\par
relate when a year or two later I rejoined my wife and friends, and I\par
felt that a good record won in a fight for "free Cuba" would make men\par
willing to forget my past.\par
\par
I found my westward march frequently interrupted by spooks--some rock,\par
stump or bush would, to my suspicious eye, take on the human form until\par
I thought it was a sentry on guard and meant danger. Once or twice I\par
sought the shelter of the jungle and spent a long time watching for some\par
sign of movement. On one occasion I painfully made a circuit of nearly a\par
mile to pass a projecting mass of bushes in the belief that there were\par
men behind it. The air was balmy as on a June night at home. I trudged\par
along with my two bottles of water slung across my shoulder tied to a\par
cord, and between them and my revolvers and cartridges I was pretty well\par
loaded down.\par
\par
Nowhere during the night did I come across any fresh water, but was\par
fated to have a water adventure before daylight which I did not relish.\par
Soon after midnight I sat down on the sand well in the shadow of some\par
palmetto trees and had a very enjoyable lunch of bread and dried beef,\par
washed down by water from my bottle; then lighting a cigar and reclining\par
at full length on the dry sand I passed a pleasant half hour enjoying\par
the fine Havana. I looked forward to the hours of daylight to be spent\par
reclining at ease in the jungle with many anticipations of pleasure. I\par
had a supply of fine cigars, plenty to think about, and the\par
consciousness of having overcome serious difficulties gave me a feeling\par
of elation--then my surroundings were so novel and I was fond of outdoor\par
life.\par
\par
At 4 o'clock the sky put on a ragged edge of gray in the east, and\par
feeling pretty well satisfied with my progress I began to think of\par
selecting a retreat for the hours of daylight. Suddenly I found myself\par
upon what was evidently the neck of a swamp extending far and wide into\par
the land. I had discovered during the night that there was a\par
well-traveled road skirting and following the beach at a distance of a\par
few hundred yards, but there was danger of my meeting some one there, so\par
I stuck to the beach.\par
\par
In the middle of the swamp was a clear space of water with marshy banks.\par
As it was nearly daylight, and being in no hurry, my presence in the\par
country unknown, and in no immediate danger, I determined to halt and\par
not tackle the swamp until nightfall again. Then, if seen by any one, I\par
would have some hours of darkness to make myself scarce in the\par
neighborhood.\par
\par
Turning to follow the edge of the swamp I saw before me on a little\par
lower level than where I stood in the sand what appeared a plot of vivid\par
green grass, and without any precaution stupidly stepped with my full\par
weight upon it, and instantly found myself floundering in four feet of\par
mud and water. I had fallen, and getting back on the solid ground I\par
found myself wet to the shoulders, my legs covered with mud and my\par
pistols, bread, etc., soaking with salt water. At once I ran across the\par
beach and sat down in the warm water of the sea, washing off the mud\par
as well as possible. Then I made my way into the jungle, crossing the\par
road, and going into the thicket a short distance sat down waiting for\par
daylight, purposing to remain concealed near enough to the road to see\par
all passers-by, so that I might judge what sort of people I was among.\par
\par
[Illustration: DARTMOOR CONVICT ESTABLISHMENT.--ABOUT 2000 PRISONERS.]\par
\par
As the ground where I stood was low and wet, and my clothes soaking, I\par
feared catching the fever, so made my way well back to where some fallen\par
trees had made a rift in the dense mass of trunks, creepers and foliage,\par
letting in the sunlight. There I pulled off my garments to dry, taking\par
great care not to let any of the poisonous leaves come into contact with\par
my flesh, and made myself comfortable, sitting down to lunch nearly in\par
the state of nature. I was more concerned over my damaged cigars than my\par
dampened cartridges. On examination I found the cigars but slightly wet,\par
so, spreading them out to dry along with the drapery, I lit one and\par
surveyed the position. As the moisture was already steaming out of my\par
garments I took matters cheerfully and considered the outlook good.\par
\par
Having finished one of my bottles of water, I made up my mind to carry\par
only one, and to take my chance of replenishing that. So long as my\par
health continued perfect I did not require much water; what I feared was\par
that my exposure and change of diet might make me feverish; if so, I\par
would suffer from thirst unless I struck a hilly country.\par
\par
How much company my watch was to me during those long days and nights! I\par
was never tired of examining it. About 10 o'clock I made my way to the\par
road and placed myself in a mass of foliage, where unseen by any one I\par
had quite a range of the road. Up to this hour I had not seen a soul. At\par
first I watched the little stretch of road with eagerness, but no one\par
appearing I turned my attention to watching the evolutions of a huge\par
yellow spider which was spreading its net near by. While absorbed, and\par
almost fascinated, I was suddenly roused by the sharp, quick beating of\par
hoofs on the sandy road. Giving a startled glance, I saw a man unarmed,\par
but evidently a soldier, gallop quickly by on a mule. Twenty minutes\par
later an old-fashioned cart containing four half-dressed negroes and\par
drawn by four wretched mules passed. The men were silent and downcast.\par
Before 1 o'clock thirty people had passed, several being soldiers of the\par
guardia civil (armed police).\par
\par
Then starting to spy out the land from the bushes and vines bordering\par
the swamp I could see a bridge crossing the neck of the swamp, but,\par
worst of all, quite a collection of houses at the other side, reaching\par
down to the beach, and a wharf that ran out into the water quite fifty\par
yards, with, no doubt, a guardhouse and police station among them. I saw\par
my way blocked. It seemed certain there would be sentries on guard at\par
the bridge, or so near it as to make it impossible for me to cross\par
unobserved. The swamp extended inland apparently for three or four\par
miles, and the jungle grew so dense as to make it impossible to\par
penetrate it in an effort to go around, so I determined not to venture\par
crossing the bridge, but to swim for it.\par
\par
The swamp spread on both sides of the lagoon, and there was no such\par
thing as wading in that almost liquid morass, so I tried to find by\par
daylight a place where the mud was covered with water enough at least to\par
make swimming possible, but no such place could I find.\par
\par
Everywhere a black tangled mass of rotting leaves and creepers spread,\par
making such a horrible slime that I shrank from attempting to cross it\par
to the open water. Once over that there was the same ordeal to go\par
through on the other side, and I knew I could only do it at full\par
length--that is, to lie flat and pull myself along as well as possible.\par
The simplest way was to wade out into the sea, then to swim far enough\par
outside of the pier to escape observation from any one who might chance\par
to be on it.\par
\par
But this involved the chance of a horrible death, the sea there swarming\par
with sharks, which at night come in shore. Therefore, after cogitating\par
the matter, I resolved to attempt the bridge, taking the risk of being\par
seen. It might prove fatal to be seen, as I would have to bolt back, and\par
once knowing a fugitive was in the jungle they might turn out and hedge\par
me in, unless I took the sea route. This I resolved to do, if the one by\par
the bridge proved impracticable.\par
\par
So during the afternoon I gathered a small lot of dried limbs and broke\par
them off in sufficient quantity to make a raft capable of bearing about\par
twenty pounds. On this I intended to put my revolvers, cartridges,\par
cigars, etc., and also to rest lightly on it myself, pushing it before\par
me as I swam. After dark I crossed the road into the jungle skirting the\par
beach, carrying my raft, and deposited it on the sand. Lying down in the\par
hot sand near by smoking a cigar, I waited for the moon to go down. I\par
was doing more than watch the stars and moonlit water. I was saying to\par
myself, "What a jolly world is this!"\par
\par
Then, beginning to argue of human destiny, at last I brought the\par
argument around to Ego, and decided that he was a pretty clever fellow,\par
and that the world meant to treat him well. So Ego, settling down into a\par
very comfortable frame of mind, lighting a fresh cigar and looking\par
across at the dark masses of the coral islets crowned with foliage set\par
in the mirrored waters, passed two delightful hours.\par
\par
I watched the moon go down and was not impatient, for the beauty of the\par
scene more even than the novelty of the position cast a charm over the\par
spirit and soothed the eye and mind. I wondered how many were seeking me\par
and how many thousands were speculating over my identity and\par
whereabouts, yet not one in his wildest imagination could ever picture\par
the reality of my position in all its strange and magic surroundings.\par
Through all the coming twenty years, nightly in my dungeon, the\par
magician memory would unroll that scene from his pictured chambers. It\par
was all there--the physical that the eye took in and the thoughts evoked\par
and sent swarming to the brain, there to remain engraved until life and\par
memory end.\par
\par
[Illustration: SCENE NEAR RIO JANEIRO.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXIII.\par
\par
SHARKS, SALT WATER ONES, AND OTHER THINGS.\par
\par
\par
The bridge had no protection along the side save a simple stringpiece of\par
timber. On the far side the houses rested nearly against the bridge\par
entrance, forming a street, which I had to pass through.\par
\par
The moon went down at 10, but I could hear loud voices and occasional\par
bursts of laughter until 11. Then all grew still save the night noises\par
of the woods and swamps.\par
\par
At midnight I carried my raft down to the edge of the water, then\par
leaving it there for use in case of a repulse, with my ironwood stick in\par
my left hand and my revolver in the right, I marched down to the bridge,\par
but fearing my upright figure might be seen, dark as it was, outlined\par
against the sky, I stooped and crawled along the stringpiece of timber\par
until within twenty feet of the large house at the end of the bridge.\par
Peering through the gloom I listened, but could not see or hear any\par
movement. Straightening myself up I took half a dozen paces, when, in\par
the stillness, I heard a sharp crackle that turned me to stone as the\par
flame of a wax match revealed two soldiers sitting on a bench within the\par
porch of the guardhouse not ten feet away. One had struck the match to\par
light a cigarette. The flame that betrayed them to me showed to them my\par
form outlined on the bridge.\par
\par
There was a sudden exclamation, a hail, "Quien va!" then a sudden and\par
thrilling rattle of accoutrements, but I had turned and was flying back\par
across the bridge. Suddenly a rifle shot rang out sharply on the night;\par
a second followed, but I was unharmed. In ten seconds I was beside my\par
little raft, and, pushing it before me, waded out in the shallow water.\par
When up to my knees I halted, unstrapped my revolvers and placed them on\par
the raft. Then pulling off my shoes I put them and my load on the raft,\par
fastening all with a string put there for the purpose. Sticking my knife\par
through the lapel of my coat and resting my chin on the raft I began to\par
swim, keeping well out, so as to go outside the long wharf.\par
\par
In the mean time everything was in commotion ashore. Two more shots were\par
fired, and flashes of the guns proved that a squad had turned out and\par
had crossed the bridge in hot pursuit. Then I blessed the wise\par
forethought that had led me to construct the raft. Certainly it had\par
saved me, for they would surely search the jungle.\par
\par
During the fearful excitement I had forgotten all about the sharks. In\par
the darkness I had given all my attention to trying to get a glimpse of\par
the wharf. Suddenly, near me in the calm and awful stillness, there\par
sprang out of the dark waters a large fish which fell back with a\par
splash.\par
\par
My heart stood still and my blood seemed to freeze, for to my horror I\par
fancied I saw the black fins of numberless sharks cutting the water. I\par
saw myself dragged down into the awful depths and torn limb from limb,\par
by the fierce and hungry monsters. I gave up hope and ceased my\par
swimming, expecting every minute to see the water churned into angry\par
foam by the furious sharks. Instinctively I placed my hand on the knife\par
I had thrust through the lapel of my coat for just such an emergency,\par
but strength and courage were all gone and my nerveless hand could not\par
draw it out. It seemed a long time that I waited, half dazed, for death,\par
which I hoped when it came would be swift.\par
\par
Then I began swimming again, but in a hopeless way. My nerve was all\par
gone. I fancied I was ringed around with the black-finned devils, and\par
thought I could discern the currents from their waving tails; but I kept\par
on swimming, pushing my raft before me, until suddenly I was thrilled\par
through by my foot striking the bottom.\par
\par
Making a rush for the shore, and once there, heedless of the fact that I\par
was in the rear of the houses, I fell down in the sand, weak and\par
panting, and there I lay until strength enough to walk came to me. Then,\par
taking my baggage from the raft, and cutting the cords that bound it\par
together, I started on. Courage and confidence soon came back, and I\par
kept steadily on for three hours, passing several small salt water\par
inlets, but no fresh water to fill my now empty bottle.\par
\par
At the first sign of day I went just within the border of the jungle,\par
and lying down was soon asleep, and sleeping soundly, too, for waking I\par
found the sun high in the heavens, and, looking at my watch, saw it was\par
9 o'clock. At the same time I discovered that I was hungry, with no food\par
save a small piece of dried beef and not a drop of water in my bottle.\par
\par
The salt water lagoon, or inlet, where I had my adventure of the\par
previous night was marked on my map as a river, but it was not. However,\par
I did not worry over the water question, as I knew I was near the hilly\par
country surrounding the town of Alguizor, an important military\par
headquarters, and I was confident of soon meeting some creek flowing\par
from the hills. As for food, there were to be found in the dense jungle,\par
where the soil was moist and wet, the holes of the nut crabs. They were\par
large and fat--that is, appeared to be fat--and I knew that with plenty\par
of them in the jungle I should not suffer from hunger.\par
\par
Before starting inland for the day I turned to look at the blue waters\par
rippling under a light breeze, and glancing in the sun, only a few yards\par
away, I smiled to think of the phantoms my fears had conjured up, but\par
for all that I resolved that no more night swims in the sea should find\par
place in my programme.\par
\par
I made my way with difficulty through the tangled woods, but had gone\par
nearly a mile before I came to the road. After a cautious survey from my\par
shelter, I stepped out on it, and looking away to the west I saw\par
cultivated hills with teams and people moving about; I also saw the road\par
became two--the right-hand one led away from the coast into the hills,\par
the one to the left continued to skirt the beach. Both roads were well\par
traveled, and I knew I was near the tobacco belt, which is cultivated\par
throughout its entire length, from the Gulf to the Caribbean Sea, for a\par
breadth of twenty miles, its western border touching the province of\par
Pinar del Rio. Forty miles beyond that border the rebels held the town\par
of San Cristoval, but I had made up my mind to follow the coast until I\par
reached the hamlet and harbor of Rio de San Diego, fifty miles south\par
from San Cristoval, then to strike north to the town of Passos, twenty\par
miles west of San Cristoval. Once past San Diego, I would be well within\par
the rebel lines, and could safely show myself, although I determined not\par
to do so voluntarily until I was at Passos.\par
\par
The roundabout way I was traveling doubled the distance, but, aside from\par
getting outside the lines of the Spanish patrols, I was in no particular\par
hurry, and my mode of life was hardening and fitting me for the service\par
in which I was to embark. I counted upon taking ten days, or rather\par
nights, to reach San Diego, and five from there to Passos, where I would\par
make myself known to the rebel chiefs as an American volunteer in the\par
cause of Cuban liberty. And, I thought, what a change of scene for Mr.\par
F. A. Warren. From the Bank of England to a volunteer in a rebel camp in\par
Cuba!\par
\par
[Illustration: MILITARY SUPPRESSING REVOLT OF CONVICTS AT CHATHAM.]\par
\par
I crossed the road and entered the jungle to pass the day, but as the\par
ground was dry the trees and vines were not so closely matted, making it\par
easier to move about, and a far more agreeable place it was for a\par
daylight picnic than the jungle where I had passed the day before.\par
But no crabs showed themselves, and as there was no animal life to be\par
found, there was nothing but my piece of dried beef to be had "to go\par
into the interior," so I dined off that; then, lighting one of my\par
precious cigars, lay down in a sort of fairy bower to enjoy myself, and\par
succeeded. During the entire day no sight or sound of human form or\par
voice came to me, nor yet of animal life, save only a mateless bird,\par
garbed in green that flitted around. Of course, not a drop of water this\par
whole day long for me, and, though I was moderately thirsty, I did not\par
suffer, despite the fact that I smoked several cigars. But I felt that I\par
must have food and drink that night, whatever risk I incurred in\par
securing it. I determined, therefore, to start early on my journey and\par
get food before the country people were all in bed. As soon as night\par
fell I stepped out on the road and cautiously started westward. Knowing\par
there must be some town or hamlet near by, I purposed to enter, spy out\par
some shop and watch until the shopkeeper was alone, then enter and\par
purchase a supply of such food as he had, then march out and disappear\par
as quickly as possible.\par
\par
Soon after starting I came to a small place such as the poor whites of\par
the country inhabit, and seeing two women in the doorway I walked in,\par
and with a salute and "Buenas noches, senoritas," I asked for water\par
(agua); they responded with alacrity and brought me some in a cocoanut\par
shell. I saw it was vile stuff, with an earthy taste, but thirsty as I\par
was it tasted like nectar. There was some food on a wooden dish inside,\par
and I suppose they saw me looking at it, for the older woman ran in and\par
returned bringing me two roasted plantains and a rice cake. Just then I\par
discovered a man inside and two others came up from the rear of the\par
house, or I would have purchased food of the women; but, seeing them, I\par
thanked the ladies, and, saying good night, disappeared in the darkness.\par
Picking up the empty bottle I had left in the road I walked on,\par
feasting as I went on my roasted plantains. How nice they tasted!\par
\par
A mile ahead I came to a tumbledown roadhouse, with quite a crowd of\par
loud-voiced men standing around, who evidently had been indulging in the\par
fiery aguardiente sold there. Like the Levite and priest, I passed by on\par
the other side, giving the place a wide berth. Soon after I entered a\par
town or hamlet of a dozen houses. Two or three passed me in the darkness\par
with a "Buenas noches, senor," to which I mumbled some reply, they\par
doubtless taking me for a neighbor. Two uniformed men, evidently police\par
or soldiers, were lounging in the only shop, and I dared not enter until\par
they were gone. Planting myself in a deep shadow, I sat down waiting for\par
them to go out, but they showed no sign of moving until a shrill voice\par
from a female throat issued from a nearby house, bidding one of the\par
loungers to lounge no more just then, and he, hurriedly obeying the\par
summons, went; soon his companion followed; then, leaving my empty\par
bottle in the road, and with my hand on the revolver in my outside\par
pocket, I entered the shop. The easy-going Cuban shopkeeper paid no\par
particular attention to me, did not even stop rolling the cigarette he\par
was making. After deliberately lighting it, he lazily responded to my\par
"Buenas noches, senor," I saw bread, cakes and ham, and ordered of each;\par
then, seeing some Spanish wine, I took a bottle; also a bottle of\par
pickles. Producing a $10 Spanish bank note, I paid the bill, and emerged\par
into the night with the precious load, and so strong was the animal\par
instinct of hunger upon me that I would have fought to death sooner than\par
surrender the provisions I carried.\par
\par
Picking up my empty bottle I looked out for a chance to fill it as I\par
walked through the town on the main road, which went straight west, but\par
intending to abandon it as soon as I came to the fields and found it was\par
safe to sit down for a feast, then make my way to the beach, now some\par
two miles away, and put in a good distance before daylight. But for two\par
mortal hours the road was bordered by impenetrable walls of cactus and\par
bayonet grass, and to make the matter worse the moon came out from\par
behind the clouds and poured a flood of light on the open road. Twice\par
men on horseback passed me, coming from the opposite direction, and both\par
times I sank down in the shadow of the cactus, both times with revolver\par
in hand, but dreading an encounter, as the noise of firing might wake a\par
hornets' nest about my ears.\par
\par
At last I came to a road which entered a field. I was soon over the bars\par
and found myself in an old tobacco plantation, now partly planted in\par
Spanish beans. Crossing a couple of fields at the foot of the hills and\par
in going over a triangular piece of ground, I found the ruins of a\par
house, and nearby a small stream of water. I was in luck, and, taking a\par
good drink and filling my bottle, I sat down in a convenient shadow and\par
spread out my eatables. They were a goodly sight, and consisted of four\par
pounds of good ham, a dozen good-sized sweet cakes, two loaves of bread,\par
a bottle of pickles and one of wine, and one of water. I began with a\par
drink of wine, then followed ham and bread and cake for dessert, all\par
washed down with a fine long drink of water. Then lighting a cigar I\par
stretched myself at full length and spent a delightful hour star-gazing.\par
\par
Then I arose, took another drink of wine, but as it was not particularly\par
select, threw the remainder away, and, filling both bottles from the\par
brook, I prepared to march.\par
\par
How I wish the kodak fiend existed then and that one of them had\par
happened along just then to take a snap shot at me as I stood there in\par
full marching order, with my water bottles slung over my shoulders, my\par
eatables tied up in a large silk handkerchief, with my garments all in\par
tatters, the result of thorns and creepers snatching at them in my\par
jungle trampings; but, worst of all, my trusty and precious walking\par
boots were beginning to show signs of rough usage.\par
\par
I struck the road leading to the beach and marched westward, but it was\par
an unknown land, and I was in constant fear of running against some\par
military post or patrol, being thus constantly delayed by long halts to\par
watch some suspicious object or by making long detours to avoid them.\par
Once I had a fright. Two men on horseback riding on the sandy road were\par
almost on me before I saw or heard them, and I only had time to sink\par
into the shadow as they passed almost within reach of my hand. Both were\par
smoking the everlasting cigarette, and were engaged in earnest talk.\par
Daylight came and found me not more than eight or ten miles further on\par
my journey, but I was very well content as I pitched my camp for the\par
day. I had a royal feast, then, after a cigar, lay down to sleep in\par
another fairy bower and slept until noon, and awoke to find myself\par
wondering how matters were going with Capt. Curtin in Havana, rather\par
amused over the state of chagrin I knew he must be in. I thought of a\par
possible future meeting some years ahead, when, all danger over, I would\par
see and chaff him over a bottle of Cliquot and the $50,000 he wouldn't\par
have, and how I went all the same and saved the money.\par
\par
I realized I must be frugal or my provisions would never hold out; so,\par
after a light lunch, I began to make my way slowly to the beach through\par
the tangled maze of trees and vines. Coming in sight of the blue waters\par
I lay down to sleep again and awoke when the stars were out. The moon\par
would not go down till late, but as there was a deep, broad shadow cast\par
by the trees I walked in it.\par
\par
Good food and the long day of rest restored my strength. All my\par
confidence returned, and I made good progress. At last the moon went\par
down, and then I pressed rapidly forward, always with revolver in hand,\par
ready for instant action. I think I made fully twenty-five miles this\par
night, but as the coast was indented my progress in a straight direction\par
was not more than half that distance. Just as it began to grow gray in\par
the east I came out on a wide inlet. It ran deep into the land. I\par
recognized it from my map as Puerto del Gato, and then I knew I was in\par
the province of Pinar del Rio and almost out of danger.\par
\par
I went into the bush again and pitched camp, waiting for daylight to\par
come and reveal my surroundings. Pitching camp consisted in scraping a\par
few leaves together and lying down; but this morning I was too excited\par
to sleep. I felt that I was near my goal, after having safely gone\par
through many dangers. Once across the Puerto del Gato, two nights of\par
travel would place me outside of the farthest Spanish pickets and bring\par
me among friends, far beyond chance of pursuit, and I also knew that the\par
mere knowledge of my presence in the rebel camp would cause all thought\par
of pursuit to be dropped.\par
\par
When daylight came I stood and looked around. Across the inlet, twenty\par
miles away, I could see only dark masses of green, with no sign of life.\par
To the north the land was hilly, with houses here and there in the\par
distance, and signs of animal life. I cautiously searched the shore for\par
a mile in the hope of finding a boat to cross to the other shore of the\par
inlet, but none was in sight.\par
\par
About 9 o'clock I saw smoke off at sea, and soon I made out a small\par
Spanish gunboat coming rapidly up. Dropping anchor about a mile up the\par
inlet, she sent a boat ashore. I was feeling sleepy, and, going into the\par
woods again, I took a light lunch, and, emptying one bottle of water,\par
lay down to sleep, resolved to make my plans when I awoke. I did not\par
like the appearance of this gunboat; it seemed to promise the presence\par
of the enemy in force around me, besides being a visible manifestation\par
of the power of that enemy.\par
\par
When I awoke from my nap I started on a cautious spying out of the land,\par
making my way toward the head of the inlet, but keeping always under the\par
protection of the woods. While going cautiously along I was startled by\par
the notes of a bugle ringing out some military call not far away, and a\par
moment later the gunboat replied with a gun, then steamed out to sea.\par
Continuing my progress through the woods I came to the road, and, hiding\par
securely in a thicket where I could see unseen, I watched. Soon I heard\par
the sound of voices, and then a detail of armed men passed, going\par
leisurely east, escorting an empty wagon drawn by four mules. It meant\par
much, these armed escorts, showing they were in the face of the enemy.\par
Several others passed during the hour of my watch. Then, with many\par
cautious glances up and down the road, I slipped quietly across and\par
crept for two hours through the jungle. Making my way to the side of the\par
bay, I saw that I had left the military post behind me. There were white\par
barracks and a wharf with people walking on it, and here the road and\par
beach were one. This much discovered, I went a safe distance into the\par
jungle and lay down to have a good sleep, feeling I would need all my\par
energy and strength for the coming night, as it promised to be a\par
critical one, especially as I could not afford to wait for the moon to\par
go down, and would not have the shelter of darkness, for the moonlight\par
was so powerful that one could easily read print by it.\par
\par
I slept until dark, and awoke refreshed, then lunched and nearly\par
finished my last bottle of water. I had only sufficient food for two\par
more light meals. After lunch I smoked for an hour, star-gazing and\par
philosophizing. At 9 o'clock, emerging into the road, I started\par
cautiously out, walking in the shadow of the jungle as much as possible.\par
I thought the head of the inlet was about ten miles away, and expected\par
to find a military post or at least a picket stationed there.\par
\par
       *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
Daylight once more. But it found me happy and content, for the\par
difficulties of the passage of the wide inlet, which had confronted me\par
the night before, had all been surmounted. I was now in a densely wooded\par
point on the western side of the bay. Between me and San Diego lay a\par
wild no man's land of fifty miles. That meant only two nights more of\par
peril and uncertainty, and it was all straight going. So far as the\par
coast line was concerned I was outside of the Spanish lines. Tired out\par
and very well contented, just as the sun rose fiery red above the\par
horizon, I lay down and was at once in dreamland. At noon, hungry and\par
with only a few ounces of food to satisfy my hunger, I woke. Finishing\par
my last bit of ham and bread, I lighted a cigar and set about planning.\par
Pulling out my little map, I began to scan it for the thousandth time.\par
About six miles to the north was the little town of San Miguel. Between\par
me and San Diego lay fifty miles of wild country swept by fire and\par
sword, without an inhabitant and without food. Hungry as I already was,\par
I felt it would not do to undertake a two days' journey through that\par
wilderness without eating. Of course I made a mistake. I was clear of\par
the toils, and I ought to have taken every and any chance rather than\par
enter the enemy's lines again.\par
\par
I resolved, soon after night came, to set out for San Miguel, watch my\par
chance to enter a shop and purchase food, then, beating a hasty retreat,\par
strike out across the country straight for San Diego, there to find\par
myself among friends in the rebel camp.\par
\par
I set out and without any particular adventure arrived about 9 o'clock\par
at San Miguel. It proved to be a hamlet with the houses ranged close\par
together on opposite sides of the streets. The moonlight cast a deep\par
shadow on one side, while the opposite side was almost like day. I stood\par
in the deep shadow watching. The first building was evidently a police\par
or military barrack. The door was wide open, but no one was visible\par
inside. About five doors off was a shop, but the door was closed, and\par
from where I stood there appeared no sign of life within. I waited about\par
ten minutes, and rashly concluding that there was no one save the\par
proprietor there, I stepped out of the shadow into the moonlight and\par
hurrying across the street, put my hand on the door, opened it and\par
stepping within found myself in the presence of twenty soldiers, all\par
gossiping, smoking or gambling. Belts and cartridge boxes along with\par
bayonets decorated the walls or were lying about on boxes and barrels.\par
\par
All eyes were turned on me. I saw myself in a fearful trap and nothing\par
but consummate coolness could keep them from questioning me. My heart\par
beat fast, but with an affectation of indifference I saluted and said:\par
"Buenos noches, senores." They all returned my salutation, but looked at\par
each other eagerly, each waiting for the other to question me.\par
\par
I stepped to the counter and asked for bread; two loaves were given me.\par
I picked up some cakes and paid for them. From the door I turned, and\par
putting all my dignity into a bow, I said: "Good night, gentlemen." They\par
all seemed held by a spell, but they looked and were dangerous as death.\par
I closed the door, fully realizing my peril, feeling the storm would\par
break the instant I was out of sight. Fortunately there was no one near,\par
and I ran swiftly across the street into the protecting shadow and\par
crouched down in a dark space between two houses. The cactus-like weeds\par
grew there and pricked me, but I heeded them not, for that instant the\par
soldiers poured out of the shop, an angry and excited mob, buckling on\par
their belts, cartridge boxes and bayonets as they ran. Some had their\par
muskets, others hastened to get them and all save two stragglers rushed\par
out of the town in the direction from which I had entered. I wondered at\par
this, but soon discovered the reason. Some few women, hearing the\par
tumult, came into the street, but seeing nothing, went in again; the\par
stragglers all disappeared and the street was quiet.\par
\par
[Illustration: UNDERGROUND PASSAGE AND STAIRS LEADING TO OLD BAILEY\par
DOCK.]\par
\par
I came out of my corner and hurried in the shadow down the road in the\par
opposite direction to the course followed by my pursuers. Arriving at\par
the last house at the foot of the street, I found myself confronted by a\par
small river, quiet and apparently deep, with all the space from the\par
last house to the river one impassable barrier of giant cactus, I had\par
either to swim the river or turn back, and I ought to have plunged in as\par
I was, revolver and all, the distance over being short; and, as I am an\par
expert swimmer, I could easily have got across, loaded down as I was.\par
But a contemptible trifle had weight enough to cause me to adopt the\par
suicidal course of turning back.\par
\par
The fierce animal instinct of hunger was on me, the smell of the food\par
enraged me, and I thought if I swam the stream the cakes and bread I\par
carried would be soaked and probably lost, for I had them loose in my\par
arms; beside, I was overconfident of my ability to escape my pursuers.\par
They had marched by the road that led behind the village to the bridge\par
crossing the river some distance up; evidently, not seeing me, they took\par
it for granted I knew of the bridge, and had gone that way.\par
\par
To appease at once my hanger, in a fatal moment I retraced my steps. As\par
I passed a house three women came out. They spoke to me, and in my\par
excitement, instead of saying good evening in Spanish (buenas noches), I\par
said good morning (buenas dias). They, of course, saw I was a stranger.\par
\par
Just then four soldiers came hurriedly into the street from the road,\par
and I was forced to leave the women and crouch down in my former hiding\par
place. Then they did what women seldom do--betrayed the fugitive.\par
Calling to the soldiers, they pointed out the place I was in. All four\par
came running, and in a moment were almost on top of me. I presented my\par
revolver and snapped the trigger twice without exploding the cartridges;\par
they were too close or too excited to use their muskets, but all four\par
grappled with me, and naturally used me pretty roughly.\par
\par
There was a terrific hullabaloo, as in response to their cries their\par
comrades came running in. By the time they had hustled me across the\par
street into the shop there was a mob of half a hundred around me. Soon\par
the commander, a captain, appeared. I wish I could say he was a\par
gentleman, but he was not. He was a little, peppery young fellow,\par
apparently with negro blood in his veins, and dictatorial and insulting\par
in manner.\par
\par
Surely I was an object--a tramp in appearance--but with a diamond ring\par
on my finger (which I had taken from my pocket and slipped on), a\par
revolver strapped to my waist and a splendid chronometer in my pocket.\par
Such an object had never before loomed on their horizon. Was not one\par
glance enough to show that I must be a notable rebel, and there was but\par
one doom for such.\par
\par
My desperate situation cast out all fear, and I was cold and haughty.\par
Flourishing my police passport, I informed him that I was Stanley W.\par
Parish of New York, a correspondent of the New York Herald, and he had\par
better look out what he was about.\par
\par
But it was evident that police passports made out in Havana had no\par
currency in the face of the enemy; but at any rate it proved that\par
whatever my intentions might be, I had at least hailed last from Havana,\par
and not from the rebel camp, and this would prevent my peppery captain\par
from enjoying the pleasure of standing me up in the morning, to be\par
fusilladed, such being the law for all captives in the savage contest.\par
\par
Down my gentleman sat on a barrel, pompous and important, and ordered me\par
to be searched. All this time a dozen hands were holding me fast. I told\par
my officer he was a fool and a clown, but my captors began to go through\par
my pockets, and speedily there was a heap of gold and paper money on the\par
barrel, and my little friend fingered it with a covetous eye. I had my\par
$10,000 in bonds pinned in the sleeve of my undershirt. This they\par
missed, but found all else I carried. In the mean time there was an\par
eager audience looking on, absorbed in the interest of the scene.\par
\par
There was a collection indeed on that barrel. Beside my ring, there were\par
five other valuable diamonds, my chronometer, which with its regular\par
beat and stem-winding arrangement was a great curiosity. Then the heap\par
of money was a loadstone for all their hungry eyes. The captain was\par
making out an inventory and statement, while I stood white with rage to\par
see the half-breeds, blacks, browns and yellows, handle my property so\par
freely. I was especially in a rage with the impudent captain, who had\par
the nerve to put my watch in his pocket. Absorbed by the interest of the\par
scene, my captors had insensibly loosened their hold, and I determined\par
to have some satisfaction out of the captain. Suddenly seizing one of\par
the revolvers before I could be stopped I gave him a stinging blow with\par
it and sprang on him. We rolled on the floor, and there was a scene. I\par
was dragged off by fifty hands, every one trying to seize me, if only by\par
one hand. My captain got up with the blood running down his face, and,\par
rushing to a peg, he seized a sabre bayonet and flew at me like a mad\par
bull. I shouted at him in Spanish, calling him a cur and coward, bidding\par
him to come on. He was not unwilling, while my captors held me firmly\par
exposed to his assault. Another second would have ended my life, when a\par
woman spectator, who stood near nursing a child, threw her arms around\par
him; this, joined to my indifference, for I continued my jeers and\par
taunts, changed his purpose, to my disappointment, for I preferred death\par
to going back to Havana.\par
\par
     "From Wall Street to Newgate" is replete with stirring incidents,\par
     marvelous adventures, hair-breadth escapes and remarkable\par
     experiences, such as few men have met with. They are narrated in\par
     any easy, picturesque style, evincing sincerity and candor, with no\par
     attempt at sensation or exaggeration. The truth told is stranger\par
     than fiction, and history may well be challenged to produce another\par
     life into which has come so many varied and bewildering events, or\par
     to disclose another character, trained in a religious home, having\par
     culture and an unusual business talent, whose deflection from the\par
     path of honor has stirred to its very depths the entire civilized\par
     world.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXIV.\par
\par
ONE LOVELY JUNE MORNING INTO PLYMOUTH HARBOR WE SAIL.\par
\par
\par
Ten days after the events recorded in the last chapter I sailed once\par
more into Havana. This time a prisoner. Two days after my capture, by\par
order of the Captain-General of Cuba, I was put on board the little\par
gunboat Santa Rita, a wretched little tub that steamed four miles an\par
hour and took eight days going from Puerto Novo on the south to Havana.\par
\par
I was taken by a guard of soldiers, not to the police barracks, but to\par
the common prison, where an entire corridor was cleared of its inmates\par
to make room for me and my guards. Pinkerton was the first man to call.\par
He, of course, was delighted to see me. While giving me credit for my\par
escape, he told me he did not purpose to have me leave him again, and\par
having permission from the authorities, he or some of his men intended\par
to keep me company night and day. Of course I respected him for his\par
honest determination to do his duty. He really was an altogether good\par
fellow, and showed me all possible courtesy and consideration; in fact,\par
on his first visit he brought me a letter from my wife, along with a box\par
of cigars and a bottle of wine on his own account.\par
\par
One of his men, by the name of Perry, used to sleep in my little room\par
with me, and every morning Mr. P. would relieve him, remaining until\par
dinner time. We had many long talks on all sorts of subjects, and he\par
gave me many inside histories of famous criminal cases which he had been\par
engaged in. In time we became very good friends.\par
\par
He also gave me full particulars of the really extraordinary way in\par
which he discovered my presence in the West Indies and the reason which\par
led him to conclude that F. A. Warren and I were one. William Pinkerton\par
ordered him to look up the New York end of the business and see if he\par
could discover the identity of Warren. He was one of the many working on\par
the case, but to him belongs the credit of establishing my identity,\par
also of locating my whereabouts and of effecting my arrest.\par
\par
When ordered on the case he knew no more about me or the forgery than\par
what he read in the newspapers. He soon made up his mind that I was an\par
American, and that I was a resident either of New York or Chicago. This\par
because I was so young and evidently had a good knowledge of finance and\par
financial matters. So he determined to seek for a clue to F. A. Warren in\par
Wall street. He procured a list of the names of every banker and broker\par
in New York, and then spent some time in interviewing them, his one\par
question being "Now, who is he?" With their assistance he soon made out\par
a list of nearly twenty possible Warrens, and speedily narrowed it down\par
to four, my name being one of the four. He soon located my home, and\par
began making cautious inquiries on the spot from neighbors and others.\par
He discovered that I was believed to be in Europe, and had been there\par
before, and that when I last returned I had paid off debts and\par
apparently had plenty of money. He had become convinced of my identity,\par
but if I were Warren--where was I?\par
\par
Without arousing suspicion, he heard from some of my acquaintances a\par
saying of mine that whenever I had a bank account, I should live in the\par
tropics. So he reported to his superiors that in his opinion F. A. Warren\par
and I were one, and he believed that, if in America at all, I might be\par
found at some fashionable resort in Florida.\par
\par
He concluded to go to Florida, and visit the various resorts. Upon his\par
arrival at St. Augustine, he sent letters to several of the West India\par
islands, including Martinique, Jamaica and Cuba, inquiring for the names\par
and descriptions of all wealthy young Americans lately arrived. One\par
letter he sent to Dr. C. L. Houscomb, then the leading American doctor in\par
Havana, who, replying to his inquiry, gave my name among others. After\par
my arrest Dr. Houscomb told me how grieved he was to have betrayed me,\par
but that he thought that Pinkerton was a newspaper man, and wanted the\par
information as a matter of news.\par
\par
With this letter in his hand, Pinkerton found a plain path before him.\par
To go ahead of my story a little, I will say here that eventually the\par
bank authorities made him a considerable present in cash, along with\par
their congratulations over his clever detective work. Capt. John Curtin\par
is to-day well and hearty, a prosperous man and very generally respected\par
by the citizens of San Francisco, where he lives.\par
\par
About ten days after my arrival he brought me a New York Herald\par
containing these dispatches:\par
\par
     (Special to New York Herald.)\par
\par
                                            Madrid, April 12, 1873.\par
\par
     The American Ambassador, Gen. Sickles, has formally notified Senor\par
     Castelar that the American Government will consent to the surrender\par
     to the British Government of Bidwell, now under arrest in Havana\par
     upon charge of being concerned in the Bank of England forgery.\par
\par
\par
     (Special to New York Herald.)\par
\par
                                            London, April 12, 1873.\par
\par
     To the great gratification of the authorities here, official\par
     confirmation is given to the rumor that the Spanish Government has\par
     concluded to grant the extradition of Bidwell, now under arrest in\par
     Havana. There seems to be no doubt that Bidwell is the mysterious\par
     Frederick Albert Warren, and there is a very general curiosity to\par
     see him. Many conflicting stories have been published of his\par
     extraordinary escape and equally extraordinary capture. The Times'\par
     report had it that he was mortally wounded, and that he had on his\par
     person when captured diamonds to an enormous value, which had\par
     disappeared soon after. Sergeants Hayden and Green of the Bow\par
     Street force and Mr. Good of the bank of England sail on the Java\par
     to-morrow to escort Bidwell to London.\par
\par
So the web was closing in on me. Of my daily sad interviews with my wife\par
I will say nothing here. But could I have foreseen that this woman, on\par
whom I had settled a fortune, would have married another soon after my\par
sentence, I should not have felt so sorrowful on her account. In due\par
time Green, Hayden and Good arrived, and were introduced to me. I did\par
not give in, but made, by the aid of my friends, a hard fight to\par
persuade the Captain-General to suspend the order for my delivery, and\par
succeeded for a time.\par
\par
At last, after many delays and many plans, early one May morning I was\par
taken to the mouth of the harbor. There the boat of the English warship\par
Vulture was in waiting, and I was formally transferred to the English\par
Government, and Curtin. Perry, Hayden and Green went on board with me.\par
Soon after she steamed out of the harbor. Later in the day the Moselle,\par
the regular passenger steamer to Plymouth and Southampton, came out, and\par
about ten miles out at sea was met by the Vulture's boat, and I and my\par
four guardians were transferred to her.\par
\par
At last I was off for England, and it looked very much as if Justice\par
would weigh me in her balance after all, the more certainly because I\par
found my wife on the Moselle. I had secretly resolved never to be taken\par
back, but intended the first night out of Havana to jump overboard,\par
possibly with a cork jacket, or something to help to keep me afloat. The\par
waters of the gulf were warm, there were many passing ships, and I\par
would take my chance of surviving the night and being picked up. But,\par
very cleverly, Curtin decided to send my wife with me and treat me like\par
any other cabin passenger, rightly divining I would not kill her by\par
committing suicide or going over the side on chances.\par
\par
I was well treated all the way over, but every night my prayer was that\par
we might run on an iceberg or go down, so that my wife might be spared\par
long years of agony and me from the misery and degradation of prison\par
life.\par
\par
I had obtained a position in Havana for one of my servants, but Nunn was\par
returning with me, feeling very badly and most unhappy over the sure\par
prospect of my future misery. I was pleased to think he had held on to\par
the money I had given him. Altogether, he was quite $2,000 ahead, and I\par
wanted to make it $5,000. He certainly deserved it for his constancy and\par
affection.\par
\par
One lovely June day we sailed into Plymouth, there to land mail and such\par
passengers as wanted to take the express to London. I instructed my wife\par
to go to Southampton while I went ashore with my guardians.\par
\par
From the London Times, June 10, 1873:\par
\par
     "Among the passengers who landed at Plymouth yesterday morning from\par
     the royal mail steamer Moselle was Bidwell, otherwise F. A. Warren,\par
     in charge of Detective Sergeant Michael Hayden and William Green,\par
     accompanied by Capt. John Curtin and Walter Perry of Mr.\par
     Pinkerton's staff. They were joined by Inspector Wallace and\par
     Detective Sergeant William Moss of the city police, who had come\par
     down from London the previous night to meet the steamer.\par
\par
     [Illustration: CHATHAM--CONVICTS AT LABOR.]\par
\par
     "It being known that Bidwell was expected from Havana in the\par
     Moselle, an enormous crowd assembled in Milbay pier to await the\par
     return of the steam tender with the mail, in order to get a sight\par
     of the prisoner, and so great was the crowd that it was with some\par
     difficulty that Bidwell and his escort managed to reach cabs, and\par
     were driven to the Duke of Cornwall Hotel adjoining the railway\par
     station. They left by the 12.45 train for London. A crowd of\par
     20,000 persons were present to see them off, and cheered Bidwell\par
     heartily.\par
\par
     "Bidwell will be taken before the Lord Mayor in the justice room at\par
     the Mansion House this morning."\par
\par
Accompanied by my escort of six, I arrived in London one bright Spring\par
morning, just as the mighty masses of that great Babylon were thronging\par
in their thousands toward Epsom Downs, where on that day the Derby, that\par
pivotal event in the English year, was to be run. All London was astir,\par
and had put on holiday attire, while I, now a poor weed drifting to rot\par
on Lethe's wharf, was on my way to Newgate.\par
\par
Newgate! Then it had come to this! The Primrose Way wherein I had walked\par
and lived delicately at the expense of honor, ended here!\par
\par
"Whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap," was written by one\par
Paul. The wisdom of many was here and condensed in the wit of one, and\par
one with the shrewdest insight into things and a practical knowledge of\par
human history.\par
\par
I was a prisoner in Newgate. Newgate! The very name casts a chill; so,\par
too, does a sight of that granite fortress rising there in the heart of\par
mighty London. Amid all the throbbing life of that great Babylon it\par
stands--chill and grim--and has stood a prison fortress for 500 years.\par
Through all those linked centuries how many thousands of the miserable\par
and heartbroken of every generation have been garnered within its cold\par
embrace! What sights and sounds those old walls have seen and heard! As\par
I paced its gloomy corridors that first night, pictures of its past rose\par
before me so grim and terrible that I turned shuddering from them, only\par
to remember that I, too, had joined the long unending procession ever\par
flowing through its gates, which had heaped its walls to the top with\par
one inky sea of misery.\par
\par
In the cruel days of old many a savage sentence had fallen from the lips\par
of merciless judges, but none more terrible than the one which was to\par
fall on us from the lips of their ferocious imitator, Justice Archibald.\par
\par
I found my three friends already prisoners there, and a sad party we\par
were. When we said good-bye that night on the wharf at Calais, where we\par
sat star-gazing and philosophizing, we little anticipated this reunion.\par
\par
What a rude surprise it was to find how things were conducted in this\par
same Newgate. I took it for granted--since the law regarded us as\par
innocent until we were tried and convicted--that we could have any\par
reasonable favor granted us there which was consistent with our safe\par
keeping. But no. The system of the convict prison was enforced here, and\par
with the same iron rigor. Strict silence was the rule along with the\par
absolute exclusion of newspapers and all news of the outside world. The\par
rules forbid any delicacy or books being furnished by one's friends from\par
the outside. This iron system is as cruel as unphilosophical, for,\par
pending trial, the inmates are more or less living in a perfect agony of\par
mind, which drives many into insanity or to the verge of insanity, as it\par
did me. How can one, then, when the past is remorse--and the present and\par
future despair--find oblivion or raze out the written troubles of the\par
brain save in absorption in books.\par
\par
When Claudo is doomed to die and go "he knew not where," peering into\par
the abyss, the fear strikes him that in the unknown he may be "prisoned\par
in the viewless winds" and blown with restless violence round about this\par
pendant world. A terrible figure! It filled at this time some corner of\par
my brain and would not out. It went with me up and down in all my walks\par
in Newgate.\par
\par
[Illustration: PRINCIPAL WARDERS, WOKING PRISON. No. 1 Scott, No. 2.\par
Metherell.]\par
\par
[Illustration: ASSISTANT WARDERS, DARTMOOR PRISON.]\par
\par
If I had the pen of Victor Hugo, what a picture I would draw of a mind\par
consciously going down into the fearful abyss of insanity, making mighty\par
struggles against it, yet looking on the cold walls shutting one in and\par
weighing down the spirit, feeling that the struggle is ineffectual,\par
the fight all in vain, for the dead, blank walls are staring coldly on\par
you, without giving one reflex message, bearing on their gray surface no\par
thought, no response of mind. For they have been looked over with\par
anxious care to discover if any other mind had recorded there some\par
thought which would awake thought in one's own, and help to shake off\par
the fearful burden pressing one to earth. As a fact, a man so situated\par
does--aye, must--make an effort to leave some visible impress of his\par
mind as a message to his kind. It is a natural law, and the instinct is\par
part of one's being. It is a passion of the mind--a longing to be united\par
to the spiritual mass of minds from which the isolated soul is suffering\par
an unnatural divorce of hideous material walls.\par
\par
It is this law which makes the savage place his totem on the rocks, and\par
it is, thanks to the same instinct, that this very day our savants are\par
finding beneath the foundations of the temples and palaces which once\par
decked the Phoenician plain, the baked tablets which tell us the family\par
histories, no less than the story of the empires of those days. When the\par
impress was made on the soft clay to be fire-hardened, each writer felt\par
or hoped in the long ages in the far-off unknown,\par
\par
  "When time is old and hath forgot itself,\par
  When water drops have worn the streets of Troy\par
  And blind oblivion swallowed cities up,\par
  And mighty States, characterless, are grated\par
  To dusty nothing"----\par
\par
then some thought, some message from their minds, there impressed on the\par
senseless clay, would be communicated to some other mind, and wake a\par
response there.\par
\par
Many a time, with a brain reeling in agony, did I turn and stare blankly\par
at those walls, and, in a sort of dumb stupor, search them over in hope\par
to find some word, some message impressed there, some scratch of pen or\par
finger nail. It might be a message of misery, some outcry from a wounded\par
spirit, some expression of despair.\par
\par
Had there been one such--had there been! Every one of my predecessors\par
had left a message on that smooth-painted wall, but the red-tape\par
official rogues--the stultified images sans reason, sans all\par
imagination--had, after the departure of each one, carefully painted\par
over all such legacies.\par
\par
The hideous cruelty of it all! My blood, boils even now, when I think of\par
it. Even in the days of Elizabeth the keepers of the Tower of London had\par
enough human feeling to leave untouched the inscriptions made by Raleigh\par
and others, and there they are to-day, and to-day wake a response in the\par
heart of every visitor that looks on them.\par
\par
[Illustration: A GANG IN BLOUSES MARCHING OUT.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXV.\par
\par
RUNNING THE GAUNTLET.\par
\par
\par
My life at Newgate was an ordeal such as I hope no reader of this will\par
ever undergo. Day by day I saw the world slipping from under my feet,\par
and the net drawing its deadly folds closer around me. Soon we all were\par
forced to realize there was no escape for any of us.\par
\par
Of course, we were all guilty and deserved punishment--I need not say we\par
did not think so then--but the evidence was most weak, and had our trial\par
taken place in America under the too liberal construction of our laws,\par
undoubtedly we all would have escaped. But in England there is no court\par
of criminal appeal, as with us, and when once the jury gives a verdict,\par
that ends the matter. The result is that if judges are prejudiced, or\par
want a man convicted, as in our case, he never escapes. The jury is\par
always selected from the shopkeeping class, and they are horribly\par
subservient to the aristocratic classes. They don't care for\par
evidence--they simply watch the judge. If he smiles, the prisoner is\par
innocent. If he frowns, then, of course, guilty.\par
\par
With us when a man is charged with an offense against the laws he\par
engages a lawyer--one is sufficient and quite costly enough. In England\par
they are divided into three classes, viz.: solicitors, barristers and\par
Queen's Counsels.\par
\par
The solicitor takes the case and transacts all the business connected\par
with it. A barrister is the lawyer who is employed by the solicitor to\par
conduct the case in court and make the pleadings. He never comes in\par
contact with the client, but takes the brief and all instructions from\par
the solicitor. The Queen's Counsel is a lawyer of a higher rank, and\par
whenever his serene lordship takes a brief he must, to keep up his\par
dignity, "be supported" by a barrister. So my reader will perhaps\par
understand the raison d'etre of the proverb, "The lawyers own England."\par
As no solicitor can plead in court, so no Queen's Counsel will come in\par
direct contact with a client, and must be "supported" by a barrister.\par
Ergo, any unfortunate having a case in court must fee two, if not three\par
legal sharks to represent him, if represented at all.\par
\par
We employed as solicitor a Mr. David Howell of 105 Cheapside, and a\par
thoroughgoing, unprincipled rascal he proved to be. He was a small,\par
spare, undersized man, with little beady eyes, light complexion, red\par
hair, and stubby beard, and when he spoke it was with a thin reedy\par
voice. From first to last he managed our case in exactly the way the\par
prosecution would have desired. He bled us freely, and altogether we\par
paid him nearly $10,000, and our defense by our eight lawyers--four\par
Queen's Counsels and four barristers--was about the lamest and most\par
idiotic possible.\par
\par
We early came to the unanimous conclusion that in our country Howell\par
would have had to face a jury for robbing us, and that but one of our\par
eight lawyers had ability enough to appear in a police court here to\par
conduct a hearing before an ordinary magistrate.\par
\par
I do not propose to enter into the details of our preliminary hearings\par
before the Lord Mayor at the Mansion House, or of the trial. Both the\par
hearings and trial were sensational in the highest degree, and attracted\par
universal attention all over the English-speaking world. Full-page\par
pictures of the trial appeared in all the illustrated journals of Europe\par
and America, and our portraits were on sale everywhere.\par
\par
After many hearings before Sir Sidney Waterlaw, we were finally\par
committed for trial.\par
\par
Editorial from the London Times of Aug. 13, 1873:\par
\par
THE BANK FORGERIES.\par
\par
     "Monday next has been fixed for the trial, and the depositions\par
     taken before the Lord Mayor at the Justice Room of the Mansion\par
     House by Mr. Oke, the chief clerk, have been printed for the\par
     convenience of the presiding judge and of the counsel on both\par
     sides. They extend over 242 folio pages, including the oral and\par
     documentary evidence, and make of themselves a thick volume,\par
     together with an elaborate index for ready reference. Within living\par
     memory there has been no such case for length and importance heard\par
     before any Lord Mayor of London in its preliminary stage, nor one\par
     which excited a greater amount of public interest from first to\par
     last. The Overend Gurney prosecution is the only one in late years\par
     which at all approaches it in those respects, but in that the\par
     printed depositions only extended over 164 folio pages, or much\par
     less than those in the Bank case, in which as many as 108 witnesses\par
     gave evidence before the Lord Mayor, and the preliminary\par
     examinations--twenty-three in number from first to last--lasted\par
     from the first of March until the 2d of July, exclusive of the time\par
     spent in remands."\par
\par
From the London Times, Aug. 10, 1873:\par
\par
     "On the opening of the August sessions of the Old Bailey Central\par
     Criminal Court. The court and streets were much crowded from the\par
     beginning, and continued so throughout the day. Alderman Sir Robert\par
     Carden, representing the Lord Mayor; Mr. Alderman Finis, Mr.\par
     Alderman Besley, Mr. Alderman Lawrence, M.P., Mr. Alderman Whetham\par
     and Mr. Alderman Ellis, as commissioners of the Court, occupied\par
     seats upon the bench, as did also Alderman Sheriff White.\par
\par
     "Sheriff Sir Frederick Perkins, Mr. Under-Sheriff Hewitt and Mr.\par
     Under-Sheriff Crosley, Mr. R. B. Green, Mr. R. W. Crawford, M.P.,\par
     Governor of the Bank. Mr. Lyall, Deputy Governor, and Mr. Alfred de\par
     Rothschild were present. The members of the bar mustered in force,\par
     and the reserved seats were chiefly occupied by ladies. Mr.\par
     Hardinge Gifford, Q.C. (now Lord Chancellor of the British Empire),\par
     and Mr. Watkin Williams, Q.C. (instructed by Messrs. Freshfield,\par
     the solicitors of the bank), appeared as counsel for the\par
     prosecution."\par
\par
For eight mortal days the final trial dragged on, and there we were\par
pilloried in that horrible dock--a spectacle for the staring throngs\par
that flocked to see the young Americans who had found a pregnable spot\par
in the impregnable Bank of England.\par
\par
The misery of those eight days! No language can describe it, nor would I\par
undergo it again for the wealth of the world.\par
\par
The court was filled with fashionables, ladies as well, who flocked to\par
stare at misery, while the corridors of the Old Bailey and the street\par
itself were packed with thousands eager to catch a glimpse of us. The\par
Judge, in scarlet, sat in solemn state, with members of the nobility or\par
gouty Aldermen in gold chains and robes on the bench beside him. The\par
body of the court was filled with bewigged lawyers--a tippling lot of\par
sharks and rogues, always after lunch half tipsy with the punch or dry\par
sherry which English lawyers drink, jesting and cracking jokes,\par
unmindful of the fate of their clients. Capt. Curtin and a score of\par
detectives were present.\par
\par
No fewer than 213 witnesses were called by the prosecution. Of these\par
about fifty were from America, and by them they traced our lives for\par
many years before. As the forged bills were all sent by mail it was\par
necessary to convict us by circumstantial evidence. The evidence was all\par
very weak, save only in that remarkable matter of the blotting paper.\par
Our conviction was a foregone conclusion.\par
\par
The jury retired to consider their verdict shortly after 7 o'clock, and\par
on returning into court after the lapse of about a quarter of an hour\par
they gave in a verdict of guilty against all of the four prisoners.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXVI.\par
\par
"NOTHING LEFT US BUT A GRAVE, THAT SMALL MODEL OF THE BARREN EARTH," WITH\par
DISHONOR FOR AN EPITAPH.\par
\par
\par
Judge Archibald proceeded to pass sentence. He began with the\par
interesting and truthful remark: "I have anxiously considered whether\par
anything less than the maximum penalty of the law will be adequate to\par
meet the requirements of this case, and I think not." We had information\par
that a few days previously a meeting of judges had been held and that he\par
had been advised to pass a life sentence. What he really meant to say\par
was that he had anxiously considered whether anything less would be\par
adequate to satisfy the Bank of England. He went on to say that we had\par
not only inflicted great loss on the bank, but had also seriously\par
discredited that great institution in the eyes of the public. He\par
continued: "It is difficult to see the motives for this crime; it was\par
not want, for you were in possession of a large sum of money. You are\par
men of education, some of you speak the Continental languages, and you\par
have traveled considerably. I see no reason to make any distinction\par
between you, and let it be understood from the sentence which I am about\par
to pass upon you that men of education"--and he might have added, what\par
he undoubtedly thought, Americans--"who commit crimes which none but men\par
of education can commit must expect a terrible retribution, and that\par
sentence is penal servitude for life, and I further order that each one\par
of you pay one-fourth of the costs of prosecution--L49,000, or $245,000\par
in all."\par
\par
And, after all, what aroused so greatly his indignation? It was simply\par
this--because we were youngsters and Americans, and had successfully\par
assaulted the fondly imagined impregnable Bank of England, and, worse\par
still, had held up to the laughter of the whole world its red-tape\par
idiotic management, for had the bank asked so common a thing as a\par
reference the fraud would have been made impossible.\par
\par
Let my reader contrast this modern Jeffreys, his savage tirade, and, for\par
an offense against property, this most brutal sentence, with the\par
treatment of the Warwickshire bank wreckers. Greenaway, the manager of\par
this bank, and three of the directors by false balance sheets and\par
perjured reports for years had looted the bank, finally robbing the\par
depositors of L1,000,000, several of whom committed suicide and\par
thousands more of whom were ruined.\par
\par
They were tried, convicted, and in being sentenced were told that, being\par
men of high social position, the disgrace in itself was a severe\par
punishment; therefore, he should take that fact into consideration, and\par
ended by sentencing two to eight months', one to twelve and one to\par
fourteen months' imprisonment.\par
\par
We were sentenced late at night--nearly 10 o'clock--a smoky, foggy\par
London night. The court was packed, the corridors crowded, and when the\par
jury came in with their verdict the suppressed excitement found vent.\par
But when the vindictive and unheard-of sentence fell from the lips of\par
this villain Judge an exclamation of horror fell from that crowded\par
court.\par
\par
We turned from the Judge and went down the stairs to the entrance to the\par
underground passage leading to Newgate. There we halted to say farewell.\par
\par
[Illustration: BEFORE THE GOVERNOR--ASSISTANT WARDER REPORTING A\par
PRISONER FOR TALKING.]\par
\par
To say farewell! Yes. The Primrose Way had come to an end, but we were\par
comrades and friends still, and in order that in the gloom of the\par
slow-moving days and the blackness and thick horror of the years to come\par
we might have some thought in common, we then and there promised--what\par
could we poor, broken bankrupts promise?\par
\par
Where or to what in the thick horror enshrouding us could we turn? We\par
had\par
\par
  "Nothing left us to call our own save death,\par
  And that small model of the barren earth\par
  Which serves as paste and cover to our bones;"\par
\par
nothing but a grave, that\par
\par
  "Small model of the barren earth,"\par
\par
with dishonor and degradation for our epitaph!\par
\par
But there, in the very instant of our overwhelming defeat, standing in\par
the dark mouth of the stone conduit leading from the Old Bailey to the\par
dungeons of Newgate, by virtue of the high resolve we made, we conquered\par
Fate at her worst, and by our act in establishing a secret bond of\par
sympathy in our separation dropped the bad, disastrous past, and\par
starting on new things planted our feet on the bottom round of the\par
ladder of success, feeling that, with plenty of faith and endurance,\par
Fortune, frown as she might now, must in some distant day turn her wheel\par
and smile again.\par
\par
And what was this act? Why, it was a simple one, but bore in it the germ\par
of great things.\par
\par
As we halted there in the gloom we swore never to give in, however they\par
might starve us, even grind us to powder, as we felt they would\par
certainly try to do. We knew that in their anxiety about our souls they\par
would be sure kindly to furnish each with a Bible, and we promised to\par
read one chapter every day consecutively, and, while reading the same\par
chapter at the same hour, think of the others. For twenty years we kept\par
the promise. Then, making the resolve mentioned in the beginning of this\par
book, I marched back to my cell. The door was opened and closed behind\par
me, leaving me in pitch darkness--a convict in my dungeon. Dressed as I\par
was I lay down on the little bed there, and through all that long and\par
terrible night, with a million dread images rushing through my brain, I\par
lay passive, with wide-open eyes, staring into the darkness, conscious\par
that sanity and insanity were struggling for mastery in my brain, while\par
I, like some interested spectator, watched the struggle; or, again, I\par
was struggling in the air with some powerful but viewless monster form,\par
that clutched my throat with iron fingers, but whose body was impalpable\par
to the grasp of my hands. A mighty space, an eternity of time and\par
daylight came. Then, like one in a dream, I rose mechanically, and,\par
finding the pin I had secreted, I stood on the little wooden bench, and,\par
impelled by some spiritual but irresistible force, I scratched on the\par
wall the message I had resolved to leave:\par
\par
  "In the reproof of chance\par
  Lies the true proof of men."\par
\par
Then I thought of my friends and my promise, and, like one in a dream, I\par
took the ill-smelling and dirty little Bible from the shelf, and,\par
turning to the first chapter, read:\par
\par
    "And the spirit of God moved upon the waters." ...\par
  "And God said let there be light, and there was light."\par
\par
Then the book fell from my hand, and I remembered no more. My mind had\par
gone whirling into the abyss.\par
\par
I was sentenced on Wednesday. For three days, from Thursday to Sunday,\par
my mind was a blank. I have no recollection of my removal under escort\par
from Newgate to Pentonville.\par
\par
On Sunday, the fourth day of my sentence, like one rousing from a\par
trance, I awoke to find myself shaven and shorn, dressed in a coarse\par
convict uniform, in a rough cell of white-washed brick. The small window\par
had heavy double bars set with thick fluted glass, which, while\par
admitting light, foiled any attempt of the eye to discern objects\par
without. In the corner there was a rusty iron shelf. A board let into\par
the brickwork served for bed, bench and table. A zinc jug and basin for\par
water, with a wooden plate, spoon and salt dish (no knife or fork for\par
twenty years!) completed the furnishings.\par
\par
As I was looking around in a helpless way a key suddenly rattled in the\par
lock and, the door opening, a uniformed warder stepped in and, giving me\par
a searching look, said in a rough voice: "Come on; you'll do for chapel;\par
you have put on the balmy long enough." His kindly face belied his rough\par
tones, and I followed him out of the door and soon found myself in the\par
prison chapel. None was present, and I was ordered to sit on the front\par
bench at the far end. The benches were simply common flat boards ranged\par
in rows. Soon the prisoners came in singly, marching about two yards\par
apart, and sat on the benches with that interval between them--that is,\par
in the division of the chapel where I sat, it being separated from the\par
rest by a high partition. Soon a white-robed, surpliced clergyman came\par
in, and the service began; but I had no eye or ear, nor any\par
comprehension save in a dim manner, as to what was going on. My brain\par
was trying to connect the past and the present, feeling that something\par
terrible had befallen me, but what it was I could not understand. When\par
the services were over I returned under the escort of the warder, who,\par
when I arrived at my cell, ordered me to go in and close the door, which\par
I did, banging it behind me. It had a spring lock, and when I heard the\par
snap of the catch and looked at the narrow, barred window, with its\par
thick, fluted glass admitting only a dim light, I remembered everything.\par
Like a flash it all came to me, and I realized the full horror of my\par
position. Sitting down on the little board fastened to the wall, serving\par
as bed, seat and table, I buried my face in my hands and began to\par
ponder. Regrets came in floods, with remorse and despair, hand in hand,\par
when, realizing that it was madness to think, I sprang up, saying to\par
myself the hour and minute had come for me to decide--either for\par
madness and a convict's dishonored grave, or to keep the promise I had\par
made to my friends--never to give in, but to live and conquer fate.\par
\par
I determined then and there to live in the future, and never to dwell on\par
the horrible present or past. Then I remembered the last scene in\par
Newgate and my promise to accompany my friends step by step, day by day,\par
in our readings. Finding a Bible on the little rusty iron shelf in the\par
corner, and this being the fourth day of our sentence, I turned to the\par
fourth chapter. It gives the story of Cain's crime and punishment, and I\par
read the graphic narrative with an intensity of interest difficult to\par
describe. When I read, "And Cain said unto the Lord, my punishment is\par
greater than I can bear. Behold, thou hast driven me out this day from\par
the face of the earth," I felt that the cry of Cain in all its intense\par
naturalness, in its remorse and despair, was my own, and I was overcome.\par
Laying the book down, I walked the floor for an hour in agony, until\par
fantastic images came thronging thick and fast to my brain. I realized\par
that my mind was going and felt I must do something to make me forget my\par
misery.\par
\par
I opened the Bible at random and my eye caught the word "misery." I\par
looked closely at the verse and read:\par
\par
"Thou shalt forget thy misery, and remember it as waters that pass\par
away."\par
\par
I threw the book down, crying with vehemence, "That's a lie! God never\par
gives something for nothing." Soon I opened the book again and looked at\par
the context. Those of my readers who care to do so can do the same. The\par
verse is Job xi., 16. The context begins at verse 13. From that hour I\par
never despaired again.\par
\par
The same day I began committing the Book of Job to memory, and worked\par
for dear life and reason. I became interested, and my interest in that\par
wondrous poem deepened until the study became a passion. Thus I turned\par
the whole current of my thoughts into a new channel. Reason came back,\par
and with it resolution and courage and strength.\par
\par
I was in Pentonville Prison, in the suburbs of London. All men convicted\par
in England are sent to this prison to undergo one year's solitary\par
confinement. At the completion of the year they are drafted away to the\par
public works' prisons, where, working in gangs, they complete their\par
sentences.\par
\par
Of my experience in Pentonville during my year of solitude it suffices\par
to say that, passing through a great deal of mental conflict, I found I\par
had grown stronger and was eager for transfer to the other prison, where\par
I could for a few hours each day at least look on the sky and the faces\par
of my fellow men.\par
\par
At last the day of transfer came, and, escorted by two uniformed and\par
armed warders, I was taken to the famous Chatham Prison, twenty-seven\par
miles from London on the river Medway....\par
\par
"You were sent here to work, and you will have to do it or I will make\par
you suffer for it," was the friendly greeting that fell on my ears as I\par
stood before a pompous little fellow (an ex-major from the army) at\par
Chatham Prison one lovely morning in 1874.\par
\par
I had arrived there under escort but an hour before, strong in the\par
resolve to obey the regulations if I could, and never to give in if I\par
had a fair chance; also with a desperate resolve never to submit to\par
persecution, come what might, and these resolutions saved me--but only\par
by a steady and dogged adherence to them on many occasions, through many\par
years and amid surroundings that might well make me--as it did and does\par
many good men--desperate and utterly reckless.\par
\par
After a few more remarks of a very personal and pungent nature the\par
little fellow marched off with a delicious swagger and an heroical air.\par
I at once turned to the warder and asked, "Who is that little fellow?"\par
"The Governor!" he gasped out. "If he had only heard you!" and then\par
followed a pantomime that implied something very dreadful. Then I\par
marched off to the doctor, and next to the chaplain, who (knowing who I\par
was) asked me if I could read and write, to which I meekly replied,\par
"Yes, sir;" but apparently being doubtful upon the point he gave me a\par
book. Opening it and pretending to read, I said in a solemn tone of\par
voice: "When time and place adhere write me down an ass." He took the\par
book from me, looked at the open page, gazed solemnly in my face with a\par
funny wagging of his head, as much as to say, "you will come to no\par
good," and followed the little major.\par
\par
Then my cicerone took me into the main building, filled up to the brim\par
with what seemed to be little brick and stone boxes, and, halting in\par
front of one, said, "This is your cell." Looking around to see if it was\par
safe to talk, he began to question me rapidly about my case, and getting\par
no satisfaction he wound up the questioning with the remark: "Well, you\par
tried to take all our money over to America." Then, becoming\par
confidential, he told me what wicked fellows the other prisoners were,\par
chiefly because they went to the Governor and reported the officers,\par
charging them with maltreatment and bullying particularly, and knocking\par
them about generally. Of course, the warders never did such things, but\par
were really of a very lamblike and gentle nature. In order to back up\par
their lies the prisoners would knock their own heads against the walls\par
and then swear by everything good that some one of the warders had done\par
it. I said, perhaps he had.\par
\par
Well, he said, perhaps an officer might give a man "a little clip," but\par
never so as to hurt him, and "only in fun, you know." I felt at the time\par
that I would never learn to appreciate Chatham "fun," but on the very\par
next day I was convinced of it when a man named Farrier pulled out from\par
his waistband a piece of rag, and, unrolling it, produced two of his\par
front teeth with the information that a certain warder had struck him\par
with his fist in the mouth and knocked them out.\par
\par
But to return to my narrative. After many "wise saws and modern\par
instances," he locked me up in the little brick and stone box and\par
departed, having first informed me that I "would go out to labor in the\par
morning."\par
\par
I looked about my little box with a mixture of curiosity and\par
consternation, for the thought smote me with blinding force that for\par
long years that little box--eight feet six inches in length, seven feet\par
in height and five feet in width, with its floor and roof of\par
stone--would be my only home--would be! must be! and no power could\par
avert my fate.\par
\par
On the small iron shelf I found a tin dish used by some previous\par
occupant, and smeared inside and out with gruel. There being no water in\par
my jug, when the men came in for dinner, I, in my innocence, asked one\par
of the officers for some water to wash the dish. He looked at me with\par
great contempt and said: "You are a precious flat; lick it off, man.\par
Before long you won't waste gruel by washing your tin dish. You won't be\par
here many days and want to use water to clean your pint."\par
\par
After dinner I saw the men marched out to labor, and was amazed to see\par
their famished, wolfish looks--thin, gaunt and almost disguised out of\par
all human resemblance by their ill-fitting, mud-covered garments and\par
mud-splashed faces and hands. I myself was kept in, but the weary,\par
almost ghastly spectre march I had witnessed constantly haunted me, and\par
I said, "Will I ever resemble them?" And youthful spirit and pride\par
rushed to the front and cried, "Never!"\par
\par
Night and supper (eight ounces of brown bread) came at length, and I\par
rose up from my meal cheerful and resolute to meet the worst, be it what\par
it might short of deliberate persecution, with a stout heart and faith\par
that at last all would be well.\par
\par
In the morning I arose, had my breakfast (nine ounces of brown bread and\par
one pint of gruel), and was eager to learn what this "labor" meant. I\par
was prepared for much, but not for the grim reality. I had been ordered\par
to join eighty-two party--a brickmaking party, but working in the "mud\par
districts." So we, along with 1,200 others, marched out to our work, and\par
as soon as we were outside of the prison grounds I saw a sight that,\par
while it explained the mud-splashed appearance of my spectral array, was\par
enough to daunt any man doomed to join in the game. Mud, mud everywhere,\par
with groups of weary men with shovel, or shovel and barrow, working in\par
it. A sort of road had been made over the mud with ashes and cinders,\par
and our party of twenty-two men, with five other parties, moved steadily\par
on for about a mile until we came to the clay banks or pits. Fortunately\par
we had a very good officer by the name of James. He wanted the work\par
done, and used his tongue pretty freely; still he was a man who would\par
speak the truth, and treated his men as well as he dared to do under the\par
brutal regime ruling in Chatham. He speedily told me off to a barrow and\par
spade, and I was fully enlisted as barrow-and-spade man to Her Majesty.\par
\par
A steam mill, or "pug," like a monster coffee mill, was used for mixing\par
the clay and sand and delivering it in form of bricks below, where\par
another party received them and laid them out to dry, preparatory to\par
burning. Our duty was "to keep the pug going"--keep it full of clay to\par
the top. The clay was in a high bank; we dug into it from the bottom\par
with our spades, and filled it as fast as possible into our barrows. In\par
front of each man was a "run," formed by a line of planks only eight\par
inches in width, and all converging toward and meeting near the "pug."\par
The distance we were wheeling was from thirty to forty yards, end the\par
incline was really very steep; but that in itself would not have been so\par
bad, but the labor of digging out the clay was severe, and that\par
everlasting "pug" was as hungry as if it were in the habit of taking\par
"Plantation Bitters" to give it an appetite.\par
\par
One had no period of rest between the filling of one's barrow and the\par
start up the run. In an hour's time my poor hands were covered with\par
blood blisters, and my left knee was a lame duck indeed, made so by the\par
slight wrench given it each time I struck in my spade with my left foot;\par
but I made no complaint. About 10 o'clock the man next to me with an\par
oath threw down his spade and vowed he would do no more work. Putting on\par
his vest and packet, he walked up to the warder, and quite as a matter\par
of course turned his back to him and put both hands behind him. The\par
warder produced a pair of handcuffs, and without any comment handcuffed\par
his hands in that position, and then told him to stand with his back to\par
the work. No one took the slightest notice and the toil did not slacken\par
for an instant, but one man was out of the game, and we had to make his\par
side good.\par
\par
Noon came at last. We dropped our spades, hastily slipped on our jackets\par
and at once set off at a quick march for the prison. I naturally looked\par
at the various gangs piloting their way through the mud and all steering\par
in a straight line for the Appian way whereon we were, for, as all roads\par
lead to Rome, so all the sticky ways "on the works" led to the prison.\par
Our laconic friend was trudging on behind the party, and to my surprise\par
I noticed that several of the other parties had un enfant perdu, hands\par
behind his back, marching in the rear, and as soon as we reached the\par
prison each poor sheep in the rear fell out quite as a matter of course.\par
When all the men were in, a warder came up and gave the order, "Right\par
turn! Forward!" and off the poor fellows marched to the punishment cells\par
for three days' bread and water each, and no bed, unless one designates\par
an oak plank as such. It was all very sad; 'twas pitiful to see the\par
matter-of-fact way in which every one concerned took it all.\par
\par
So my first day in the mud and clay came to an end, and I found myself\par
once more in my little box with a night before me for rest and thought.\par
Although I had suffered, yet there were grounds for gratitude and hope,\par
and I felt that I might regard the future steadily and without despair.\par
\par
[Illustration: VISITOR TRYING ON THE HANGMAN'S IR ON PINIONING BELT AT\par
NEWGATE.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXVII.\par
\par
HENCEFORTH A LIGHT WAS TO STREAM THROUGH THE FLUTED GLASS OF MY WINDOW.\par
\par
\par
The first day was over, but it seemed to me that something more must\par
come. That what I had gone through could mean the life of a day must\par
surely be impossible. Was there nothing before me but isolation so\par
complete that no whisper from the outside world could reach me, that\par
world which compared with the death into which I was being absorbed\par
seemed the only world of the living?\par
\par
Had I actually nothing to look for but the most repulsive work under the\par
most repulsive conditions? I said there must be surely some change, that\par
wheeling mud forever was not the doom of any man and could certainly not\par
be mine.\par
\par
I looked about my little cell, the stillness of the grave without, the\par
utter solitude within. The ration which formed my supper was on the\par
table, eight ounces of black bread. Try as I might to cheat myself with\par
hope, I knew that hope for many a long year there was none, that so far\par
as the most vindictive sentence could compass it, for many a long year\par
the earth with her bars was about me.\par
\par
No "De Profundis" cry could ever ascend from the abyss to the bottom of\par
which I had fallen. What was outside of me had nothing but the hideous.\par
\par
But although the visible seemed corruption, and the things which my\par
soul, and body, too, had refused to touch were become my sorrowful meat,\par
yet I could not but feel that the invisible, that part of me which no\par
bars could hold and no man deprive me of, was still my own, and that in\par
it I might and would find sufficient to support what I began to feel\par
was, after all, the only man.\par
\par
To face the actualities of the position was the first thing; not to\par
cheat myself, the second. I had seen the sort of men I was to be with. I\par
set to work to study and to understand the kind of life we were to live\par
together.\par
\par
At early dawn we rose, receiving immediately after the nine ounces of\par
bread and pint of oatmeal gruel which composed breakfast. At 6.30, to\par
chapel to hear one of the schoolmasters drone through the morning\par
prayers of the English Church service, and listen to some hymn shouted\par
out from throats never accustomed to such accents. Then the morning\par
hours would drag slowly on in the Summer's sun and Winter's blast until\par
the noon hour; then there was the long march back from the scene of my\par
toil to the prison for dinner. Arriving there, each man went to his\par
cell, closing his door, which snapped to, having a spring lock. Soon\par
after a dinner is given consisting of sixteen ounces of boiled potatoes\par
and five ounces of bread, varied on three days of the week with five\par
ounces of meat additional. At 1 o'clock the doors were unlocked and we\par
marched out to our work again. At night, returning to the prison, eight\par
ounces of black bread would be doled out for supper. Then came the hours\par
between supper and bedtime, when shut in between those narrow walls one\par
realized what it was to be a prisoner.\par
\par
In the corner of the cell there was a board let into the stonework.\par
There was a thin pallet and two blankets rolled up together during the\par
day in a corner of the cell that served for bedding, but so thin and\par
hard was the pallet that one might almost as well have slept on the\par
board. For the first few weeks this bed made my bones ache. Most men\par
have little patience and small fortitude, and this bed kills many of\par
the prisoners. I mean breaks their hearts, simply because they have not\par
the wit to accept the matter philosophically and realize that they can\par
soon become used to any hardship. It took six months for my bones to\par
become used to the hard bed, but for the next nineteen years I used to\par
sleep as sweetly on that oak board as I ever did or now do in a bed of\par
down, only, like Jean Valjean, in "Les Miserables," I had become so used\par
to it that upon my liberation I found it impossible for a time to sleep\par
in a bed.\par
\par
On a little rusty iron shelf, fixed in the corner, was our tinware.\par
Although called tinware, it really was zinc, and was susceptible,\par
through much hard work, of a high polish, but this "polishing tinware"\par
was a fearful curse to the poor prisoner. It consisted of a jug for\par
water and a bowl for washing in and a pint dish for gruel. There were\par
strict and imperative orders, rigidly enforced, that this tinware should\par
be kept polished, the result being that the men never washed themselves,\par
and never took water in their jugs, for if they did their tinware would\par
take a stain--"go off," as it was termed--the result being that if the\par
poor devil washed and kept himself clean he would be reported and\par
severely punished for having dirty tinware.\par
\par
A prisoner is not permitted to receive anything from his friends or\par
communicate with them in any way, save only once in three months he is\par
permitted to write and receive a letter, provided he is a good character\par
and has not been reported for any infraction of the rules for three\par
months; for if reported for any cause, however trifling, the privilege\par
of writing is postponed for three months, and, as a matter of fact, more\par
than half of the men never get a chance to write during their\par
imprisonment.\par
\par
A visit of half an hour once in three months is permitted, but this is a\par
favor that is only granted upon the same condition as the privilege of\par
letter writing.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXVIII.\par
\par
WHAT, THESE TEDIOUS DETAILS AGAIN.\par
\par
\par
It will be well to present here some account of those who were to rule\par
my life for so many years.\par
\par
The Board of Prison Commissioners have their headquarters at the Home\par
Office in Parliament street, London, and are under the control of the\par
Home Secretary of State. One of these visits each of Her Majesty's\par
convict establishments once a month, in order to try any cases of\par
insubordination which are of too serious a nature for the governor of\par
the prison to adjudicate upon, he not being permitted to order any\par
penalty beyond a few days of bread and water and loss of a limited\par
number of remission marks.\par
\par
The head authority at each prison is the governor, of whom the largest\par
establishments, like Chatham, have two. Next comes the deputy\par
governors--the medical officer and an assistant doctor; the chaplains\par
and schoolmasters, Protestant and Catholic. There are four grades of\par
prison warders, viz., the chief warder, principal warders, warders and\par
assistant warders. The chief warder, of course, stands first in the\par
list, and his duties, if honestly executed, render him the most\par
important, as he is the most responsible of the prison officials, save,\par
perhaps, the medical officer, who is the autocrat of the place. But, in\par
case anything goes wrong, he is the man who gets all the blame, and when\par
matters run smoothly and well, the governor gets all the thanks. During\par
the absence of the governor the deputy takes his place, and in turn the\par
chief warder performs the duties of the deputy governor's office. As all\par
business passes through the chief's hands, he must be a fair scholar,\par
though sometimes a principal warder who understands bookkeeping is\par
detailed to assist him. He must be of strict integrity, a thorough\par
disciplinarian, and of a character to make him respected both by his\par
superiors and inferiors in position. The warders of all grades are under\par
his command, and must fear him for his inflexibility in punishing any\par
breach of regulations, and have confidence in his disposition to act\par
justly toward them, he being the one on whom the governor relies for all\par
information regarding their conduct. It is on the reports of the chief\par
warder that the governor acts in all cases involving their promotion,\par
reprimands or fines, and their application for leave of absence must be\par
approved of and signed by him. It is clear that unless he is very\par
straight in the performance of his duties, he would soon place himself\par
in the power of some of the warders, who would not fail to take\par
advantage of any knowledge of his derelictions to benefit themselves,\par
and to the detriment of discipline and good order. Under the English\par
Government the salary of a man possessing these superior qualifications\par
is between $500 and $600 a year and his uniform. This is of blue cloth,\par
the sleeves and collar of his coat and his cap embroidered with gold\par
lace. On alternate days, at the prison where I was confined, he came on\par
duty at 5 a.m. in Summer and 5.30 in Winter, and left the prison at 4\par
p.m., leaving in charge a principal warder, coming on duty the following\par
morning at 7 a.m. At 6 o'clock p.m., after receiving the reports from\par
the ward officers, stating the number of prisoners each has just locked\par
up, and thus seeing that all are safe, he locks with his master key the\par
gates and outer doors of the main buildings, and before finally retiring\par
for the night he must lock the outer gate, so that no one but the\par
governor can get in or out--each watchman being locked into the ward\par
which he is set to guard. There are bells in his room connecting with\par
the various wards, and in case of sickness or any other emergency, he is\par
the man who is aroused. It is the chief warder who keeps everything\par
connected with the prison in running order, and whatever goes wrong the\par
cry is for the chief, and he is sent for, be it day or night.\par
\par
In a large establishment there are a dozen or more principal warders.\par
These are the lieutenants of the chief, and have general supervision of\par
the working parties. Their pay is about $400 a year and uniforms. There\par
are of the other two grades, warders and assistant warders, from two to\par
three thousand employed in all Her Majesty's prisons in Great Britain\par
and Ireland. Warders and assistant warders are provided with a short,\par
heavy truncheon, which each carries in his hand or in a leather sheath\par
which hangs from his belt, to which is also attached a sort of cartouch\par
box in which he keeps the keys, which are fastened to a chain, the other\par
end to his belt. When about to leave the prison, on going off duty, he\par
must hang up the belt and attachments in the chief warder's office.\par
Their pay, besides uniforms, which are of blue cloth, is $350 a year for\par
warders and $300 for assistant warders. All promotions are by seniority.\par
In case of transfer by authorities to any other prison, they retain\par
their position in the line of promotion, but if they volunteer or make\par
application to be transferred they have to begin at the bottom in\par
reckoning the length of service for promotion. When the authorities wish\par
to transfer warders, it is usual for them to call for volunteers, of\par
whom they find a sufficient number anxious for a change, unless the\par
transfer is to an unpopular station, such as Dartmoor, which is among\par
the bogs, and a lonely, bleak place.\par
\par
[Illustration: THEY DO IT DIFFERENTLY IN CHINA.]\par
\par
[Illustration: THEY DON'T USE STRAIGHT-JACKETS IN PERSIA.]\par
\par
Warders are exempted from doing night duty, which is all done by the\par
assistant warders, who are on that service one week out of three.\par
Although when on night duty they had the day for sleep and recreation,\par
I never saw one who did not detest it, because they must remain on duty\par
continuously for twelve hours, and must not read, sit down nor lean\par
against anything, nor have their hands behind them. These military\par
regulations apply as well to the whole time they are on duty in the\par
prison, day or night. A few years ago the time of daily duty was reduced\par
to twelve hours, with one hour at noon for dinner. Besides this, at\par
times they must do a good deal of extra duty. Each is allowed ten days\par
annual holiday, but is frequently obliged to take it piecemeal, a day or\par
two at a time, so that he cannot go far away from the scene of his\par
servitude. Their duties require unflagging attention and never-ceasing\par
vigilance, which must be a heavy tax on the brain, and the twelve hours\par
must be passed in standing or walking about. In fact, they are subjected\par
to military discipline, or rather despotism, and any known infraction of\par
the rules subjects them to penalties according to the nature of the\par
offense. Leaning against a wall, sitting down, etc., for a first\par
offense, they are mulcted in a small sum--12 to 60 cents, usually--and\par
are put back in the line of promotion. The fines go to the Officers'\par
Library fund. I knew one officer, Joseph Matthews, who had been\par
assistant warder twenty years, and, being frequently set back for doing\par
some small favor to prisoners, was discharged from the service in 1886,\par
without a pension, for some slight breach of regulations. He had a wife\par
and six children, and had worked twenty years for less than $7 per week.\par
For giving a convict a small bit of tobacco, a heavy fine, suspension,\par
and in case it was not the first offense, expulsion from the service\par
without a pension. For acting the go-between and facilitating\par
correspondence with the friends of convicts, expulsion--possibly\par
imprisonment. One of the assistant warders, who was convicted of having\par
received a bribe of L100 from one of us at Newgate, was expelled from\par
the service and imprisoned eighteen months. Another at Portsmouth\par
Prison underwent the same fate, save that his term was but six months,\par
for sending and receiving letters for a prisoner, and similar cases are\par
of frequent occurrence.\par
\par
The warders and assistant warders are the ones who come in direct and\par
constant contact with prisoners, and when the eye of no superior\par
authority is on them, or nothing else to deter, they are "hail fellow\par
well met" with such of the convicts as are unprincipled enough to curry\par
favor with and assist them in covering up their peccadilloes from their\par
superiors. They naturally recoil at the hardness and parsimony of the\par
Government toward them, evading the performance of duties when they can,\par
and I have heard more than one say: "Why should we care what prisoners\par
do, so long as we don't get into trouble? The Government grinds us down\par
to twelve hours' daily duty on just pay enough to keep body and soul\par
together; then, if we complain, tells us that we can leave if we like,\par
as there are others ready to step into our places. Bah! what do we care\par
for the Government? It is of no benefit to us; the big guns get big pay,\par
and the higher up the office the more the pay and the less the work. To\par
be sure, we can go out of the prison to sleep, but otherwise we are\par
bound as closely as you are." Yet these very warders, the moment any\par
superior authority appears on the scene, are as obsequious and fawning\par
as whipped dogs, and recoup themselves for this forced humiliation by\par
taking it out of such of the convicts as fail to curry their favor, or\par
offend, or make them trouble. Surely their office is a very responsible\par
one, and it is blind, false economy to retain low-priced men in such a\par
position. The present English system of penal servitude is perfect on\par
paper, but the moral qualities of most of the warders and assistant\par
warders preclude all possibility of the reformation of those in their\par
charge.\par
\par
Notwithstanding the expositions of the English delegates at the\par
international meetings, prison reform has never yet been tried in Great\par
Britain and Ireland. In other words, all efforts in that direction have\par
been defeated by placing convicts in the immediate charge of a class of\par
men who, by education and training, possess none of the qualifications\par
requisite for such a responsible position.\par
\par
In so far as forms are concerned, the business of the prison is carried\par
on most systematically. There are blank forms which cover everything,\par
from provisioning the prison to bathing the men, and these must be\par
filled in and signed by the warder in charge of the particular work\par
being done. For example, every week he must fill in the proper form and\par
certify that every man in his ward has had a bath. I have known men to\par
go unbathed for many months, simply because they did not wish to bathe,\par
and it saved the warder trouble--nearly all others in the ward only\par
bathed about once a month, and yet at the stated times the officer\par
filled up and signed the form, certifying to the superior authorities\par
that those in his ward had been bathed at the regulation times.\par
\par
A great majority of the officers are soldiers who have been invalided or\par
pensioned off after doing the full term for which they enlisted--twelve\par
years--and of sailors in the same condition. In order to encourage\par
enlistment into the army and navy, the Government gives discharged\par
soldiers and sailors the preference in the civil service, apparently\par
heedless as to their moral qualifications. Indeed, it would be\par
difficult, if not impossible, to ascertain about these, for the very\par
nature and present requirements of these services tend to harden and\par
make men conscienceless, subservient and fawning toward their superiors,\par
and tyrannical to those in their power.\par
\par
As to those in the prison service, there are many who would be good men\par
in a situation suited to their acquirements, and there are but a few of\par
those who are brought into immediate contact with the men--who, in fact,\par
virtually hold the power of life and death over them--whose influence is\par
of an elevating or reforming kind. Indeed, I have heard many of them\par
telling or exchanging obscene stories with prisoners, and using the\par
vilest language and bandying thieves' slang, in which they become\par
proficient. I am bold to say that at least one-half of all I have known\par
are in morals on a level with the average prisoner, or, as I have heard\par
more than one assistant warder say, "Too much of a coward to steal,\par
ashamed to beg and too lazy to work"--therefore became a soldier, then a\par
warder. This may, at the moment, have been spoken in a jesting way, but\par
it is none the less true.\par
\par
What can be expected in the way of refinement and good morals from a\par
class of men who entered the army or navy, coming, as they did in most\par
cases, from the untaught and mind-debased multitude with which that land\par
of drink and debauchery swarms?\par
\par
It will be seen from the foregoing that very much is expected from them,\par
and in order to fulfill the very hard terms of their contract with the\par
Government, and keep their places, they are forced to resort to\par
trickery, deception and perjury, until these, in their attitude toward\par
their employer, the Government, become second nature, readily resorting\par
to lies to clear themselves from blame, even in trivial matters, to save\par
themselves from a sixpence fine. There are jealousies among themselves,\par
but when it is a question of deceiving or keeping any neglect of duties\par
or violences against prisoners from the superior authorities they all\par
unite as one man and affirm or swear to anything they think the position\par
requires.\par
\par
A real pleasure was derived from those prisoners' friends, the rats and\par
mice, which I easily tamed and taught to be my companions.\par
\par
[Illustration: "COME ON. YOU ARE FREE."--Page 480.]\par
\par
Not long after my arrival a prisoner gave me a young rat which became\par
the solace of an otherwise miserable existence. Nothing could he cleaner\par
in its habits or more affectionate in disposition than this pet member\par
of a despised race of rodents. It passed all its leisure time in\par
preening its fur, and after eating always most scrupulously cleaned\par
its hands and face. It was easily taught, and in course of time it could\par
perform many surprising feats. I made a small trapeze, the bar being a\par
slate pencil about four inches long, which was wound with yarn and hung\par
from strings of the same; and on this the rat would perform like an\par
acrobat, appearing to enjoy the exercise as much as the performance\par
always delighted me. I made a long cord out of yarn, on which it would\par
climb exactly in the manner in which a sailor shins up a rope; and when\par
the cord was stretched horizontally it would let its body sway under and\par
travel along the cord, clinging by its hands and feet like a human\par
performer.\par
\par
A rat's natural position when eating a piece of bread is to sit on its\par
haunches, but I had trained this rat to stand upright on its feet, with\par
its head up like a soldier. Placing it in front of me on the bed, I\par
would hand it a piece of bread, which it would hold up to its mouth with\par
its hands while standing erect. Keeping one sharp eye on me and the\par
other on its food, the moment it noticed that I was not looking it would\par
gradually settle down upon its haunches. When my eyes turned on it it\par
would instantly straighten itself up like a schoolboy caught in some\par
mischief. It always showed great jealousy of my tame mice, and I had to\par
be very careful not to let it get a chance to get at one. On one\par
occasion I was training one of the mice, and did not notice that the rat\par
was near. Suddenly, like a flash, it leaped nearly two feet, seizing the\par
mouse by the neck precisely as a tiger seizes its prey. Although I\par
instantly snatched it away, it was too late, the one fierce bite having\par
severed the jugular.\par
\par
I have mentioned mice, and indeed they were most interesting pets,\par
easily trained and as scrupulously clean and neat as any creature of a\par
higher race could be. I at times had a half dozen of them, which I had\par
caught in the following simple way: I first stuck a small bit of bread\par
on the inside of my pint tin cup, about half way down; then turning it\par
bottom up on the floor, I raised one edge just high enough so that a\par
mouse could enter, and let the edge of the cup rest on a splinter. It\par
would not be long before one would enter, and as it could not reach the\par
bread otherwise it stood up, putting its hands against the sides of the\par
cup, thus over-balancing it, causing the cup to drop, and simple mousie\par
would find itself also a prisoner.\par
\par
Although there was an order that no prisoner should be permitted to have\par
any kind of pets, especially rats and mice, and as the prison swarmed\par
with these, the warders had become tired of being obliged to turn over\par
the cells and prisoners daily in search of these contraband favorites,\par
the loss of which generally provoked the owners to insubordination; in\par
consequence of which there was a tacit understanding that they were not\par
to be interfered with, provided they were kept out of sight when the\par
governor made his rounds.\par
\par
Nothing could overcome the jealousy of my otherwise gentle rat when it\par
saw me petting a mouse, and it would watch for an opportunity to spring\par
upon its diminutive rival and put a speedy end to its career.\par
\par
I had one mouse which to its other accomplishments added the following:\par
It would lie in the palm of my open hand, with its four legs up in the\par
air, pretending to be dead, only the little creature kept its bright\par
eyes wide open, fixed on my face. As soon as I said, "Come to life!" it\par
would spring up, rush along my arm and disappear into my bosom like a\par
flash.\par
\par
[Illustration: 1 Austin ----. 2 Geo. McDonald. 3 Officer. 4 Geo. Bidwell.\par
5 Officer. 6 Noyes. 7 Mr. Straight, Q.C. McDONALD SPEAKING TO MR.\par
STRAIGHT, Q.C., DURING THE TRIAL.]\par
\par
I had a mouse trained the same as the one above described, and was in\par
dread lest a warder should see and destroy it. Therefore, in the hope of\par
getting a guarantee for its safety, one day when the medical officer on\par
his round came to my cell with his retinue I put my mouse through the\par
"dead dog" performance. The little fellow lay exposed in my hand with\par
one of its twinkling eyes fixed on me, and the other on these strangers.\par
Such was its confidence in me that it went through the performance\par
perfectly, and when I gave the signal in an instant it was in my (as the\par
poor thing believed) protecting bosom. The doctors laughed, and the\par
retinue of course followed suit--if they had frowned the latter would\par
have done likewise. The doctors appeared so pleased that I felt certain\par
they would order the warder, as was in their power, to let me keep my\par
harmless pet, the sole companion of my solitude and misery, unmolested.\par
\par
They went outside the cell and lingered; in a moment then the warder\par
came in, and after a struggle got the mouse out of my bosom and put his\par
heel upon it. I am not ashamed to confess that I cried over the loss of\par
this poor little victim of overconfidence in human beings.\par
\par
I once procured a beetle with red stripes across its wing-sheaths, and\par
trained it to show some degree of intelligence. This was for months the\par
sole companion of my solitude, but it was at last discovered in my\par
possession and taken away.\par
\par
I made friends with the flies, and found that they displayed no small\par
degree of intelligence. I soon had a dozen tamed, and in the course of\par
my long observations I discovered, among other things, that the males\par
were very tyrannical over the fair sex, and tried to prevent them from\par
getting any of the food. In the Summer mornings at daylight they would\par
gather on the wall next my bed and wait patiently until I placed a\par
little chewed bread on the back of my hand, when instantly there was a\par
rush, and the first one who got possession, if a male, tried to prevent\par
the rest from alighting, and would dart at the nearest, chasing it in\par
zig-zags far away. In the mean time another would have attained\par
possession, and it went for the next corner, and for a long time there\par
would be a succession of fierce encounters, until at last all had made\par
good their footing and feasted harmoniously; for as fast as one\par
succeeded in alighting it was let alone. Sometimes a male would take\par
possession of my forehead, and, in case I left him unmolested, he would\par
keep off intruders on what he evidently considered his domain by darting\par
at them in a ferocious manner. On one occasion I noticed a fly that had\par
one of its hind legs turned up, apparently out of joint. As it was\par
feeding on my hand I tried to put my finger on the leg to press it down.\par
During three or four such attempts it moved away, after which it\par
appeared to recognize my kind intention and stood perfectly still while\par
I pressed on the leg. It may be unnecessary to add that I failed in\par
performing a successful surgical operation.\par
\par
As the Winter approached the flies began to lose their legs and wings;\par
those that lost their wings would walk along the wall until they came to\par
the usual waiting spot, and as soon as I put a finger against the wall\par
the maimed creature would crawl to the usual place on my hand for\par
breakfast. Indeed, the long years of solitude had produced in me such an\par
unutterable longing for the companionship of something which had life\par
that I never destroyed any kind of insect which found its way into my\par
cell--even when mosquitoes lit on my face I always let them have their\par
fill undisturbed, and felt well repaid by getting a glimpse of them as\par
they flew and with the music of their buzzing.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XXXIX.\par
\par
THE DAYS O' SUMMER MERRILY SPENT IN THE LAND OF THE HEATHER.\par
\par
\par
In the cell next to mine was a prison genius named Heep, who was one of\par
the most singular characters I ever met. As I shall have occasion to\par
speak of him frequently, I may as well give here a sketch of his life as\par
related to me by himself. He was born in the town of Macclesfield, near\par
Manchester, in 1852, of respectable mechanics, or tradespeople as they\par
are called in England. His father died when Heep was about 5 years of\par
age, and after a time his mother married a carpenter and joiner of the\par
place.\par
\par
Young Heep was a lively child, up to all sorts of tricks, and does not\par
remember the time since he could walk that he was not in some mischief,\par
and, as he remarked, "took to all sorts of deviltry as naturally as a\par
duck to water." As long as his father lived there was not much check on\par
his mischievous propensities, but his stepfather proved to be a severe\par
and stern judge, and brought him to book for every irregularity,\par
thrashing him most unmercifully for each offense. His mother could not\par
have filled her maternal duty very judiciously, judging from the fact\par
that before he was 12 years old she set him to follow and watch his\par
stepfather to the house of a woman of whom she was jealous. The boy\par
possessed great natural abilities, and in good hands would have turned\par
out something different than a life-long prison drudge. He was handsome,\par
genteel in appearance, an apt scholar, though very self-willed and\par
headstrong, and as he grew up his naturally hot temper became\par
uncontrollable. At an early age he had discovered that by threats of\par
self-injury he could bend his parents to his wishes, but found in his\par
stepfather one who would put up with no nonsense; even when he cut\par
himself so as to bleed freely, instead of the coveted indulgence it only\par
procured him an additional thrashing.\par
\par
At 15 he had become ungovernable at home, and his father had him put in\par
the county insane asylum, where he remained a year and a half. While\par
there he caused so much trouble that the attendants were only too glad\par
when he escaped and went to Liverpool. Here he succeeded in getting a\par
situation with a dealer in bric-a-brac, rare books and antiquities. In a\par
short time the proprietor placed so much confidence in his integrity\par
that he gave him the charge of his place during his own absences, and\par
young Heep was not long in taking advantage of his position to rob his\par
employer by taking a book or other article which he sold to some one of\par
his master's customers. This went on for some time until on one occasion\par
he took the book to a shop kept by a woman to whom he had previously\par
sold several articles and offered it for a sovereign. She examined it\par
and found that it was an ancient, illuminated Greek manuscript, worth\par
fifty times more than the price young Heep asked for it, and, suspecting\par
something wrong, she told him to come again for the money the next\par
evening. At the appointed time he entered the place and was confronted\par
by his master, who contented himself with upbraiding him for his perfidy\par
and discharging him from his service.\par
\par
At this period of his career he had contracted vicious habits, the most\par
pernicious for him being that of drink, for when sober he was in his\par
right mind, but the moment the drink was in his common sense departed,\par
and he became a raving maniac, ready to fight or perpetrate any other\par
act of folly. Up to this time he had never been tempted to steal only in\par
order to supply means for improper indulgences.\par
\par
Not long after being discharged from his situation he was found by the\par
police acting in so insane a manner under the influence of drink that\par
the magistrate before whom he was taken had him sent to the Raynell\par
lunatic asylum. Here, being perfectly reckless, he carried on all sorts\par
of games which made him obnoxious, although making himself very useful\par
in work which he liked, such as gardening, etc. He also took up fancy\par
painting and soon became a skillful copyist of prints of any\par
description, enlarging or reducing, and painting them in oil or water\par
colors. He also became a good decorator and scene painter, besides\par
devoting time to various studies, including music.\par
\par
At last he found means to effect his escape and lay in hiding until\par
night; then as he had on the asylum clothes, which would betray him, he\par
went back and got in through the window of the tailors' shop, which was\par
in an isolated building, and exchanged the clothes he had on for a suit\par
belonging to one of the attendants. Thinking himself now safe from\par
recognition he started off across the country, but had not gone more\par
than twenty miles when, in passing through a small town, a policeman who\par
had just heard of the escape from Raynell arrested him on suspicion.\par
\par
The Raynell authorities sent some one to identify him; he was taken\par
back, tried on the charge of stealing the attendant's suit of clothes,\par
which he still had on, was convicted by the usual intelligent jury and\par
sentenced to five years' penal servitude.\par
\par
He finished his term of imprisonment at Chatham, and instead of being\par
set at liberty was sent under guard back to the asylum!\par
\par
According to English law, if a person confined in a lunatic asylum\par
escapes and keeps away fourteen days he cannot after that be arrested,\par
unless he commits fresh acts of insanity.\par
\par
After several futile attempts he at last made good his escape and\par
obtained work with a farmer, where he remained safe for thirteen days,\par
and was congratulating himself that in less than another day he would be\par
free, when his thoughts were broken off by the appearance of two\par
attendants who seized and carried him back to the asylum.\par
\par
The events above narrated had driven him into a state of desperation at\par
what he felt to be gross injustice, and he carried on in such a way that\par
the doctor ordered his head to be shaved and blistered as a punishment,\par
the straitjacket and all other coercive measures having been of no\par
avail. The night watchman had orders to watch him closely, but he kept\par
so sharp an eye on the watchman that he caught him asleep, and, creeping\par
to the closet window, which he had previously tampered with, crept out,\par
and after climbing the low wall found himself on a raw November night,\par
with the rain falling in torrents, a stark-naked,\par
head-shaved-and-blistered but once more a free man. In this condition he\par
wandered on throughout the night, and just before daylight he entered a\par
cemetery to find that refuge among the dead of which he thought himself\par
so cruelly deprived by the living.\par
\par
Beneath the entrance to the church there was a passage which led to some\par
family vaults in the basement, and he crept down the passage to seek\par
some shelter for his nude body from the driving rain, which had chilled\par
him through. While groping about in the dark his hand rested on\par
something soft, which, to his unbounded delight, proved to be an old\par
coat which had probably been left there by the sexton and forgotten. He\par
remained hidden all day, and traveled through the fields all night,\par
during which he found a scarecrow, from which he transferred to his own\par
person its old hat and trousers.\par
\par
He said that although so hungry, he never had felt so happy as he did at\par
finding himself once more dressed up. After proceeding a few miles\par
farther, he ventured into a laborer's cottage in quest of food, which\par
was given him, and with it a pair of old boots. As dilapidated, ragged,\par
vagabond-looking, honest people are common in England, no questions were\par
asked, and he proceeded on his way rejoicing in that freedom of which he\par
had been deprived for ten years or more.\par
\par
Amid all his pranks he had never been charged with idleness, and now\par
worked at odd jobs about the farms until he had procured a decent suit\par
of clothes, when he applied to a master house painter for work as a\par
journeyman, though he had never done anything of that kind. The master,\par
pleased with his appearance, gave him a trial, but the first job showed\par
such ignorance of the art of house painting that he was forthwith\par
discharged with half a day's wages. However, he had picked up some\par
valuable hints, and being very apt by the time he had been more or less\par
summarily discharged from half a dozen places he had become a good\par
workman, and henceforth had no trouble about retaining any situation as\par
long as he refrained from beer and restrained his temper; but at the\par
slightest fault-finding on the part of the master he would fly into a\par
passion and throw up the situation, and this, especially, if he\par
suspected that anything had leaked out about his imprisonment.\par
\par
While at work with a companion at painting the interior of a gentleman's\par
residence near Bradford a word or two was dropped which made him believe\par
his fellow workman had become aware of his being an ex-convict. Quitting\par
work, he went to a public house, passing the rest of the day in\par
carousing. About midnight, while on his way to his boarding house, it\par
occurred to him that he had noticed a good many valuable things about\par
the gentleman's house which he could obtain. No sooner thought than\par
done; the entrance was in a moment gained; he had just consciousness\par
enough left to gather a few things, then lie down by the side of them\par
and fell into a drunkard's sleep, in which the servants found him when\par
they came down in the morning. A constable was sent for, he was given in\par
charge, tried, convicted of the crime of burglary and sentenced to seven\par
years' penal servitude.\par
\par
His former term of five years had made him proficient in all the dodges\par
of prison life, and he felt justified in his own mind in using all his\par
craft in order to put in his seven years as easily as possible. As he\par
had been in Raynell asylum, he knew that by "putting on the balmy" so as\par
to be sent to the lunatic department he would not be subjected to the\par
prison rules and be as well off as he had been in the free asylum.\par
Persistent attempts at suicide by cutting himself in the arms and legs\par
with a piece of glass so as to bleed freely accomplished his purpose.\par
Being placed with the other convict lunatics, he made himself useful,\par
but on account of his bad temper and overbearing, quarrelsome\par
disposition, obnoxious to his fellow prisoners.\par
\par
Eventually he was discharged with an eighteen months' ticket-of-leave\par
and $2.50 as capital for a new departure.\par
\par
He went to Liverpool, procured a passage on board a freight steamer to\par
America, which he paid for by working at painting. Landing at New York,\par
he made his way to Norfolk, Va., where he procured work as a painter.\par
Owing to his infirmity of temper he did not keep his place long, and\par
after knocking about for a few months he took a freak to return to\par
England--the last place of all for any man who has once been a prisoner.\par
\par
[Illustration: George Bidwell\par
\par
AFTER IMPRISONMENT. (From Photo. by Stuart, Hartford.)]\par
\par
Once more in his native land, he procured work without difficulty at\par
house painting, but, as usual, remained in one place but a very short\par
time. His earnings, like those of a great majority of the working\par
class in England, were squandered in the public house.\par
\par
Soon after the events just recorded, Heep concluded to visit his old\par
home in Macclesfield. He accordingly threw up his situation, and arrived\par
at the railway station an hour before the train was due. In order to\par
while away the time he entered a public house and drank several glasses\par
of ale. The compartment which he entered happened to be empty, and as\par
usual whenever he indulged his appetite for anything containing alcohol,\par
he was soon quite out of his mind and fancied that some one on the train\par
was coming to murder him, and leaped headlong from the train, which was\par
going at the rate of forty miles an hour. This came to a standstill, he\par
was taken on board again, not seriously injured, and left at Wrexham in\par
Denbighshire, from which he was sent to the Denbigh Insane Asylum. This\par
being a Welsh institution, did not, according to Heep, possess those\par
facilities for enjoying life which were so liberally supplied to the\par
inmates of the Raynell asylum near Liverpool. Accordingly he behaved\par
himself with so much propriety that the doctor discharged him as cured.\par
\par
Not long after his return he got work near Manchester at painting in a\par
block of new houses where the plumbers were at work putting in the gas\par
and water pipes. On a Saturday, when he left work at noon, he met a\par
young plumber who was out of a job. This man said he knew where he could\par
earn a sovereign if he had tools to do a job in a butcher shop, and told\par
Heep that if he would go to the houses where he had been painting and\par
borrow a few plumbers' tools and assist him he would divide the amount.\par
Heep went back, but finding that the master plumber and all his men had\par
gone (Saturday afternoon in England being a half-holiday for laborers),\par
he took the few tools required, went and finished the job by 7 p.m.;\par
then instead of taking the tools back, they went into a public house\par
where they caroused till midnight, when they separated, Heep taking the\par
tools to his boarding house. On Monday he started early, so as to get\par
the tools back before the other workmen arrived. On nearing the houses\par
he passed a policeman who walked a little lame. He turned his head to\par
look back, and the policeman happened to do the same thing, and seeing\par
Heep looking at him his suspicions were aroused. Turning back, he came\par
up and asked him what he had in the two bosses (tool baskets). Heep\par
informed him, and on further questioning showed him the key to the house\par
from which he had taken the tools, and asked him to accompany him there,\par
which he did. They entered, Heep putting back the tools, and showed the\par
policeman where he had been painting and wished him to stay until the\par
master came in half an hour. This the policeman declined to do, and took\par
the tools and told Heep to come to the police station.\par
\par
Heep lost his temper and began cursing him. The policeman went to the\par
door, and seeing another just passing beckoned him in, and the two\par
marched him to the station. The plumber was sent for, and was induced to\par
make a charge against Heep and value the stolen goods at ten shillings.\par
Seeing that the police were bound to make a case against him, he seized\par
the plumber's knife and cut his throat, severing the windpipe. The\par
doctor was sent for, he was transferred to the jail hospital, and in the\par
course of two or three weeks was well enough to appear before the\par
magistrate, though he could not speak, and was bound over for trial.\par
\par
In the mean time the police had discovered that he had served two penal\par
terms, on the strength of which, when convicted, the magistrate\par
sentenced him to ten years' penal servitude.\par
\par
At the trial he had not yet recovered the use of his voice, nor did he\par
have any one to defend him, for at that time, unlike the present, the\par
Crown did not furnish a lawyer for the defense of those who were unable\par
to employ one at their own expense. When the magistrate was about to\par
pronounce the sentence, he said that as the prisoner had escaped from\par
ordinary asylums he should send him to a place from which he could not\par
escape--meaning a prison.\par
\par
[Illustration: BANK OF ENGLAND SCENE.--VISITOR HOLDING L1,000,000\par
($5,000,000) BANK OF ENGLAND NOTES.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XL.\par
\par
WE WILL FERRY YOU OVER JORDAN THAT ROLLS BETWEEN.\par
\par
\par
Once convicted of a crime in England it is impossible, unless a man has\par
money or friends, for him to obtain an honest livelihood unless he is\par
the happy possessor of a trade. All the great corporations demand\par
references that will cover a series of years of the applicant's life,\par
and, above all, strict inquiry is made as to his last employer. This\par
cuts the ground out from under the feet of the unfortunate, and feeling\par
that England can no longer be a home to him he turns his eyes as a\par
matter of course to America.\par
\par
A fair percentage of the prisoners are men who perhaps under great\par
temptation, or while under the influence of drink, have broken the laws,\par
but yet are honorably minded and resolved in future to lead an honest\par
life. Such are not undesirable citizens; but there is another class,\par
that of the professional criminal; with these the prisons swarm, and,\par
worse yet, the slums and saloons of the great cities are breeding\par
thousands more that will take the places of those now on the stage.\par
\par
The conditions of society in England are such that the procession of\par
criminals is an unending one. The society that creates the criminal also\par
has established a system of police repression that makes the life\par
history of society's victim one of misery, until such time when the\par
criminal, growing wise by experience, shakes the dust of English soil\par
off from his feet and transfers himself, a moral ruin, to our country,\par
here to become a curse and a burden.\par
\par
This flow of moral sewage to our shores is constant and unceasing. Our\par
Government has frequently protested against it, but with no success, for\par
the officials in England indignantly deny that the State either\par
encourages or assists the exodus of her criminal classes; but from my\par
personal knowledge I know this to be false. The officials over there\par
have found out an effectual way to rid themselves of their discharged\par
prisoners as fast as their sentences expire, and cast them on our\par
shores, and this is so ingenious a way that the wrong can never be\par
brought home to them.\par
\par
During my twenty years' residence in Chatham I suppose nearly half as\par
many thousands asked me for information about America, and at least 95\par
per cent. assured me that when released they would "join the society"\par
and depart at once for that happy hunting ground--that Promised Land\par
which charms the imagination no less of the criminal than of the honest\par
poor of the Old World. In every English prison the walls are decorated\par
with placards, gorgeous in hue, of rival firms appealing to the readers\par
for patronage. "Join us," they all say; and every prisoner knows the\par
appeal "join us" means if you do we will ferry you over the Jordan that\par
rolls between this desert land and the plains flowing with milk and\par
honey on the other side. The "firms" I mention are those arch humbugs,\par
the Prisoners' Aid Societies of England.\par
\par
Elizabeth Fry, who made "aid to prisoners" fashionable and a society fad\par
in England, has much to answer for. Prisoners' Aid Societies have sprung\par
up in every quarter of England, and having a rich soil, and under the\par
fostering care of the Government, have flourished with a rank and\par
luxuriant growth. These societies draw their nourishment from English\par
soil, but, unhappily for us, their tall branches hang over our wall and\par
their ripened fruit falls on our ground.\par
\par
From the time a prisoner becomes accustomed to his surroundings until\par
the hour of his release the one thing ever uppermost in his thoughts,\par
the one distracting subject and cause of anxious solicitude, is the\par
question, "Which society shall I join?" It is a tolerably safe venture\par
to predict that he will "join" "The Royal Prisoners' Aid Society of\par
London," which society is happy in having Her Gracious Majesty and a\par
long list of illustrious lords and ladies for "governors." What that may\par
mean no one knows. Certainly no benefit from these people ever accrues\par
to the discharged prisoners, but who can describe the glory that falls\par
on the four or five reverend gentlemen, sons, nephews or brothers of\par
deans or bishops, high-salaried secretaries of this particular society,\par
who pose at the annual meeting in Exeter Hall, before a brilliant\par
audience, and after have the felicity of seeing their report in the\par
church and society journals and their names connected with such exalted\par
people.\par
\par
The way the Government over there accomplishes its purpose of getting\par
rid of its criminal population at our expense and at the same time is\par
able to answer the charges of our Government with disavowal is this:\par
\par
The Home Secretary alone possesses the pardoning power for the United\par
Kingdom, and directly controls every prison, his fiat being law in all\par
things to every official as well as to every inmate. He has officially\par
recognized and registered at the Home Office every prisoners' aid\par
society in England, Scotland and Wales, and in order to boom them he\par
gives to every discharged prisoner an extra gratuity of L3 provided he\par
"joins" a prisoners' aid society on his discharge, the result being that\par
all do so. England is a small and compact country, and the police have\par
practically one head, and that head is the Home Secretary. Under the\par
circumstances the system of police espionage is so perfect that whenever\par
a discharged prisoner is reconvicted for another crime he cannot escape\par
recognition, and in all such cases the Home Secretary notifies the\par
particular aid society who received the prisoner on his discharge of the\par
fact, very much to the vexation of the officials of the society, who are\par
all anxious for a good record in reforming men that come officially\par
under their auspices. They publish that all who are never reported as\par
reconvicted are reformed, and all love to make a big showing for the\par
money subscribed at the all-important annual meeting, the result being\par
that all the men hustled out of the country by the society count as\par
reformed men.\par
\par
These societies are supported by subscriptions, which all go in salaries\par
and office rents. The assistance given to the discharged prisoner is\par
limited to the L3 extra gratuity given the society by the Government on\par
the prisoner's behalf. The London societies have an agreement with the\par
Netherlands Line and the Wilson Line of steamers to "take to sea" for L2\par
10s. all "workingmen" they send to them. I have talked to thousands of\par
men who "joined the society," most of whom intended to go to America,\par
and I have talked to scores who had "joined," but who, unluckily for\par
themselves, not leaving England, were reconvicted and sent back to\par
Chatham. Throughout twenty years I conversed with several thousand men\par
who joined the society avowing they were going to America, and were\par
never heard of again in England, and have also known some scores of men\par
who passed through the hands of the society agents, yet were afterward\par
reconvicted. Therefore I am in a position to speak with authority on the\par
important question of England dumping her criminal population on our\par
shores.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLI.\par
\par
"WELL MY MAN, WHAT DO YOU INTEND TO DO?" "I WANT TO GO TO AMERICA, SIR."\par
"TUT! TUT! YOU MEAN YOU WANT TO GO TO SEA!" "YES, SIR; I WANT TO GO TO\par
SEA."\par
\par
\par
The Royal Society and The Christian Aid Societies, presided over by a\par
Rev. Mr. Whitely, enjoy a bad pre-eminence in this respect. The year\par
before my release the latter stated at the annual meeting that six\par
thousand discharged prisoners had passed through his society, and I\par
venture to assert that five thousand of these found their way to this\par
country through the assistance of this society. These two societies have\par
been boomed to an incredible extent, and it would be a curious study if\par
any report could be had as to how the large subscriptions were actually\par
expended.\par
\par
For the sake of making my narrative clear, I will here only speak of the\par
first-named society.\par
\par
[Illustration: LEAVING LIVERPOOL.--GEORGE BIDWELL'S FAREWELL TO JOHN\par
BULL.]\par
\par
Two months before release the prisoner must inform the warder that he\par
intends to join the Royal Society. He notifies the Home Office, which in\par
turn notifies the society and forwards a warrant for L3. The prisoner\par
upon discharge takes a certain train for London, and is met upon his\par
arrival at the station by an agent of the society. This agent ranks as a\par
servant, is usually an ex-prisoner and is always paid 21 shillings a\par
week. He pilots his man at a certain hour before the Reverend Secretary,\par
and here follows a verbatim report of the dialogue between the great\par
man and the poor, timid and dreadfully embarrassed ex-prisoner:\par
\par
Great Man--Well, my man, what do you intend to do?\par
\par
Ex-Prisoner--I want to go to America.\par
\par
Great Man--Tut! tut! my man; you mean you want to go to sea.\par
\par
Ex-Prisoner (taking the hint)--Yes, sir; I want to go to sea.\par
\par
Great Man--Very well, my man. Go with this agent, who will fix it with\par
the ship captain so you can go to sea.\par
\par
If a steamer of either line named is about to sail he is taken on board\par
at once goes to the steerage, and just before sailing the agent hands\par
him a ticket and the criminal is safely off for America. England is rid\par
of a bad subject, and the Royal Society has one more "reformed" man to\par
put in its report. In addition to the L3 gratuity the ex-prisoner has\par
been paid L1, L2 or L3 in addition, provided his sentence had been at\par
least five years. The society is not a cent out of pocket over him, and\par
forlorn and friendless he lands here with from $2 to $15 in his pocket.\par
He has got the cheap suit of clothes he wears, one handkerchief and one\par
pair of stockings extra. It is almost certain he will speedily drift\par
into crime, spending the remainder of his life in prison, and finally\par
dying there or in the poorhouse.\par
\par
There is just one way this evil can be stopped--I might say two ways.\par
The first, and a method that would be effectual in stopping the influx\par
of criminals from all countries, is to let Congress put a tax of $30 or\par
$50 on the steamship companies for every passenger not an American\par
citizen whom they bring to America. Not one discharged criminal in a\par
thousand could meet the tax in addition to the fare. The only other way\par
possible would be for our Government to request the English Government\par
to furnish them with photographs, marks and measurements of all\par
discharged criminals. Then have them copied and sent to the\par
Immigration Commissioners of our ports. But that would involve a radical\par
change in these boards and their methods. Efficiency there under our\par
corrupt system is, I fear, hopeless.\par
\par
I visited Ellis Island a few days ago and saw how they passed a shipload\par
of immigrants in a few minutes, and as I looked I felt it was hopeless\par
to expect any efficient measures to throw back the foul tide that is\par
polluting our shores.\par
\par
Seldom as men of the criminal class once safe in America ever return to\par
England, yet they do now and then return. In the two or three cases that\par
came under my observation it was very much to their loss and grief, for\par
they only came back to undergo another term.\par
\par
One day, in 1890, a man working in my party slipped a note into my hand\par
that had been given him for me in chapel that morning. As in similar\par
cases, I secreted the note, and when safe in my little room I read it.\par
The writer said he had lately come down from London, and was most\par
anxious to get into my party in order to have a chance to talk with me.\par
He said he had been living in Chicago and could give me all the news. He\par
ended the note by stating he was being murdered by hard work, and\par
implored me to try and get him into my party, where it was not so hard.\par
This I was most anxious to do, as in my party you could talk almost with\par
impunity. To have a man near me fresh and only a year before in Chicago\par
would be like a letter from home and also a newspaper. Therefore, I\par
determined to get Foster in my party if possible. At this time I had\par
been seventeen years a resident, and was, in fact, the oldest\par
inhabitant, and had some little influence in a quiet way. About eleven\par
years before I had been put in the party, and had a chance to learn\par
bricklaying, and having become an expert in the art was given charge of\par
the bricklaying. I was on the best of terms with our officer, so when, a\par
day or two later, one of our men was so fortunate (in the Chatham view\par
of it) as to meet with an accident and be admitted to that heaven, the\par
infirmary, I told my officer to ask for Foster to replace him. He did\par
so, and he, very much to his gratification, found himself by my side,\par
with a trowel instead of a shovel in his hand. We worked side by side,\par
Winter and Summer, storm and shine, for two years, and in spite of\par
myself I began soon to like the man. His chief and only virtues were\par
truthfulness and fair-mindedness toward his friends--rare and\par
incongruous virtues for a professional burglar; nevertheless, he\par
possessed them in a marked degree. This is a statement to make a cynic\par
smile, and is one of those cases where the result is justifiable; yet,\par
however the cynic may smile, there is plenty of all-around good faith in\par
the world, and there is no nation, race or color, no clique, religion\par
nor social strata, that has a monopoly of the article. Good faith and\par
truth grow in unlikely places, as I have found in my career, for I have\par
looked on life from both sides, and to look on it from the seamy side is\par
instructive, indeed, for then the mask is off and the true character is\par
revealed. I have been away down in the depths, and for years have toiled\par
cheek by jowl, through sunshine and storm, in blinding snows and pelting\par
rain, with my brother men under conditions too brutal and demoralizing\par
to be understood if described--conditions where the very worst side of\par
human character would naturally be thought to come to the front, and I\par
came out of the fierce struggle in that pit of death with conclusions as\par
to the human animal that are decidedly favorable, and I am inclined to\par
the view that man was born almost an angel, and that, in spite of the\par
fearful temptations of the world into which he has been thrust, much of\par
the angelic pottery abides.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLII.\par
\par
MANY A MAN MORE DANGEROUS WRITES ALDERMAN AFTER HIS NAME.\par
\par
\par
Foster's experience during his four years' residence in Chicago was\par
decidedly novel, and it had evidently brightened his wits--that is,\par
increased his cunning without adding to his honesty. And as I think it\par
will interest my reader to get a view of life from the actor's own\par
standpoint, I will relate one of the many stories he told me during the\par
years we worked together.\par
\par
Upon Foster's release from his first term of imprisonment he joined the\par
Christian Aid Society of London, and Mr. Whitely, the secretary,\par
promptly "sent him to sea," as he has thousands of others. In due time\par
he arrived in New York, but as he had heard much of Chicago he\par
determined to go there. He arrived penniless, but within an hour ran\par
against an old friend in the person of a former partner in the art of\par
burglary who had been a fellow prisoner with him in London. This man's\par
name was Turtle, and Mr. Whitely had only "sent him to sea" two brief\par
years before. It was plain from his magnificent diamond ring, pin and\par
big bank roll, freely displayed, that the seafaring life of the former\par
protege of the London Prison Aid Society was a profitable occupation. He\par
was delighted to meet Foster, and took him to a tailor's at once and\par
fitted him out liberally, at the same time handing him $250, just for\par
pocket money. When, on the next day, Foster stated to his friend that\par
he was ready to undertake a burglary, Turtle was displeased, and said:\par
"No; we are on the honest game, which pays better." What that was will\par
appear. Turtle had a large private inquiry office, with two of the city\par
detectives for side partners, who turned over to him all business in\par
which there was a prospect of mutual profit. All imaginable schemes of\par
villainy were concocted and executed there, and with perfect impunity,\par
too. For Turtle had the ear of all the magistrates, and was in with all\par
the gangs that made the City Hall of Chicago the worst and vilest den of\par
robbers that encumbers this earth.\par
\par
What cause the pessimist has for his boding views when in cities like\par
New York, Quaker Philadelphia, Chicago and San Francisco, the City\par
Halls, those centres of municipal life, hold and are ruled by the worst\par
and most dangerous gangs of criminals sheltered by any roof in any city!\par
\par
Alas! that the centre which should be the purest stream within the city\par
should be a foul cesspool, sending out poisonous vapors to pollute the\par
life of the citizens!\par
\par
Universal suffrage in our great centres is a corrupt tree and its fruits\par
must needs be poisonous.\par
\par
Turtle gave his friend Foster a welcome at his office and at once\par
enrolled him on his staff, but virtually made him a member of the firm.\par
So, between the two Police Headquarters thieves and the two English\par
ones, they had a combination indeed.\par
\par
Many stories Foster told me during the years of our intercourse that\par
were novel and strange, and gave me a view of the social world seldom\par
seen. Here is a specimen:\par
\par
One day a countryman appeared at Police Headquarters in Chicago and\par
announced that he had been robbed of $20,000, and showed how his coat\par
pocket had been cut open and the money taken. This, he explained, had\par
been done in a crowd. It was a strange place for a man to carry so\par
large a sum, and, still stranger, the pocket was cut on the inside. Of\par
course, a pickpocket in the rare event of cutting the pocket of an\par
intended victim must of necessity cut the pocket from the outside. The\par
countryman had fallen at Headquarters to the tender mercies of the two\par
partners of Turtle. One glance at the pocket showed them there was a\par
colored gentleman in the woodpile, and as there was $20,000 in the deal\par
somewhere, they determined to have some share of it. They, of course,\par
pretended to believe the story of the countryman, but for fear some of\par
the other Headquarters men might hear and want a share, they hurried him\par
away from the office over to the Sherman House; then one went to\par
Turtle's office and posted him on the situation. The countryman was\par
anxious to leave town, but on various pretenses they held him for two\par
days, but as he stoutly affirmed that the lost money was his own they\par
were puzzled to solve the mystery; but their knowledge of human nature\par
was such that they felt certain that if they could only arrive at the\par
bottom the old gentleman would not be quite as white as he pretended to\par
be. He came from an obscure mountain town in East Tennessee, and while\par
they fancied a trip there might solve matters they feared to lose their\par
victim--for victim these human tigers determined the countryman should\par
be. The second day they resolved on decisive measures to get at the\par
truth, and at the same time secure some plunder, provided the\par
Tennesseean had any cash.\par
\par
So far Turtle and Foster had not been seen by the victim. The detectives\par
asked the countryman to remain one more night to see if they could not\par
catch the men who had robbed him. That afternoon one of Turtle's staff\par
secured a room at the same hotel, and, seizing an opportunity, slipped\par
into the countryman's chamber and concealed some burglar tools under the\par
mattress of his bed and in his carpet bag. This once done, they marched\par
the "guy" along Clark street, and, as arranged, Turtle and one of his\par
staff met them, and shaking hands with the two detectives asked if they\par
were arresting their companion for a job. Upon their saying he was a\par
wealthy gentleman from the South, Turtle burst out laughing, and said he\par
knew him for an old-time burglar, and if they would search his house\par
they would find stolen goods, and ended by saying, "Bring him down to my\par
office and I will show you his picture." The detectives now changed\par
their tones and threatened to arrest him. He having, as the sequel will\par
show, a bad conscience, became frightened. Then they arrested him, and\par
announced that they were going to search his room at the hotel. This\par
they did, taking him along. Of course, they found what they had\par
previously hidden, very much to the terror of the countryman, who,\par
lashed by a bad conscience, began to think he was in a fix. The friends\par
of the hour before now became threatening bullies, promising to get him\par
ten years for the possession of burglar tools. They took him to Turtle's\par
office, and there stripping him they found to their disappointment that\par
he had no money, but found carefully folded up in an inner pocket a\par
postoffice receipt for a registered letter sent from Nashville to St.\par
Paul. They kept him a prisoner that night while Turtle left by the first\par
train for St. Paul with the receipt in his pocket. The next morning\par
found him in St. Paul, and a few minutes later he walked out of the\par
office with the registered letter, which proved to be a bulky one.\par
Tearing it open he found it full of United States bonds and greenbacks,\par
amounting in all to $20,000. The next day all save $1,000, reserved for\par
the victim, was divided among the four birds of prey. That day the\par
victim was taken before a friendly magistrate and fully committed to\par
await in jail the action of the Grand Jury. Twenty-four hours later a\par
tool called on him at the jail, and gave him the option of taking $1,000\par
and getting out of town by the first train or getting ten years for the\par
possession of burglar tools. The poor fool, with trembling eagerness,\par
accepted the first part of the ultimatum, and within an hour a bail\par
bond was filled up, and darkness found the baffled old man speeding\par
westward, never again to look on his own people.\par
\par
But how was he a baffled old man? He had embarked in a scheme of\par
villainy, but had been beaten at his own game by sharper rascals. From\par
whom did he steal the money? Read:\par
\par
In a small Tennessee town there lived a widow whose husband had been\par
killed in the Confederate army and who found herself, like so many more\par
Southern ladies at the close of the war, impoverished, and with a family\par
of children to be provided with bread. But it seems she was a brave\par
body, and with a head for business. She opened a small hotel in\par
Nashville, and by reason of her history, no less than her excellent\par
hostelry, she thrived apace, and, investing all her savings in newly\par
started industrial enterprises in Nashville, her small investments\par
brought in large returns, which were reinvested, until at 40, finding\par
herself mistress of a competency, she quit business and went to spend\par
the remainder of her days where she was born. The hero of the adventure\par
in Chicago was not only her neighbor, but had been the comrade of her\par
husband through the deadly fights of the war. She naturally turned to\par
him as a friend for advice. He first asked her to be his wife, and upon\par
her refusal he began to urge her to dispose of all her interests in\par
Nashville and reinvest her money in the nearby city of Knoxville. At\par
last she consented, and sent him to Nashville with authority to act as\par
her agent. He disposed of her property, except the old hotel. He was\par
paid $20,000 on her account, and once with the money in his possession\par
he determined to keep it. It was a cowardly deed, and dearly did he pay\par
for it. He wrote her he was going to Chicago, and would take the money\par
with him, as he would only remain for a day. To Chicago he came, and, as\par
related, robbed himself, sending off the money in a registered letter to\par
himself. Then he appeared at Police Headquarters with his cut pocket\par
and clumsy story, which appeared in the next morning's paper. He sent a\par
marked copy of the paper to the lady, and at the same time wrote a\par
hypocritical letter stating that he was so heartbroken over losing her\par
money that he did not have the courage to look her in the face, and\par
never should until such time as he could repay the money. He said he was\par
going to California to work, and when he had enough she would see him\par
again, but not before.\par
\par
[Illustration: "I RESOLVED TO LEAVE A MESSAGE OF HOPE AND HIGH\par
RESOLVE."]\par
\par
How easy it is for a man to become an unspeakable villain, and how\par
nicely this one was hoisted with his own petard!\par
\par
Eventually this catastrophe proved a blessing to the widow. It drove her\par
back to her hotel again, and soon after she became the wife of one of\par
the bravest and best men Tennessee ever produced. I was so interested in\par
the fate of this lady that when in Nashville in 1893 I tried to hunt her\par
up. I found several who knew the whole story, and from them I heard her\par
after history and a full confirmation of Foster's narrative.\par
\par
Foster remained four years in Chicago and flourished. He and Turtle\par
became very influential in politics and partners in a combine of\par
rascally Aldermen and police magistrates that robbed the city and the\par
citizens with impunity. But unluckily for him, he one day took it into\par
his head to pay a visit to his old haunts in England, there to display\par
his diamonds and bank roll to such of his former cronies as happened to\par
be at liberty. On arriving in London he began to play the role of a rich\par
American, but was recognized by the police, an old charge raked up\par
against him, arrested, promptly placed on trial, found guilty and\par
sentenced to ten years' imprisonment. Although the possessor of\par
considerable property, he is to-day toiling at Chatham like a slave and\par
probably if he lives he will come out a broken man. It is a certainty\par
that the very day he is liberated he will "go to sea," being sent by a\par
prisoners' aid society, and a few days later become an ornament to that\par
good city of Chicago. Once there, his ambition will not be satisfied\par
until he takes his seat as Alderman, becoming one of the City Fathers.\par
Many more immoral and dangerous than he write Alderman after their names\par
in that windy city.\par
\par
[Illustration: BIDWELL PICKING OAKUM.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLIII.\par
\par
A BATTERED HULK STRANDED ON A SHORE TO WHICH NO TIDE RETURNS.\par
\par
\par
I am glad to say that during the almost lifetime I passed at Chatham\par
there were only a scant half dozen Americans who came down to keep me\par
company. One, Stoneman by name, interested me. He was a man of great\par
nerve and quick apprehension, and very truthful, therefore I found his\par
stories of his adventures most interesting, besides the fact that his\par
history was another proof of the truth that wrongdoing never pays.\par
Stoneman was of good parentage, and had entered the army in 1861, making\par
a good record up to and including the battle of Gettysburg. There, owing\par
to a quarrel with his captain, he deserted, and became a bounty jumper,\par
making a large amount of money, but when the war ended, finding his\par
occupation gone, he entered upon a life of crime, starting out first as\par
a very successful express robber. The last robbery he engaged in in that\par
line was on the New Haven road near Norwalk. His share amounted to some\par
thousands, but he was literally bowled out, and by a singular\par
circumstance. One of his confederates by the name of Riley had been\par
arrested, and was confined at Norwalk. He engaged as counsel for his\par
chum a well-known criminal lawyer of New York by the name of Stuart, and\par
arranged with him to go up to Norwalk to see Riley the following day.\par
Although Stoneman had plenty of money, he told Stuart he had none, but\par
Riley had. Then he gave Riley's wife $2,500, and told her to be present\par
at the interview between the lawyer and her husband. At the interview\par
Riley told him he would give him $2,500 if he cleared him or $1,000 if\par
he got him off with a sentence of two years or less. Stuart was hungry\par
as a shark to finger the money, and writing out a receipt for the full\par
amount inserted the conditions agreed upon. Putting the money in his\par
pocket he started back to New York with Mrs. Riley. Stoneman was on the\par
train waiting for them, and as soon as they started he joined them. It\par
happened the train was crowded, and they had to stand. It seems some\par
pickpocket saw Stuart pull out the money, and determined to get it from\par
him. On the arrival of the train in New York he succeeded in doing so.\par
Stoneman had hurried out of the station, and, of course, knew nothing of\par
the loss. So soon as Stuart discovered his loss he blamed him for it,\par
and, being in a fury, he flew to Police Headquarters, secured the\par
services of a friendly detective, and, going to the hotel that he knew\par
Stoneman frequented, had him arrested on a charge of robbing him. The\par
end of it all was that Stuart and the detectives got all his money, and\par
then, knowing him to be a daring man, one that would neither forget nor\par
fear to avenge his wrong, to get him out of the way they betrayed him to\par
the Connecticut police as one of the express robbers. He was sent to\par
Norwalk to stand his trial, was convicted and sentenced to five years,\par
and sent to Weathersfield. Being a good mechanic, he was put in the\par
blacksmith shop, and there, with an eye to the future, he did what is\par
frequently done by professional gentlemen in our prisons, made a\par
complete and most finely tempered set of burglar tools. They were too\par
bulky to be smuggled out by friendly warders, so he secreted them in the\par
shop where he worked and ruled. Many of the prisoners in Weathersfield\par
are expert workmen, and from the machine shops there a high class of\par
work is turned out. Among other workshops, there is one for the\par
manufacture of silver-plated ware. Stoneman had made chums with one of\par
the prisoners who held a confidential position in the silverware\par
manufactory. As Stoneman's sentence was the first to expire, he gave him\par
points, and it was plotted between them that the prison itself should be\par
burglarized by Stoneman on a certain night after his release. The\par
confidential man was to leave the way clear to the safe where the silver\par
bars used in the business were stored. He in due time was liberated,\par
with the customary injunctions from the warden and officers "not to come\par
back any more." He did come back, but in a way entirely unanticipated by\par
them.\par
\par
He, of course, knew the whole routine of the place, the stations of the\par
guards, and that the wall after 8 p.m. was left entirely unguarded. The\par
second night after his liberation found him beneath the wall with no\par
other implements than a light ladder of the right height. In a minute he\par
was on top, had pulled his ladder up and lowered it inside.\par
\par
Once inside, every inch of the place was familiar to him, and he had a\par
clear field. The shops, although inside of the boundary walls, were\par
quite separate from the main building, where the men, closely guarded,\par
were confined. He entered the familiar room where he so long had worked,\par
and easily placed his hands on his (to him) precious kit of tools, and\par
carried his jimmies, wedges, sledges, bits, braces, drills, etc., to the\par
wall, and then landed them safe outside. Then he returned and entered\par
the room where the plunder he sought lay. Thanks to his friend, the way\par
was easy, and his art was not required to secure it. There were 600\par
ounces in silver bars, a pretty good load in avoirdupois, but he only\par
made one journey of it, mounted the wall and speedily was over.\par
\par
Stoneman was a long-headed fellow. He had taken, without the owner's\par
leave, one of the many boats on the banks of the near-by river. He\par
carried his plunder and tools down to the boat, and pulled across the\par
river, two miles down, to where quite a stream empties into the\par
Connecticut. He pulled some distance up it; then putting everything into\par
bags he sank them in the creek. Then drifting back into the Connecticut\par
River again he threw his ladder over and turned the boat adrift. At 7\par
o'clock the next morning he was in New York.\par
\par
In due time, in the idiom of the professionals, he "raised his plant,"\par
and the burglar's kit manufactured in the Connecticut State Prison did\par
what Stoneman considered yeoman service. With all his art and cunning,\par
justice would not be cajoled by him, but weighed him in her balance, to\par
a good purpose too. His success in his particular line was great, but he\par
paid dearly for it all. Many times he escaped detection, but not always.\par
Not to escape, but to be brought to the bar, means a fearful gap in the\par
life of a criminal. He was, as I say, famous in certain circles for his\par
success in his lawless course, yet in the twenty years between 1865 and\par
1886 he passed sixteen years in captivity. In that year he went to\par
England with a confederate, and a few hours later in London they\par
snatched a parcel of money from a bank messenger in Lombard street. Both\par
were caught in the act, and sentenced at the Old Bailey to twenty years\par
each. To-day Stoneman is toiling under brutal task-masters, and it is\par
all but certain he will perish at his task, friendless, alone, unpitied.\par
Better so even, for should he ever be freed it will not be until the\par
twentieth century is well on its way to the have beens of time, then\par
only to find himself a battered hulk stranded on a shore from which the\par
tide has ebbed forever.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLIV.\par
\par
I FIND THE FENIANS WITH ME IN THE TOILS.\par
\par
\par
I had, of course, for many years heard much of the Fenian prisoners in\par
the English prisons, particularly Sergeant McCarty and William O'Brien.\par
Soon after my arrival at Chatham I was placed in the same party with\par
them. We were all three strongly drawn together, but were shy of being\par
the first to speak. Of course, it was strictly against the rules to\par
talk, but as a matter of fact the prisoners find many opportunities for\par
talking, particularly if they do their work. The officers are reported\par
and fined if their men fall behind in their task, so if a man is any way\par
backward in working the officer keeps his weather eye open, and reports\par
him for any infraction of the rules.\par
\par
One day, soon after they were put in my party, I gave O'Brien a hand in\par
fixing his run. We spoke a few words. The ice was broken; we soon became\par
fast friends, and our friendship remained unbroken until their happy\par
release some years after. They were fine, manly fellows, and I in time\par
came to have a warm affection for them.\par
\par
McCarty had for nearly twenty years been a sergeant in the English army.\par
He had come out of the Indian mutiny with a splendid record, and had\par
been recommended for a commission. But while wearing the British\par
uniform, his heart was warm for Ireland and her cause, so when, in 1867,\par
his battery being then stationed in Dublin, he was informed many devoted\par
adherents to the Fenian cause had determined to try and seize Dublin,\par
with a view of starting a wide revolt against English domination,\par
perilous as it was, he cast his lot in with them, and speedily found\par
sufficient adherents in his own field battery to seize it and bring it\par
into action against the English. The plan miscarried. Sergeant McCarty,\par
along with many others, was arrested and tried for treason; as a matter\par
of course was speedily convicted, and sentenced to be hanged, drawn and\par
quartered. This sentence was commuted to penal servitude for life.\par
\par
O'Brien was an enthusiastic youngster of 17, and an ardent patriot. He\par
had enlisted in a regiment then stationed in Ireland for no other reason\par
than to familiarize himself in military affairs, also to win over\par
recruits to the Fenian cause, and when the revolt began to be in a\par
position to seize arms. The result of it all, so far as my two friends\par
were concerned--they found themselves by my side in the great Chatham\par
ship basin loading trucks with mud and clay, and that upon a diet of\par
black bread and potatoes. The cars, or trucks, held four tons, there\par
were three men to a truck, and the task was nineteen trucks a day, and\par
between the urging of officers, frightened themselves for fear the task\par
might not be done, and the mud and starvation, it was despairing work.\par
\par
The punishments were not only severe, but were dealt out with a liberal\par
hand. The men, as a rule, were willing to work, but between weakness,\par
brought on by perpetual hunger, and the misery of the incessant bullying\par
of the officers, some few suicided every year, but many more did worse\par
to themselves; that is, the poor fellows, seeing nothing but misery\par
before them, would when the trucks were being shifted on the rail\par
deliberately thrust an arm or leg under the wheels and have it taken\par
off. No less than twenty-two did this in 1874. Of course, the object was\par
to get out of the mud. When once a man's leg or arm was off he would no\par
longer be able to handle a shovel, and would necessarily be placed in\par
an inside or cripples party and set to work picking oakum or breaking\par
stones, with the result that, being free from severe toil and sheltered\par
from the storms, they would not be so hungry. Then, again, they could\par
more easily escape being reported, and that meant much.\par
\par
[Illustration: CONDEMNED TO BE HANGED.]\par
\par
[Illustration: WEIGHING OFFICE, BANK OF ENGLAND.]\par
\par
There was never anything but black bread for breakfast and supper, save\par
only one pint of gruel with the bread for breakfast. For dinner every\par
day we got a pound of boiled potatoes and five ounces of black bread;\par
three days a week five ounces of meat--that is, fifteen ounces a week\par
for a man toiling hard in the keen sea air. We were always on the verge\par
of starvation; our sufferings were terrible. In our hunger there was no\par
vile refuse we would not devour greedily if opportunity occurred.\par
\par
O'Brien was a slight, delicate fellow, quite unfitted for the hardships\par
and toil he was subjected to, but he was a high-spirited, brave\par
youngster, and his spirit carried him through, while many a man better\par
fitted physically to endure the toil gave in and died, or became utterly\par
broken down, and would be sent away to an invalid station a physical\par
wreck. McCarty and I used to do extra work so as to shield O'Brien, and\par
so long as our trucks were filled on time the officer made no complaint.\par
The prisoners were certainly very good to each other, and usually did\par
all in their power to help and cheer up the weaker men.\par
\par
In 1877 my two friends were liberated. I was glad to see them go, but I\par
missed them sadly. But McCarty had suffered too much. He only survived\par
his liberation a few days, dying in Dublin, to the grief of all Ireland.\par
O'Brien started a tobacco store in Dublin, where he still is.\par
\par
I knew all of the dynamiters--Curtin, Daily, Dr. Gallagher, Eagan, etc.\par
However misguided, yet they meant to serve their country, and dearly\par
have they paid for their zeal. I pitied poor Gallagher. The strain on\par
his spirit was too great. He soon broke down, and his dejected, forlorn\par
looks, his stooping shoulders and listless walk made me and all think\par
his days were numbered; but he had immense vitality and still lived when\par
I was liberated; but he was truly a pitiable object, and if he is ever\par
to live to breathe the air a free man then his friends must secure a\par
speedy release, for he is slowly sinking into his grave.\par
\par
[Illustration: RETROSPECTIONS.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLV.\par
\par
IN MOOD AS LONELY, IN PLIGHT AS DESPERATE AS HIS.\par
\par
\par
I have related how, the Sunday after my sentence, in my despair I took\par
the little Bible off the shelf. The other books I had at Chatham besides\par
the Bible were a dictionary and "The Life of the Prophet Jeremiah."\par
Once, soon after my arrival in Chatham, I took the Jeremiah down from\par
the shelf, but speedily put it back and made a vow never to take it down\par
again; and I never did. It remained in view on the little shelf for\par
nineteen years, while I sat there watching it rot away. The dictionary\par
is a good book, but grows tiresome at times. As for the Bible, there is\par
no discount on that. For fourteen years I was a careful student of its\par
sacred pages. Every Sunday of that fourteen years, from 12 o'clock until\par
2, I used to walk the stone floor of my cell preaching a sermon with no\par
audience but my dictionary and "The Life of the Prophet Jeremiah." I at\par
first began my Bible studies and my sermons as a means to occupy my\par
thoughts and keep my mind bright. It saved my life and reason. I need\par
hardly say that I became tolerably familiar with the book, and I had the\par
great advantage of studying the Bible without a commentary.\par
\par
I thought in my enthusiasm I should never tire of the Bible, but after\par
ten or twelve years I began to grow weary of it, and grew very hungry\par
for other mental food. I wanted a Shakespeare, for with him to keep me\par
company I could no longer be in the desolation of solitude. At last I\par
determined to get my friends to try for me. I had learned the Bible\par
almost by heart; the smallest incidents in the life of the Prophet\par
Jeremiah were much more familiar to me than the history of the civil\par
war, and Anathoth took on proportions which made it as real as New York\par
and far more important. The desperate efforts I had made to keep myself\par
from falling into the condition of so many I had seen drooping to idiocy\par
and death were, I felt, successful, and any occupation which kept alive\par
the intellect could not but be beneficial. I was hungry, starving for\par
mental food. Never had books appeared so attractive, never was kingdom\par
so cheerfully offered for a horse as I would have offered mine for an\par
octavo. My friends had written for me to the Government, but with no\par
success. At last they had interested the American Minister in London,\par
who promised to write to the Home Secretary for me, but a year had\par
slipped by and I had heard nothing.\par
\par
Jeremiah continued with me, and it seemed he was to remain with me to\par
the end. But a change was coming.\par
\par
Can I ever forget the day it happened! Can I ever cease to remember the\par
delight, the incredulity, the astonishment of that happy day! I had come\par
in at night hungry, cold, wet and miserable. I made my way a little\par
depressed to my cell. As I was about to step across the threshold I saw\par
a book lying on my little wooden bed. Amazed and astonished, I hesitated\par
to enter. Small as such a circumstance appears, the very sight of the\par
book brought on a weakness. I feared to pick it up, a horrible dread\par
seized me that it might be a new Bible, and I was unwilling to risk\par
another disappointment. The footprint on the sand was not more\par
suggestive nor more awe-inspiring to Robinson Crusoe than the appearance\par
of that book was to me. In mood as lonely, in plight as desperate as\par
his, there lay before me a sight as unlooked for and, as it seemed, as\par
full of meaning as the footprint was to Robinson.\par
\par
At last I pulled myself together, determined to end the suspense and\par
know what was before me. I picked up the book, and who can understand\par
the delight, the joy, the rapture even, with which I read on the title\par
page, "The Works of William Shakespeare." In an instant I became a new\par
man. If ever one human being felt gratitude to another I felt it at that\par
moment for the American Minister. To him I owed it that henceforth a new\par
light was to stream through the fluted glass of my window, that\par
henceforth a new world was opened up for me to live in, and the world\par
seemed lighter to me. Many a month and year afterward my cell was filled\par
and my heart cheered by the multitude of friends the divine William\par
provided for me.\par
\par
About the time I received my Shakespeare another piece of happy fortune\par
befell me. A smallpox scare was existing outside, and all hands in the\par
prison were ordered to be vaccinated. When the doctor came around a few\par
days afterward to examine the effects of the operation he found my arm\par
so swollen that he directed me to be taken to the hospital.\par
\par
For twenty-five days I had full opportunity to learn what the girl in\par
Dickens' "Little Dorritt" meant when she called the hospital an\par
"'eavenly" place. It was the first time I had ever been admitted, and\par
the change from the horrible mud hole to the rest and comfort of a cell\par
in the hospital was indeed almost "'eavenly." With nothing to do but to\par
read my Shakespeare, the cravings of hunger for the first time since my\par
imprisonment satisfied, I was tempted to believe--I did partly\par
believe--that the world had few positions pleasanter than mine.\par
\par
Godliness with contentment is undoubtedly great gain. Contentment alone\par
without the godliness is no poor thing, and was I not content? Few,\par
indeed, of all the thousands who have toiled in that torturing prison\par
house have ever been or are likely ever to be so content as I was.\par
\par
How true it is that happiness is altogether relative, and that it is\par
divided much more evenly among men than we are willing to believe! A\par
mere respite from an intolerable position, a single book to keep the\par
mind from cracking, transformed gloom and misery into light and at least\par
comparative happiness.\par
\par
After a time I began to watch the effects of the unnatural life upon\par
others. They arrived full of resolution, buoyed often by hopes which\par
they were soon destined to find delusive. The short-time men, those with\par
seven or ten year sentences, could face the prospect hopefully. To them\par
the day would come when the prison gate must swing back and the path to\par
the world be open once more. But no such hope cheers the long-timers,\par
the men with twenty years and life, who quickly learn how great the\par
proportion is of their number who find relief only in the box smeared\par
with black which incloses what is left of them in the grave. Every day I\par
used to see the effects on them of hunger and torment of mind. The first\par
part visibly affected was the neck. The flesh shrinks, disappears and\par
leaves what look like two artificial props to support the head. As time\par
wears on the erect posture grows bent; instead of standing up straight\par
the knees bulge outward as though unable to support the body's weight,\par
and the man drags himself along in a kind of despondent shuffle. Another\par
year or two and his shoulders are bent forward. He carries his arms\par
habitually before him now, he has grown moody, seldom speaks to any one,\par
nor answers if spoken to. In the general deterioration of the body the\par
mind keeps equal step; and so unfailing is the effect that even warders\par
wait to see it, and remark to each other that so and so is "going off."\par
When the sufferer begins to carry his arms in front every one\par
understands that the end is coming. The projecting head, the sunken eye,\par
the fixed, expressionless features are merely the outward exponents of\par
the hopeless, sullen brooding within. Sometimes the man merely keeps on\par
in that way, wasting more and more, body and mind, every day, until at\par
last he drops and is carried into the infirmary to come out no more.\par
\par
Truly I was looking on life from the seamy side.\par
\par
Before my own experience had taught me I used to think at times when\par
such a subject ever came into my mind at all: "What must be the thoughts\par
and anticipations of a man condemned to separation from other men, to\par
lead an unnatural life under the strained and artificial conditions of\par
prison?" The change is so violent, it comes so suddenly, the unknown\par
possibilities are so terrible, the sufferings naturally implied are so\par
inevitable, that had any one gifted with a knowledge of futurity shown\par
me that such experience was to be mine I would have thought it utterly\par
impossible that such horrors could be withstood by ordinary strength.\par
\par
The delights of pleasure are seldom equal to the anticipation of them,\par
and it is probable that the pain of suffering is more unbearable in the\par
shrinking expectation than when affliction actually opens her furnace\par
door and commands us to enter. Perhaps there is a compensation of some\par
kind in nature, a provision to deaden feeling when a death stroke\par
falls--some merciful dispensation by which we fail to realize or to\par
understand in its exactness the meaning of the stroke which is crushing\par
us.\par
\par
The man rescued from drowning or from asphyxiation has felt no pain. The\par
animal that falls beneath the rush and the murderous claws of a beast of\par
prey seems to fall into a torpor-like indifference, under the influence\par
of which he meets with no great suffering the death his captor brings\par
him. Probably all great suffering comes accompanied with a reserve of\par
strength or with a power of resistance which may even spring from\par
weakness, but which invests the sufferer with courage, and perhaps, too,\par
with hope, to meet it. [Transcriber's note: words are missing here on\par
the original] but the pitiless application of a discipline designed with\par
consummate skill to find out all the weak points of a man's inner armor\par
and to inflict the utmost possible suffering upon him, I used to ask\par
myself if it could be possible that I was really the man upon whom so\par
hideous a fate had fallen.\par
\par
The blackness of darkness was round about me. Infinite despair stood\par
ready to seize me. It seemed an amazement that life should be forced to\par
remain with him who longs for death, who would rejoice exceedingly and\par
be glad could he find the grave. But when the first horrible numbness of\par
the shock was disappearing, when the first glimmering perception came to\par
me that "as a man's day so shall his strength be," I began to suspect,\par
and soon to know, that in many ways the reality was not so terrible as\par
imagination pictured it.\par
\par
However ample the provision be which men may make to inflict suffering\par
upon other men, however well and successfully they may apply the\par
provision, they cannot alter men's nature. That will assert itself under\par
all circumstances. The fact that a man is restrained of his liberty by\par
no means alters his nature. The things he liked or disliked when he was\par
at liberty he will like or dislike when a prisoner, and he is not long\par
in finding that "whatsoever a man soweth that shall he also reap" is\par
just as certainly true of the seed he plants in inclosed ground as it is\par
of what he scatters in the open field.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLVI.\par
\par
IF PAIN IS NOT AN EVIL, IT CERTAINLY IS A VERY GOOD IMITATION.\par
\par
\par
The world inside of the walls has a public opinion of its own, and it is\par
at least quite as often just as the public opinion whose sphere is not\par
circumscribed by stone walls and iron bars. The man who accepts the\par
situation, resolved to get his hand as easily as possible out of the\par
tiger's mouth, soon becomes known as a sensible fellow, willing to give\par
others no trouble and anxious to have no trouble given him. Such a man\par
will rarely be molested.\par
\par
Patient, uncomplaining endurance always excites pity and sympathy. The\par
most ignorant, the most brutal warder will scarcely oppress the man who\par
goes quietly and unresistingly along the thorny road stretched out\par
before him; who, not taking the thorns for roses, is not disappointed at\par
finding few roses among the thorns.\par
\par
Those, however, who are determined to see the rough side of prison life\par
may easily do so; the appliances are there and they will certainly be\par
accommodated. An English prison is a vast machine in which a man counts\par
for just nothing at all. He is to the establishment what a bale of\par
merchandise is to a merchant's warehouse. The prison does not look upon\par
him as a man at all. He is merely an object which must move in a certain\par
rut and occupy a certain niche provided for it. There is no room for the\par
smallest sentiment. The vast machine of which he is an item keeps\par
undisturbed upon its course.\par
\par
Move with it, and all is well. Resist, and you will be crushed as\par
inevitably as the man who plants himself on the railroad track when the\par
express is coming. Without passion, without prejudice, but also without\par
pity and without remorse, the machine crushes and passes on. The dead\par
man is carried to his grave and in ten minutes is as much forgotten as\par
though he had never existed.\par
\par
The plank bed, the crank, the bread-and-water diet, unauthorized but\par
none the less effectual clubbing at the hands of warders, the cold in\par
the punishment cells penetrating to the very marrow of the bones,\par
weakness, sickness and unpitied death are the certain portion of the\par
rebel.\par
\par
Some are found idiotic enough to invite such a fate, though fewer now\par
than formerly. The progress of education in England during the last\par
twenty years, and the philanthropic efforts of many societies and\par
private persons, but above all the covert but successful efforts of the\par
authorities to deport them to this country instantly after their\par
release, have had an immense effect in thinning the ranks of prison\par
inmates. The Judges, too, have been forced by public opinion to be much\par
less severe than they used to be, and that counts for much even in the\par
inside of prisons.\par
\par
Nothing can be more capricious than the sentences they pass. In very few\par
cases does the law set any limit. "Life or any term not less than five\par
years" is the usual reading of the statute books, and the consequence\par
naturally is that one Judge will give his man five years, while another\par
will condemn his to twenty years for precisely the same crime committed\par
under precisely the same circumstances as the first one.\par
\par
Another great blot on the English judicial system is that no court of\par
appeal exists to which a sentence might be referred for review, so that\par
the most unjust and unequal sentences are constantly passed from which\par
there is no appeal but in the forlorn hope--rather, entire\par
hopelessness--of a petition to the Home Secretary. I have often seen a\par
man who had been sentenced to five years for murder working by the side\par
of another whose sentence was twenty years for some crime against\par
property. Such contrasts, of course, excite great discontent, and in\par
some cases are the reason why men set up a hopeless resistance to what\par
they feel to be persecution and injustice.\par
\par
It always seemed to me that the standpoint of the Board of Directors,\par
established in 1864, and which continued without change until very\par
recently, was altogether wrong. They appeared to think that in their\par
dealings with other men the only course was to be the application of\par
"force, iron force," as one of the governors expressed it. The very\par
great majority require no such application, and the few difficult ones\par
could easily be managed in another way. Certainly it is necessary that\par
all prison discipline be penal, but it is not necessary that it be\par
ferocious and inhuman, as certainly is the English. Starvation, the\par
crank, the plank bed, the fearful cold of the cells are not measures\par
necessary in dealing with any man.\par
\par
Whatever they could think of to harden, to degrade, to insult, to\par
inflict every form of suffering, both physical and mental, which a man\par
could undergo and live, was embodied in the rules they made. Their\par
prisons were to be places of suffering and of nothing but suffering.\par
\par
So far as the directors were concerned the regulations were carried out\par
to the letter, but each prison is under the control of a resident\par
governor, with a deputy governor to assist him. These gentlemen are\par
always men of good social position, retired officers of the army, who\par
have seen the world and have experience in controlling men. They are\par
rarely inclined to unnecessary severity, but are generally willing to\par
apply the rules with as much consideration as such rules admit. The\par
governor's discretion, however, is limited, but daily contact more or\par
less with men whom he sees to differ very little from free men, and whom\par
he sometimes finds to be even better than many he knows who are not, but\par
who perhaps ought to be, on the wrong side of the bars, makes him\par
unwilling to throw too many sharp points on the path which has to be\par
trodden by men for whom he often cannot help feeling considerable\par
sympathy.\par
\par
I have more than once heard governors express their disapproval of the\par
starvation system and of the ferocity of treatment toward men who some\par
day or other must go back to society.\par
\par
Under such governors the new arrival speedily finds out that to a\par
certain extent his comfort depends upon himself. No man can make a bad\par
thing good or trick himself into believing that suffering is pleasure.\par
If pain be not an evil, it is an exceedingly good imitation, and the\par
wisest philosopher is just as restless under the toothache as the most\par
perfect idiot.\par
\par
[Illustration: PENTONVILLE PRISON.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLVII.\par
\par
HIS ROW BECOMES FILLED WITH VERY SHARP-EDGED STONES INDEED.\par
\par
\par
The inhabitant of a cell has a very rough row to hoe under any\par
circumstance, and it has to be hoed, but there is no necessity for him\par
to fill his row with stones and to plant roots in it himself. He soon\par
finds his level, and the impression he makes on his arrival is the one\par
which, as a rule, clings to him to the end.\par
\par
When prison air and prison influence have succeeded in incasing a man\par
with the sort of moral hardbake that renders him callous to those\par
feelings which at first so gall the raw spots, he finds himself watching\par
with curiosity the shapings of newcomers. Some announce immediately on\par
arrival that they cannot possibly be there more than a month or two;\par
their arrest was a mistake, and their uncle, the member of Parliament,\par
is now busily engaged making representations to the Home Secretary. One\par
of the very few amusements prisoners have is in watching the important\par
fellows, the men whose friends could do so much for them if they would\par
only let them know where they are. Sometimes a chap who has perhaps been\par
a body servant or something of the kind, who has picked up the kind of\par
veneer he could catch by aping his master, will furnish food for smiles\par
to every one he comes in contact with during his stay. He never receives\par
a letter without explaining confidentially to every one that another\par
aunt whose favorite he was has just died, leaving him L10,000 in cash,\par
not to speak of a trifle or two in the shape of half a dozen houses.\par
These gentlemen are immediately furnished with a name which becomes much\par
better known than their own, and whenever they have delivered themselves\par
of their periodical brooding of lies the news goes smiling round that\par
Billy Treacle's aunt has died again and left him another fortune.\par
\par
So long as their inventions do no more harm than make them ridiculous,\par
they are only laughed at and let alone, but when one of them develops a\par
talent for invention which molests or injures others, especially when it\par
takes the form of confidential communication to the governor of what he\par
sees, and still more of what he does not see, such retribution as both\par
prisoners and officers can inflict is not long in falling. His row\par
becomes filled with very sharp-edged stones indeed, and roots which tear\par
his hands painfully. Nearly always these boastings are fathered by an\par
absurd vanity--a desire ever to appear what they are not, and while they\par
think they are deceiving others they deceive no one but themselves.\par
\par
One case I remember, though, was an exception. One young fellow made\par
such use of his invention, and the story is so interesting and\par
instructive as showing with what lofty respect English gentlemen are\par
educated for the rights of property, that I shall relate it.\par
\par
Four or five years after I went to Chatham a young fellow named\par
Frederick Barton arrived with a ten years' sentence for forgery. His\par
appearance and manners were very much in his favor, and his conduct so\par
confirmed the good first impression that he speedily became a favorite\par
with everybody from the governor down.\par
\par
Some three years had slipped by when one day he asked me if I would\par
prepare a petition which he might send to the Home Secretary in the hope\par
of obtaining a commutation of sentence. I liked the youngster very well\par
and readily consented, but told him that I doubted very much if he\par
would get anything. The petition was sent, and in a few days the usual\par
answer was returned, "No grounds." He told me of his ill luck, and I\par
said to him: "Look here, so long as you send up whining petitions asking\par
for mercy both you and they will be treated with contempt. If you wish\par
to get that English gentleman in the Home Office to do anything for you,\par
make him believe you are a millionaire; you will see whether he will do\par
anything then for you or not." He laughed merrily at that. "A\par
millionaire! Why, I haven't a sixpence. My father is only a private\par
coachman at Tunbridge Wells." "That is nothing at all," I said; "if you\par
will be guided by me, and let me manage things for you, I will have a\par
petition sent in for you from the outside, and I feel sure we can get\par
you out." An idea had just flashed into my mind, and I was eager to try\par
it.\par
\par
At first he was a little timid about the venture, fearing that I might\par
get him into trouble, but when he became convinced that I would do\par
nothing of the kind he consented. I had a warder in the prison who in\par
consideration of an occasional tip used to act as my postman, sending my\par
letters to my friends and bringing in theirs to me. This was a deadly\par
offense against the rules, but as the permitted correspondence was\par
outrageously limited I saw no reason why I should deprive myself of\par
letters when I had the chance to have them, and as I took good care that\par
the great men in London should get no inkling of my misdeeds I dare say\par
their hearts did not grieve after what their eyes did not see.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLVIII.\par
\par
HE TELEGRAPHED THE NEWS TO MY WARDER, AND BARTON WENT ON HIS WAY\par
REJOICING.\par
\par
\par
My warder friend supplied me with writing materials. I prepared one\par
letter, which I had him copy, and another in my own handwriting. Both\par
were directed to Barton, and informed him that his rich uncle had lately\par
died and had left him one hundred and sixty thousand pounds in money and\par
sixteen thousand acres of cotton land in India. He was also informed\par
that his father had gone to India to look after the property, and that\par
upon his return a petition would be presented to the Home Secretary, who\par
it was hoped would grant his release. These two letters my warder sent\par
to a friend of mine in London with a note from me requesting him to post\par
them immediately. I told Barton what I had done, at the same time\par
cautioning him to guard the closest secrecy. Two days afterward the\par
letters arrived, and I directed my protege to spread the news as much as\par
possible, to tell all the warders he saw and to show them his letters.\par
We had at that time in the prison a wideawake but tricky fellow named\par
George Smith. He had been clerk to an important firm of auctioneers in\par
London, and had been sentenced by probably the most savage judge on the\par
bench, Commissioner Ker, to fourteen years' imprisonment for receiving a\par
quantity of stolen silverware, which he had his employers sell for him.\par
He was about to be released, and I determined to make use of him, but\par
without letting him know the truth, for I knew that if he suspected he\par
was merely doing a good turn for the chum he left behind him, he, like\par
the Home Secretary himself, without the right kind of inducement would\par
have left his friend to stop where he was until the bottomless pit was\par
frozen over hard enough to hold a barbecue on it. Barton, by my\par
directions, told Smith of his good fortune, and that he hoped on his\par
father's return to be liberated. Smith then did exactly what I expected\par
and wanted him to do. He said there was no need to wait until then; he\par
was going to be released in a few days, and "if you like I will send in\par
a petition for you; it can't do you any harm, and it may get you\par
released immediately." Barton at once accepted the offer, and told him\par
that if successful the post of manager on the Indian estate would be at\par
his disposal. He also suggested to ask me to write the petition. Smith\par
managed to see me in the course of the day, and, supposing me to have no\par
knowledge of the matter, explained the situation and asked me to write\par
the petition. Needless to say, I promised everything asked for, and\par
added that I would make it my business to have the petition in London at\par
some place where he could find it the day of his discharge.\par
\par
[Illustration: BANK-NOTE STORE-ROOM, BANK OF ENGLAND.]\par
\par
[Illustration: VISITORS AT NEWGATE STANDING OVER THE BURYING-VAULT DOOR\par
LEADING TO THE BLACK-MARIA.]\par
\par
The petition was prepared, setting forth all the interesting facts for\par
the edification of the right honorable gentleman in the Home Office, and\par
after being submitted to Barton and Smith, sent to the latter's address\par
in London.\par
\par
Millbank is a gigantic prison in the heart of London every one of the\par
thousand cells of which cost the Government L300 to build. This is the\par
establishment where David Copperfield visited Mr. Uriah Heep when that\par
gentleman was under a cloud, and heard him express the wish that\par
"everybody might get 'took up' so that they could learn the error of\par
their ways." For many years all London men whose sentences had expired\par
were brought here for release, and here Smith came a few days after the\par
petition was posted. On the morning of his discharge and within an\par
hour after passing through the gates of Millbank he left the petition\par
personally at the Home Office. Two days afterward one of the clerks\par
acknowledged its receipt, accompanied with the gratifying assurance that\par
it was under consideration. A week later Mr. Smith was notified that the\par
release would be granted. He immediately telegraphed the news to my\par
warder, who told me, and I told Barton. Two days more and the release\par
came down, Barton went on his way rejoicing and every one was glad at\par
his happy fortune. The only one who felt much disappointment was very\par
likely poor Smith, who never heard of his friend again.\par
\par
[Illustration: SCHOOL AND A TRADE, OR JAIL.]\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XLIX.\par
\par
I FLUSTER THE GREAT JUPITER OF MY LITTLE WORLD.\par
\par
\par
The successful issue of this little enterprise gave me great\par
satisfaction. There was, of course, nothing in it for me, nor did I want\par
anything, but it furnished me with an excellent standpoint from which to\par
address the Home Secretary should the occasion ever arise.\par
\par
The occasion did arise some time after, and I utilized it in this way: A\par
friend of mine had come over from America to see me and to try if it\par
were not possible to obtain some reduction in the sentence. My postman\par
warder was away at the moment, so letter-carrier facilities were cut\par
off. I wanted very much indeed to communicate with my friend, and\par
applied to the Home Secretary explaining the position and asking him to\par
let me write two letters immediately. At the end of eight weeks an\par
answer came back that the Home Secretary had carefully considered the\par
application and could find no sufficient grounds for advising Her\par
Majesty to grant the prayer thereof. The next day I obtained a petition\par
sheet from the governor and wrote the following petition:\par
\par
"To the Right Hon. Sir William V. Harcourt, Secretary of State for the\par
Home Department:\par
\par
"The petition of, etc., humbly showeth: That two months ago I petitioned\par
the Home Secretary for permission to write two letters, explaining the\par
urgency of the occasion and pointing out that the request was by no\par
means unusual. Yesterday the answer arrived telling me, with as much\par
truth, I have no doubt, as kindness, the anxiety with which the right\par
honorable gentleman has been for eight weeks considering the petition.\par
\par
"I hasten to express to the Home Secretary the regret I cannot but feel\par
at the thought of causing him so much concern, which I sincerely trust\par
has had no prejudicial effect upon his health. I regret this the more as\par
there was really no necessity for requiring eight whole weeks of his\par
time to the inevitable great neglect of the public business, for no man\par
who owns or who is known to be able to get a half sovereign ever has the\par
slightest difficulty in sending out as many clandestine letters as he\par
chooses. This, of course, is an infraction of the rules, and any\par
reasonable man would rather get along in a friendly spirit with the\par
prison authorities than be at war with them, but when trifling favors\par
which it requires but to stretch out the hand to take are refused,\par
rules, prison authorities and the Home Secretary himself are\par
contemptuously set aside and the forbidden favor taken.\par
\par
"I trust that this knowledge will save the Home Secretary any repetition\par
of the anxiety he has suffered on this occasion, but while regretting my\par
want of success in petitions for myself I desire to thank the right\par
honorable gentleman for the kind attention he pays to my petitions for\par
others.\par
\par
"The Home Secretary will perhaps remember his merciful consideration of\par
the case of Mr. Frederick Barton, whom he released some short time ago,\par
but it will perhaps be news to him to hear that it was I who invented\par
Mr. Barton's fortune and wrote the petition which furnished the grounds\par
for advising Her Most Gracious Majesty to extend her royal clemency to\par
the deserving young man. The result of my petition by no means surprised\par
me, for I was always confident that an English gentleman could never be\par
guilty of the solecism against English customs implied by keeping in\par
prison a young gentleman who could perform so meritorious an act as to\par
fall heir to many bags of gold and sixteen thousand acres of cotton land\par
in India.\par
\par
"Mr. Barton had previously petitioned for mercy pointing out that he was\par
but 17 years old at the time of his arrest, and asking that his extreme\par
youth might plead for him. This petition the Home Secretary treated with\par
very proper contempt, but it was really delightful to contrast that\par
contempt with the respectful and instant attention shown to the\par
petition of the young heir.\par
\par
"I have a difficulty in expressing the comfort with which I saw an\par
English Home Secretary, with all the power of the Empire in his hands to\par
protect him against imposition, releasing a criminal after reading a\par
sheet of foolscap covered with lies, which had been left at the Home\par
Office by a released convict within half an hour after passing through\par
the gates of Millbank. It is but the merest justice, however, to add\par
that poor Mr. Smith, the presenter of the petition, was as badly\par
humbugged as the Home Secretary himself. The glitter of gold was flashed\par
before his eyes as it was before the eyes of Sir William Vernon\par
Harcourt, and with equal effect.\par
\par
"To me this effect was certain, as not the slightest doubt existed in my\par
mind that the moment it became a question of great sums of money all\par
distinctions would vanish and pickpocket and Home Secretary would\par
scramble on to the same foothold.\par
\par
"The result, it is unnecessary to add, perfectly justified me. As I\par
watched the lucky Frederick set out to return to the stable he came from\par
it occurred to me that had he understood German, which he did not, nor\par
English either, for that matter, he might have whispered joyfully to\par
himself, in the words of another dealer in ways that are dark and tricks\par
which are vain:\par
\par
  "'Es ist gar hubsch von einem grossen Herrn,\par
  So menschlich mit dem Teufel selbst zu sprechen.'\par
\par
     "Doubtless, however, the Home Secretary will feel, as I do myself,\par
     that he acted in this matter in accordance with the commonest\par
     dictates of duty, and I beg to assure him that, having every\par
     facility for sending out as many letters as I please, I shall never\par
     again cause him weeks of anxious consideration. Respectfully\par
     submitted,\par
\par
                                                  "AUSTIN BIDWELL."\par
\par
Whatever Sir William Vernon Harcourt may have thought about the\par
petition, he said nothing, but I dare say he did not feel flattered. It\par
required no small daring to send it, but as I knew I had nothing to hope\par
from him I could look with perfect equanimity upon any consequences\par
likely to follow.\par
\par
The governor of the prison did not dare to violate the regulations by\par
refusing to send my petition, written as it was on an official form and\par
duly entered on the books of the establishment, but he sent for me in\par
hot haste. Assuming a threatening air, he demanded how I dared to play\par
such monkey tricks. Officially the governor was a hot member and\par
enforced an iron discipline both with wardens and the men, but\par
personally he was not a bad fellow, so I merely laughed and asked him if\par
he was a critic and reviser of petitions; therefore, a local Home\par
Secretary. He saw I was not to be intimidated, and almost begged of me\par
not to do so any more. As he was a pretty good fellow, and I had no wish\par
to cause him any embarrassment, I readily promised, provided I was\par
permitted now and then to write a special letter. This permission he\par
intimated would not be withheld, and there, so far as the governor was\par
concerned, the incident ended. But so unheard-of a document emanating\par
from a prisoner created a sensation among the officers, who all came to\par
know of the matter, and added several degrees to whatever respect they\par
were inclined to have for me.\par
\par
As there is no attempt at humor in this book, and since I am on the\par
subject of petitions, I will give here a copy of one sent by a fellow\par
prisoner who was somewhat of a character and whose name was Niblo Clark.\par
\par
To some of the prisoners the art of reading and writing is an all but\par
insoluble mystery. Every man is allowed a small slate, and many of the\par
prisoners spend an incredible amount of painful toil and mental\par
wrestling in preparing a petition, which, by the way, never does any\par
good. Poor Niblo for a whole year, through all the Summer's warmth and\par
Winter's frost, spent his spare hours producing this petition, and I\par
think my reader will agree with me that it is a masterpiece of its\par
kind.\par
\par
       PETITION.\par
\par
       Register No. Y 19.  Name, Niblo Clark,\par
       Present Age, 40. Confined in Chatham Prison.\par
       Date of Petition, January 15, 1890.\par
\par
           CONVICTED.          CRIME.        SENTENCE.     REMARKS.\par
       When.   Where.\par
       1880. Old Bailey,      Burglary.       15 Years.    In Hospital.\par
                London.                                    Troublesome.\par
\par
     To the Right Honorable Henry Mathews, Her Majesty's Principal\par
     Secretary of State for the Home Department:\par
\par
     The Petition of Niblo Clark Humbly Sheweth--\par
\par
     The Right Honorable Secretary the great benefit your humble\par
     petitioner would derive by a speedy removal from this damp and\par
     foggy inhospitable Climate to a milder one; the atmostphere here\par
     his thoroughly prejudicial to your petitioners health and causes me\par
     to be a great Sufferer i am Suffering from asthma accompanied with\par
     bad attacks of Chronic bronchitis and have been now 3 long years\par
     Confined to a bed of Sickness in a Sad and pitable Condition and\par
     upon those Clear grounds and physical proofs your petitioner humbly\par
     prays that it may please the Right Honorable Secretary to order my\par
     removal to a warmer and milder Climate necessity also compels me to\par
     complain of repeated acts of injustice and Cruely committed again\par
     me, and which in some respects Might Justly undergo the imputation\par
     of ferocity there are numbers and frivolous and false charges\par
     conspired against me and every time i am discharged from here the\par
     Governor takes them Seperate one each and trys to murder me: i have\par
     been No less then Six weeks at one time on bread and Water\par
     accompanied with a little penal Class and all the officers are\par
     incouraged to practise all kinds of barbarious maltreatment against\par
     me and other sick men--theres is one officer here place here for\par
     the express purpose of tantelizing me and other his Name is Warder\par
     Newcombe this officer sir has barbariously struck and assaulted\par
     patients on there Sick bed and Several has complained of it to the\par
     Governor--But i am Sorry to say its greatly fostered and incouraged\par
     especially upon me it is quite useless to complain of anything to\par
     the Governor.\par
\par
  Right Honourable Sir i humbly beg that you will listen to my woe\par
  for what i Suffer in Chatham prison the one half you do not Know\par
  From repeated attacks of this frightful disease i am getting worse\par
   each day\par
  So i humbly trust you will have me removed without the least delay\par
\par
  In making my request in poetry Sir i hope you wont think i am Joking\par
  for the greatest favour you can bestowe upon me is to Send me back to\par
    Woking\par
  For in this damp and foggy Climate its impossible to ever get better\par
  So i humbly trust in addition to this you will grant me a Special letter\par
\par
  Another little case i wish to State if you Sir will Kindly listen\par
  has it would Cause a Vast amount of talk all round and about the prison\par
  I mean if Niblo Clark Should be sent upon some public Works\par
  it would cause more talk then the late dispute between the russians and\par
   the turks\par
\par
  in foggy wheather with my disease it would be impossible to larst one\par
    hour\par
  and if you doubt the accuracy of what i say i refere to doctor Power\par
  or any other naval doctor or one from the army garrison\par
  they one and all would say the Same and likewise Doctor Harrison\par
\par
  Since my reception in this here prison i have been a most unfortunate man\par
  and i will tell you the why and wherefore as well as i possibly Can\par
  for every time i been in this hospital its the whole truth what i Say\par
  for my medical treatment i assure Sir i have dearly had to pay\par
\par
  A regular marked man i have been for them all its well known to Captain\par
   Harris\par
  for the list of reports against me would reach from this place to paris\par
  So i humbly beg Right Honourable Sir you will grant this humble petition\par
  for i am sorry to State i have nothing to pay having lost both health\par
   and remission\par
\par
  Such Cruel injustice to poor Sick men is far from being just and right\par
  but to report Sick patients in hospital is the officers Chief delight\par
  But perhaps kind Sir you might imagine that they only do this to a dodger\par
  But its done to all--Austin Bidwell as well and likewise to poor Sir\par
   Roger (Tichborne).\par
\par
  like Savage lions in this infirmary the Officers about are walking\par
  to Catch and report a dying poor man for the frivolous Charge of talking\par
  and when we go out from hospital our poor bodies they try to Slaughter\par
  by taking these reports one at the time and Killing us on bread and water\par
\par
  I am suffering a Chest and throat disease a frightful Chronic disorder\par
  and to go out from hospital is attempting Suicide to get heaps of bread\par
   and Water\par
  for it is such cruel treatment made me as i am and brought me to the\par
   Verge of the grave\par
  So in conclusion Right Honourable Sir a removal i humbly Crave\par
\par
if this petition should not be sent prisoners abstain from further\par
writting who will explain his case more Clearly to the Visiting director\par
and i wish to have this petition Submitted to the director by your truly\par
humble servant Niblo Clark.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER L.\par
\par
IT WAS NIGHT; SILENCE AND GLOOM HAD SETTLED DOWN ON THE INMATES.\par
\par
\par
By a refinement of cruelty we had been separated and sent to prison wide\par
apart; for twenty years I had not seen the face of one of my friends.\par
But there was an invisible bond between us that no tyranny could break.\par
How blessed the happy forethought that made us, in that dark hour, amid\par
our despair, make that promise!\par
\par
Ten years had slowly dragged by, 1883 came, and my devoted family felt\par
that I, and my comrades, too, had paid, as was right, our due to\par
justice, and we ought to be liberated. They determined that it would not\par
be their fault if I remained in captivity. So that year my sister came\par
to England and remained permanently there. She worked bravely and well,\par
but year after year passed without result. None of us was prepared for\par
the vindictive fury of the Bank of England--its power was all-potent\par
with the Government. George had been bedridden for years, and was slowly\par
dying. At length, in 1887, the medical officer of the prison certified\par
his speedy death was certain, and the Government released him to die;\par
but he resolved that he would not die until we were free. With liberty\par
and hope health came slowly back, and he devoted every hour to working\par
for our liberation; but for a time devoted in vain. More than once had I\par
seen the prison emptied and filled again. Of all the life prisoners I\par
had met there on my arrival, or who for years after had joined me, I was\par
the sole survivor.\par
\par
One by one sickness or insanity, born of despair, had laid them in the\par
prison graveyard or buried them in the asylum. Out of more than seventy\par
life prisoners none had lived to be liberated, and determined appeared\par
the Bank of England directors that I should not form an exception; but\par
that if ever the prison doors were opened to me it should be only when\par
so near death that I might join the many who had gone before.\par
\par
My fate seemed inevitable, but never for a moment did I cease to believe\par
that Fortune's frowns would one day disappear and that I should yet\par
again feel the warmth and sunshine of her smile. From his sick bed, and\par
in his health, our comrade never ceased his efforts. He succeeded in\par
interesting James Russell Lowell and many others in my behalf. The\par
President asked the English Government officially to grant my release.\par
Mr. Blaine, the Secretary of State, sent a very strong letter through\par
Minister Lincoln in London, and I thought when told of it that my day to\par
go was not far away.\par
\par
It will interest Americans, perhaps, to hear that the representations of\par
the President and of the Secretary of State of the United States met the\par
same courtesy as was shown to all the previous ones. Still, George was\par
not discouraged. He sent agents to England, who managed to interest the\par
newspapers in the matter, and never did he cease, until by the\par
statements of the press upon the ferocity of my treatment, the\par
reproaches of my friends and the representations of many I had never\par
seen, including Lady Henry Somerset, Mrs. Helen Densmore (then residing\par
in London) and the Duke of Norfolk, at last the Home Secretary felt the\par
pressure, and all unwillingly--"much against his will," as he termed\par
it--was forced to order my release.\par
\par
       *       *       *       *       *\par
\par
"Thou shalt forget thy misery and remember it as waters that pass\par
away."\par
\par
Twenty years had passed away since I had bade my friends good-bye under\par
the Old Bailey, and now 1893 had come. It was a frosty February night,\par
and I was alone in that little room with its arched roof and stone\par
floor. It was past 7 o'clock, and the prison gloom and stillness had\par
settled down on all the inmates, when suddenly there came the noise of\par
hurrying feet that echoed strangely from the arched roof as the warders\par
tramped loudly on the stone floor of the long hall. A rush of feet, or,\par
indeed, anything that broke the horrible stillness at that hour, was\par
startling. They were the feet of the reserve guard, which was never\par
called in save when the patrol who glided around the corridors in\par
slippered feet discovered some suicide. Many a heartbroken man had I\par
known in that twenty years who in his despair ended his misery thus.\par
\par
While wondering who the unfortunate could be I heard their steps\par
mounting the stairway leading to my landing, and then a sudden thrill\par
shot through me as they turned down the corridor toward my cell. My\par
heart stood still as I thought, could they be coming for me? I had a\par
sudden frenzy of fear that they might pass my door, but no, they came\par
straight on, halted, and Ross, a principal officer--I had known him\par
twenty years--gave a thundering rap on my door and shouted, "I want\par
you!" Then a key rattled in the lock, the door was thrown open and three\par
friendly faces looked in. Faint, deadly white, trembling like a\par
frightened child, I started to my feet trying to speak, but no sound\par
came from my lips for a moment. At last I stammered, "What's the\par
matter?" Ross thrust his form through the door, and with face close to\par
mine said the thrilling words, "You're free!" I cried, "I don't believe\par
you!" and Ross said: "Come on, my boy; it's all right."\par
\par
Like one in a dream I passed out through the door of that little cell\par
whose grim, narrow walls had frowned on me for a score of years and had\par
in vain tried to crush my spirit.\par
\par
Still like one in a dream I went down that long hall listening only to\par
the strange sound of my own footsteps and saying to myself: "It is all a\par
dream. I will awake, as I have from thousands of like dreams, and find\par
myself again in my dungeon."\par
\par
I was led into the outer office, where some papers were read to me, and\par
then others given me to sign, but I listened or signed like one in a\par
maze. Suddenly I saw Ross thrust the key into the outer door. That\par
roused me, and the thought flashed into my mind, now I will see a star.\par
\par
The heavy door rolled on its hinges, the ponderous gate was flung back.\par
Stepping out, I intuitively looked up, and a sudden awe fell upon me,\par
for there, like a revelation, shone the Milky Way, with its millioned\par
arch of radiant suns. At the sight of that miracle of glory, my heart\par
beat fast. I realized that I was free, with health and strength, with\par
courage to begin again the battle of life, and in my irrepressible\par
emotion I cried aloud, and my cry was like a prayer--"God is good."\par
\par
